window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("1corinthians", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"1 Corinthians","chapters":16,"bigPicture":{"subtitle":"The Cross in the Most Ambitious City in Greece","paragraphs":["Stand on the narrow neck of land that joins mainland Greece to the Peloponnese and you are standing in the most strategic four miles of the ancient Mediterranean. On the west, the port of Lechaeum faces Italy. On the east, the port of Cenchreae faces Asia. Between them ran the diolkos, a paved stone trackway on which ships were dragged overland on wheeled platforms so captains could avoid the deadly voyage around the southern cape. Every cargo, every sailor, every new idea and every new god crossed that isthmus — and the city that sat on it took a cut of all of it. That city was Corinth. And around AD 50 a tentmaker with a recent beating behind him walked into it alone.","This was not old Greek Corinth. The Roman general Lucius Mummius had levelled that city in 146 BC, and for a century the site lay largely empty. Then in 44 BC Julius Caesar refounded it as a Roman colony and filled it with freed slaves, veterans, and ambitious nobodies. Within a hundred years it was the capital of the province of Achaia, rich, loud, and new money to its bones — a city where nobody's grandfather had been anybody, and where everybody was therefore desperate to be seen as someone. Status was the air Corinth breathed. Men erected inscriptions to their own generosity. Orators were followed like celebrities. The Isthmian Games, second only to Olympia, were held a few miles away every two years. (You will hear one honest caution in this study more than once: the famous claim that a thousand temple prostitutes worked the shrine of Aphrodite on the Acrocorinth comes from the geographer Strabo and describes the older Greek city, and many historians doubt it for the Roman one. You do not need the legend. The letter itself tells you plainly enough what Corinth was like.)","Paul stayed eighteen months — “He lived there a year and six months, teaching the word of God among them” (Acts 18:11) — longer than almost anywhere else he planted. He worked with his hands beside Aquila and Priscilla, refugees from Claudius' expulsion of the Jews from Rome. The ruler of the synagogue, Crispus, believed with his whole household. And when the proconsul Gallio threw the case against Paul out of court, he left historians a gift: an inscription found at Delphi dates Gallio's term in Achaia to around AD 51–52, which makes Paul's time in Corinth one of the firmest fixed points in the entire chronology of the New Testament. Then Paul left. And a few years later, from Ephesus across the Aegean (16:8), he received news that broke his heart.","The news came from three directions. People from Chloe's household reported quarrels (1:11). There were rumours of something worse — a sexual sin the pagans themselves would not tolerate (5:1). And a delegation arrived with a letter from the church full of questions — about marriage, about meat offered to idols, about spiritual gifts, about the collection — which Paul answers one by one, marking each with the same little phrase, “Now concerning…” (7:1, 7:25, 8:1, 12:1, 16:1, 16:12). He had already written them once; that earlier letter is lost (5:9). So 1 Corinthians is not a treatise. It is a pastor's reply to an inbox — and that is exactly why it is the most practical book Paul ever wrote. Here is the church at its most gifted and its most carnal at the same time: speaking in tongues and suing each other, celebrating the Lord's Supper while the poor went home hungry, proud of their tolerance while one of their members slept with his father's wife.","And into that city of climbers Paul carried one message, and refused to dress it up: “For I determined not to know anything among you, except Jesus Christ, and him crucified” (2:2). Every problem in the letter is answered from that one place. Factions? Was Paul crucified for you? Lawsuits? You were bought with a price. Pride in knowledge? The brother for whose sake Christ died. Chaos in worship? The love that does not seek its own way. Denial of the resurrection? Then Christ is not raised, and you are still in your sins. Sixteen chapters, a dozen pastoral crises, and one cross standing in the middle of all of them. If Romans showed you the gospel as a cathedral, 1 Corinthians shows you the gospel as a hospital — and the patients are people exactly like you."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"Romans","intro":"The Holy Spirit placed 1 Corinthians directly after Romans, and the order is deliberate even though it is not the order of the calendar. Paul wrote 1 Corinthians first — from Ephesus, around AD 54 — and wrote Romans two or three years later, sitting inside the very church this letter is trying to rescue. The canon reverses them so that you learn the doctrine before you watch it tested:","points":[{"title":"From Strangers to Children","body":"Romans is a letter of introduction to a church Paul had never visited, and it reads like it: carefully argued, courteous, laying out the gospel in order so that strangers could trust him. First Corinthians is written to people he personally brought to Christ, and it reads like a father at the kitchen table. “I don’t write these things to shame you, but to admonish you as my beloved children. For though you have ten thousand tutors in Christ, you don’t have many fathers. For in Christ Jesus, I became your father through the Good News” (4:14–15). In Romans Paul persuades. In Corinth he pleads, warns, rebukes, teases, threatens a rod and offers gentleness in the same sentence (4:21). The shift teaches you something before you read a single chapter: truth that was proclaimed to strangers has to be lived out among family, and family is where it gets hard."},{"title":"From the Doctrine to the Floor","body":"Romans 6 told you that your old self was crucified with Christ and that you must not let sin reign in your mortal body. First Corinthians shows you what that sounds like in a congregation where a man has taken his father's wife, believers are dragging each other before pagan judges, and some members are visiting prostitutes and calling it freedom. Paul's answer is Romans 6 spoken in the past tense to real people with real histories: “Such were some of you, but you were washed. But you were sanctified. But you were justified in the name of the Lord Jesus, and in the Spirit of our God” (6:11). Notice the order. Justification is the last verb in the sentence, not the first — because these people already knew the doctrine. What they had lost was the connection between the doctrine and their bodies."},{"title":"One Body — First Drawn, Then Bleeding","body":"In Romans the body of Christ is a calm sentence: “For even as we have many members in one body, and all the members don’t have the same function, so we, who are many, are one body in Christ, and individually members one of another” (Romans 12:4–5). In 1 Corinthians that same body is torn and arguing. The word Paul uses in his opening appeal is σχίσμα (schísma, G4978) — a rip in a garment — “that there be no divisions among you” (1:10). Chapter 12 then takes the Romans sentence and turns it into a full-length anatomy lesson, with an eye telling a hand it is not needed and a foot sulking because it is not a hand. Romans gave you the doctrine of the body. Corinth shows you what happens when gifted members forget it, and chapter 13 is the cure written between the two chapters on gifts."},{"title":"The Weak Brother, Now With a Menu","body":"Romans 14 laid down the principle about food and conscience: “Don’t destroy with your food him for whom Christ died” (Romans 14:15). Corinth is where that principle has to survive an actual dinner invitation — meat that had been offered in a pagan temple and then sold in the market, or served at a feast in the temple's own dining rooms. Paul answers with the same phrase, almost word for word: “And through your knowledge, he who is weak perishes, the brother for whose sake Christ died” (8:11). The knowledge the strong were proud of was correct. Their love was missing. Romans gave you the rule. First Corinthians shows you the cost of keeping it — and Paul's own decision, which ought to stop you cold: “Therefore if food causes my brother to stumble, I will eat no meat forever more” (8:13)."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"We Preach Christ Crucified — the Power of God and the Wisdom of God","anchorRef":"1 Corinthians 1:23–24","paragraphs":["The whole letter turns on one blunt sentence: “For Jews ask for signs, Greeks seek after wisdom, but we preach Christ crucified; a stumbling block to Jews, and foolishness to Greeks, but to those who are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God” (1:22–24). Feel how offensive that was. The cross, σταυρός (staurós, G4716), was not jewellery in the first century. It was the most degrading death Rome had — reserved for slaves, rebels and the lowest criminals, so shameful that polite Romans avoided even speaking of it. Paul walks into the most status-hungry city in Greece and announces that God's wisdom is a crucified Jew. To a Greek that is μωρία (mōría, G3472) — the root of our word moron. It is the dumbest thing a man could say in public.","And Paul does not soften it. He leans into it. “I determined not to know anything among you, except Jesus Christ, and him crucified” (2:2) is not a description of a narrow preaching style; it is a strategy. He came, he says, “in weakness, in fear, and in much trembling” (2:3) — deliberately without the polished rhetoric Corinth paid to hear — “that your faith wouldn’t stand in the wisdom of men, but in the power of God” (2:5). If the gospel had been sold to them with brilliance, it could be bought back from them by someone more brilliant. A faith that rests on a performance is always one better performance away from collapse.","Watch the cross answer every problem in the book. The Corinthians were boasting about their leaders; the cross says God chose the foolish, the weak, the despised and the things that are not, “that no flesh should boast before God” (1:29). They were proud of their freedom; the cross says “you were bought with a price” (6:20). They were proud of their knowledge; the cross says the weak brother is one “for whose sake Christ died” (8:11). They were turning the Lord's Supper into a status meal; the cross says “you proclaim the Lord’s death until he comes” (11:26). Some were denying the resurrection; the cross and the empty tomb are “first of all” (15:3), the thing on which everything else stands or falls.","Paul never got over it. Years later he wrote to the Galatians, “But far be it from me to boast, except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, through which the world has been crucified to me, and I to the world” (Galatians 6:14). And he wrote to Rome from the same city of Corinth, “I am not ashamed of the Good News of Christ, because it is the power of God for salvation” (Romans 1:16) — the very word, δύναμις (dýnamis, G1411), that he uses here in 1:18 and 1:24. Corinth taught him that the cross is not merely how you get into the Christian life. It is the measuring rod for every decision inside it. Read every chapter of this letter with one question: where is the cross in this? Because Paul always knows, and by chapter 16 you will too."]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 16 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"You Belong to Christ — Not to a Personality","body":"The first sin Paul addresses is not immorality. It is fan clubs. “Each one of you says, ‘I follow Paul,’ ‘I follow Apollos,’ ‘I follow Cephas,’ and, ‘I follow Christ’” (1:12). Corinth had turned its teachers into celebrities and its church into competing brands, and Paul attacks it before anything else because it is the root of everything else. His answer is staggering: “Therefore let no one boast in men. For all things are yours, whether Paul, or Apollos, or Cephas, or the world, or life, or death, or things present, or things to come. All are yours, and you are Christ’s, and Christ is God’s” (3:21–23). You do not belong to the preacher. The preacher belongs to you — a gift God gave for your growth. And you belong to Christ. Any loyalty that competes with that, however spiritual it sounds, is the first crack in a split church."},{"title":"Your Body Is Not Your Own","body":"Corinth had a slogan, and Paul quotes it back to them twice: “All things are lawful for me” (6:12; 10:23). Many in the church apparently believed that what you did with your body did not touch your spirit — the body was a shell, temporary and unimportant. Paul flattens that idea with a doctrine the Greek world found absurd: the body is going to be raised. “Now God raised up the Lord, and will also raise us up by his power” (6:14). So the body matters forever. “Or don’t you know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit who is in you, whom you have from God? You are not your own, for you were bought with a price. Therefore glorify God in your body” (6:19–20). This is the sentence that makes holiness physical. It reaches your sexuality, your appetite, your rest, your phone, and your habits, and it tells you that every one of them belongs to Someone who paid for them with blood."},{"title":"Nothing Done in Love Is Ever Wasted","body":"The Corinthians had every gift and very little love. They spoke in tongues, prophesied, knew mysteries — and Paul tells them that without ἀγάπη (agápē, G26), all of it is “sounding brass, or a clanging cymbal” (13:1). The great love chapter was not written for a wedding. It was written as a rebuke to gifted people who were using their gifts to rank each other. And then, two chapters later, Paul gives love its eternal reason. Because Christ is raised, the dead will be raised, the perishable will put on the imperishable, and death itself will be swallowed up. “Therefore, my beloved brothers, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the Lord’s work, because you know that your labor is not in vain in the Lord” (15:58). Put chapters 13 and 15 together and you have the whole ethic of the Christian life: love is the only thing that lasts, and the resurrection guarantees that not one act of it will be lost."}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"The Name on Your Banner","body":"Every Corinthian faction would have told you their loyalty was spiritual. The Paul group loved the founder. The Apollos group loved good teaching. The Cephas group loved the original apostles. And the Christ group — the most dangerous of all — loved being above the other three. Before you begin, ask honestly: whose name do you quietly carry? A ministry, a preacher, a denomination, a style of worship, a stream of teaching you consider superior? None of those is wrong to value. But if you would feel more loyalty to it than to a brother who belongs to a different one, you are standing in Corinth. Write down the name, and hold it loosely before chapter 1 asks you, “Is Christ divided?”"},{"title":"The Thing You Call Freedom","body":"“All things are lawful for me” was not a pagan slogan. It was a Christian one — very likely a twisted echo of Paul's own preaching about grace. Which means the most dangerous lies in this letter are half-truths that started in the pulpit. So name the area of your life where you have been saying, in effect, “I am free; it doesn’t matter.” What you watch, what you drink, how you spend, where you go, who you are alone with. Paul will not answer you with a list of rules. He will answer you with two questions you should begin asking now: does it build up, and will it bring me under its power? Hold that area before God as you read, and let the letter do its surgery."}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Father, I come into this letter as a Corinthian. I live in a world that runs on status, that rewards the clever and the impressive, that tells me my worth depends on being seen — and I confess that much of that world lives in me. I have boasted in men. I have carried names and loyalties that divided me from my brothers and sisters. I have called things freedom that were really chains. I have known the right answers and loved too little. So before I read the first verse, I bring You the one thing this letter will keep pointing me to: the cross of Your Son. Let me determine, like Paul, not to know anything except Jesus Christ and Him crucified. Where my faith rests on the wisdom of men, move it onto the power of God. Where I have been proud of my gifts, show me that without love I am nothing but noise. Where I have treated my body as my own, remind me that I was bought with a price, and teach me to glorify You in it. Holy Spirit, You are the One who searches all things, even the deep things of God — open these sixteen chapters to me. Heal what is divided in me and in Your church. And by the time I reach the last chapter, let me be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in Your work, knowing that my labor is not in vain in You. In Jesus' name, Amen.”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"Is Christ Divided? — The Word of the Cross and the God Who Chooses the Nobodies","themeTitle":"“God Built His Church Out of the People Corinth Overlooked”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Paul, called to be an apostle of Jesus Christ through the will of God, and our brother Sosthenes,","understanding":"Paul's first word about himself is not a title but a summons: κλητός, called. He did not volunteer for apostleship and he did not earn it; it came “through the will of God.” In a city where every man was angling to be recognised, the letter opens with a man who insists that his position was handed to him. And notice the second name. Sosthenes may well be the synagogue ruler of Acts 18:17 who was beaten in front of Gallio's judgment seat in Corinth — the Corinthians would have known exactly who he was. If so, the man who once took a beating for opposing Paul now co-signs Paul's letter as “our brother.”","words":[{"strongs":"G2822","hebrew":"κλητός","translit":"klētós","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"}]},{"v":2,"text":"to the assembly of God which is at Corinth; those who are sanctified in Christ Jesus, called to be saints, with all who call on the name of our Lord Jesus Christ in every place, both theirs and ours:","understanding":"Before Paul says one word about their sins, he tells them who they are. They are the ἐκκλησία of God — not Paul's church, not Apollos' church — and they are “sanctified,” a perfect participle: set apart, a finished fact, already done in Christ Jesus. Then they are “called to be saints.” Holy is what they are, and holy is what they are called to become. And immediately he widens the room: they belong “with all who call on the name of our Lord Jesus Christ in every place.” A church that thought it was the centre of the Christian world is reminded in its very first sentence that it is one congregation among many.","words":[{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctified"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G1941","hebrew":"ἐπικαλέομαι","translit":"epikaléomai","english":"call on"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Grace to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.","understanding":"The standard Pauline greeting — but read it against what is coming. Grace, χάρις, is the undeserved favour that the whole letter will keep pointing back to (“What do you have that you didn’t receive?” 4:7). Peace, εἰρήνη, is the Hebrew shalom carried into Greek — wholeness, everything in right relation. A church about to be rebuked for quarrels is first blessed with peace, and its source is named twice: God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ, standing side by side as one source of one blessing.","words":[{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"}]},{"v":4,"text":"I always thank my God concerning you, for the grace of God which was given you in Christ Jesus;","understanding":"This is remarkable. Paul is about to confront factions, incest, lawsuits, drunkenness at the Lord's Table and denial of the resurrection — and he begins by thanking God for them, “always.” He is not flattering them. Look at what he thanks God for: not their behaviour, but “the grace of God which was given you.” Paul can give thanks for a mess of a church because he is looking at what God has put in them before he looks at what they have done with it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2168","hebrew":"εὐχαριστέω","translit":"eucharistéō","english":"thank"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"}]},{"v":5,"text":"that in everything you were enriched in him, in all speech and all knowledge;","understanding":"Πλουτίζω — to make wealthy. Corinth was a city obsessed with getting rich, and Paul tells them they already are, “in everything,” and specifically in the two things they prized most: λόγος (speech, eloquence) and γνῶσις (knowledge). Hold on to those two words, because they are the very gifts Corinth will misuse for the rest of the letter. Paul starts by acknowledging that the gifts are real. The problem in Corinth was never a lack of gifts.","words":[{"strongs":"G4148","hebrew":"πλουτίζω","translit":"ploutízō","english":"enriched"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"speech"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"}]},{"v":6,"text":"even as the testimony of Christ was confirmed in you:","understanding":"Βεβαιόω is a legal and commercial word: to confirm, to guarantee, to make a thing legally valid. The testimony about Christ that Paul preached was ratified in them — the gifts they received were God's own seal that the message was true. Their spiritual experience was not the foundation of the gospel; it was the confirmation of it.","words":[{"strongs":"G3142","hebrew":"μαρτύριον","translit":"martýrion","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G950","hebrew":"βεβαιόω","translit":"bebaióō","english":"confirmed"}]},{"v":7,"text":"so that you come behind in no gift; waiting for the revelation of our Lord Jesus Christ;","understanding":"They lack no χάρισμα — no grace-gift. The word is built on charis: a gift is grace taking a specific shape. But notice the posture Paul attaches to it: “waiting for the revelation of our Lord Jesus Christ.” The Corinthians behaved as if they had already arrived (4:8 — “You have already become rich. You have come to reign without us.”). Paul reminds them that every gift is given to people who are still waiting. The gifts are for the road, not proof that you have reached the destination.","words":[{"strongs":"G5302","hebrew":"ὑστερέω","translit":"hysteréō","english":"come behind"},{"strongs":"G5486","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","english":"gift"},{"strongs":"G602","hebrew":"ἀποκάλυψις","translit":"apokálypsis","english":"revelation"}]},{"v":8,"text":"who will also confirm you until the end, blameless in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ.","understanding":"Ἀνέγκλητος — unaccused, with no charge that can be made to stick. This is courtroom language for the Day of the Lord, and here is the astonishing thing: Paul says Christ will present this church, with all its sins, as blameless. Not because they are, but because He “will also confirm you until the end” — the same verb as verse 6. The God who guaranteed the testimony will guarantee the people.","words":[{"strongs":"G950","hebrew":"βεβαιόω","translit":"bebaióō","english":"confirm"},{"strongs":"G410","hebrew":"ἀνέγκλητος","translit":"anénklētos","english":"blameless"}]},{"v":9,"text":"God is faithful, through whom you were called into the fellowship of his Son, Jesus Christ, our Lord.","understanding":"The ground under the whole thanksgiving: “God is faithful.” Not the church, not Paul — God. And what He called them into is κοινωνία, a shared life, a partnership, the common ownership of something precious — fellowship with His Son. That is the word that makes verse 10 so painful. They were called into one fellowship with Christ, and they were tearing it into parties.","words":[{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G2842","hebrew":"κοινωνία","translit":"koinōnía","english":"fellowship"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Now I beg you, brothers, through the name of our Lord, Jesus Christ, that you all speak the same thing, and that there be no divisions among you, but that you be perfected together in the same mind and in the same judgment.","understanding":"Now the appeal, and it comes in the name of the Lord — the tenth time in ten verses Paul has named Jesus Christ. Σχίσμα is a tear in cloth; Jesus used it of a new patch ripping an old garment (Matthew 9:16). Against it Paul sets καταρτίζω — the word for mending nets (Matthew 4:21), for setting a broken bone, for restoring something to working order. He is not asking them to agree on everything. He is asking them to be repaired into one mind and one purpose.","words":[{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"beg"},{"strongs":"G4978","hebrew":"σχίσμα","translit":"schísma","english":"divisions"},{"strongs":"G2675","hebrew":"καταρτίζω","translit":"katartízō","english":"perfected together"},{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G1106","hebrew":"γνώμη","translit":"gnṓmē","english":"judgment"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For it has been reported to me concerning you, my brothers, by those who are from Chloe’s household, that there are contentions among you.","understanding":"Paul names his source. Chloe's people — probably members of her household, servants or business agents travelling between Corinth and Ephesus — had told him. He does not hide behind “some people are saying.” Honest confrontation names where the report came from, so it can be tested. And the word for what they reported is ἔρις: quarrelling, wrangling, rivalry — the word Paul lists among the works of the flesh in Galatians 5:20.","words":[{"strongs":"G2054","hebrew":"ἔρις","translit":"éris","english":"contentions"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Now I mean this, that each one of you says, “I follow Paul,” “I follow Apollos,” “I follow Cephas,” and, “I follow Christ.”","understanding":"Four slogans. “I follow Paul” — the founder's loyalists. “I follow Apollos” — the eloquent Alexandrian who had watered what Paul planted (Acts 18:24–28). “I follow Cephas” — Peter, probably admired by those who prized the original apostles and Jewish roots. And “I follow Christ” — the most spiritual-sounding and perhaps the worst of all, because it turns Jesus into the badge of one faction against the others. None of the leaders had asked for a fan club. The congregation built them."},{"v":13,"text":"Is Christ divided? Was Paul crucified for you? Or were you baptized into the name of Paul?","understanding":"Three questions, each expecting a horrified “No!” Μερίζω means to divide into portions, to parcel out. Can Christ be cut up and handed out to rival parties? Was Paul crucified for you? Were you baptized into the name of Paul? Paul goes after his own fan club first. He refuses to let anyone build loyalty to him on top of the cross.","words":[{"strongs":"G3307","hebrew":"μερίζω","translit":"merízō","english":"divided"},{"strongs":"G4717","hebrew":"σταυρόω","translit":"stauróō","english":"crucified"},{"strongs":"G907","hebrew":"βαπτίζω","translit":"baptízō","english":"baptized"}]},{"v":14,"text":"I thank God that I baptized none of you, except Crispus and Gaius,","understanding":"Crispus was the synagogue ruler who believed with his whole household (Acts 18:8). Gaius was wealthy enough to host the whole church (Romans 16:23). Two prominent converts, and Paul is glad he baptized almost nobody else — not because baptism does not matter, but because in Corinth a baptism had become a membership card for a party."},{"v":15,"text":"so that no one should say that I had baptized you into my own name.","understanding":"The reason is precise: so that nobody could claim to have been baptized “into my own name.” In the Greek world, initiation into a cult tied you to the one who initiated you. Paul wants no disciples tied to Paul."},{"v":16,"text":"(I also baptized the household of Stephanas; besides them, I don’t know whether I baptized any other.)","understanding":"An unpolished, honest aside — Paul remembers mid-sentence that he also baptized Stephanas' household, and then admits he cannot remember whether there were any others. This is not careless; it is the point. If baptism were about the baptizer, he would have kept a list. He did not keep one. (Stephanas will appear again in 16:15 as “the first fruits of Achaia.”)"},{"v":17,"text":"For Christ sent me not to baptize, but to preach the Good News—not in wisdom of words, so that the cross of Christ wouldn’t be made void.","understanding":"The thesis of the next four chapters in one sentence. Paul's commission was to εὐαγγελίζω — to announce good news — and to do it “not in wisdom of words,” the polished rhetoric Corinth paid its orators for. Why? Because the cross could be κενόω — emptied, voided, made hollow. A gospel sold by brilliance gets its power from the brilliance. Paul refused to let the messenger's skill compete with the message's power.","words":[{"strongs":"G2097","hebrew":"εὐαγγελίζω","translit":"euangelízō","english":"preach the Good News"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G2758","hebrew":"κενόω","translit":"kenóō","english":"made void"},{"strongs":"G4716","hebrew":"σταυρός","translit":"staurós","english":"cross"}]},{"v":18,"text":"For the word of the cross is foolishness to those who are dying, but to us who are saved it is the power of God.","understanding":"“The word of the cross” — ὁ λόγος ὁ τοῦ σταυροῦ, the message whose whole content is the cross. To those who are perishing, it is μωρία, the root of our word moron. To those being saved, it is δύναμις, power. Notice both participles are present tense: perishing, being saved. Everybody who hears the gospel is already on a road, and their reaction to the cross shows which road they are on.","words":[{"strongs":"G4716","hebrew":"σταυρός","translit":"staurós","english":"cross"},{"strongs":"G3472","hebrew":"μωρία","translit":"mōría","english":"foolishness"},{"strongs":"G622","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","english":"dying"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saved"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"}]},{"v":19,"text":"For it is written, “I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, I will bring the discernment of the discerning to nothing.”","understanding":"Paul quotes Isaiah 29:14, where God condemned the political wisdom of Jerusalem's leaders who thought they could save the nation by clever alliances. God promised to bring that wisdom to nothing. Paul says God has done it again — in a single afternoon outside Jerusalem, every human system for reaching God was exposed as useless.","words":[{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G4680","hebrew":"σοφός","translit":"sophós","english":"wise"},{"strongs":"G4907","hebrew":"σύνεσις","translit":"sýnesis","english":"discernment"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Where is the wise? Where is the scribe? Where is the lawyer of this world? Hasn’t God made foolish the wisdom of this world?","understanding":"Three figures, each a pillar of the ancient world. The σοφός, the Greek philosopher. The γραμματεύς, the Jewish scribe and scholar of the law. The συζητητής, the debater, the professional disputant who could argue any side for a fee. Where are they? The question echoes Isaiah 33:18. All three looked at the cross and missed God. God has made their wisdom μωραίνω — insipid, flavourless, foolish.","words":[{"strongs":"G4680","hebrew":"σοφός","translit":"sophós","english":"wise"},{"strongs":"G1122","hebrew":"γραμματεύς","translit":"grammateús","english":"scribe"},{"strongs":"G4804","hebrew":"συζητητής","translit":"syzētētḗs","english":"lawyer (debater)"},{"strongs":"G3471","hebrew":"μωραίνω","translit":"mōraínō","english":"made foolish"}]},{"v":21,"text":"For seeing that in the wisdom of God, the world through its wisdom didn’t know God, it was God’s good pleasure through the foolishness of the preaching to save those who believe.","understanding":"Here is the divine irony: in the wisdom of God, the world's wisdom could not find Him. God then “was pleased” — εὐδοκέω, it was His good delight — to save people through the κήρυγμα, the thing preached. Not the brilliance of the preaching but the content of the proclamation. The message that looks most foolish is the one God chose to save through.","words":[{"strongs":"G2106","hebrew":"εὐδοκέω","translit":"eudokéō","english":"good pleasure"},{"strongs":"G2782","hebrew":"κήρυγμα","translit":"kḗrygma","english":"preaching"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believe"}]},{"v":22,"text":"For Jews ask for signs, Greeks seek after wisdom,","understanding":"Two cultures, two demands. Jews asked for σημεῖα — signs, proof of power, a Messiah who would break Rome. Greeks sought σοφία — a philosophy elegant enough to satisfy the mind. Both are ways of saying: God, meet me on my terms. Every generation has its version.","words":[{"strongs":"G4592","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","english":"signs"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"}]},{"v":23,"text":"but we preach Christ crucified; a stumbling block to Jews, and foolishness to Greeks,","understanding":"And Paul's answer refuses both terms. “We preach Christ crucified” — the participle is perfect: Christ, the one who was crucified and remains the crucified one. To Jews that is a σκάνδαλον — originally the trigger-stick of a trap, the thing you trip over. Deuteronomy 21:23 said a hanged man was cursed by God; a crucified Messiah was a contradiction in terms. To Greeks it is μωρία — foolishness, because no wise man would worship a god who died like a slave.","words":[{"strongs":"G2784","hebrew":"κηρύσσω","translit":"kērýssō","english":"preach"},{"strongs":"G4717","hebrew":"σταυρόω","translit":"stauróō","english":"crucified"},{"strongs":"G4625","hebrew":"σκάνδαλον","translit":"skándalon","english":"stumbling block"},{"strongs":"G3472","hebrew":"μωρία","translit":"mōría","english":"foolishness"}]},{"v":24,"text":"but to those who are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God.","understanding":"But to the κλητοί — the called, the same word Paul used of himself in verse 1 — Christ is precisely what both groups were looking for. The Jews wanted power: Christ is the power of God. The Greeks wanted wisdom: Christ is the wisdom of God. The cross does not deny their longings. It answers them in a way they never expected.","words":[{"strongs":"G2822","hebrew":"κλητός","translit":"klētós","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"}]},{"v":25,"text":"Because the foolishness of God is wiser than men, and the weakness of God is stronger than men.","understanding":"The most daring sentence in the paragraph. Paul speaks of “the foolishness of God” and “the weakness of God” — the cross, the moment when God looked most foolish and most weak — and says that even that is wiser and stronger than the best humanity can produce. God at His apparently weakest outclasses man at his strongest.","words":[{"strongs":"G3474","hebrew":"μωρός","translit":"mōrós","english":"foolishness"},{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weakness"}]},{"v":26,"text":"For you see your calling, brothers, that not many are wise according to the flesh, not many mighty, and not many noble;","understanding":"Now look at yourselves, Paul says. “Your calling” — κλῆσις, the moment God called you. Not many σοφοί (educated), not many δυνατοί (influential, powerful), not many εὐγενεῖς (well-born, from good families). Note “not many” — not “not any.” Erastus and Gaius were people of means. But the church in the city of climbers was mostly made of people the city did not notice.","words":[{"strongs":"G2821","hebrew":"κλῆσις","translit":"klēsis","english":"calling"},{"strongs":"G4680","hebrew":"σοφός","translit":"sophós","english":"wise"},{"strongs":"G1415","hebrew":"δυνατός","translit":"dynatós","english":"mighty"},{"strongs":"G2104","hebrew":"εὐγενής","translit":"eugenḗs","english":"noble"}]},{"v":27,"text":"but God chose the foolish things of the world that he might put to shame those who are wise. God chose the weak things of the world, that he might put to shame the things that are strong;","understanding":"Three times in two verses: ἐκλέγομαι, God chose. This is not God making the best of a weak recruitment. It is His deliberate policy. He chose the μωρά, the foolish, to put the wise to shame; the ἀσθενῆ, the weak, to shame the strong. Καταισχύνω — to disgrace, to make someone blush. God's choices are designed to embarrass human pride.","words":[{"strongs":"G1586","hebrew":"ἐκλέγομαι","translit":"eklégomai","english":"chose"},{"strongs":"G3474","hebrew":"μωρός","translit":"mōrós","english":"foolish"},{"strongs":"G2617","hebrew":"καταισχύνω","translit":"kataischýnō","english":"put to shame"},{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weak"}]},{"v":28,"text":"and God chose the lowly things of the world, and the things that are despised, and the things that are not, that he might bring to nothing the things that are:","understanding":"The list descends. The ἀγενῆ — literally the unborn, people with no pedigree, nobody's children. The ἐξουθενημένα — the despised, treated as zero. And then the bottom: τὰ μὴ ὄντα, “the things that are not” — people who, socially, did not exist. God chose them to καταργέω, to render idle and useless, “the things that are.” God's preferred building material is what the world throws away.","words":[{"strongs":"G36","hebrew":"ἀγενής","translit":"agenḗs","english":"lowly"},{"strongs":"G1848","hebrew":"ἐξουθενέω","translit":"exouthenéō","english":"despised"},{"strongs":"G2673","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","english":"bring to nothing"}]},{"v":29,"text":"that no flesh should boast before God.","understanding":"The purpose clause behind the whole policy. “That no flesh” — σάρξ, humanity in its frailty and self-reliance — “should boast before God.” Καυχάομαι is one of Paul's favourite words, and it appears more in the Corinthian letters than anywhere else in the New Testament. Corinth ran on boasting. God arranged the church so that nobody in it could boast about how they got there.","words":[{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G2744","hebrew":"καυχάομαι","translit":"kaucháomai","english":"boast"}]},{"v":30,"text":"Because of him, you are in Christ Jesus, who was made to us wisdom from God, and righteousness and sanctification, and redemption:","understanding":"The great reversal: “Because of him, you are in Christ Jesus.” Your position comes from God, not from your pedigree. And Christ “was made to us wisdom from God” — and then three words that unpack what that wisdom is: δικαιοσύνη, righteousness (your standing before God); ἁγιασμός, sanctification (your holiness before God); ἀπολύτρωσις, redemption (your release, bought by a ransom). Everything Corinth was trying to get through status, Christ is.","words":[{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G38","hebrew":"ἁγιασμός","translit":"hagiasmós","english":"sanctification"},{"strongs":"G629","hebrew":"ἀπολύτρωσις","translit":"apolýtrōsis","english":"redemption"}]},{"v":31,"text":"that, according as it is written, “He who boasts, let him boast in the Lord.”","understanding":"Paul closes with Jeremiah 9:24, where God tells the wise man not to glory in his wisdom, the mighty man not in his might, the rich man not in his riches, but to glory in knowing the LORD. The very three categories of verse 26. So there is a place for boasting after all — in the Lord. The boast does not die. It moves.","words":[{"strongs":"G2744","hebrew":"καυχάομαι","translit":"kaucháomai","english":"boasts"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Torn Garment","verseRef":"v. 10-13","image":"Picture a single seamless robe laid on a table — the kind of garment that took a weaver weeks to make in one piece. Now picture four people taking hold of it by four corners, each one shouting a different name, each one pulling. You can hear the threads begin to give. That sound is σχίσμα. It is the word Jesus used when He warned that a patch of unshrunk cloth would tear an old garment and “a worse hole is made” (Matthew 9:16). And then Paul asks the question that should freeze every hand on the cloth: “Is Christ divided?” Can you cut Him into four pieces and give one to each faction?","meaning":"The opposite word in verse 10 is καταρτίζω — the word fishermen used for mending nets and doctors used for setting a broken bone. Division is not a difference of opinion; it is damage to the body of Christ, and it needs repair, not management. Notice too that none of the leaders asked for a following. Paul, Apollos and Peter were never rivals. The factions were built from below by people who wanted to belong to the winning side. And notice where the repair begins. Paul does not go after the Apollos party first. He goes after his own supporters: “Was Paul crucified for you?” The one with the most to gain from a faction is the first to dismantle it."},{"title":"The Trap-Stick and the Laughter","verseRef":"v. 18-23","image":"Two scenes. First, a forest path, and lying across it a bent sapling — the σκάνδαλον, the trigger-stick of a hunter's trap. A man walking proudly with his eyes up trips over it, and the snare closes. That is the cross to a Jew who expected a Messiah who would crush Rome. Second scene: an Athenian dinner party. Someone mentions that a new religion worships a Jewish carpenter executed as a criminal on the edge of an obscure province, and the table bursts out laughing. That is the cross to a Greek: μωρία, foolishness. In Athens, Luke records, some called Paul a babbler, and when he spoke of the resurrection, some mocked (Acts 17:18, 32).","meaning":"The cross offends both kinds of pride — the religious pride that wants God to prove Himself by power, and the intellectual pride that wants God to satisfy the mind on its own terms. Paul refuses to adjust the message to avoid either offence. He preaches “Christ crucified,” and he lets the cross do what only the cross can do: divide the hearers into those who are perishing and those who are being saved. If your presentation of the gospel never offends anyone, ask whether you have removed the cross from it. The stumbling block is not a flaw in the message. It is the message."},{"title":"The Roll Call of Nobodies","verseRef":"v. 26-28","image":"Imagine Corinth's town council at its annual gathering — men in fine togas, names carved on public monuments, families with influence, orators admired across Achaia. Now imagine the church gathered on a Sunday evening in Gaius' courtyard: dockworkers from Lechaeum smelling of salt, a slave who slipped out after the evening meal, a tentmaker, a few women whose husbands do not know where they are, a freedman still paying off his former master. A handful of wealthier people too — but mostly people the council would never have looked at. Paul says: look around the room. This is who God chose.","meaning":"The descent in verse 28 is deliberate. The lowly — people with no pedigree. The despised — people treated as zero. And finally “the things that are not” — people who, socially, did not exist. God chose them “that he might bring to nothing the things that are.” This is not God settling for what He could get. It is His strategy, repeated three times in two verses: God chose. He builds His church out of the people the world overlooks, so that when something glorious rises from it, nobody can say a human being did it. If you feel unqualified, you are exactly the kind of person God works through."},{"title":"Four Things Christ Became","verseRef":"v. 30-31","image":"Picture a beggar standing outside a palace gate with four empty hands of need. He needs wisdom — he cannot find his way. He needs righteousness — he is guilty before the king. He needs holiness — he is too filthy to enter. He needs redemption — he is a slave who belongs to another master. Then the gate opens, and the King's Son walks out, and does not hand him four gifts. He gives Himself, and the beggar finds that the Son is the wisdom, the righteousness, the holiness and the ransom he needed.","meaning":"Corinth wanted status, wisdom and standing, and it was trying to get them by attaching itself to the right teacher. Paul says: you already have them — not as achievements but as a Person. Christ “was made to us wisdom from God, and righteousness and sanctification, and redemption.” You do not need to boast in Paul, Apollos or Cephas, because everything they could offer you is already yours in Christ. And so the boast does not die; it gets a new address. “He who boasts, let him boast in the Lord.” Whatever you are tempted to boast about this week, trace it back to its source, and you will find a gift."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Greeting to a Sanctified Mess (v. 1-3)","body":"Paul, called; the church, sanctified and called; and the whole letter set inside a wider fellowship of “all who call on the name of our Lord Jesus Christ in every place.”"},{"label":"Thanksgiving for Grace Given (v. 4-9)","body":"Before any rebuke, Paul thanks God for the gifts He has given them — especially speech and knowledge, the very gifts they will misuse — and anchors their future not in their faithfulness but in God's."},{"label":"The Appeal Against Divisions (v. 10-17)","body":"Reports from Chloe's household; four factions; three devastating questions; and Paul's refusal to let anyone build a party around him — ending in the thesis that the gospel must not be preached in a way that empties the cross."},{"label":"The Word of the Cross Versus the Wisdom of the World (v. 18-25)","body":"The cross divides the hearers; it exposes the futility of every human route to God; it offends Jewish demands for signs and Greek demands for wisdom; and it reveals the foolishness of God as wiser and His weakness as stronger than anything human."},{"label":"Exhibit A: The Corinthians Themselves (v. 26-31)","body":"God deliberately chose the foolish, the weak, the lowly and the despised, so that no one could boast — and made Christ their wisdom, righteousness, sanctification and redemption, so that every boast is redirected to the Lord."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Why Paul Thanks God for a Church That Was Failing","paragraphs":["Read verses 4 to 9 knowing what is coming in the rest of the letter — factions, incest, lawsuits, prostitution, drunkenness at the Lord's Supper, chaos in worship, denial of the resurrection — and the thanksgiving seems almost unbelievable. “I always thank my God concerning you.” Is Paul being diplomatic? No. Look at what he actually thanks God for. Not their love: they had little. Not their maturity: he will call them babies in 3:1. He thanks God for “the grace of God which was given you in Christ Jesus.” Every clause in the thanksgiving is about what God has done: you were enriched, the testimony was confirmed in you, you lack no gift, He will confirm you, God is faithful. The church is the grammatical object of nearly every verb. God is the subject.","That is the first lesson of 1 Corinthians and it is easy to miss. Paul can face the worst church in the New Testament without despair because he is not looking first at what they are doing; he is looking at what God has done and what God has promised to finish. “Who will also confirm you until the end, blameless in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ” (v. 8). Ἀνέγκλητος — unaccused, beyond any charge — is a breathtaking word to use of these people, and Paul uses it deliberately. Their final standing does not rest on their present performance. It rests on verse 9: “God is faithful.” A pastor who can only give thanks for healthy churches will not survive long in ministry, and a believer who can only give thanks for himself on good days will not survive long in faith.","But notice the edge inside the thanks. The gifts Paul names are “all speech and all knowledge” — λόγος and γνῶσις — and the reader who knows the letter will smile, because those are precisely the gifts Corinth turned into weapons. They were proud of their eloquence (2:1–5), proud of their knowledge (8:1), proud of their tongues (14). Paul does not deny the gifts are real. He thanks God for them. And then he spends fifteen chapters showing what happens when real gifts are separated from love. The problem was never that the gifts were false. The problem was what the people did with true ones. And verse 7 sets the posture for every gift: “waiting for the revelation of our Lord Jesus Christ.” The gifts are equipment for people still on the road, not trophies for people who think they have arrived."]},{"heading":"Four Names on Four Banners: The Anatomy of a Divided Church","paragraphs":["Read the four slogans of verse 12 carefully, because every church in every century has its own version. “I follow Paul” — the people who were there at the beginning, the founder's loyalists, who measure everything by how it was done originally. “I follow Apollos” — the people impressed by eloquence and scholarship; Apollos was “an eloquent man” and “mighty in the Scriptures” (Acts 18:24), and Corinth loved a gifted speaker. “I follow Cephas” — the people who valued apostolic credentials and perhaps Jewish practice, attaching themselves to the man who walked with Jesus. And “I follow Christ” — which sounds like the right answer and was probably the most arrogant of all, because it turned Jesus into one party's private property and looked down on everyone else for needing human teachers.","Paul's answer is three questions, each designed to make them recoil. “Is Christ divided?” The verb μερίζω means to divide into portions — can you carve Christ up and distribute Him to rival groups? “Was Paul crucified for you?” Note that Paul uses his own name, not Apollos'. He goes after his own fan club first. “Or were you baptized into the name of Paul?” Baptism into a name meant transfer of ownership; you belonged to the one into whose name you were baptized. And then the unforgettable moment of honesty in verses 14 to 16, where Paul thanks God he baptized almost nobody — Crispus, Gaius, oh, and the household of Stephanas, and beyond that he cannot remember. A man who wanted followers would have kept count.","One point must be stated plainly, because this passage is easily misused: Paul is not saying baptism is unimportant. In Romans 6:3–4 he grounds union with Christ in baptism, and in 12:13 of this letter he says “in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body.” What he is saying in verse 17 is that his specific commission was to preach, and that baptism had become, in Corinth, a badge of loyalty to whoever performed it. When a sacrament becomes a membership card for a faction, Paul would rather not touch it. And then he states the principle that drives the next three chapters: the gospel is not preached “in wisdom of words, so that the cross of Christ wouldn’t be made void.” Κενόω means to empty out. You can empty the cross of its power by surrounding it with so much human brilliance that people end up trusting the brilliance."]},{"heading":"The Foolishness of God: Why the Cross Offends Everybody","paragraphs":["To feel verses 18 to 25 you have to forget two thousand years of crosses on steeples and around necks. In the first century, crucifixion was the death Rome reserved for slaves, rebels and the lowest criminals — deliberately public, deliberately slow, deliberately humiliating. The Roman orator Cicero called it the cruellest and most disgusting penalty and said the very word should be kept far from a Roman citizen's thoughts, eyes and ears. And Paul walks into a proud Roman colony and announces that the central event of history, the revelation of God's wisdom and power, is a Jewish man nailed to one. One of the earliest surviving images of the crucifixion is a piece of graffiti from Rome, usually dated to the second or third century, showing a man worshipping a crucified figure with a donkey's head, and the scrawled words “Alexamenos worships his god.” That was how the world saw it: a joke.","Paul names two kinds of offence. “Jews ask for signs” — they wanted a demonstration of power, a Messiah who would come in strength and break the oppressor. A crucified Messiah was a σκάνδαλον, the bent stick of a trap you trip over, because Deuteronomy 21:23 pronounced anyone hanged on a tree to be under God's curse. “Greeks seek after wisdom” — they wanted a philosophy elegant enough to satisfy a cultivated mind. A god who dies in agony is μωρία, foolishness, because the Greek ideal of the divine was serene, untouchable and incapable of suffering. Both demands are a way of saying the same thing: God must meet me on my terms. And the cross meets nobody on their terms.","But look at verse 24. “To those who are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God.” The cross does not deny what either group longed for. It answers both — with a power that conquered sin and death rather than Rome, and a wisdom that reconciles a holy God to guilty people rather than merely explaining the universe. Then verse 25 says the most daring thing in the passage: “the foolishness of God is wiser than men, and the weakness of God is stronger than men.” At the moment God looked most foolish and most weak — dying on a cross — He was doing the wisest and strongest thing ever done. And verses 26 to 31 prove it with the most humbling evidence available: the congregation itself. God chose the foolish, the weak, the lowly, the despised and the “things that are not” — three times the verb ἐκλέγομαι, He chose — “that no flesh should boast before God.” The church is not God's compromise with poor material. It is His argument."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["Paul did not invent the theology of verses 26 to 29. He learned it from the One he preached. Jesus prayed, in one of the few prayers of His that the Gospels record word for word: “I thank you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, that you hid these things from the wise and understanding, and revealed them to infants. Yes, Father, for so it was well-pleasing in your sight” (Matthew 11:25–26). Notice the phrase “well-pleasing” — it is the same idea Paul uses in verse 21: “it was God’s good pleasure through the foolishness of the preaching to save those who believe.” God does not merely tolerate His choice of the humble. He delights in it. And Mary had sung it over the unborn Christ before He drew breath: “He has put down princes from their thrones. And has exalted the lowly. He has filled the hungry with good things. He has sent the rich away empty” (Luke 1:52–53). James repeats it to his own congregation: “Didn’t God choose those who are poor in this world to be rich in faith, and heirs of the Kingdom which he promised to those who love him?” (James 2:5). The New Testament speaks with one voice: God's kingdom is built from the bottom up.","Then follow verse 31 forward. Paul quotes Jeremiah's “He who boasts, let him boast in the Lord” here and again, word for word, in his second letter to the same church: “But ‘he who boasts, let him boast in the Lord’” (2 Corinthians 10:17). In between those two letters he learned it in his own body — a thorn he begged three times to have removed, and the answer “My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness” (2 Corinthians 12:9). The theology of 1 Corinthians 1, that God works through weakness so that no flesh can boast, became Paul's personal autobiography. And later still he wrote to the Philippians what verse 30 looks like when it takes over a life: “Yes most certainly, and I count all things to be a loss for the excellency of the knowledge of Christ Jesus, my Lord, for whom I suffered the loss of all things, and count them nothing but refuse, that I may gain Christ” (Philippians 3:8). When Christ is your wisdom, righteousness, sanctification and redemption, every other credential becomes rubbish by comparison.","And verse 10 has its root in the upper room. On the night before He died, Jesus prayed for the people who would believe through the apostles' word: “that they may all be one; even as you, Father, are in me, and I in you, that they also may be one in us; that the world may believe that you sent me” (John 17:21). Unity among believers is not a nice-to-have for church harmony. Jesus ties it to evangelism: the world's belief depends on it. That is why Paul treats the Corinthian factions as the first and most urgent problem in the letter, before immorality and before doctrine. A divided church preaches a divided Christ, and a divided Christ convinces no one. Paul later tells another church to be “eager to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace” (Ephesians 4:3) — keep, not create. The unity is a gift already given. The church's job is not to manufacture it but to stop tearing it."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Divisions / schism","hebrew":"σχίσμα","translit":"schísma","strongs":"G4978","verses":"v. 10 (again in 11:18 and 12:25)","insight":"Strong's: “a split or gap (‘schism’), literally or figuratively.” The literal sense is a tear in fabric — Jesus used it of an unshrunk patch that rips an old garment and makes “a worse hole” (Matthew 9:16) — and of a crowd divided over Him (John 7:43). Paul uses it three times in this letter, and the progression is revealing: divisions over leaders (1:10), divisions at the Lord's Supper between rich and poor (11:18), and the goal of a body with “no division” (12:25). The Corinthian schisms were not over major doctrine. They were over personalities, status and pride. That is how most church splits begin: not with heresy, but with loyalty to the wrong thing."},{"english":"Perfected together / mended","hebrew":"καταρτίζω","translit":"katartízō","strongs":"G2675","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “to complete thoroughly, i.e. repair (literally or figuratively) or adjust.” This is the word used when James and John were “mending their nets” in the boat (Matthew 4:21), and it was used in Greek medical writing for setting a dislocated or broken bone. It is also the word of Galatians 6:1 — “restore such a one in a spirit of gentleness.” Paul's remedy for division is not uniformity of opinion. It is repair: putting torn nets back into working order so they can catch fish again, putting a dislocated joint back so the body can move. A divided church is not just unpleasant; it is broken equipment, and God wants it mended for use."},{"english":"Cross","hebrew":"σταυρός","translit":"staurós","strongs":"G4716","verses":"v. 17, 18 (and the verb σταυρόω, “crucified,” in v. 13, 23)","insight":"Strong's: “a stake or post (as set upright), i.e. (specially), a pole or cross (as an instrument of capital punishment); figuratively, exposure to death, i.e. self-denial; by implication, the atonement of Christ.” Every part of that definition is in this chapter. It was an instrument of execution — the degradation Rome reserved for slaves. It became a figure of self-denial — the cross Jesus told His disciples to take up. And it became shorthand for the atonement itself: “the word of the cross” (v. 18) means the entire gospel. Paul's whole strategy for Corinth is captured in his refusal to let the cross be “made void” (v. 17). Anything you add to the gospel to make it more impressive risks subtracting the power."},{"english":"Foolishness","hebrew":"μωρία","translit":"mōría","strongs":"G3472 (adjective μωρός, mōrós, G3474; verb μωραίνω, mōraínō, G3471)","verses":"v. 18, 21, 23 (noun); v. 25, 27 (adjective); v. 20 (verb)","insight":"Strong's defines μωρία as “silliness, i.e. absurdity,” and the adjective μωρός as “dull or stupid (as if shut up), i.e. heedless, (morally) blockhead, (apparently) absurd.” This is the word family that gives English the word moron. The verb μωραίνω, Strong's says, means “to become insipid” — and Jesus used it of salt that has lost its flavour (Matthew 5:13). That is the irony of verse 20: the world thought the cross was moronic, but God made the world's wisdom tasteless. Paul uses this word family more in 1 Corinthians 1–4 than in all the rest of his letters combined. He is willing to be called a fool, because he knows whose foolishness he is carrying."},{"english":"Boast / glory","hebrew":"καυχάομαι","translit":"kaucháomai","strongs":"G2744","verses":"v. 29, 31 (×2)","insight":"Strong's: “to vaunt (in a good or a bad sense).” The family of this word — boast, boasting, ground for boasting — appears roughly sixty times in Paul's letters, and more than half of those are in 1 and 2 Corinthians. That statistic tells you about the city. Corinth ran on boasting: public inscriptions to one's own generosity, rhetorical contests, social climbing. Paul does not tell the church to stop boasting altogether. He tells them to change the object. Verse 29 shuts down boasting “before God”; verse 31 opens it up again “in the Lord.” The human heart was made to glory in something. The gospel does not destroy the instinct. It redirects it to the only One who deserves it."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the loyalties that quietly divide you from other believers, the hunger to be impressive that empties your witness of power, and the shame you carry about being ordinary.","items":[{"title":"Whose Name Is on Your Banner?","body":"You would never say “I follow Paul” out loud. But listen to how you talk about other Christians. Is there a church, a preacher, a denomination, a style of worship you are slightly contemptuous of? Is there a teacher whose name makes you defensive when someone criticises him? Is there a stream of Christianity you secretly consider beneath you? That is Corinth. The Spirit is not asking you to give up your convictions. He is asking whether your loyalty to a human leader or movement is stronger than your love for a brother who belongs to a different one. Paul's question is aimed at you today: “Is Christ divided?” If you have to answer, “No, but my group is right,” you have just picked up a banner. Put it down."},{"title":"The Brilliance That Empties the Cross","body":"Paul could have preached in Corinth with rhetorical fireworks. He chose not to, “so that the cross of Christ wouldn’t be made void.” Now think about how you present your faith — at work, online, in conversation, in ministry. How much of it is about making yourself look intelligent, articulate, spiritual or impressive? How much of your prayer life in public is aimed at the people listening? The Spirit wants you to see this: when people walk away impressed with you, the cross has been made a little emptier. When they walk away confronted with Christ, it has been made full. You cannot make both happen at once. Which one are you aiming for?"},{"title":"God Chose You on Purpose","body":"You have probably told yourself at some point that God uses other people — the gifted ones, the confident ones, the ones with the right background and education — and that you are a sort of spiritual afterthought. Verses 26 to 28 tear that lie apart. God chose. Three times. He chose the foolish, the weak, the lowly, the despised, the things that are not — and He did it deliberately, so that nobody could boast. Your ordinariness is not a disqualification. It is your qualification. Whatever is missing from your résumé is the space where God intends to show His power. Ask Him today: “Lord, what do You want to do through the very thing I am ashamed of?” And then be ready for the answer."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Banner Check","body":"Write down every Christian leader, ministry, church or movement you feel strong loyalty to. Then write down one group of believers you find it difficult to respect. Ask honestly: is my disagreement with them about the gospel itself — the cross and resurrection of Christ — or is it about style, personality, or preference? If it is not about the gospel, confess the contempt to God and ask Him to show you one thing you could learn from them."},{"title":"The Emptied-Cross Check","body":"Think about the last three times you talked about your faith with someone. What did you most want them to think afterward — about Christ, or about you? Write it down. If it was about you, you are not alone; Corinth's disease is deep in all of us. But name it, because what you name you can surrender."},{"title":"The Boast Check","body":"Finish this sentence honestly: “The thing I am most proud of about myself is ______.” Now trace it back. Where did the ability, the opportunity, the health, the mind, the family, the education come from? Verse 30 says “because of him, you are in Christ Jesus.” Ask yourself: if this thing were taken away tomorrow, would my sense of worth survive? If not, it has become a ground of boasting before God, and it needs to be handed back."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Mending One Torn Thread","body":"This week, identify one believer from a different church, tradition or group than yours — or one person in your own church you have quietly distanced yourself from. Reach out to them with no agenda except fellowship: buy them coffee, pray with them, or send a message thanking God for something specific in their life. Do not debate. Do not correct. Do what καταρτίζω means — mend a net. If the division is serious, ask God whether you need to apologise for anything, and do it."},{"title":"Boasting in the Right Place","body":"Tonight, take a sheet of paper and draw two columns. In the left column write everything you would naturally be tempted to boast about — achievements, gifts, background, possessions, spiritual experiences. In the right column, beside each one, write how it came to you as a gift. Then at the bottom of the page write verse 30 in full: “Because of him, you are in Christ Jesus, who was made to us wisdom from God, and righteousness and sanctification, and redemption.” Read the right column out loud as thanksgiving. Then say verse 31 aloud: “He who boasts, let him boast in the Lord.” Keep the page somewhere you will see it the next time you feel the urge to be impressive."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I thank You for the grace You have given me in Christ Jesus. I thank You that I am enriched in Him, that I come behind in no gift, and that You — not I — will confirm me until the end, blameless in the day of my Lord Jesus Christ. You are faithful, and You called me into the fellowship of Your Son. Lord, I confess the banners I have carried. I have said, in my heart if not with my mouth, ‘I follow this one, I follow that one,’ and I have looked down on brothers and sisters for whom Christ was crucified. Christ is not divided, and I will not divide Him. Mend me, Lord. Repair the torn places between me and Your people, and make me one mind and one purpose with them. I confess that I have wanted to be impressive. I have tried to make the gospel look clever, and I have wanted people to admire me more than they adore You. Forgive me for emptying the cross. Let the word of the cross be the power of God in my life and in my mouth. Father, I thank You that You chose me. I was not wise by the world's standards, not powerful, not noble — and You chose me on purpose, so that no flesh would boast before You. Take the very things I am ashamed of and use them to bring to nothing the things that are proud. Christ Jesus, You are my wisdom, my righteousness, my sanctification, and my redemption. I have nothing that I did not receive. So I lay down every boast and I boast in You alone. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Gideon's Three Hundred","ref":"Judges 7:1-22","relatedVerses":"v. 26-29","summary":"Gideon gathers thirty-two thousand men against the Midianites, and God says the army is too big — not too small. The reason is exactly the reason of verse 29: if Israel wins with that many, they will boast that their own hand saved them. So God sends home everyone who is afraid, and twenty-two thousand leave. Then He tests the remaining ten thousand at the water, and keeps only the three hundred who lapped like dogs. With three hundred men carrying trumpets, empty jars and torches, Gideon routs an army described as numerous as locusts. God deliberately reduces His instruments until victory can only be explained by Him. That is 1 Corinthians 1:27–29 in a battlefield: God chooses the weak things of the world, so that no flesh can boast before Him."},{"title":"A Shepherd Boy and Five Stones","ref":"1 Samuel 16:1-13; 17:32-51","relatedVerses":"v. 27-28","summary":"When Samuel comes to Jesse's house to anoint the next king, Jesse parades seven impressive sons and does not even bother to call the youngest, who is out with the sheep. Samuel is ready to anoint Eliab, the eldest, on looks alone, but God tells him that man looks at the outward appearance while the LORD looks at the heart. The boy nobody thought worth summoning is the one God chose. Shortly afterward that same boy walks out with a sling and five stones against a giant in bronze armour, refusing Saul's armour because he had not proved it. The whole army of Israel sees the strong put to shame by the weak. David becomes Israel's greatest king precisely because his story could never be credited to David."},{"title":"The Synagogue Ruler Who Was Beaten","ref":"Acts 18:1-17","relatedVerses":"v. 1, 14","summary":"When Paul came to Corinth, he worked as a tentmaker with Aquila and Priscilla and reasoned in the synagogue every Sabbath. Opposition grew until he left the synagogue and moved next door, to the house of Justus. Then Crispus, the synagogue ruler, believed with all his house — one of the few people Paul would later remember baptizing. The Jewish leaders brought Paul before the proconsul Gallio, who threw the case out, and the crowd turned on Sosthenes, the new synagogue ruler, and beat him in front of the judgment seat while Gallio ignored it. Years later, a letter goes to Corinth from “Paul... and our brother Sosthenes.” We cannot prove it is the same man, but if it is, the story is the gospel in miniature: two synagogue rulers, one of them once beaten as Paul's opponent, both brought to the foot of the cross that their own synagogue had called a stumbling block."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"Christ Crucified and the Mind of Christ — The Wisdom Only the Spirit Can Reveal","themeTitle":"“A Faith That Stands on a Performance Will Fall to a Better One”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"When I came to you, brothers, I didn’t come with excellence of speech or of wisdom, proclaiming to you the testimony of God.","understanding":"Paul goes back to the day he arrived. He did not come with ὑπεροχή — superiority, prominence, the elevated style of a professional orator — either of speech or of wisdom. In Corinth that was almost an insult to the audience. Rhetoric was a public entertainment, and visiting speakers were judged on their delivery the way athletes are judged on their times. Paul is reminding them that he deliberately refused to compete. What he carried was “the testimony of God” — some manuscripts read “the mystery of God” — a message he was handing on, not a performance he was giving.","words":[{"strongs":"G5247","hebrew":"ὑπεροχή","translit":"hyperochḗ","english":"excellence"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"speech"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G3142","hebrew":"μαρτύριον","translit":"martýrion","english":"testimony"}]},{"v":2,"text":"For I determined not to know anything among you, except Jesus Christ, and him crucified.","understanding":"Κρίνω — I decided, I came to a verdict. This was a decision, not a temperament. Paul could argue with philosophers (Acts 17 shows he could quote their own poets). But he resolved to know nothing among them “except Jesus Christ, and him crucified.” Not merely Christ, but Christ as the crucified one — the very part of the message Corinth would find most embarrassing. He did not lead with the least offensive truth and work up to the cross. He planted the cross at the front door.","words":[{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"determined"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G4717","hebrew":"σταυρόω","translit":"stauróō","english":"crucified"}]},{"v":3,"text":"I was with you in weakness, in fear, and in much trembling.","understanding":"One of the most disarming admissions in Paul's letters. He came “in weakness, in fear, and in much trembling.” Ἀσθένεια, φόβος, τρόμος. He had been beaten and jailed in Philippi, chased out of Thessalonica and Berea, and mocked in Athens. Luke tells us the Lord had to speak to him in a night vision in Corinth: “Don’t be afraid, but speak and don’t be silent” (Acts 18:9). The great apostle arrived shaking. That is not a flaw in the story. It is the point of it.","words":[{"strongs":"G769","hebrew":"ἀσθένεια","translit":"asthéneia","english":"weakness"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G5156","hebrew":"τρόμος","translit":"trómos","english":"trembling"}]},{"v":4,"text":"My speech and my preaching were not in persuasive words of human wisdom, but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power,","understanding":"Two ways to convince people. Πειθός — persuasive, the art of the enticing argument, from the same root as the Greek goddess of persuasion. Or ἀπόδειξις — a technical rhetorical term for a compelling proof, which Paul hijacks: his proof was a demonstration “of the Spirit and of power.” Paul is not anti-intellectual; Acts says he reasoned and persuaded in the synagogue every Sabbath. What he refused to do was let the result depend on his technique. The convincing was the Spirit's work.","words":[{"strongs":"G3981","hebrew":"πειθός","translit":"peithós","english":"persuasive"},{"strongs":"G585","hebrew":"ἀπόδειξις","translit":"apódeixis","english":"demonstration"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"}]},{"v":5,"text":"that your faith wouldn’t stand in the wisdom of men, but in the power of God.","understanding":"The purpose of the whole strategy. “That your faith wouldn’t stand in the wisdom of men, but in the power of God.” If Paul had argued them into it, someone cleverer could argue them out of it. A faith resting on a preacher's brilliance is only as secure as the preacher's reputation. Paul wanted their faith resting on something that could not be out-argued: an encounter with the power of God.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"}]},{"v":6,"text":"We speak wisdom, however, among those who are full grown; yet a wisdom not of this world, nor of the rulers of this world, who are coming to nothing.","understanding":"But Paul is not against wisdom — he is against the wrong kind. “We speak wisdom… among those who are full grown.” Τέλειος means mature, complete, fully developed. There is real depth in the gospel, and it is for those who have grown. But it is “not of this world” — the word is αἰών, this age — and not of “the rulers of this age, who are coming to nothing.” The present participle is vivid: they are already in the process of being rendered powerless. Every empire that thinks it is permanent is passing away as you read.","words":[{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"full grown"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"world (age)"},{"strongs":"G758","hebrew":"ἄρχων","translit":"árchōn","english":"rulers"},{"strongs":"G2673","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","english":"coming to nothing"}]},{"v":7,"text":"But we speak God’s wisdom in a mystery, the wisdom that has been hidden, which God foreordained before the worlds for our glory,","understanding":"Μυστήριον in the New Testament does not mean something mysterious that nobody can understand. It means a secret that God kept hidden and has now chosen to reveal. This wisdom was ἀποκρύπτω — hidden away — and προορίζω — marked out in advance, before the ages. And it was planned “for our glory.” Before the universe existed, God had already designed the way He would bring you into glory. You were on His mind before the world was.","words":[{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"},{"strongs":"G613","hebrew":"ἀποκρύπτω","translit":"apokrýptō","english":"hidden"},{"strongs":"G4309","hebrew":"προορίζω","translit":"proorízō","english":"foreordained"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]},{"v":8,"text":"which none of the rulers of this world has known. For had they known it, they wouldn’t have crucified the Lord of glory.","understanding":"The rulers of this age did not understand it — and the proof is that they crucified “the Lord of glory.” It is one of the most explosive titles in the New Testament: the One who hung naked and bleeding on a Roman cross was the Lord to whom all glory belongs. Who are “the rulers”? At the human level, certainly Pilate, Herod, Caiaphas and the Sanhedrin (compare Acts 3:17, “you did this in ignorance, as did also your rulers”). Many interpreters also hear the spiritual powers behind them. Either way the point is the same: the powers that crucified Jesus walked straight into God's plan and did not see it.","words":[{"strongs":"G758","hebrew":"ἄρχων","translit":"árchōn","english":"rulers"},{"strongs":"G4717","hebrew":"σταυρόω","translit":"stauróō","english":"crucified"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]},{"v":9,"text":"But as it is written, “Things which an eye didn’t see, and an ear didn’t hear, which didn’t enter into the heart of man, these God has prepared for those who love him.”","understanding":"This verse is constantly quoted at funerals as a description of heaven — but read the next verse. Paul draws on Isaiah 64:4 (blended with phrases from elsewhere in Isaiah) to describe what no human eye, ear or heart could discover by its own means: what God has prepared for those who love Him. And verse 10 says God has revealed it — already, now, by His Spirit. The verse is not mainly about what you will see after you die. It is about what you can know now that you could never have discovered without God showing you.","words":[{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eye"},{"strongs":"G2090","hebrew":"ἑτοιμάζω","translit":"hetoimázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"}]},{"v":10,"text":"But to us, God revealed them through the Spirit. For the Spirit searches all things, yes, the deep things of God.","understanding":"“But to us, God revealed them.” Ἀποκαλύπτω — to take the cover off. And the One who takes the cover off is the Spirit, who ἐρευνάω — searches, investigates, explores — “all things, yes, the deep things of God.” Βάθος is the word for the depths of the sea. The Holy Spirit is not a force that gives you good feelings. He is a Person who knows the bottom of the ocean of God, and He is the One who teaches you.","words":[{"strongs":"G601","hebrew":"ἀποκαλύπτω","translit":"apokalýptō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G2045","hebrew":"ἐρευνάω","translit":"ereunáō","english":"searches"},{"strongs":"G899","hebrew":"βάθος","translit":"báthos","english":"deep things"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For who among men knows the things of a man, except the spirit of the man, which is in him? Even so, no one knows the things of God, except God’s Spirit.","understanding":"An analogy anyone can feel. Nobody really knows what is going on inside you except your own spirit. Others can guess from your face, but only you know your thoughts. In the same way, only God's Spirit knows the things of God. Which means that no amount of human research, intellect or religious effort can get inside God's mind from the outside. God has to open it from the inside.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knows"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"}]},{"v":12,"text":"But we received, not the spirit of the world, but the Spirit which is from God, that we might know the things that were freely given to us by God.","understanding":"Two spirits and two worldviews. “The spirit of the world” is the prevailing mindset of the age — what everybody assumes. “The Spirit which is from God” is the Holy Spirit, given so that “we might know the things that were freely given to us by God.” Χαρίζομαι — to give as a favour, gratuitously, the verb form of grace. One of the Spirit's main jobs is to help you understand what you have already been given.","words":[{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G5483","hebrew":"χαρίζομαι","translit":"charízomai","english":"freely given"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Which things also we speak, not in words which man’s wisdom teaches, but which the Holy Spirit teaches, comparing spiritual things with spiritual things.","understanding":"Paul's teaching came in words “which the Holy Spirit teaches” — διδακτός, taught, instructed. And then a phrase that can be read several ways: πνευματικοῖς πνευματικὰ συγκρίνοντες — “comparing spiritual things with spiritual things,” or “interpreting spiritual truths to spiritual people,” or “explaining spiritual realities in spiritual words.” All three are grammatically possible, and all three are true. The Spirit gives both the content and the vocabulary for it.","words":[{"strongs":"G1318","hebrew":"διδακτός","translit":"didaktós","english":"teaches"},{"strongs":"G4793","hebrew":"συγκρίνω","translit":"synkrínō","english":"comparing"},{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Now the natural man doesn’t receive the things of God’s Spirit, for they are foolishness to him, and he can’t know them, because they are spiritually discerned.","understanding":"Ψυχικός — the “natural” man. Strong's notes it is built on ψυχή, the soul — life as it is lived at the level of ordinary human faculties. This person does not δέχομαι — welcome, accept — the things of the Spirit. They seem like μωρία, foolishness, to him. And he cannot know them, because they are ἀνακρίνω — examined, discerned — spiritually. This is not an insult to intelligence. It is a statement about faculties: you cannot see colour with your ears, and you cannot discern the things of God without the Spirit of God.","words":[{"strongs":"G5591","hebrew":"ψυχικός","translit":"psychikós","english":"natural"},{"strongs":"G1209","hebrew":"δέχομαι","translit":"déchomai","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G3472","hebrew":"μωρία","translit":"mōría","english":"foolishness"},{"strongs":"G350","hebrew":"ἀνακρίνω","translit":"anakrínō","english":"discerned"}]},{"v":15,"text":"But he who is spiritual discerns all things, and he himself is judged by no one.","understanding":"The spiritual person — πνευματικός, the one who has the Spirit — “discerns all things.” The same verb ἀνακρίνω. He can evaluate the world from God's perspective. But he “is judged by no one”: no merely natural person is in a position to evaluate him correctly. This is not a licence for spiritual arrogance — in chapter 3 Paul will tell the Corinthians they are not spiritual at all. It is a statement about where true discernment comes from.","words":[{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual"},{"strongs":"G350","hebrew":"ἀνακρίνω","translit":"anakrínō","english":"discerns"}]},{"v":16,"text":"“For who has known the mind of the Lord, that he should instruct him?” But we have Christ’s mind.","understanding":"Paul quotes Isaiah 40:13 — “Who has known the mind of the Lord?” — which expects the answer: nobody. And then he gives the most astonishing answer in the chapter: “But we have Christ’s mind.” Νοῦς — mind, understanding, way of thinking. Not that believers know everything God knows, but that through the Spirit they have been given access to the way Christ thinks. And the mind of Christ, as this chapter has already shown, is the mind of the cross.","words":[{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G4822","hebrew":"συμβιβάζω","translit":"symbibázō","english":"instruct"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Man Who Arrived Shaking","verseRef":"v. 1-5","image":"Picture the road from Athens to Corinth, about fifty miles along the coast. A man walks it alone, carrying his tools in a leather bag. He has scars on his back from Philippi. He has been chased out of Thessalonica and Berea. A few weeks ago he stood on the Areopagus in front of Stoic and Epicurean philosophers and some of them laughed at him when he spoke about a man raised from the dead. Now he is approaching the loudest, proudest, richest city in Greece. And he is afraid. Not mildly nervous — afraid, trembling, in weakness. He finds a tentmaking shop, rents a corner of it, and starts sewing leather.","meaning":"Paul does not hide this from the Corinthians; he reminds them of it. Why? Because he wants them to understand where their faith came from. It did not come from his brilliance — he had none to show them. It did not come from his confidence — he arrived shaking. It came from “demonstration of the Spirit and of power.” If you are waiting until you feel strong, articulate and confident before you share Christ, you are waiting for something Paul himself never had in Corinth. The gospel does not need your strength. It needs your obedience. The weakness is the very place where God's power gets the credit."},{"title":"The Rulers at the Foot of the Cross","verseRef":"v. 6-8","image":"Picture the scene at Golgotha from the perspective of the powerful. Pilate washes his hands and signs the order, confident that he has kept the peace for Caesar. Caiaphas goes home satisfied that one man has died for the nation. Herod's soldiers have had their fun with a purple robe. Behind them all, the darkness — the principalities and powers Paul names elsewhere — rejoicing that the Holy One is dead. Every ruler of this age thinks it has won. And on the cross, unrecognised, hangs “the Lord of glory.”","meaning":"This is the hidden wisdom of God: the moment the rulers of this age thought they were destroying Jesus was the moment God was destroying their power. “For had they known it, they wouldn’t have crucified the Lord of glory.” They walked into God's plan with their eyes closed. And they are, Paul says in verse 6, “coming to nothing” — present tense, already in the process of collapse. Whatever power is pressing on you today, whatever system or authority or darkness seems to be winning, remember that every one of them is on a timer, and the cross has already set it."},{"title":"The Diver to the Bottom of the Sea","verseRef":"v. 10-12","image":"Imagine standing on the deck of a ship over the deepest trench in the ocean, miles of black water below you. You want to know what is down there. You can lower a rope, but it runs out. You can shout, but nothing answers. You can study the surface for a lifetime and never see the floor. Then someone comes up from below — someone who has lived in those depths and knows every ridge and creature on the seabed — and climbs aboard and begins to describe it to you.","meaning":"That is the Holy Spirit. Βάθος — the depths — is the word Paul uses for “the deep things of God,” and the Spirit is the One who “searches all things.” No human method can reach the bottom of God: not philosophy, not religion, not experience. Only the Spirit, who knows God from the inside the way your spirit knows you from the inside, can tell you what is there. And here is the wonder of verse 12: He was given to you “that we might know the things that were freely given to us by God.” Most believers live their whole lives at the surface of what they have received. The Spirit wants to take you down."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"How Paul Preached in Corinth (v. 1-5)","body":"Paul recalls his arrival: no rhetorical superiority, a deliberate decision to know nothing but Christ crucified, personal weakness and fear, and a demonstration of the Spirit and power — so that their faith would rest on God, not men."},{"label":"God's Hidden Wisdom (v. 6-9)","body":"There is a true wisdom for the mature, but it belongs to God, not to this passing age. It was hidden, planned before the ages for our glory, and the rulers who crucified the Lord of glory never understood it."},{"label":"Revealed by the Spirit (v. 10-13)","body":"What no eye, ear or heart could discover, God has revealed through His Spirit, who searches His depths — and the Spirit both gives the truth and teaches the words to express it."},{"label":"The Natural Person and the Spiritual Person (v. 14-16)","body":"The person without the Spirit cannot receive or understand the things of God; the person with the Spirit discerns them — because believers have been given the mind of Christ."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Fear and Trembling in the City of Orators","paragraphs":["To feel the force of verses 1 to 5 you need to know what a visiting speaker was expected to be in Corinth. Rhetoric was the great public art of the Greco-Roman world, and orators were celebrities. Crowds gathered to hear them the way modern crowds gather for concerts. They were judged on their delivery, their appearance, their voice, their elegance and their ability to move an audience — and they were paid accordingly. When Paul says he did not come with “excellence of speech or of wisdom,” he is describing a deliberate refusal to play that game. His critics in Corinth would later say exactly this about him: “his bodily presence is weak, and his speech is despised” (2 Corinthians 10:10). Paul does not dispute it. He says it was the point.","There is a popular interpretation that Paul had tried clever philosophy in Athens, failed, and therefore resolved in Corinth to preach only the cross. Be careful with it. Acts 17 does not record Athens as a failure — Dionysius the Areopagite, a woman named Damaris and others believed (Acts 17:34) — and the Areopagus speech is itself centred on repentance, judgment and the resurrection of Jesus. What is clear is that Paul arrived in Corinth bruised and afraid. He had been beaten in Philippi, driven out of Thessalonica and Berea, and mocked in Athens. And the Lord had to come to him in a night vision with words that only make sense if he needed them: “Don’t be afraid, but speak and don’t be silent; for I am with you” (Acts 18:9–10). The decision of verse 2 was not a change in theology. It was a decision about strategy, made by a frightened man who had concluded that the only thing worth risking his life on was the cross itself.","And notice why. Verse 5: “that your faith wouldn’t stand in the wisdom of men, but in the power of God.” Paul understood something that every age of the church has to relearn. Whatever you use to win people is what you must use to keep them. If you win them with brilliance, you will have to keep being brilliant, and they will leave for someone more brilliant. If you win them with entertainment, you will have to keep entertaining them. If you win them with the cross and the power of the Spirit, their faith will stand on something no rival can out-perform. Paul is not anti-intellectual — he wrote Romans. He is anti-dependency on the messenger. The aim of all true preaching is to make the preacher unnecessary to the hearer's faith."]},{"heading":"Hidden Wisdom and the Rulers Who Did Not See It","paragraphs":["Verse 6 makes a surprising turn. Having spent a chapter and a half attacking wisdom, Paul says, “We speak wisdom, however.” The Christian faith is not anti-wisdom; it is anti-pride. There is a wisdom so deep that it is reserved for the τέλειοι — the mature, those who are full grown. But it is a wisdom of a completely different kind from what Corinth valued. It is “not of this world,” and the rulers of this world do not understand it. It is μυστήριον — not a puzzle for clever people to solve, but a secret God kept and then chose to reveal. It was hidden, and it was planned “before the worlds for our glory” (v. 7). The cross was not God's emergency response to a plan that went wrong. It was His intention from before creation.","Who are “the rulers of this world” in verses 6 and 8? Honest readers divide. Some take them to be human authorities: Pilate, Herod, Caiaphas, the Sanhedrin — the people who literally crucified Jesus. Peter uses similar language in Acts 3:17: “I know that you did this in ignorance, as did also your rulers.” Others hear the spiritual powers behind those human rulers — the principalities and powers Paul names in Ephesians 6:12 and Colossians 2:15, where at the cross Christ, “having stripped the principalities and the powers, … made a show of them openly” (Colossians 2:15). The word ἄρχων is used of both in the New Testament (Jesus calls Satan “the prince of this world” in John 12:31 using the same word). The strongest reading is probably that Paul has both levels in view, the human rulers as the visible instruments of the invisible powers. Either way the point is spectacular: the authorities who crucified Jesus thought they were ending His influence, and they were in fact executing the plan of God that would end theirs.","Then verse 9, which is almost always misused. “Things which an eye didn’t see, and an ear didn’t hear, which didn’t enter into the heart of man, these God has prepared for those who love him.” People read it as a description of heaven — something so wonderful that we cannot even imagine it now. But verse 10 begins with “But”: “But to us, God revealed them through the Spirit.” Paul's point is not that we cannot know these things. It is that we could not have discovered them by eye, ear or heart — by observation, by tradition, by intuition — and that God has now revealed them by His Spirit. The things God has prepared for those who love Him are not only future. They include the gospel itself, the cross and its wisdom, which no human mind would ever have invented."]},{"heading":"The Spirit Who Knows God From the Inside","paragraphs":["Verses 10 to 16 give one of the clearest descriptions of the Holy Spirit's teaching ministry in the New Testament, and the argument is beautifully simple. Who knows what is going on inside a human being? Only that person's own spirit (v. 11). You can watch someone's face, hear their words and guess at their motives, but only they know their thoughts from the inside. In the same way, only God's Spirit knows the things of God. Which means that no human being, however brilliant, can reach into God's mind from the outside. Philosophy can speculate about God. Religion can reach towards Him. But only the Spirit of God can reveal Him, because only the Spirit knows Him from within. This is also, incidentally, one of the strongest statements of the Spirit's deity in Paul: the Spirit relates to God as a man's spirit relates to the man, and He searches “the deep things of God.” Nobody less than God can do that.","Then verse 14 introduces two kinds of people. The ψυχικὸς ἄνθρωπος — the “natural man,” the person living only at the level of ordinary human faculties — “doesn’t receive the things of God’s Spirit, for they are foolishness to him, and he can’t know them, because they are spiritually discerned.” This explains why intelligent people can look at the cross and see nothing but a dead man, and why some very simple people can look at it and weep with understanding. The issue is not intelligence; it is equipment. Spiritual things require a spiritual faculty to discern them, just as music requires hearing and colour requires sight. The person who has the Spirit — the πνευματικός — “discerns all things.” He has been given a new way of seeing.","And then the climax, the boldest phrase in the chapter: “But we have Christ’s mind.” Paul has just quoted Isaiah 40:13 — “For who has known the mind of the Lord, that he should instruct him?” — a question that expects the answer: no one. And Paul answers: we have. Not because believers have become omniscient, but because through the Spirit they have been given access to the way Christ thinks. But notice where this lands. Chapter 2 began with Christ crucified, and the mind of Christ is the mind of the crucified one — the mind Paul will describe in Philippians 2 as humbling itself, emptying itself, and becoming obedient to death. Having the mind of Christ does not make you superior to other people. It makes you willing to go lower than them. And that is exactly what Corinth, with all its spiritual gifts, did not yet have, as chapter 3 is about to make painfully clear."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["Jesus promised exactly the ministry Paul describes in verses 10 to 13. In the upper room He told His disciples: “But the Counselor, the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in my name, he will teach you all things, and will remind you of all that I said to you” (John 14:26). And again: “However when he, the Spirit of truth, has come, he will guide you into all truth, for he will not speak from himself; but whatever he hears, he will speak. He will declare to you things that are coming. He will glorify me, for he will take from what is mine, and will declare it to you” (John 16:13–14). Notice how exactly that matches 1 Corinthians 2. The Spirit takes the things of Christ and reveals them; He does not speak on His own authority; He teaches the words. Paul's claim that he spoke “not in words which man’s wisdom teaches, but which the Holy Spirit teaches” (v. 13) is the fulfilment of that promise in the life of an apostle.","Verse 16 has a striking parallel in Romans, written a few years later from Corinth. At the end of his great argument about God's plan for Israel and the nations, Paul breaks into praise and quotes the same verse from Isaiah: “Oh the depth of the riches both of the wisdom and the knowledge of God! How unsearchable are his judgments, and his ways past tracing out! ‘For who has known the mind of the Lord? Or who has been his counselor?’” (Romans 11:33–34). There, the quotation expresses the overwhelming mystery of God's ways. Here, it is answered: “But we have Christ’s mind.” Both are true at once. God's depths are unsearchable to human effort; and the Spirit who searches them has been given to us. Humility and confidence belong together in the Christian mind.","And the mystery of verse 7 is unpacked in Paul's later letters. To the Colossians he writes of “the mystery which has been hidden for ages and generations. But now it has been revealed to his saints, to whom God was pleased to make known what are the riches of the glory of this mystery among the Gentiles, which is Christ in you, the hope of glory” (Colossians 1:26–27). That is the same word, the same hiddenness, the same revelation, and the same “glory” as 1 Corinthians 2:7. And in Colossians 2:3 he says of Christ: “in whom are all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge hidden.” The wisdom Corinth was looking for in orators and philosophers was hidden all along in a crucified Messiah. The world could not see it. The Spirit has shown it to you."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Demonstration / proof","hebrew":"ἀπόδειξις","translit":"apódeixis","strongs":"G585","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's gives the simple sense “manifestation,” but in Greek rhetoric ἀπόδειξις was a technical term: the compelling proof that clinches an argument, the moment the orator's case becomes undeniable. This is its only appearance in the New Testament, and Paul uses it with deliberate irony. He refused the orator's tricks of persuasion (πειθός, “persuasive,” v. 4), and yet he says the Corinthians did receive an irrefutable proof — not of his skill but “of the Spirit and of power.” The proof that convinced them was not a better argument. It was God showing up. That is why their faith could “stand in the power of God” (v. 5)."},{"english":"Mystery","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","strongs":"G3466","verses":"v. 7 (and v. 1 in some manuscripts)","insight":"Strong's: “a secret or ‘mystery’ (through the idea of silence imposed by initiation into religious rites).” Corinth knew this word from the mystery religions — the secret cults of Demeter, Isis and Dionysus, whose initiates were sworn never to reveal what they had seen. Paul takes the word and turns it inside out. In the New Testament a mystery is not a secret kept from people; it is a secret God has now chosen to tell. It was “hidden” and “foreordained before the worlds,” and now it is proclaimed openly to anyone who will listen. The gospel is not an exclusive club for the initiated. It is God's open secret, shouted from the rooftops."},{"english":"Searches / investigates","hebrew":"ἐρευνάω","translit":"ereunáō","strongs":"G2045","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “to seek, i.e. (figuratively) to investigate.” The same verb is used of the prophets who “searched diligently” concerning the salvation to come (1 Peter 1:10 uses a compound of it), and of the Jews who “search the Scriptures” (John 5:39). Here it describes the Holy Spirit's relationship to God Himself: He “searches all things, yes, the deep things of God.” It does not imply that the Spirit is ignorant and needs to find out. It describes His complete, penetrating knowledge — the way a man's spirit knows the man (v. 11). And this is the One who has been given to you as your teacher."},{"english":"Natural (soulish) person","hebrew":"ψυχικός","translit":"psychikós","strongs":"G5591 (contrast πνευματικός, pneumatikós, G4152)","verses":"v. 14 (contrast πνευματικός, “spiritual,” in v. 13, 15)","insight":"Strong's defines ψυχικός as “sensitive, i.e. animate,” and explicitly distinguishes it “from G4152 (πνευματικός), which is the higher or renovated nature.” It is built on ψυχή, the soul — the natural life every human being has. The ψυχικός is not necessarily a wicked person; he may be cultured, religious and brilliant. What he lacks is the Spirit. Jude uses the same word for people who cause divisions: they are ψυχικοί, “sensual,” not having the Spirit (Jude 19). Paul's point is about capacity: without the Spirit, the things of God are not merely difficult; they look foolish. That is why the gospel must always be accompanied by prayer for the Spirit to open blind eyes."},{"english":"Mind","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","strongs":"G3563","verses":"v. 16 (×2)","insight":"Strong's: “the intellect, i.e. mind (divine or human; in thought, feeling, or will); by implication, meaning.” Νοῦς is the whole inner faculty of perception and judgment — not just intelligence, but the way you see, weigh and decide. In Romans 12:2 Paul says believers are transformed “by the renewing of your mind” — the same word. And in 1 Corinthians 1:10 he appealed to them to be perfected together “in the same mind.” Now he tells them what that one mind is: “we have Christ’s mind.” Unity in the church does not come from everyone agreeing with each other. It comes from everyone being conformed to Christ's way of thinking — which is the way of the cross."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: your belief that you must be impressive before God can use you, the foundation your own faith is actually standing on, and the depths of God you have been content never to explore.","items":[{"title":"You Do Not Have to Be Impressive","body":"You have probably excused yourself from witnessing, serving, teaching or praying publicly because you are not good with words, not confident, not knowledgeable enough. Read verse 3 again: the apostle Paul arrived in Corinth “in weakness, in fear, and in much trembling.” He was not faking humility. He was afraid. And God built one of the most significant churches in the New Testament through a frightened man who refused to rely on his own polish. The Spirit is saying: your fear is not a disqualification. Your weakness is the stage on which His power gets the credit. What have you been waiting to feel strong enough to do? Do it in weakness this week."},{"title":"What Is Your Faith Standing On?","body":"Verse 5 is a diagnostic. Is your faith resting on the wisdom of men or on the power of God? Ask yourself honestly: if your favourite preacher fell into scandal tomorrow, would your faith shake? If someone presented you with an argument you could not answer, would you collapse? If your church split, would you stop following Christ? If any of those would topple you, your faith is standing partly on human wisdom — on a personality, a performance, an institution, an argument. The Spirit wants to move the foundation. Ask Him for a fresh encounter with the power of God, something no rival can out-argue and no scandal can undo."},{"title":"Living at the Surface","body":"The Spirit searches the deep things of God, and He has been given to you “that we might know the things that were freely given to us by God.” Yet most believers live their whole lives at the surface — a few familiar verses, a few routine prayers, a faith that never goes down into the depths. The Spirit is inviting you to dive. When was the last time you sat with a passage of Scripture until it opened? When did you last ask the Spirit to show you something about God you have never seen before, and then waited long enough for Him to answer? You have been given the mind of Christ. Do not leave it unexplored."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Foundation Check","body":"Write down the three things that most strengthen your faith. Then for each one, ask: is this the power of God, or the wisdom of men? A preacher, a book, an argument, a church community — these are good gifts, but if they are the foundation, your faith is vulnerable. If your list has no encounter with God Himself on it, ask why."},{"title":"The Weakness Check","body":"What is the one area where you feel weakest when it comes to serving God — speaking, praying aloud, explaining your faith, leading, giving? Write it down. Now ask: have I been treating this as a reason to hold back, or as a place where God could show His power? Paul turned his trembling into a testimony. What would it look like to do the same?"},{"title":"The Natural-Mind Check","body":"Think of one area of your life — money, relationships, career, your body, your future — where you make decisions entirely by ordinary human reasoning and never ask what the Spirit says. That is where you are living as a ψυχικός, a natural person, even though you have the Spirit. Name it, and invite the mind of Christ into it."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Witness in Weakness","body":"This week, share something of Christ with one person in a way that feels weak to you. Do not prepare a polished argument. Before you go, pray verse 4 over yourself: “Lord, let my speech be not in persuasive words of human wisdom, but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power.” Then simply tell them what Jesus crucified means to you, in your own plain words. Afterwards, write down what happened — not whether they agreed, but what you noticed God doing."},{"title":"Diving With the Spirit","body":"Choose one short passage — 1 Corinthians 2:9–12 is a good one. Set aside thirty minutes. Read it slowly three times. Then pray: “Holy Spirit, You search the deep things of God. Show me what has been freely given to me.” Sit in silence for at least ten minutes. Write down anything that comes to you — a phrase that stands out, a connection, a conviction, a sense of God's love. Do not rush to conclusions. Repeat this practice twice more this week with the same passage, and notice how much more it opens each time."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I confess that I have wanted to be impressive. I have measured myself by eloquence and confidence and knowledge, and I have held back from serving You because I felt weak. But Paul came to Corinth in weakness, in fear and in much trembling, and You built Your church through him. So I bring You my weakness today. I determine, like him, not to know anything except Jesus Christ and Him crucified. Let my faith not stand in the wisdom of men, but in Your power. Where my faith has been resting on personalities and arguments and performances, move it onto the rock of Your power. Lord of glory, the rulers of this age did not know You and they crucified You — and in doing it, they walked into Your plan. Thank You that every power pressing on my life is coming to nothing, and that You prepared glory for me before the ages. Holy Spirit, You search all things, even the deep things of God. I do not want to live at the surface of what I have been given. Take me down into the depths. Teach me what has been freely given to me. Open my eyes where I have been living like a natural man, deciding by my own reasoning without asking You. And thank You, Lord, that I have the mind of Christ — the mind that humbled itself and went to the cross. Let that mind be in me today. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Moses and the Mouth He Did Not Trust","ref":"Exodus 3:1-4:17","relatedVerses":"v. 1-5","summary":"At the burning bush, God commissions Moses to confront Pharaoh, and Moses produces excuse after excuse. Who am I? What will I tell them? What if they will not believe me? And finally the one that sounds most like Corinth's standards in reverse: he is not eloquent, he is slow of speech and of tongue. God's answer is not to make Moses a great orator. It is to remind him who made man's mouth, and to promise to be with his mouth and teach him what to say. When Moses still resists, God gives him Aaron — but the power that confronts Egypt is never Moses' rhetoric. It is the signs and the plagues and the parted sea. Paul stands in Moses' tradition: a reluctant, weak messenger, through whom God makes it unmistakable that the power belongs to Him."},{"title":"The Night Vision in Corinth","ref":"Acts 18:1-11","relatedVerses":"v. 3","summary":"Luke gives us the scene behind Paul's “fear and much trembling.” Paul has come from Athens, and in Corinth he is working by hand as a tentmaker and preaching in the synagogue. When opposition rises he moves next door, but the threat does not go away. Then one night the Lord speaks to him in a vision: do not be afraid, but speak and do not be silent, for I am with you, and no one will attack you to harm you, for I have many people in this city. Why would the risen Christ need to tell His great apostle not to be afraid? Because he was afraid. And the result of that word was eighteen months of ministry and a church that would outlast the empire. The courage did not come from Paul. It came from a promise spoken in the dark."},{"title":"Unlearned Men Who Had Been With Jesus","ref":"Acts 4:1-22","relatedVerses":"v. 4-5, 14-16","summary":"Peter and John are arrested after healing a lame man and are brought before the same Jewish rulers who had condemned Jesus. Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, preaches Christ crucified and risen to their faces. And Luke records the council's astonishment: when they saw the boldness of Peter and John, and perceived that they were unlearned and ignorant men, they marveled, and they recognized that they had been with Jesus. Two fishermen with no formal training silenced the educated elite — not by superior argument, but by the Spirit and by a healed man standing in front of everyone. That is 1 Corinthians 2:4–5 in a courtroom: the demonstration of the Spirit and of power, so that faith rests on God and not on men."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Milk, Fields and Fire — God's Farm, God's Building, God's Temple","themeTitle":"“The Fire Will Not Ask How Big It Was — Only What It Was Made Of”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Brothers, I couldn’t speak to you as to spiritual, but as to fleshly, as to babies in Christ.","understanding":"Paul turns the last verse of chapter 2 on its head. They claimed to be πνευματικοί, spiritual people — and he says he could not speak to them that way. Instead they were σάρκινοι, made of flesh, and νήπιοι, infants. The word means literally “not speaking,” a child too young to talk. Corinth was proud of its eloquence, and Paul tells them that spiritually they had not learned to talk yet. Notice, though: “babies in Christ.” They are in Christ. The rebuke is to real believers who never grew up.","words":[{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual"},{"strongs":"G4560","hebrew":"σάρκινος","translit":"sárkinos","english":"fleshly"},{"strongs":"G3516","hebrew":"νήπιος","translit":"nḗpios","english":"babies"}]},{"v":2,"text":"I fed you with milk, not with meat; for you weren’t yet ready. Indeed, not even now are you ready,","understanding":"Γάλα, milk, and βρῶμα, solid food. When Paul first came, milk was right; you do not feed steak to a newborn. The problem is the next sentence: “Indeed, not even now are you ready.” Years have passed. They have gifts, teachers, knowledge, and tongues. And they still need a bottle. Spiritual age is not measured by how long you have been a Christian or how much you know. It is measured by what you can digest.","words":[{"strongs":"G1051","hebrew":"γάλα","translit":"gála","english":"milk"},{"strongs":"G1033","hebrew":"βρῶμα","translit":"brōma","english":"meat (solid food)"}]},{"v":3,"text":"for you are still fleshly. For insofar as there is jealousy, strife, and factions among you, aren’t you fleshly, and don’t you walk in the ways of men?","understanding":"The proof of immaturity is not lack of knowledge but the presence of ζῆλος, jealousy, and ἔρις, strife. And here the word changes to σαρκικοί, fleshly in character — not just made of flesh but governed by it. Then a cutting question: “don’t you walk in the ways of men?” The Greek says “walk according to man” — you are living like ordinary unconverted people. They thought their gifts proved they were spiritual. Paul says their quarrels proved they were not.","words":[{"strongs":"G4559","hebrew":"σαρκικός","translit":"sarkikós","english":"fleshly"},{"strongs":"G2205","hebrew":"ζῆλος","translit":"zēlos","english":"jealousy"},{"strongs":"G2054","hebrew":"ἔρις","translit":"éris","english":"strife"},{"strongs":"G1370","hebrew":"διχοστασία","translit":"dichostasía","english":"factions"}]},{"v":4,"text":"For when one says, “I follow Paul,” and another, “I follow Apollos,” aren’t you fleshly?","understanding":"Paul names the slogans again from 1:12 — “I follow Paul,” “I follow Apollos” — and attaches them to the diagnosis. Party spirit is not a sign of spiritual seriousness. It is a symptom of the flesh. When you need a human leader to define your identity, you are acting like a child who needs to say whose gang he belongs to.","words":[{"strongs":"G4559","hebrew":"σαρκικός","translit":"sarkikós","english":"fleshly"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Who then is Apollos, and who is Paul, but servants through whom you believed; and each as the Lord gave to him?","understanding":"“Who then is Apollos, and who is Paul?” Notice he says “who” in some manuscripts and “what” in others — and the point is the same either way: they are διάκονοι, servants. The word originally meant a table waiter. That is all the great preachers are: waiters bringing the food. And each did only what “the Lord gave to him.” Nobody fights over which waiter brought the meal. You thank the one who cooked it.","words":[{"strongs":"G1249","hebrew":"διάκονος","translit":"diákonos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believed"}]},{"v":6,"text":"I planted. Apollos watered. But God gave the increase.","understanding":"Three verbs and three tenses. Paul φυτεύω, planted — a single past act, the founding of the church. Apollos ποτίζω, watered — also completed. But God αὐξάνω — and here the tense changes to the imperfect: God was giving the growth, continuously, all along. The men did their moments. God was doing the work the whole time.","words":[{"strongs":"G5452","hebrew":"φυτεύω","translit":"phyteúō","english":"planted"},{"strongs":"G4222","hebrew":"ποτίζω","translit":"potízō","english":"watered"},{"strongs":"G837","hebrew":"αὐξάνω","translit":"auxánō","english":"gave the increase"}]},{"v":7,"text":"So then neither he who plants is anything, nor he who waters, but God who gives the increase.","understanding":"The conclusion is humbling for everyone in ministry: neither the planter nor the waterer “is anything.” Not that their work is useless — Paul works harder than anyone — but that, compared to the One who makes things grow, they are not the point. No farmer has ever made a seed sprout. He can plant and water. Life comes from somewhere else.","words":[{"strongs":"G837","hebrew":"αὐξάνω","translit":"auxánō","english":"gives the increase"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Now he who plants and he who waters are the same, but each will receive his own reward according to his own labor.","understanding":"The planter and the waterer “are the same” — literally “are one.” They are not rivals but one team with one purpose. Yet each will receive his own μισθός, wages, “according to his own κόπος,” labour. Note what is rewarded: not results but toil. God pays for faithful labour, not for visible success. The man who planted and saw nothing grow will be paid for his planting.","words":[{"strongs":"G3408","hebrew":"μισθός","translit":"misthós","english":"reward"},{"strongs":"G2873","hebrew":"κόπος","translit":"kópos","english":"labor"}]},{"v":9,"text":"For we are God’s fellow workers. You are God’s farming, God’s building.","understanding":"Three times “God's” in one verse, each placed first in the Greek for emphasis: God's fellow workers, God's farm, God's building. Συνεργός — co-workers, from which we get synergy. The great honour of ministry is not that you are important, but that you get to work alongside God in His field. Γεώργιον is a cultivated field; οἰκοδομή is a building under construction. The image now switches from agriculture to architecture.","words":[{"strongs":"G4904","hebrew":"συνεργός","translit":"synergós","english":"fellow workers"},{"strongs":"G1091","hebrew":"γεώργιον","translit":"geṓrgion","english":"farming"},{"strongs":"G3619","hebrew":"οἰκοδομή","translit":"oikodomḗ","english":"building"}]},{"v":10,"text":"According to the grace of God which was given to me, as a wise master builder I laid a foundation, and another builds on it. But let each man be careful how he builds on it.","understanding":"Ἀρχιτέκτων — chief builder, master craftsman, the root of our word architect. Paul calls himself “wise” — after two chapters of attacking wisdom — but he immediately grounds it in “the grace of God which was given to me.” He laid the θεμέλιος, the foundation, and others ἐποικοδομέω, build on top of it. Then the warning that governs the next five verses: “let each man be careful how he builds on it.” Not whether, but how.","words":[{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G753","hebrew":"ἀρχιτέκτων","translit":"architéktōn","english":"master builder"},{"strongs":"G2310","hebrew":"θεμέλιος","translit":"themélios","english":"foundation"},{"strongs":"G2026","hebrew":"ἐποικοδομέω","translit":"epoikodoméō","english":"builds on"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For no one can lay any other foundation than that which has been laid, which is Jesus Christ.","understanding":"There is only one foundation, and it has already been laid: Jesus Christ. No one can lay another one. Every true church, every true ministry, every true Christian life stands on the same rock. The question is never whether you have the right foundation if you are in Christ. The question is what you are putting on top of it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2310","hebrew":"θεμέλιος","translit":"themélios","english":"foundation"}]},{"v":12,"text":"But if anyone builds on the foundation with gold, silver, costly stones, wood, hay, or stubble;","understanding":"Two sets of three materials. Gold, silver, costly stones — precious, durable, fireproof. Wood, hay, stubble — cheap, quick, and combustible. The list descends in value and rises in flammability. You could build a hut of straw in an afternoon; a temple of marble takes years. The temptation in ministry and in life is always to build fast and cheap and impressive-looking. Paul says the materials will be tested.","words":[{"strongs":"G5557","hebrew":"χρυσός","translit":"chrysós","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G696","hebrew":"ἄργυρος","translit":"árgyros","english":"silver"},{"strongs":"G5093","hebrew":"τίμιος","translit":"tímios","english":"costly"},{"strongs":"G3586","hebrew":"ξύλον","translit":"xýlon","english":"wood"},{"strongs":"G5528","hebrew":"χόρτος","translit":"chórtos","english":"hay"},{"strongs":"G2562","hebrew":"καλάμη","translit":"kalámē","english":"stubble"}]},{"v":13,"text":"each man’s work will be revealed. For the Day will declare it, because it is revealed in fire; and the fire itself will test what sort of work each man’s work is.","understanding":"“The Day” — the Day of the Lord, the day of judgment — will δηλόω, make it plain. It is revealed in πῦρ, fire, and the fire δοκιμάζω — tests, assays, the word used for testing metal in a furnace. Notice what is tested: “what sort” of work it is — its quality, not its size. Corinth was impressed by quantity, noise and display. The fire will not ask how big it was, only what it was made of.","words":[{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"Day"},{"strongs":"G1213","hebrew":"δηλόω","translit":"dēlóō","english":"declare"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G1381","hebrew":"δοκιμάζω","translit":"dokimázō","english":"test"}]},{"v":14,"text":"If any man’s work remains which he built on it, he will receive a reward.","understanding":"If the work μένω — remains, survives the fire — the builder receives a reward. Paul does not define the reward here, and it is wise not to speculate beyond him. What is certain is that faithful work done on Christ will survive the Day of judgment, and that God will acknowledge it.","words":[{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G3408","hebrew":"μισθός","translit":"misthós","english":"reward"}]},{"v":15,"text":"If any man’s work is burned, he will suffer loss, but he himself will be saved, but as through fire.","understanding":"Κατακαίω — burned down completely. If the work is consumed, the builder ζημιόω, suffers loss — he forfeits the reward. “But he himself will be saved, but as through fire.” The picture is of a man escaping a burning house with nothing but his life. This verse is about believers — people built on Christ — whose works are tested, not about unbelievers, and it is about works being tested, not souls being purged. It offers no support for the later doctrine of purgatory. It does offer a sobering possibility: a saved life with nothing to show for it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2618","hebrew":"κατακαίω","translit":"katakaíō","english":"burned"},{"strongs":"G2210","hebrew":"ζημιόω","translit":"zēmióō","english":"suffer loss"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saved"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Don’t you know that you are a temple of God, and that God’s Spirit lives in you?","understanding":"The image tightens one more time: from field, to building, to ναός — not the whole temple complex but the inner sanctuary, the holy place where God's presence dwelt. And the “you” is plural: you, together, are a temple of God. The church gathered in Corinth was the place where God lived. “Don’t you know?” — Paul asks this ten times in this letter. They knew so much, and they had forgotten the most basic things.","words":[{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G3611","hebrew":"οἰκέω","translit":"oikéō","english":"lives"}]},{"v":17,"text":"If anyone destroys God’s temple, God will destroy him; for God’s temple is holy, which you are.","understanding":"The most severe warning in the chapter, and it is a play on one verb: “If anyone φθείρω — ruins, corrupts, destroys — God’s temple, God will φθείρω him.” The context is division. The people tearing the Corinthian church apart with factions were not merely being unpleasant; they were vandalising God's house, and God takes that personally. “God’s temple is holy, which you are.” A church is not a club you can break. It is a sanctuary you can desecrate.","words":[{"strongs":"G5351","hebrew":"φθείρω","translit":"phtheírō","english":"destroys"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Let no one deceive himself. If anyone thinks that he is wise among you in this world, let him become a fool, that he may become wise.","understanding":"“Let no one deceive himself.” Ἐξαπατάω — thoroughly deceive, the word Paul uses of the serpent deceiving Eve (2 Corinthians 11:3). Self-deception is the subtlest kind. Anyone who thinks he is wise by the standards of this age must become a μωρός — a fool in the world's eyes — in order to become truly wise. The way up is down. You cannot add the cross to your existing wisdom. You have to let it overturn it.","words":[{"strongs":"G1818","hebrew":"ἐξαπατάω","translit":"exapatáō","english":"deceive"},{"strongs":"G4680","hebrew":"σοφός","translit":"sophós","english":"wise"},{"strongs":"G3474","hebrew":"μωρός","translit":"mōrós","english":"fool"}]},{"v":19,"text":"For the wisdom of this world is foolishness with God. For it is written, “He has taken the wise in their craftiness.”","understanding":"Paul quotes Job 5:13, from the speech of Eliphaz — one of Job's friends whose overall counsel God later rebukes. But Paul cites this particular statement as Scripture, and it is true: God δράσσομαι, catches, the clever in their πανουργία, their craftiness, their trickery. The schemes that seem smartest are the very nets in which God catches the schemers.","words":[{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G1405","hebrew":"δράσσομαι","translit":"drássomai","english":"taken"},{"strongs":"G3834","hebrew":"πανουργία","translit":"panourgía","english":"craftiness"}]},{"v":20,"text":"And again, “The Lord knows the reasoning of the wise, that it is worthless.”","understanding":"A second quotation, from Psalm 94:11. The Lord knows the διαλογισμοί — the reasonings, the internal calculations — of the wise, and that they are μάταιοι: empty, futile, vain. The Hebrew of the psalm says the thoughts of man; Paul applies it to the wise, which is exactly the category Corinth admired.","words":[{"strongs":"G1261","hebrew":"διαλογισμός","translit":"dialogismós","english":"reasoning"},{"strongs":"G3152","hebrew":"μάταιος","translit":"mátaios","english":"worthless"}]},{"v":21,"text":"Therefore let no one boast in men. For all things are yours,","understanding":"The conclusion of the first major section of the letter: “let no one boast in men.” And then the astonishing reason: “For all things are yours.” They were trying to belong to Paul or Apollos. Paul reverses it. You do not belong to them. They belong to you.","words":[{"strongs":"G2744","hebrew":"καυχάομαι","translit":"kaucháomai","english":"boast"},{"strongs":"G3956","hebrew":"πᾶς","translit":"pâs","english":"all things"}]},{"v":22,"text":"whether Paul, or Apollos, or Cephas, or the world, or life, or death, or things present, or things to come. All are yours,","understanding":"The list explodes outward. Paul, Apollos, Cephas — the leaders they were fighting over. Then the world, life, death, things present, things to come — everything in existence and everything in time. All of it is yours. Death is yours: it serves you now, the door into Christ's presence. The future is yours. Why would anyone shrink themselves into a faction when the whole universe has been put at their service?","words":[{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"}]},{"v":23,"text":"and you are Christ’s, and Christ is God’s.","understanding":"And then the order of ownership: you are Christ's, and Christ is God's. Everything belongs to you because you belong to Christ, and Christ belongs to the Father. You are not the top of the chain; you are owned. But you are owned by the One who owns everything. That is why no human loyalty can compete. You already have the best master there is.","words":[{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"A Baby With a Beard","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a grown man — broad shoulders, a beard, a booming voice, an expensive coat — sitting in a high chair and crying for his bottle. He can recite poetry, debate philosophy and quote whole chapters of Scripture. But put two other men in the room who get more attention than he does and he throws his food on the floor. That is what Paul sees when he looks at Corinth. Gifted. Knowledgeable. Articulate. And spiritually still in nappies, fighting over whose turn it is.","meaning":"Paul's diagnosis of spiritual immaturity is not what most people expect. He does not say the Corinthians lacked knowledge; they had plenty. He does not say they lacked gifts; they lacked none (1:7). He says the evidence of their infancy was jealousy and strife. Maturity is not measured by what you know, how long you have been saved, or how spectacular your gifts are. It is measured by how you treat people who are honoured more than you. A Christian who cannot rejoice when someone else is praised is still drinking milk, no matter how much he can quote."},{"title":"The Farmer Who Cannot Make Anything Grow","verseRef":"v. 6-9","image":"Watch two farmers in the same field. The first one ploughs and plants in early spring — back bent, hands blistered, seed bag on his hip. Weeks later the second one comes with buckets from the stream and waters every row through the dry months. Then one morning both of them stand at the edge of the field and watch green shoots breaking the soil. Neither of them made it happen. Neither of them could have. They could put the seed in the ground and water it, but the life inside the seed, the miracle of germination, the push of a green stem through soil — that came from God. And it was happening the whole time they were working.","meaning":"The tense of the verbs in verse 6 is the sermon. Paul planted (once). Apollos watered (once). God was giving the growth (continuously). The human contributions were moments; the divine work was constant. That changes how you see every ministry, including your own. You are responsible for planting and watering — faithfully, carefully, without cutting corners. You are not responsible for the growth, and you cannot produce it. If you take credit for it, you are stealing from God. If you despair because you cannot see it yet, you are forgetting who is working underground. And notice verse 8: you are paid for your labour, not your harvest."},{"title":"The City That Had Burned","verseRef":"v. 12-15","image":"The Corinthians knew what fire did to a city. In 146 BC the Roman general Mummius had captured old Corinth, and the city was sacked and burned. Picture it: wooden roofs collapsing, thatched sheds turning to ash in seconds, the smoke visible for miles across the gulf. When the flames died down, the wood, hay and straw were gone. What remained standing were the stone columns — some of the columns of the old Temple of Apollo still stand in Corinth today — and the metal that had melted and pooled in the rubble. Now picture the Day of the Lord as a fire like that, passing over every life built on Christ. Some lives come through it like stone. Others vanish like a straw roof, and the builder runs out of the burning house with nothing but the clothes he is wearing.","meaning":"This is not a picture of unbelievers being judged; they are not on the foundation at all. It is a picture of believers whose works are being tested. Everyone in view is building on Christ. The question is the material. Gold, silver and costly stones are slow, expensive and durable — work done with pure motives, in obedience, with love, according to the truth. Wood, hay and stubble are quick, cheap and impressive for a while — work done for applause, for numbers, in the flesh, with shortcuts. The fire tests “what sort” of work it is, not how big it is. And verse 15 is one of the most sobering sentences Paul ever wrote: it is possible to be saved and yet arrive with nothing."},{"title":"The Sanctuary in the Courtyard","verseRef":"v. 16-17","image":"In Jerusalem, at the centre of the great temple complex with its courts and porches and crowds, there was an inner sanctuary — the ναός — where the presence of God dwelt. Only priests entered the Holy Place, and only the high priest entered the Holy of Holies, once a year, with blood. Now picture a small group of believers gathered in a courtyard in Corinth on a Sunday evening: slaves, dockworkers, a few wealthier families, all quarrelling about which preacher was best. Paul looks at that room and says: this is the ναός. Here is where God lives now.","meaning":"The “you” in verses 16 and 17 is plural. Paul is not yet talking about the individual body (that comes in 6:19). He is talking about the gathered church as the sanctuary of God's presence. And the warning is terrifying precisely because of that: whoever destroys — corrupts, ruins — God's temple, God will destroy him. The context is division. The people splitting Corinth into parties thought they were having a debate about preachers. Paul says they were vandalising the Holy of Holies. Treat the church — the actual, flawed, frustrating people you worship with — as the dwelling of God, because it is."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Still Infants (v. 1-4)","body":"Paul could not speak to them as spiritual people because they were still fleshly — babies who need milk — and the proof was their jealousy, strife and factions over human leaders."},{"label":"Servants in God's Field (v. 5-9)","body":"Paul and Apollos are only servants; one planted, one watered, but God gave the growth — so the workers are one, each rewarded for his labour, all of them God's fellow workers in God's field."},{"label":"Builders on One Foundation (v. 10-15)","body":"Paul laid the only foundation, Jesus Christ; everyone else must take care how they build on it, because the Day will test each one's work by fire — some will be rewarded, others will suffer loss though they themselves are saved."},{"label":"The Temple of God (v. 16-17)","body":"The church together is God's sanctuary where His Spirit lives; whoever destroys it will be destroyed by God."},{"label":"All Things Are Yours (v. 18-23)","body":"Abandon worldly wisdom; stop boasting in men; everything — leaders, the world, life, death, present and future — belongs to you, because you belong to Christ, and Christ belongs to God."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Measure of Maturity: Not What You Know but How You Treat Rivals","paragraphs":["Chapter 3 opens with one of the most deflating sentences ever written to a church. The Corinthians believed they were πνευματικοί, spiritual people — the very word Paul had just used in 2:15. They had tongues, prophecy, knowledge, and eloquent teachers. And Paul says, in effect: I could not talk to you that way. You were σάρκινοι — made of flesh — and νήπιοι, babies. Worse: “Indeed, not even now are you ready.” This is not a description of new converts. Paul had spent eighteen months with them, Apollos had taught them since, and several years had passed. They had been Christians long enough to be eating meat, and they were still on milk.","What is the evidence? Not lack of information — they were proud of their knowledge. Not lack of experience — they had more spiritual gifts than any church Paul wrote to. The evidence is verse 3: “insofar as there is jealousy, strife, and factions among you, aren’t you fleshly, and don’t you walk in the ways of men?” Paul's word shifts from σάρκινος (made of flesh) in verse 1 to σαρκικός (characterised by the flesh) in verse 3, and the point sharpens: being made of flesh is not a sin, but being governed by it is. Their quarrels about preachers were not a sign of theological seriousness. They were the behaviour of ordinary unconverted people. “Walk according to man,” the Greek says — you are living as if the cross had never happened.","This reframes spiritual maturity entirely. In Corinth, and in many churches today, maturity is measured by gifts, knowledge, years of service or public visibility. Paul measures it by the presence or absence of jealousy and strife. The writer of Hebrews makes a similar diagnosis — “You have come to need milk, and not solid food” (Hebrews 5:12) — and defines maturity as having “senses exercised to discern good and evil” (5:14). Maturity is not a bigger head. It is a trained conscience and a heart that can rejoice when someone else is honoured. If you want to know how mature you are, do not ask how much you know. Ask how you respond when the person you consider your rival is praised in front of you."]},{"heading":"Wood, Hay and Stubble: The Coming Fire and What It Will Test","paragraphs":["Verses 10 to 15 are among the most important verses in the New Testament about the judgment of believers, and they are frequently misunderstood in two opposite directions. First, be clear about who is in view. Everyone in this passage is building on the foundation, “which is Jesus Christ” (v. 11). This is not about the final judgment of unbelievers. It is about the evaluation of the work of believers — and in the immediate context, especially the work of those who teach and lead the church, since Paul is talking about himself, Apollos and those who build after them. Paul will say the same thing in his second letter to this church: “For we must all be revealed before the judgment seat of Christ; that each one may receive the things in the body, according to what he has done, whether good or bad” (2 Corinthians 5:10).","Second, notice what is tested. Not the person's salvation — that rests on the foundation, which cannot be shaken. What is tested is “what sort of work each man’s work is” (v. 13). The quality, not the quantity. The six materials of verse 12 are a deliberate contrast: gold, silver and costly stones are precious and fireproof; wood, hay and stubble are cheap and burn instantly. You can build a straw hut in an afternoon and it will look like a building. A stone temple takes years. Paul does not define the materials precisely, and it is better not to over-allegorise them. But the rest of the letter shows what he has in mind: work built with the wisdom of the cross versus the wisdom of the world; work done in love versus work done for self (chapter 13); teaching that is true versus teaching that flatters. The Day will reveal it, because God “will both bring to light the hidden things of darkness, and reveal the counsels of the hearts” (4:5). Motives are part of the material.","Third, be honest about verse 15, because it has been bent out of shape. Some later traditions used it to support the doctrine of purgatory — a process of purifying souls after death. But the fire here tests works, not people; it happens on “the Day,” not over a period after death; and the person is “saved” already, with the fire describing the manner of his escape, not a process that saves him. The image is of a man running out of a burning house with his life and nothing else. That should sober every believer. It is possible to be truly saved and yet to stand before Christ having built a life that does not survive the fire — a life of busy, visible, impressive straw. The question is not whether you are building. Everyone is. The question is what you are building with."]},{"heading":"You Do Not Belong to Them — They Belong to You","paragraphs":["Verses 16 and 17 lift the stakes of division higher than anyone in Corinth had imagined. The church is not just a field or a building; it is the ναός, the inner sanctuary, the place where God's presence lives. In the Old Testament, the presence of God dwelt in the tabernacle and then in the temple in Jerusalem. Now, Paul says, it dwells in the gathered people of God. That is why the warning of verse 17 is so severe. Φθείρω means to ruin, corrupt or destroy, and Paul uses it twice in the same sentence: whoever ruins God's temple, God will ruin. The people who were splitting the church into rival factions were not merely being disagreeable. They were defacing God's house, and God takes the vandalism of His sanctuary personally. Notice that Paul does not say this about pagans attacking the church from outside. He says it about members tearing it apart from within.","Then Paul brings the whole section, chapters 1 to 3, to a close. “Let no one deceive himself” (v. 18). The Corinthians believed that attaching themselves to the right teacher made them wise. Paul tells them that real wisdom begins by becoming a fool in the world's eyes — by accepting the message of the cross that the world laughs at. He quotes two Old Testament texts. Job 5:13 says God catches the wise in their own craftiness; Psalm 94:11 says the Lord knows the reasonings of the wise are futile. (It is worth noting honestly that the first comes from Eliphaz, one of Job's friends whose overall counsel God rebukes in Job 42:7. Paul is not endorsing everything Eliphaz said; he is citing a statement that is true and consistent with the rest of Scripture.)","And then the breathtaking conclusion: “Therefore let no one boast in men. For all things are yours, whether Paul, or Apollos, or Cephas, or the world, or life, or death, or things present, or things to come. All are yours, and you are Christ’s, and Christ is God’s” (vv. 21–23). The Corinthians had their ownership upside down. They thought they belonged to their leaders — “I am of Paul.” Paul says the leaders belong to them; they are gifts God gave for the church's good. Everything belongs to them: the world, life, even death, which now serves the believer as the door into Christ's presence. Why would anyone shrink themselves down to the size of a party when the whole universe has been placed at their service? And yet the chain does not end with “you.” You are Christ's, and Christ is God's. You are free from every human master because you have been bought by the only Master who matters."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["Paul's foundation of verse 11 has its root in the words of Jesus Himself. At the end of the Sermon on the Mount Jesus told the story of two builders: “Everyone therefore who hears these words of mine, and does them, I will liken him to a wise man, who built his house on a rock. The rain came down, the floods came, and the winds blew, and beat on that house; and it didn’t fall, for it was founded on the rock” (Matthew 7:24–25). Jesus' story tests the foundation; Paul's passage tests the building on top of it. Together they cover both questions: are you on the rock, and what are you building there? And Peter, preaching before the very council that condemned Jesus, called Him “the stone which was regarded as worthless by you, the builders, which has become the head of the corner” (Acts 4:11). The rejected stone is the only foundation.","The temple image of verses 16 and 17 grows across the New Testament. Jesus Himself said, “Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up,” and John explains, “But he spoke of the temple of his body” (John 2:19, 21). The presence of God that dwelt in the tabernacle and the Jerusalem temple came to live in the body of Jesus. Then, through the Spirit, that dwelling extends to His people. Paul tells the Ephesians they are “built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Christ Jesus himself being the chief cornerstone; in whom the whole building, fitted together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord; in whom you also are built together for a habitation of God in the Spirit” (Ephesians 2:20–22). Peter says the same: “You also, as living stones, are built up as a spiritual house, to be a holy priesthood” (1 Peter 2:5). And at the end of the story the image reaches its fulfilment: “I saw no temple in it, for the Lord God, the Almighty, and the Lamb, are its temple” (Revelation 21:22).","And the judgment of believers' works in verses 13 to 15 is repeated in Romans, written from Corinth a few years later: “But you, why do you judge your brother? Or you again, why do you despise your brother? For we will all stand before the judgment seat of Christ… So then each one of us will give account of himself to God” (Romans 14:10, 12). Notice how Paul uses this truth in both letters: not to frighten believers about their salvation but to stop them judging and despising each other. If each of us will give account of our own building, we have no business tearing down someone else's. The coming fire is the best argument against present factions."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Babies / infants","hebrew":"νήπιος","translit":"nḗpios","strongs":"G3516","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “not speaking, i.e. an infant (minor); figuratively, a simple-minded person, an immature Christian.” The word is built from a negative prefix and a root meaning to speak — literally a child too young to talk. The irony would not have been lost on Corinth, a church that prided itself on eloquence and on speaking in tongues. Spiritually, Paul says, you have not learned to speak yet. Paul uses the same word positively in 14:20 — “in malice be babies, but in thoughts be mature.” There is a right way to be a child: innocent of evil. Corinth had chosen the wrong way: immature in understanding and sophisticated in quarrelling."},{"english":"Fleshly (governed by the flesh)","hebrew":"σαρκικός","translit":"sarkikós","strongs":"G4559 (compare σάρκινος, sárkinos, G4560)","verses":"v. 3, 4 (σάρκινος in v. 1)","insight":"Strong's defines σαρκικός as “pertaining to flesh, i.e. (by extension) bodily, temporal, or (by implication) animal, unregenerate,” and σάρκινος as “similar to flesh.” The difference matters. In verse 1, Paul says they were σάρκινοι — made of flesh, as every newborn is; that is simply their condition as infants. In verse 3 he says they are still σαρκικοί — characterised and governed by the flesh, which is a moral charge. A baby is not to blame for being a baby. A grown man who still behaves like one is. The progression from verse 1 to verse 3 is the progression from a season of immaturity, which is normal, to a refusal to grow, which is not."},{"english":"Fellow workers / co-labourers","hebrew":"συνεργός","translit":"synergós","strongs":"G4904","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's: “a co-laborer, i.e. coadjutor.” This is the word from which English gets synergy — working together. Paul uses it of his ministry partners repeatedly — Priscilla and Aquila are his συνεργοί (Romans 16:3), Timothy is his συνεργός (Romans 16:21). Here he uses it of himself and Apollos in relation to God: “we are God’s fellow workers.” The honour of ministry is not that God needs you but that He invites you to work alongside Him in His field. A servant who understands that will never compete with another servant. You do not fight with the other workers in your master's field. You work beside them."},{"english":"Test / prove (by assay)","hebrew":"δοκιμάζω","translit":"dokimázō","strongs":"G1381","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's: “to test (literally or figuratively); by implication, to approve.” In ordinary Greek this was the word for assaying metal — testing gold and silver in a furnace to see whether they were genuine. The same family gives us δόκιμος, approved, the word Paul uses in 2 Timothy 2:15 for a worker “approved by God.” Notice that the purpose of testing is approval, not destruction. Gold put through the fire comes out purer and proven. The fire of verse 13 is not God's way of hunting for faults; it is His way of revealing what was real. The only things it destroys are things that were never going to last anyway."},{"english":"Temple / inner sanctuary","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","strongs":"G3485","verses":"v. 16, 17 (×2)","insight":"Strong's: “a fane, shrine, temple.” The New Testament has two main words for temple. Ἱερόν refers to the whole temple complex with its courts and porches — the place where Jesus overturned tables. Ναός refers to the sanctuary itself, the dwelling of God, the Holy Place and the Holy of Holies. Paul uses ναός here, and again in 6:19 and 2 Corinthians 6:16. The church is not just a religious complex where people gather. It is the inner sanctuary where God dwells. That is why division is not a minor problem in Paul's eyes. To tear the church apart is to tear down the walls of the Holy of Holies."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the immaturity you have mistaken for spirituality, the materials you are actually building your life with, and the people in your church you have stopped treating as God's dwelling place.","items":[{"title":"Still on Milk","body":"You may know a great deal of Scripture. You may have been a Christian for years. You may serve, pray, give and even teach. But the Spirit wants to ask you the question Paul asked Corinth: how do you respond when someone else is honoured? When another person's ministry grows faster than yours, when another believer is praised in front of you, when your opinion is passed over for someone else's — what happens inside you? Jealousy and strife are the marks of a baby, not a giant. The Spirit is not condemning you. He is telling you that you are meant to grow, and that the way to solid food runs straight through the death of your need to be first."},{"title":"What Are You Building With?","body":"Everyone builds. The question is the material. Look honestly at your work — your ministry, your career, your family, your service. How much of it is gold, silver and costly stones: slow, costly obedience, done with love and pure motives, according to the truth? And how much is wood, hay and stubble: fast, cheap, impressive for a moment, done for applause or numbers or self-protection? The fire is coming, and it will not ask how big your work was, how many people saw it or how much they praised it. It will ask what it was made of. The Spirit is asking you to change the materials now, while there is still time to build."},{"title":"You Are Standing in the Holy of Holies","body":"There is a church you are frustrated with. There are believers you find difficult, a leader you resent, a group you complain about. The Spirit wants you to see them the way Paul saw Corinth: “Don’t you know that you are a temple of God, and that God’s Spirit lives in you?” The flawed, irritating people you worship with are the dwelling place of the Most High God. The words you say about them, the divisions you encourage, the gossip you pass on — those are not neutral. They are either building up the sanctuary or tearing it down. Ask the Spirit to show you whether your words this month have been building God's temple or vandalising it."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Maturity Check","body":"Name one person in your church, ministry, workplace or family whose success makes you uncomfortable. Write down honestly what you feel when they are praised. If the answer is jealousy, resentment or a desire to see them taken down a notch, that is the clearest evidence of verse 3 in your life. Confess it specifically, by name, to God."},{"title":"The Materials Check","body":"List the three main things you are investing your time and energy in right now. Beside each one, write one honest sentence about your motive: why are you really doing it? Then ask: if the fire of verse 13 tested this today, would it be gold, silver and costly stones, or wood, hay and stubble? Do not answer quickly. Motives are part of the material."},{"title":"The Ownership Check","body":"Verse 21 says “let no one boast in men.” Is there a human leader, teacher or movement whose approval you need more than Christ's? Would you feel lost if they rejected you? Write down the name. Then read verses 21 to 23 aloud and remind yourself: that person belongs to you as a gift from God; you belong to Christ."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Honouring a Rival","body":"This week, do the opposite of jealousy. Choose the person whose success you named in the Maturity Check. Find a genuine reason to thank God for them, and then tell them — or tell someone else, in their hearing or in writing — specifically what God is doing through them. Do it without qualification and without mentioning yourself. Then pray for their work to grow even more. You will feel the milk bottle being taken away. That is the point."},{"title":"Rebuilding With Gold","body":"Pick one area of your life or ministry where you know you have been building with straw — cutting corners, doing it for show, chasing numbers, or working without love. Write down one concrete change that would turn it into gold: a hidden act of service no one will see, a slower and more faithful approach, a conversation you have avoided, a motive you need to surrender. Do that one thing before the week ends. Then write verse 14 on a card — “If any man’s work remains which he built on it, he will receive a reward” — and keep it where you work."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I confess that I have been a baby with a beard. I have known much and grown little. I have been jealous when others were honoured, and I have stirred up strife and taken sides. Forgive me for walking in the ways of men. Take away the milk and grow me up into the meat of Your Word. Lord, I thank You that I am Your field and Your building, and that You let me work beside You. Whatever I have planted or watered, You gave the growth — and You are giving it still, underground, where I cannot see. Keep me from stealing the credit, and keep me from despairing when I cannot see the harvest. You pay for faithful labour, not for visible success. Jesus, You are the only foundation. I am standing on You. Now show me what I am building with. Burn away the wood, hay and stubble in my life now, while there is still time to rebuild. Give me the patience to build with gold, silver and costly stones, slowly, faithfully, in love. When the Day comes, let my work remain. Holy Spirit, You live in Your church. Forgive me for every word I have spoken that tore down Your temple. Teach me to see the people I worship with as Your dwelling place. And Father, thank You that all things are mine — life, death, the present, the future — because I am Christ's, and Christ is Yours. I need no other master. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Bezalel, the Spirit-Filled Master Builder","ref":"Exodus 31:1-11; 35:30-36:7","relatedVerses":"v. 10-12","summary":"When God gave Moses the plans for the tabernacle — the first dwelling place of His presence among Israel — He did not leave the building to whoever was available. He called Bezalel by name and filled him with the Spirit of God, in wisdom, understanding and knowledge, to work in gold, silver and bronze and in cutting stones. Bezalel and Oholiab built exactly according to the pattern God gave, and the people brought so much material that Moses had to tell them to stop giving. When the work was finished, the glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle. Paul calls himself a “wise master builder” in verse 10, building God's dwelling place in Corinth. The pattern is the same: the Spirit's wisdom, the right materials, faithfulness to God's design, and a building God can fill with His glory."},{"title":"The Tower That Was Built for a Name","ref":"Genesis 11:1-9","relatedVerses":"v. 10-13, 18-21","summary":"On the plain of Shinar, humanity gathered to build a city and a tower reaching to the heavens, using bricks baked hard in fire and tar for mortar. Their stated purpose was candid: let us make a name for ourselves. It was impressive, it was unified, and it was built entirely on human wisdom and for human glory. God came down, confused their language, and scattered them, and the building stopped. Babel is the opposite of 1 Corinthians 3. It shows what happens when people build to make a name for themselves rather than on the foundation God has laid. Corinth's factions were a small Babel: each group building its own reputation around its own preferred leader. God's answer to both is the same — He alone gives the increase, and no flesh will boast before Him."},{"title":"Shouting and Weeping Over a Foundation","ref":"Ezra 3:8-13","relatedVerses":"v. 10-11","summary":"When the exiles returned from Babylon to a ruined Jerusalem, one of the first things they did was lay the foundation of a new temple. The priests blew trumpets, the Levites clashed cymbals, and the people sang that the LORD is good and His lovingkindness endures forever. But many of the older priests and leaders, who had seen the glory of Solomon's temple, wept aloud at the sight of this smaller foundation, while others shouted for joy, so that no one could tell the sound of joy from the sound of weeping. A foundation is easy to underestimate because you cannot see what will be built on it yet. Paul says the only foundation that matters has already been laid — Jesus Christ — and what is built on it will be revealed in time. Do not despise the day of small foundations. What matters is what is built on them, and with what."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":4,"chapterTitle":"Stewards, Spectacles and a Father's Rod — The Apostles at the Back of the Parade","themeTitle":"“What Do You Have That You Didn’t Receive?”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"So let a man think of us as Christ’s servants, and stewards of God’s mysteries.","understanding":"Two words for what a minister actually is. Ὑπηρέτης — an assistant, an attendant; Strong's traces it to an “under-oarsman,” and whether or not the old etymology was in anyone's mind, the meaning is plain: a subordinate who does what he is told. And οἰκονόμος — a household manager, usually a trusted slave put in charge of his master's goods. The steward owned nothing. He distributed what belonged to someone else. Paul says that is how to think of preachers: they are servants handing out the “mysteries of God,” not celebrities to be ranked.","words":[{"strongs":"G5257","hebrew":"ὑπηρέτης","translit":"hypērétēs","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G3623","hebrew":"οἰκονόμος","translit":"oikonómos","english":"stewards"},{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mysteries"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Here, moreover, it is required of stewards, that they be found faithful.","understanding":"The only qualification required of a steward is that he be found πιστός — faithful, trustworthy. Not brilliant, not eloquent, not successful, not popular. Faithful. The verb is εὑρίσκω, “found”: it is discovered by inspection. Someone will audit the household, and the only question will be whether the steward did with the master's goods what the master wanted.","words":[{"strongs":"G3623","hebrew":"οἰκονόμος","translit":"oikonómos","english":"stewards"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"}]},{"v":3,"text":"But with me it is a very small thing that I should be judged by you, or by man’s judgment. Yes, I don’t judge my own self.","understanding":"Ἐλάχιστος — the smallest thing, a superlative of littleness. Paul tells the Corinthians that their verdict on him barely registers. Then he says something even more striking: “I don’t judge my own self.” The verb ἀνακρίνω is a courtroom word for a preliminary hearing, the examination of a case. And the phrase translated “man’s judgment” is literally “a human day” — set against “the Day” of the Lord in 3:13. Human courts sit on human days. Paul is waiting for the real one.","words":[{"strongs":"G1646","hebrew":"ἐλάχιστος","translit":"eláchistos","english":"very small thing"},{"strongs":"G350","hebrew":"ἀνακρίνω","translit":"anakrínō","english":"judged"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"judgment (day)"}]},{"v":4,"text":"For I know nothing against myself. Yet I am not justified by this, but he who judges me is the Lord.","understanding":"Paul's conscience is clear — “I know nothing against myself” — but he refuses to treat a clear conscience as a verdict of innocence. Δικαιόω, justified, declared righteous: not by this. A clean conscience can be a poorly informed conscience. Only one Judge has the full facts: “he who judges me is the Lord.” This is the healthiest possible relationship to self-examination: honest, but not final.","words":[{"strongs":"G4894","hebrew":"συνείδω","translit":"syneídō","english":"know against"},{"strongs":"G1344","hebrew":"δικαιόω","translit":"dikaióō","english":"justified"},{"strongs":"G350","hebrew":"ἀνακρίνω","translit":"anakrínō","english":"judges"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Therefore judge nothing before the time, until the Lord comes, who will both bring to light the hidden things of darkness, and reveal the counsels of the hearts. Then each man will get his praise from God.","understanding":"“Judge nothing before the καιρός” — before the appointed time. The Lord will φωτίζω, shine a light on, the κρυπτά of σκότος — the hidden things of darkness — and reveal the βουλαί, the intentions, the plans of the heart. Then — and this is the beautiful ending — “each man will get his praise from God.” Not exposure, but ἔπαινος, commendation. The Lord's examination is not a hunt for faults. It ends in praise for those who were faithful in hidden places.","words":[{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G5461","hebrew":"φωτίζω","translit":"phōtízō","english":"bring to light"},{"strongs":"G2927","hebrew":"κρυπτός","translit":"kryptós","english":"hidden"},{"strongs":"G4655","hebrew":"σκότος","translit":"skótos","english":"darkness"},{"strongs":"G1012","hebrew":"βουλή","translit":"boulḗ","english":"counsels"},{"strongs":"G1868","hebrew":"ἔπαινος","translit":"épainos","english":"praise"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Now these things, brothers, I have in a figure transferred to myself and Apollos for your sakes, that in us you might learn not to think beyond the things which are written, that none of you be puffed up against one another.","understanding":"Μετασχηματίζω — to change the form of something, to apply a figure. Paul has used himself and Apollos as the example so that nobody would be offended by naming the actual Corinthian party leaders. Then a famously obscure phrase: “not to think beyond the things which are written.” It most likely means: do not go beyond Scripture — the Old Testament texts he has just quoted about God humbling the wise. The purpose is clear even if the phrase is compressed: so that no one is φυσιόω, puffed up, inflated like a bellows, for one teacher against another.","words":[{"strongs":"G3345","hebrew":"μετασχηματίζω","translit":"metaschēmatízō","english":"transferred in a figure"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G5448","hebrew":"φυσιόω","translit":"physióō","english":"puffed up"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For who makes you different? And what do you have that you didn’t receive? But if you did receive it, why do you boast as if you had not received it?","understanding":"Three questions that demolish pride in one verse. Διακρίνω — who makes you different, who sets you apart from anyone else? Answer: nobody but God. What do you have that you did not receive? Answer: nothing. And if you received it, why do you boast as if you produced it? Every gift, every talent, every opportunity, every spiritual experience, every insight was given. Boasting is the sin of a man who has forgotten where his things came from.","words":[{"strongs":"G1252","hebrew":"διακρίνω","translit":"diakrínō","english":"makes different"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G2744","hebrew":"καυχάομαι","translit":"kaucháomai","english":"boast"}]},{"v":8,"text":"You are already filled. You have already become rich. You have come to reign without us. Yes, and I wish that you did reign, that we also might reign with you.","understanding":"Biting irony, three times over. “You are already filled” — κορέννυμι, stuffed, gorged like a man who has eaten too much. “You have already become rich.” “You have come to reign without us.” The Corinthians behaved as if the kingdom had fully arrived and they were already enthroned — no more suffering, no more waiting, only glory. Paul's reply carries an ache: “I wish that you did reign, that we also might reign with you.” If the kingdom had fully come, he would not be bleeding.","words":[{"strongs":"G2880","hebrew":"κορέννυμι","translit":"korénnymi","english":"filled"},{"strongs":"G4147","hebrew":"πλουτέω","translit":"ploutéō","english":"become rich"},{"strongs":"G936","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","english":"reign"}]},{"v":9,"text":"For, I think that God has displayed us, the apostles, last of all, like men sentenced to death. For we are made a spectacle to the world, both to angels and men.","understanding":"The image is drawn from a Roman triumph. A victorious general paraded through the city with his army and his captured treasure, and at the very end of the procession came the prisoners condemned to die in the arena. God, Paul says, has ἀποδείκνυμι — exhibited, put on display — the apostles “last of all,” ἐπιθανάτιοι, men sentenced to death. We have become a θέατρον — a theatre, a spectacle — to the world, to angels and to men. The whole universe is watching the apostles walk to their deaths.","words":[{"strongs":"G584","hebrew":"ἀποδείκνυμι","translit":"apodeíknymi","english":"displayed"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G1935","hebrew":"ἐπιθανάτιος","translit":"epithanátios","english":"sentenced to death"},{"strongs":"G2302","hebrew":"θέατρον","translit":"théatron","english":"spectacle"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"}]},{"v":10,"text":"We are fools for Christ’s sake, but you are wise in Christ. We are weak, but you are strong. You have honor, but we have dishonor.","understanding":"Three contrasts, dripping with sarcasm and grief. We are μωροί, fools, for Christ's sake; you are φρόνιμοι, sensible, wise, in Christ. We are weak; you are strong. You are ἔνδοξοι, glorious, held in honour; we are ἄτιμοι, without honour. The Corinthians had taken the gospel of the cross and turned it into a ladder of status. Paul shows them the men who brought it to them, standing at the bottom.","words":[{"strongs":"G3474","hebrew":"μωρός","translit":"mōrós","english":"fools"},{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weak"},{"strongs":"G1741","hebrew":"ἔνδοξος","translit":"éndoxos","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G820","hebrew":"ἄτιμος","translit":"átimos","english":"dishonor"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Even to this present hour we hunger, thirst, are naked, are beaten, and have no certain dwelling place.","understanding":"“Even to this present hour.” Not a story from the past — this is Paul's life while he writes. Πεινάω, hungry. Διψάω, thirsty. Γυμνητεύω, poorly clothed, in rags. Κολαφίζω, struck with fists — the same verb used of the soldiers beating Jesus (Matthew 26:67). Ἀστατέω, homeless, without a settled place. The apostle to the nations lives like a vagrant.","words":[{"strongs":"G3983","hebrew":"πεινάω","translit":"peináō","english":"hunger"},{"strongs":"G1372","hebrew":"διψάω","translit":"dipsáō","english":"thirst"},{"strongs":"G1130","hebrew":"γυμνητεύω","translit":"gymnēteúō","english":"are naked"},{"strongs":"G2852","hebrew":"κολαφίζω","translit":"kolaphízō","english":"beaten"},{"strongs":"G790","hebrew":"ἀστατέω","translit":"astatéō","english":"have no certain dwelling place"}]},{"v":12,"text":"We toil, working with our own hands. When people curse us, we bless. Being persecuted, we endure.","understanding":"Κοπιάω — to work to exhaustion — “with our own hands.” Greek culture regarded manual labour as beneath a philosopher or teacher; a real teacher was supported by patrons and fees. Paul made tents. Then three responses that are pure Sermon on the Mount: reviled, we bless; persecuted, we endure; slandered (next verse), we appeal kindly. He is not reporting a technique. He is describing the cross reproduced in a man.","words":[{"strongs":"G2872","hebrew":"κοπιάω","translit":"kopiáō","english":"toil"},{"strongs":"G2038","hebrew":"ἐργάζομαι","translit":"ergázomai","english":"working"},{"strongs":"G3058","hebrew":"λοιδορέω","translit":"loidoréō","english":"curse"},{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"bless"},{"strongs":"G1377","hebrew":"διώκω","translit":"diṓkō","english":"persecuted"},{"strongs":"G430","hebrew":"ἀνέχομαι","translit":"anéchomai","english":"endure"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Being defamed, we entreat. We are made as the filth of the world, the dirt wiped off by all, even until now.","understanding":"Two of the most shocking words Paul ever applied to himself. Περικάθαρμα — what is cleaned off all around, the filth swept out of a house. Περίψημα — what is scraped off, the grime wiped from a dish. In some Greek cities the related word was also used of human scapegoats, outcasts expelled or sacrificed to carry away a city's pollution. Whether or not Paul intends that echo, the plain sense is enough: we are the scum of the world, the dirt everyone wipes off. “Even until now.”","words":[{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"defamed"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"entreat"},{"strongs":"G4027","hebrew":"περικάθαρμα","translit":"perikátharma","english":"filth"},{"strongs":"G4067","hebrew":"περίψωμα","translit":"perípsōma","english":"dirt wiped off"}]},{"v":14,"text":"I don’t write these things to shame you, but to admonish you as my beloved children.","understanding":"The tone changes in a moment from irony to tenderness. Paul is not writing to ἐντρέπω — to shame them, make them hang their heads. He is writing to νουθετέω — to admonish, warn, literally “put sense into the mind.” And they are his τέκνα ἀγαπητά, beloved children. Sharp words from a father are not the same as contempt. A man who does not care whether you destroy yourself does not bother to warn you.","words":[{"strongs":"G1788","hebrew":"ἐντρέπω","translit":"entrépō","english":"shame"},{"strongs":"G3560","hebrew":"νουθετέω","translit":"nouthetéō","english":"admonish"},{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"}]},{"v":15,"text":"For though you have ten thousand tutors in Christ, you don’t have many fathers. For in Christ Jesus, I became your father through the Good News.","understanding":"A παιδαγωγός was not a teacher but a household slave who took a boy to school, carried his books, kept an eye on his behaviour and sometimes disciplined him. You might have ten thousand of them — the number is deliberately exaggerated, the largest number in Greek. But you have only one father. And Paul says: in Christ Jesus, through the gospel, I ἐγέννησα you — I begot you. He was present at their spiritual birth. That gives him a claim no later guardian could have.","words":[{"strongs":"G3807","hebrew":"παιδαγωγός","translit":"paidagōgós","english":"tutors"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"fathers"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"became your father"},{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"}]},{"v":16,"text":"I beg you therefore, be imitators of me.","understanding":"Μιμέομαι — to imitate, from which comes our word mimic. In the ancient world, a son learned a trade by watching his father and copying him. Paul does not say “do what I say.” He says “be imitators of me” — copy my life. That is a terrifying thing to say, and he says it only because he is copying someone else (11:1, “Be imitators of me, even as I also am of Christ”).","words":[{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"beg"},{"strongs":"G3402","hebrew":"μιμητής","translit":"mimētḗs","english":"imitators"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Because of this I have sent Timothy to you, who is my beloved and faithful child in the Lord, who will remind you of my ways which are in Christ, even as I teach everywhere in every assembly.","understanding":"Timothy is sent as Paul's living reminder. Paul calls him “my beloved and faithful child in the Lord” — the same πιστός of verse 2: Timothy is a steward who has been found faithful. His job is to ἀναμιμνήσκω, remind them, of Paul's “ways” — ὁδοί, roads, a pattern of life — “which are in Christ.” And these ways are not special rules for Corinth; Paul teaches them “everywhere in every assembly.” Christianity is not reinvented by each church.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G363","hebrew":"ἀναμιμνήσκω","translit":"anamimnḗskō","english":"remind"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"ways"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Now some are puffed up, as though I were not coming to you.","understanding":"Some in Corinth had become puffed up — the same verb as verse 6 — on the assumption that Paul was never coming back. Distance had made them bold. It is easy to be arrogant toward an authority you think you will never have to face.","words":[{"strongs":"G5448","hebrew":"φυσιόω","translit":"physióō","english":"puffed up"}]},{"v":19,"text":"But I will come to you shortly, if the Lord is willing. And I will know, not the word of those who are puffed up, but the power.","understanding":"Paul's reply: I am coming, ταχέως, soon — “if the Lord is willing.” Even his threat is surrendered to God's will. And when he comes he will test not their λόγος, their talk, but their δύναμις, their power. Corinth had plenty of words. Paul wants to know whether anything real is behind them.","words":[{"strongs":"G5035","hebrew":"ταχύ","translit":"tachý","english":"shortly"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"willing"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"}]},{"v":20,"text":"For God’s Kingdom is not in word, but in power.","understanding":"The principle in nine words. The kingdom of God does not consist in λόγος, talk, but in δύναμις, power. A church can have eloquent sermons, sophisticated theology and impressive speech and still have no power — no changed lives, no holiness, no love, no presence of God. The kingdom is known by what it does, not by what it says about itself.","words":[{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"}]},{"v":21,"text":"What do you want? Shall I come to you with a rod, or in love and a spirit of gentleness?","understanding":"A father's choice, put to the children: “What do you want?” Shall I come with a ῥάβδος — a rod, the stick used by a father or a paidagōgos to discipline a child — or in love and a spirit of πραΰτης, gentleness, meekness? Notice that love is set against the rod only in its form, not its substance. The rod would also be love. But Paul longs to come gently, and the choice of which father arrives is theirs.","words":[{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"want"},{"strongs":"G4464","hebrew":"ῥάβδος","translit":"rhábdos","english":"rod"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4240","hebrew":"πραΰτης","translit":"praÿtēs","english":"gentleness"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Steward With the Keys","verseRef":"v. 1-2","image":"Picture a great house in Corinth — courtyards, storerooms, slaves, a family that owns ships at Lechaeum. The master travels for months at a time. Before he leaves, he hands a heavy ring of keys to one trusted slave: the οἰκονόμος, the steward. The steward unlocks the grain stores each morning and measures out food for the household. He pays the workers. He keeps the accounts. Everything passes through his hands, and nothing belongs to him. One day the master's ship docks, and the steward hears his footsteps in the courtyard. The master asks for the books.","meaning":"That is what Paul says every preacher, and indeed every believer, is: a steward of God's mysteries, distributing what belongs to Someone else. And the only qualification for a steward is that he be “found faithful.” Not impressive. Not popular. Not successful by the standards of the household gossip. Faithful. Jesus told the same story: “Who then is the faithful and wise steward, whom his lord will set over his household, to give them their portion of food at the right times? Blessed is that servant whom his lord will find doing so when he comes” (Luke 12:42–43). The Corinthians were ranking the stewards by style. Paul says: the Master is coming back, and He will only ask one question."},{"title":"The Prisoners at the End of the Triumph","verseRef":"v. 9-13","image":"A Roman triumph was the greatest spectacle the ancient world knew. The victorious general rode in a gilded chariot through streets packed with cheering crowds. In front of him marched his legions, then wagons piled with treasure, then captured kings in chains. And at the very end, last of all, came the condemned — the prisoners who would die in the arena when the parade was over. The crowd jeered them, threw things at them, and turned to watch the next spectacle. Paul says: that is where God has put the apostles. Not at the front, beside the chariot. At the back, with the men who are going to die.","meaning":"The Corinthians had taken the gospel and turned it into a status climb, and Paul holds up the men who brought them that gospel: hungry, thirsty, in rags, punched, homeless, working with their hands, cursed, persecuted, slandered, treated as the scum of the earth. “Even to this present hour.” This is not failure; it is the shape of the cross reproduced in the lives of those who carry it. And the whole universe is watching — “a spectacle to the world, both to angels and men.” When the world sees a believer bless those who curse him, endure persecution without bitterness and answer slander with kindness, it is watching the strangest show on earth. That is the theatre God has called you to."},{"title":"Ten Thousand Babysitters and One Father","verseRef":"v. 14-17","image":"Picture a wealthy Corinthian boy walking to school in the morning. Behind him walks the παιδαγωγός — an old slave carrying his writing tablets, making sure he does not wander off, cuffing him if he misbehaves. There might be a different one every few years. They come and go. But at home there is one man whose face the boy resembles, whose voice he hears in his own, whose trade he will one day carry on. Paul says: you have had many guardians since I left — Apollos, Cephas, other teachers. Count them in the thousands if you like. But you were born through me.","meaning":"This is why Paul's tone shifts so suddenly in verse 14, from searing irony to tenderness: “I don’t write these things to shame you, but to admonish you as my beloved children.” A father's correction comes from a different place than a critic's. And notice what a spiritual father asks for: “be imitators of me.” Not loyalty to a party, not admiration, not obedience to a list of rules, but a life worth copying. That is why he sends Timothy — not with a new set of instructions but to remind them of Paul's “ways which are in Christ.” Christianity spreads like a family trade, from one life to another. Who is copying you? And who are you copying?"}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Servants and Stewards (v. 1-5)","body":"Ministers are servants of Christ and stewards of God's mysteries, and stewards must be faithful; Paul does not live by human verdicts — not even his own — but waits for the Lord, who will reveal the heart and give each one his praise."},{"label":"What Do You Have That You Didn't Receive? (v. 6-7)","body":"Paul has used himself and Apollos as an example so that no one will be puffed up for one teacher against another; every distinction and every gift is received, so boasting is absurd."},{"label":"The Corinthian Kings and the Condemned Apostles (v. 8-13)","body":"With searing irony Paul contrasts the Corinthians' imagined reign with the apostles' reality: displayed last, like men condemned to death — hungry, beaten, homeless, labouring, blessing when cursed, the scum of the world."},{"label":"A Father's Appeal (v. 14-17)","body":"Not to shame but to warn: Paul is their only father in the gospel; they should imitate him, and he is sending Timothy to remind them of his ways in Christ."},{"label":"A Rod or Gentleness? (v. 18-21)","body":"Some are arrogant as though Paul will not come; he will come soon, if the Lord wills, and test their power rather than their talk, for the kingdom of God is not in word but in power. The manner of his coming is their choice."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Freed From the Courtroom of Opinion","paragraphs":["Verses 3 to 5 contain one of the most liberating statements about human judgment in the Bible. The Corinthians were evaluating their preachers like judges at a competition — comparing styles, ranking eloquence, choosing favourites. And Paul says, almost dismissively, “with me it is a very small thing that I should be judged by you, or by man’s judgment.” The word ἐλάχιστος is a superlative: the very least thing. He is not being arrogant. He is simply refusing to let the court of human opinion sit as the final judge of his life. And the phrase “man’s judgment” is literally “a human day” — a deliberate contrast with “the Day” of 3:13. Human courts hold human days. The verdict that matters will be given on another day.","Then he goes further, and this is where most of us have never been: “Yes, I don’t judge my own self. For I know nothing against myself. Yet I am not justified by this” (vv. 3–4). Paul refuses to be ruled by his own self-assessment as well. He has examined his conscience and found nothing — and he will not treat that as acquittal. A clear conscience can be an uninformed conscience. People have done terrible things with a clear conscience, Paul himself among them — he persecuted the church in good conscience (Acts 23:1; 26:9). So he hands the final verdict to the only Judge who has all the facts: “he who judges me is the Lord.” This is the healthy middle between two diseases. One is the person crushed by others' opinions. The other is the person who acquits himself because he feels fine. Paul is free from both.","And notice how verse 5 ends. The Lord will come, bring the hidden things to light and reveal the purposes of the heart — and “then each man will get his praise from God.” Paul does not end with exposure but with ἔπαινος, commendation. For the faithful steward, the Day of the Lord is not a day of dread; it is the day when hidden faithfulness is finally recognised. Every unseen act of obedience, every prayer no one heard, every service no one thanked you for will be brought into the light — and praised. That is why you do not need to win the court of opinion now. You are waiting for a better Judge, who sees what others miss and who intends to commend."]},{"heading":"Kings Without a Cross: The Danger of Thinking You Have Arrived","paragraphs":["Verse 8 is irony as sharp as anything in the New Testament: “You are already filled. You have already become rich. You have come to reign without us.” To understand it you need to see what had gone wrong in Corinth theologically. The Corinthians apparently believed that because they had received the Spirit and His gifts, they had already entered the fullness of the kingdom. They were spiritually rich, already reigning, already above suffering, weakness and the need to wait. Scholars sometimes call this an over-realised eschatology — the belief that the blessings of the age to come have fully arrived now. It explains much of the letter: their arrogance (they had arrived), their contempt for the body (the spiritual had replaced the physical), their confusion about the resurrection (some said it had already happened or was unnecessary, 15:12).","Paul answers with the reality of the apostles' lives. Verse 9 uses the image of a Roman triumph, where the condemned prisoners came “last of all,” and the word θέατρον — a public spectacle. Then verses 11 to 13 give a list that should be read slowly: hungry, thirsty, poorly clothed, beaten with fists, homeless, labouring with their own hands. In Greek culture, a teacher who worked with his hands was despised; real philosophers were supported by patrons. Paul made tents (Acts 18:3; 20:34). And when reviled, he blessed; when persecuted, he endured; when slandered, he answered kindly. He is describing Jesus' instructions — “bless those who curse you, and pray for those who mistreat you” (Luke 6:28) — reproduced in a human life. Peter describes Jesus the same way: “Who, when he was cursed, didn’t curse back” (1 Peter 2:23).","And verse 13 contains two words that shock every reader: περικάθαρμα and περίψημα, the filth swept out of a house and the grime scraped off a dish. In some Greek cities, words from this family were used for human scapegoats — outcasts driven out or killed in times of plague to carry away the city's pollution. Whether Paul intends that echo is debated, but the effect is overwhelming either way: the men who carried the gospel to Corinth were treated as human garbage. The lesson for every age is that the kingdom has come, but it has not yet fully come. Believers already have the Spirit, already belong to Christ, already possess all things (3:21) — and they still hunger, suffer, wait and die. Any version of Christianity that promises the crown without the cross is not the gospel Paul preached. It is Corinth's error."]},{"heading":"Not in Word but in Power: A Father's Warning","paragraphs":["From verse 14 the tone shifts, and the shift itself teaches something. Paul has just used the most cutting irony he could find, and now he says, “I don’t write these things to shame you, but to admonish you as my beloved children.” The word νουθετέω means to put sense into someone's mind — to warn, to confront, to correct. It is a family word. And Paul grounds his right to use it in his fatherhood: “though you have ten thousand tutors in Christ, you don’t have many fathers. For in Christ Jesus, I became your father through the Good News” (v. 15). A παιδαγωγός was a guardian, not a parent. He came and went. The father remained. Corinth had had many teachers since Paul left, but only one man had been present at their birth.","What does a spiritual father ask for? Not admiration, not loyalty to his party, not even primarily obedience to instructions. He asks to be copied: “be imitators of me” (v. 16). That is the ancient pattern of education in a trade — the son watches the father and does what he does. It is also a terrifying thing to say, and Paul only says it because he is himself copying Christ (11:1). He then sends Timothy, “my beloved and faithful child in the Lord,” to remind them of “my ways which are in Christ, even as I teach everywhere in every assembly” (v. 17). Two things are worth noticing. Timothy is called “faithful,” the very quality required of stewards in verse 2; he has been tested and found trustworthy. And the ways Paul teaches are the same everywhere. Corinth was not special. There was no Corinthian version of Christianity.","Then the warning. Some in Corinth have become arrogant on the assumption that Paul is not coming back (v. 18). He will come soon, “if the Lord is willing,” and he will test not their talk but their power, because “God’s Kingdom is not in word, but in power” (v. 20). This is one of the great diagnostic sentences of the New Testament. A church — or a believer — can be full of words: correct doctrine, eloquent preaching, spiritual vocabulary, confident claims. And it can be empty of power: no transformed lives, no holiness, no love, no real presence of God. Paul's final question gives the Corinthians the choice: “Shall I come to you with a rod, or in love and a spirit of gentleness?” The rod and gentleness are both forms of love; the difference is in what the children do before the father arrives."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["Paul's language of stewardship in verses 1 and 2 comes straight from the parables of Jesus. “Who then is the faithful and wise steward, whom his lord will set over his household, to give them their portion of food at the right times? Blessed is that servant whom his lord will find doing so when he comes” (Luke 12:42–43). The same word, οἰκονόμος. The same quality, faithful. The same moment of inspection, when the master comes. And the same reward: in the parable of the talents, the master says, “Well done, good and faithful servant. You have been faithful over a few things, I will set you over many things. Enter into the joy of your lord” (Matthew 25:21). That is the “praise from God” of verse 5. Peter later applies the steward image to every believer, not only to preachers: “As each has received a gift, employ it in serving one another, as good managers of the grace of God in its various forms” (1 Peter 4:10). Every gift you have is a key on the steward's ring.","Verse 5's promise — “then each man will get his praise from God” — becomes, at the end of Paul's life, his own confident hope. Writing his last letter from a Roman prison, he says: “From now on, there is stored up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will give to me on that day; and not to me only, but also to all those who have loved his appearing” (2 Timothy 4:8). Notice the phrases — “the righteous judge,” “on that day.” The man who refused to be judged by Corinth or even by himself spent his life waiting for one Judge, and died expecting His praise.","And the catalogue of suffering in verses 9 to 13 grows into a fuller list in Paul's next letter to this same church, when rivals had arrived claiming to be superior apostles: “in labors more abundantly, in prisons more abundantly, in stripes above measure, in deaths often. Five times from the Jews I received forty stripes minus one. Three times I was beaten with rods. Once I was stoned. Three times I suffered shipwreck. I have been a night and a day in the deep” (2 Corinthians 11:23–25). In both letters, the argument is the same and it is the opposite of the world's. The proof of an apostle is not his status, his eloquence or his comfort. It is his conformity to the crucified Christ. Jesus had said it first: “Blessed are you when people reproach you, persecute you, and say all kinds of evil against you falsely, for my sake. Rejoice, and be exceedingly glad, for great is your reward in heaven” (Matthew 5:11–12)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Steward / household manager","hebrew":"οἰκονόμος","translit":"oikonómos","strongs":"G3623","verses":"v. 1, 2","insight":"Strong's: “a house-distributor (i.e. manager), or overseer, i.e. an employee in that capacity; by extension, a fiscal agent (treasurer).” The word combines οἶκος, house, and a root meaning to distribute or manage — it is where English gets “economy.” In a Roman household the steward was often a slave, yet he held enormous responsibility: the keys, the accounts, the food supply, the authority over other servants. Erastus in Romans 16:23 is called the οἰκονόμος of the city, its treasurer. The genius of the image is the combination of authority and ownership: the steward has great authority over goods he does not own at all. That is exactly the position of every preacher and every believer before God."},{"english":"Faithful / trustworthy","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","strongs":"G4103","verses":"v. 2, 17","insight":"Strong's: “objectively, trustworthy; subjectively, trustful.” It is the adjective from the same root as πίστις, faith. A faithful steward is a full-of-faith steward, but in this context the stress is on reliability: someone who can be trusted with what is not his. Note that Paul uses it twice in this chapter: stewards must be found faithful (v. 2), and Timothy is “my beloved and faithful child” (v. 17). The test of verse 2 has a passing example in verse 17. And 1:9 has already told you where faithfulness comes from: “God is faithful.” The faithful steward reflects the faithful Master."},{"english":"Puffed up / inflated","hebrew":"φυσιόω","translit":"physióō","strongs":"G5448","verses":"v. 6, 18, 19 (and 5:2; 8:1; 13:4)","insight":"Strong's: “to inflate, i.e. (figuratively) make proud (haughty).” The word is built on φῦσα, a pair of bellows. Picture a blacksmith's bellows filled with air: big, swollen, and empty. Of its seven occurrences in the New Testament, six are in 1 Corinthians (the seventh is Colossians 2:18). That statistic is a diagnosis of the church. They were puffed up over teachers (4:6), puffed up against Paul (4:18), puffed up while tolerating sexual sin (5:2), puffed up with knowledge (8:1). And the cure appears in 13:4: love “is not proud” — the same verb. The opposite of being inflated is not being deflated. It is love."},{"english":"Dirt wiped off / scum","hebrew":"περίψωμα","translit":"perípsōma","strongs":"G4067 (paired with περικάθαρμα, perikátharma, G4027)","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's defines περίψημα as “something brushed all around, i.e. off-scrapings (figuratively, scum),” and its partner περικάθαρμα as “something cleaned off all around, i.e. refuse (figuratively).” Both appear only here in the New Testament. Both describe what is thrown away after cleaning. In wider Greek usage, words of this family could also describe human beings used as scapegoats — outcasts expelled from a city in times of disaster to carry away its guilt. Paul may or may not intend that echo. But consider what it would mean: the apostles treated as the world's refuse, driven out to carry its curse, reproduce in their lives the pattern of the One who became a curse for us. The scum of the earth was bringing the earth its salvation."},{"english":"Power","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","strongs":"G1411","verses":"v. 19, 20 (and 1:18, 24; 2:4, 5)","insight":"Strong's: “force (literally or figuratively); specially, miraculous power (usually by implication, a miracle itself).” Δύναμις is the root of our words dynamite and dynamic. It has been running through the whole letter: the word of the cross is the power of God (1:18); Christ is the power of God (1:24); Paul's preaching was in demonstration of the Spirit and of power (2:4); their faith should rest on the power of God (2:5). Now in 4:20 it becomes a test: the kingdom of God does not consist in talk but in power. Not necessarily spectacular miracles, though it includes them — but the real, transforming action of God that changes people. Words describe the kingdom. Power is the kingdom present."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the opinions you have allowed to sit as your judge, the ingratitude hidden inside your pride, and the gap between your spiritual words and your spiritual power.","items":[{"title":"Who Is Sitting on Your Judgment Seat?","body":"Someone's opinion of you has been running your life. A parent, a pastor, a spouse, a critic, a group of peers, the comments section, or the voice in your own head that is either too harsh or too lenient. You have been waiting for their verdict. Paul says: with me it is a very small thing to be judged by you, and I do not even judge myself. The Lord is my Judge. The Spirit wants to set you free from the courtroom of opinion — not by making you careless about what people think, but by moving the real verdict to the Day of the Lord. What would you do differently this week if you genuinely believed that only one Judge's opinion mattered, and that He intends to commend the faithful?"},{"title":"What Do You Have That You Didn’t Receive?","body":"Your intelligence, your health, your gifts, your opportunities, your upbringing, your spiritual experiences, your salvation itself — the Spirit wants you to see every one of them as received. Pride is not, at root, thinking too highly of yourself. It is forgetting where your things came from. Every time you compare yourself favourably with another believer, you are boasting as if you had not received. Ask yourself today: where have I been quietly proud? The talent I think others lack? The discipline I think others should have? The understanding I think makes me superior? Hand each one back to the Giver, and watch the pride drain out."},{"title":"Words or Power?","body":"You can talk about God fluently. You know the vocabulary, the songs, the theology, the phrases. But Paul's test is uncomfortable: the kingdom of God is not in word but in power. Where is the power in your life? Is anything happening that cannot be explained by your own effort — a sin that has actually been broken, a relationship that has actually been healed, a person who has actually come to Christ, a love you could not have produced by yourself? The Spirit is not condemning you for the words. He is inviting you past them. Ask Him for the power behind the words, and be ready for Him to start with the area where your words are loudest and your life is weakest."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Steward Check","body":"List the main things God has entrusted to you right now — money, time, gifts, people, a ministry, a job, a family. Beside each one, write: if the Master returned today and asked for the account, would He find me faithful with this? Do not ask whether you have been successful. Ask whether you have been faithful."},{"title":"The Received Check","body":"Write down the one thing about yourself you are proudest of. Then write, as specifically as you can, how you received it — who gave it, what opportunities made it possible, what grace was involved. If you cannot trace it back to a gift, look harder. Then ask whether you have been using it to set yourself above others (v. 7)."},{"title":"The Imitation Check","body":"Paul said, “be imitators of me.” Could you say that to a younger believer, honestly, about your life this week? In which areas would you be glad for them to copy you, and in which would you be ashamed? Write down one area of each."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Moving the Verdict","body":"Write on a card: “He who judges me is the Lord” (v. 4). Every time this week you notice yourself replaying someone's criticism, rehearsing a defence, or fishing for approval, take out the card, read it aloud, and say: “Lord, You are my Judge. I hand this verdict to You.” At the end of the week, write down what changed in how you felt and acted."},{"title":"Blessing When Cursed","body":"Verses 12 and 13 describe a response to mistreatment that is impossible without the Spirit: “When people curse us, we bless. Being persecuted, we endure. Being defamed, we entreat.” Think of one person who has spoken against you or treated you unfairly. This week, pray for them by name every day — not that God will correct them, but that He will bless them. Then, if it is wise and possible, do one kind thing for them or speak one gracious word to them. You will be part of the spectacle the angels are watching."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, I am Your servant and a steward of what belongs to You. Nothing I hold is mine. Make me faithful with it — not impressive, not admired, but faithful. Free me from the courtroom of human opinion. I have let other people's verdicts rule my heart, and I have let my own self-assessment rule it too, sometimes condemning myself and sometimes acquitting myself too easily. Today I hand the verdict to You. You are my Judge. Bring to light what is hidden in me, reveal the purposes of my heart, and let me be found faithful on that Day. Father, I confess my pride. I have boasted as if I had not received. Everything I have came from You — my life, my gifts, my salvation. Forgive me for comparing myself with others and setting myself above them. And forgive me for wanting the crown without the cross, for living as if I had already arrived. Thank You for the apostles who walked at the back of the parade, hungry and despised, blessing those who cursed them. Make me willing to be counted with them. When I am reviled, let me bless; when I am persecuted, let me endure; when I am slandered, let me answer kindly. Holy Spirit, I do not want a kingdom of words. I want a kingdom of power. Do in me and through me what I cannot do myself. And thank You that whatever correction You bring, You bring as a Father. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Joseph, the Steward Found Faithful","ref":"Genesis 39:1-23","relatedVerses":"v. 1-5","summary":"Sold into slavery by his brothers, Joseph ends up in the house of Potiphar, an Egyptian officer. The LORD is with him, and Potiphar puts him in charge of everything he owns — the whole household passes through Joseph's hands, and Potiphar does not concern himself with anything except the food he eats. Joseph is the model οἰκονόμος. Then Potiphar's wife tries day after day to seduce him, and Joseph refuses, saying he will not betray his master's trust or sin against God. Falsely accused, he is thrown into prison, and even there he is put in charge. For years no one sees his faithfulness except God. But the hidden things are eventually brought to light, and Joseph is lifted to rule Egypt. Joseph's story is 1 Corinthians 4:2 and 4:5 together: stewards must be found faithful, and the Lord brings the hidden things to light in His own time."},{"title":"David Cuts Saul's Robe and Waits for God","ref":"1 Samuel 24:1-22","relatedVerses":"v. 3-5","summary":"King Saul is hunting David in the wilderness of En Gedi and goes alone into a cave to relieve himself — the very cave where David and his men are hiding. David's men tell him that God has delivered his enemy into his hand. David creeps forward and cuts off a corner of Saul's robe, and then even that troubles his conscience. He refuses to kill the LORD's anointed and lets Saul walk away. Then he calls out after him and says that the LORD will judge between them. David had every human reason to act as judge. He refused, because the judgment belonged to God and its time had not come. That is the spirit of verse 5: “judge nothing before the time, until the Lord comes.” David's restraint did not make him weak. It made him the kind of man God could trust with a throne."},{"title":"My Father, My Father","ref":"2 Kings 2:1-15","relatedVerses":"v. 14-17","summary":"Elijah knows his time is ending, and three times he tells Elisha to stay behind, and three times Elisha refuses to leave him. At the Jordan, Elijah asks what he can do for him, and Elisha asks for a double portion of his spirit — the inheritance of a firstborn son. As the chariot of fire takes Elijah away, Elisha cries out, “My father, my father!” Then he picks up Elijah's fallen cloak, strikes the water, and it parts, and the prophets watching say that the spirit of Elijah rests on Elisha. This is spiritual fatherhood at its best: not a guardian who instructs for a while and then leaves, but a father whose life is copied and whose mantle is carried on. Paul wanted the same for Corinth — not admirers, but sons who would imitate him as he imitated Christ, and carry the same ways into every place."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"A Little Yeast — Holiness, Discipline and Christ Our Passover","themeTitle":"“The Church That Is Proud of Its Tolerance Has Stopped Mourning Over Sin”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"It is actually reported that there is sexual immorality among you, and such sexual immorality as is not even named among the Gentiles, that one has his father’s wife.","understanding":"Ὅλως — actually, as a matter of common knowledge. This is not rumour; it is being talked about openly. Πορνεία is the broad New Testament word for sexual immorality of every kind, and this case was of a sort that “is not even named among the Gentiles”: a man “has” — the present tense suggests an ongoing relationship, probably cohabitation or marriage — “his father’s wife.” Paul does not say “his mother,” so she was most likely his stepmother. Leviticus 18:8 forbade it, and Roman law forbade it too. Pagan Corinth would have been scandalised. The church was not.","words":[{"strongs":"G3654","hebrew":"ὅλως","translit":"hólōs","english":"actually"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"Gentiles"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wife"}]},{"v":2,"text":"You are puffed up, and didn’t rather mourn, that he who had done this deed might be removed from among you.","understanding":"The shocking thing is not only the sin but the church's attitude to it. “You are φυσιόω — puffed up.” The verb is perfect: they have become inflated and remain so. Perhaps they were proud of their tolerance, of their liberty, of their freedom from old moral rules. What they should have done is πενθέω — mourn, grieve as at a funeral. The proper response to sin in the church is not pride, and it is not merely disapproval. It is grief.","words":[{"strongs":"G5448","hebrew":"φυσιόω","translit":"physióō","english":"puffed up"},{"strongs":"G3996","hebrew":"πενθέω","translit":"penthéō","english":"mourn"},{"strongs":"G142","hebrew":"αἴρω","translit":"aírō","english":"removed"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For I most certainly, as being absent in body but present in spirit, have already, as though I were present, judged him who has done this thing.","understanding":"Paul is ἄπειμι, absent, in body, but πάρειμι, present, in spirit. He has already reached a verdict — κρίνω in the perfect tense: I have judged and the judgment stands. This is apostolic authority exercised from a distance. Notice he says he has judged “him who has done this thing.” His verdict is on the man. Interestingly, the woman is not addressed; she was probably not a member of the church.","words":[{"strongs":"G548","hebrew":"ἄπειμι","translit":"ápeimi","english":"absent"},{"strongs":"G3918","hebrew":"πάρειμι","translit":"páreimi","english":"present"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"}]},{"v":4,"text":"In the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, you being gathered together, and my spirit, with the power of our Lord Jesus Christ,","understanding":"The action must be taken by the gathered church — συνάγω, assembled together — “in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ” and “with the power of our Lord Jesus Christ.” Church discipline is not a private decision by a leader. It is a solemn act of the whole congregation, carried out in Christ's name and authority, with Paul's spirit joined to theirs.","words":[{"strongs":"G4863","hebrew":"συνάγω","translit":"synágō","english":"gathered together"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"}]},{"v":5,"text":"are to deliver such a one to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus.","understanding":"One of the most difficult verses in Paul. Παραδίδωμι — to hand over — this man “to Satan.” Most interpreters understand it as removal from the fellowship of the church, putting him back into the realm outside where Satan rules (compare 1 Timothy 1:20). “For the ὄλεθρος of the flesh” is debated: it may mean physical suffering or even death, or the destruction of his sinful nature, the σάρξ in its moral sense. But the purpose is unmistakable and it is redemptive: “that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus.” The goal of discipline is not punishment for its own sake. It is rescue.","words":[{"strongs":"G3860","hebrew":"παραδίδωμι","translit":"paradídōmi","english":"deliver"},{"strongs":"G4567","hebrew":"Σατανᾶς","translit":"Satanâs","english":"Satan"},{"strongs":"G3639","hebrew":"ὄλεθρος","translit":"ólethros","english":"destruction"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saved"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Your boasting is not good. Don’t you know that a little yeast leavens the whole lump?","understanding":"Καύχημα — your boast, the thing you are glorying in. It is “not good.” Then a proverb every household knew: “a little yeast leavens the whole lump.” Ζύμη in the ancient world was not packaged yeast but a piece of old fermented dough kept back from the previous batch and worked into the new. A tiny piece spreads through all of it. Tolerated sin does not stay contained. It works its way into everything.","words":[{"strongs":"G2745","hebrew":"καύχημα","translit":"kaúchēma","english":"boasting"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2219","hebrew":"ζύμη","translit":"zýmē","english":"yeast"},{"strongs":"G3398","hebrew":"μικρός","translit":"mikrós","english":"little"},{"strongs":"G2220","hebrew":"ζυμόω","translit":"zymóō","english":"leavens"},{"strongs":"G5445","hebrew":"φύραμα","translit":"phýrama","english":"lump"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Purge out the old yeast, that you may be a new lump, even as you are unleavened. For indeed Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed in our place.","understanding":"Ἐκκαθαίρω — clean out thoroughly. The picture is the Jewish household before Passover, searching every corner for leaven and removing it (Exodus 12:15). “That you may be a νέος lump” — new, fresh — “even as you are ἄζυμοι,” unleavened. Notice the logic: become what you are. In Christ they are already unleavened; now they must live like it. Why? “For indeed Christ, our πάσχα, has been θύω — sacrificed.” The Lamb has already been slain. You cannot keep the feast with leaven in the house.","words":[{"strongs":"G1571","hebrew":"ἐκκαθαίρω","translit":"ekkathaírō","english":"purge out"},{"strongs":"G3820","hebrew":"παλαιός","translit":"palaiós","english":"old"},{"strongs":"G3501","hebrew":"νέος","translit":"néos","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G106","hebrew":"ἄζυμος","translit":"ázymos","english":"unleavened"},{"strongs":"G3957","hebrew":"πάσχα","translit":"páscha","english":"Passover"},{"strongs":"G2380","hebrew":"θύω","translit":"thýō","english":"sacrificed"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Therefore let us keep the feast, not with old yeast, neither with the yeast of malice and wickedness, but with the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth.","understanding":"Ἑορτάζω — let us keep the festival. The whole Christian life is a continual Passover celebration, because the Lamb has been slain once for all. And the feast is kept with the right bread. Not with the yeast of κακία, malice, and πονηρία, wickedness. With the unleavened bread of εἰλικρίνεια — purity, sincerity; the word may come from testing something in sunlight to see if it is clean — and ἀλήθεια, truth.","words":[{"strongs":"G1858","hebrew":"ἑορτάζω","translit":"heortázō","english":"keep the feast"},{"strongs":"G2549","hebrew":"κακία","translit":"kakía","english":"malice"},{"strongs":"G4189","hebrew":"πονηρία","translit":"ponēría","english":"wickedness"},{"strongs":"G1505","hebrew":"εἰλικρίνεια","translit":"eilikríneia","english":"sincerity"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":9,"text":"I wrote to you in my letter to have no company with sexual sinners;","understanding":"A precious historical detail: Paul had written to them before. “I wrote to you in my letter.” That earlier letter has not survived. In it he had told them not to συναναμίγνυμι — mix together with, keep company with — πόρνοι, sexually immoral people. They had apparently misunderstood it, or chose to.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"wrote"},{"strongs":"G4874","hebrew":"συναναμίγνυμι","translit":"synanamígnymi","english":"have company"},{"strongs":"G4205","hebrew":"πόρνος","translit":"pórnos","english":"sexual sinners"}]},{"v":10,"text":"yet not at all meaning with the sexual sinners of this world, or with the covetous and extortionists, or with idolaters; for then you would have to leave the world.","understanding":"Paul clarifies. He did not mean the immoral people of this world. Nor the πλεονέκται, the greedy, those who always want more; nor the ἅρπαγες, the swindlers, the grabbers; nor the idolaters. If he had meant that, “you would have to leave the world.” The church is not called to withdraw from sinners. Jesus ate with them. Paul worked alongside them making tents.","words":[{"strongs":"G4205","hebrew":"πόρνος","translit":"pórnos","english":"sexual sinners"},{"strongs":"G4123","hebrew":"πλεονέκτης","translit":"pleonéktēs","english":"covetous"},{"strongs":"G727","hebrew":"ἅρπαξ","translit":"hárpax","english":"extortionists"},{"strongs":"G1496","hebrew":"εἰδωλολάτρης","translit":"eidōlolátrēs","english":"idolaters"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":11,"text":"But as it is, I wrote to you not to associate with anyone who is called a brother who is a sexual sinner, or covetous, or an idolater, or a slanderer, or a drunkard, or an extortionist. Don’t even eat with such a person.","understanding":"The real instruction: do not associate with “anyone who is called a brother” — someone who claims the name of Christ and belongs to the church — who lives in these sins without repentance. The list grows: sexually immoral, greedy, idolater, λοίδορος (a verbal abuser, a slanderer), μέθυσος (a drunkard), swindler. Note that greed and slander stand right beside sexual sin. “Don’t even eat with such a person” — probably including the Lord's Supper and the common meals of the church. The withdrawal of table fellowship was meant to say: you cannot have both Christ and this.","words":[{"strongs":"G4874","hebrew":"συναναμίγνυμι","translit":"synanamígnymi","english":"associate"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G3060","hebrew":"λοίδορος","translit":"loídoros","english":"slanderer"},{"strongs":"G3183","hebrew":"μέθυσος","translit":"méthysos","english":"drunkard"},{"strongs":"G4906","hebrew":"συνεσθίω","translit":"synesthíō","english":"eat with"}]},{"v":12,"text":"For what do I have to do with also judging those who are outside? Don’t you judge those who are within?","understanding":"Two spheres. Those ἔξω — outside — are not Paul's concern to judge. Those ἔσω — inside — are the church's concern. The church has no mandate to police the morality of unbelievers. It has every mandate to care about the holiness of its own members. Most churches get this exactly backwards: loud about the sins of the world, silent about the sins of the pew.","words":[{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judging"},{"strongs":"G1854","hebrew":"ἔξω","translit":"éxō","english":"outside"},{"strongs":"G2080","hebrew":"ἔσω","translit":"ésō","english":"within"}]},{"v":13,"text":"But those who are outside, God judges. “Put away the wicked man from among yourselves.”","understanding":"Those outside, God judges — and He will. The church does not need to take His place. Then Paul quotes a refrain that appears repeatedly in Deuteronomy (for example 17:7; 22:24): “Put away the wicked man from among yourselves.” Ἐξαίρω — remove. In Israel those texts often called for the death penalty. In the church, the sentence is removal from fellowship — with the hope of restoration.","words":[{"strongs":"G1854","hebrew":"ἔξω","translit":"éxō","english":"outside"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judges"},{"strongs":"G1808","hebrew":"ἐξαίρω","translit":"exaírō","english":"put away"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"wicked"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Funeral That Never Happened","verseRef":"v. 1-2","image":"Picture a Corinthian house church gathered on a Sunday evening. The singing is enthusiastic. Someone speaks in tongues; someone else prophesies. People are discussing their freedom in Christ with great sophistication. And in the corner sits a man with his arm around his father's wife — everyone knows, everyone has heard, and no one says anything. Some even seem quietly proud of how broad-minded the congregation has become. Now picture what Paul says should have happened: the room going silent, the singing stopping, people weeping as they do at a funeral, because a member of the body is dying.","meaning":"Paul's main charge is not against the man but against the church: “You are puffed up, and didn’t rather mourn.” Πενθέω is the word for grieving the dead. That is the proper response to serious, unrepentant sin among God's people. Not pride in our tolerance. Not a shrug. Not gossip. And not a cold, self-righteous condemnation either. Grief. A church that has lost the ability to mourn over sin has lost something essential, and the result is always the same: it becomes proud of the very things that should break its heart. Ask yourself: what sin in your own life or in your church have you stopped grieving over because it has become normal?"},{"title":"The Search by Lamplight","verseRef":"v. 6-8","image":"It is the night before Passover in a Jewish home. The father takes a lamp and goes through the whole house — every room, every shelf, every corner of the kitchen floor — looking for leaven. Not a whole loaf; a crumb, a scrap of old dough. The children follow him, watching. When the house has been searched the leaven is gathered and burned. The next day the lamb is slain, and that evening the family eats the Passover with unleavened bread, the way their fathers did on the night God brought them out of Egypt.","meaning":"Paul sees the whole Christian life in that scene. “Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed in our place.” The Lamb has already been slain. The feast has already begun. So the leaven must go — the old dough of malice and wickedness. Notice the order: Paul does not say “clean out the leaven so that Christ will be sacrificed for you.” He says, “Christ has been sacrificed; therefore clean it out.” Holiness is not the price of redemption. It is the response to it. And notice the image of leaven itself: a small piece spreads through the whole batch. Tolerated sin never stays where you put it. Search the house, by the light of the Spirit, before it spreads."},{"title":"Two Doors and One Table","verseRef":"v. 9-13","image":"Picture two doors. The first opens onto the street — the docks, the marketplace, the workshops of Corinth. Paul walks through it every day. He sits beside idol-worshippers at the tent bench. He buys bread from greedy merchants. He shares meals with people whose lives are a moral wreck. The second door opens into the house church. Behind it is a table where believers eat the Lord's Supper together. And at that table sits a man who calls himself a brother while living in open, unrepentant sin. Paul says: at the first door, keep going in. At the second door, that man cannot sit at the table.","meaning":"This is one of the most misunderstood principles in the Christian life, and churches get it wrong in both directions. Some withdraw from the world, refusing to associate with unbelievers, and Paul says that is absurd — “you would have to leave the world.” Others welcome unrepentant sin inside the church while condemning the sins of outsiders, and Paul says that is backwards. “What do I have to do with also judging those who are outside? Don’t you judge those who are within?” The church's mission to the world is to go out into it with the gospel, eating with sinners as Jesus did. The church's responsibility to itself is to guard the table. Notice also that greed, slander and drunkenness are on the list beside sexual sin. Holiness is not selective."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Sin and the Church's Pride (v. 1-2)","body":"A man is living with his father's wife — a sin even pagans reject — and the church is puffed up instead of mourning and removing him."},{"label":"Paul's Verdict and the Church's Action (v. 3-5)","body":"Though absent, Paul has already judged; the church, gathered in Jesus' name and power, must deliver the man to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, so that his spirit may be saved."},{"label":"The Leaven and the Passover (v. 6-8)","body":"Their boasting is not good, because a little leaven spreads through the whole lump; Christ our Passover has been sacrificed, so they must clean out the old leaven and keep the feast with sincerity and truth."},{"label":"Correcting a Misunderstanding (v. 9-13)","body":"Paul's earlier letter did not mean to avoid immoral people in the world, but to refuse fellowship with a professing brother who persists in serious sin; God judges outsiders, and the church must judge those within."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Real Scandal Was Not the Sin but the Pride","paragraphs":["The sin of verse 1 was shocking, and Paul says so: it was a kind of πορνεία “not even named among the Gentiles.” A man had his father's wife — almost certainly his stepmother, since Paul would have said “mother” otherwise — and the present tense of “has” indicates an ongoing relationship, not a single act. Leviticus 18:8 prohibited it explicitly. Roman law prohibited it too; the orator Cicero, in a speech defending a client, describes a woman who married her own son-in-law as an almost unbelievable crime. Corinth, for all its reputation, would have regarded this as disgraceful. Notice too that Paul addresses only the man. The woman was probably not a believer, and Paul consistently refuses to judge those outside the church (v. 12).","But look at where Paul aims his heaviest fire. Not at the man; at the church. “You are puffed up, and didn’t rather mourn” (v. 2). The perfect tense of the verb suggests a settled state of arrogance. What were they proud of? Paul does not say directly, but the rest of the letter offers a strong clue. Corinth had a slogan, “All things are lawful for me” (6:12; 10:23), probably a distortion of Paul's own teaching about freedom from the law. It is very possible that some in the church regarded their tolerance of this relationship as evidence of how far they had moved beyond old moral categories. They were not ashamed of the sin. They were proud of their acceptance of it.","That is a pattern every generation of the church needs to recognise. There is a kind of spiritual pride that boasts of its strictness, and Jesus condemned it in the Pharisees. And there is another kind of spiritual pride that boasts of its tolerance, of how liberated and open-minded it has become, and Paul condemns it here. Both are puffed up. The right response to serious sin in the church is neither harshness nor indifference. It is grief. Πενθέω — mourning, the word used for grieving the dead. The church should have wept over this man before it ever removed him. Discipline that does not begin with grief will always become cruel. And tolerance that has lost the capacity to grieve has stopped loving the person it claims to accept."]},{"heading":"Delivered to Satan: What Church Discipline Is and Is Not","paragraphs":["Verses 3 to 5 contain some of the most solemn language in the New Testament. Paul has already judged the man, and the church is to assemble, “in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ” and “with the power of our Lord Jesus Christ,” and “deliver such a one to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus.” What does it mean? The clearest explanation is in verses 2 and 13: “that he who had done this deed might be removed from among you” and “Put away the wicked man from among yourselves.” To be delivered to Satan is to be put out of the fellowship of the church — out of the sphere where Christ's people gather under His protection and back into the world, where Satan operates as “the prince of this world” (John 12:31). Paul uses the same expression in 1 Timothy 1:20 of two men he had delivered to Satan “that they might be taught not to blaspheme.” The aim there, too, is correction.","“For the destruction of the flesh” is honestly debated. Some understand σάρξ physically: the man would experience bodily suffering, sickness or even death as a consequence (compare 11:30, where Paul says some believers had become weak, sick, or had died for abusing the Lord's Supper). Others understand σάρξ morally, as Paul often uses it: the sinful nature, the fleshly self that was driving this sin, would be destroyed by the painful experience of exclusion. The grammar allows both. What is not debated is the purpose clause: “that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus.” The goal of the whole action is the man's ultimate salvation. Church discipline in the New Testament is never merely punitive. It is a severe mercy — an emergency procedure to shock someone back to life before it is too late.","Did it work? It is possible that it did. In 2 Corinthians 2:5–8 Paul writes about a man who had been punished by the majority of the church, and urges them to “forgive him and comfort him, lest by any means such a one should be swallowed up with his excessive sorrow. Therefore I beg you to confirm your love toward him.” Whether that is the same man is genuinely debated, and many scholars think it refers to someone who personally opposed Paul during a later visit. But the pattern is the same either way. Discipline aims at repentance, and when repentance comes, restoration must follow quickly and warmly. Jesus gave the same pattern in Matthew 18:15–17: private confrontation first, then a few witnesses, then the church, and only then “let him be to you as a Gentile or a tax collector.” Each step is an opportunity to come back. The door out is always also the door back in."]},{"heading":"Christ Our Passover: Why Holiness Is the Shape of the Feast","paragraphs":["In verses 6 to 8 Paul turns from the specific case to the principle beneath it, and he uses a picture every Jewish believer in Corinth would have recognised instantly. Before Passover, every household had to remove all leaven (Exodus 12:15). In the ancient world, leaven was not dried yeast from a packet; it was a piece of old dough, kept back from the last batch, sour and fermenting, and mixed into the new. A small piece would work its way through the entire lump. That made it a natural image for corruption that spreads. Jesus used it: “Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy” (Luke 12:1). Paul uses the same proverb in Galatians: “A little yeast grows through the whole lump” (Galatians 5:9). The point is simple and serious. Tolerated sin does not stay contained. It spreads.","Then Paul says something astonishing: “For indeed Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed in our place” (v. 7). He takes the whole Passover story and lays it over the Christian life. The lamb has been slain. Its blood has been applied. The angel of death has passed over. The people have been brought out of slavery. And so the feast has begun — the whole Christian life is a continual Passover celebration — and it must be kept with the right bread. It is possible, though not certain, that Paul was writing near the actual Passover season; in 16:8 he says he will stay in Ephesus until Pentecost, which follows Passover by fifty days. Either way, the imagery would have been vivid.","Notice the logic of verse 7 carefully, because it is the logic of the whole New Testament ethic: “Purge out the old yeast, that you may be a new lump, even as you are unleavened.” Become what you are. In Christ the Corinthians are already unleavened — cleansed, forgiven, a new batch. Their task is not to earn that status but to live consistently with it. Holiness is never the price of redemption; it is the shape of a redeemed life. Then verses 9 to 13 apply it carefully. Paul's previous letter (now lost) had told them not to associate with immoral people, and they had apparently either misunderstood or twisted it. He clarifies: he did not mean the people of this world, “for then you would have to leave the world.” He meant professing believers who persist in serious, unrepentant sin. The church does not judge the world; God does. The church guards its own table. That is not hypocrisy. It is the difference between a mission and a family."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["“Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed in our place” (v. 7) is the clearest statement in Paul's letters that Jesus fulfils the Passover. John the Baptist had pointed to Him at the very beginning of His ministry: “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!” (John 1:29). John's Gospel times the crucifixion to the day of Preparation for the Passover, when the lambs were being slaughtered in the temple, and notes that none of Jesus' bones were broken, as with the Passover lamb (John 19:31–36; Exodus 12:46). Peter draws the same line: “you were redeemed, not with corruptible things, with silver or gold, from the useless way of life handed down from your fathers, but with precious blood, as of a lamb without blemish or spot, the blood of Christ” (1 Peter 1:18–19). And at the Last Supper, which took place in a Passover setting, Jesus Himself took the bread and the cup and reinterpreted them around His own body and blood — as Paul will recount to this same church in 11:23–26.","Paul's instruction for church discipline follows the pattern Jesus gave His disciples. “If your brother sins against you, go, show him his fault between you and him alone. If he listens to you, you have gained back your brother. But if he doesn’t listen, take one or two more with you... If he refuses to listen to them, tell it to the assembly. If he refuses to hear the assembly also, let him be to you as a Gentile or a tax collector” (Matthew 18:15–17). Notice Jesus' stated goal: “you have gained back your brother.” And notice how Jesus Himself treated Gentiles and tax collectors — He ate with them and called them to repentance (Matthew 9:10–13). To be put out of the church is to be treated as someone who needs to hear the gospel again, not as someone beyond hope.","The danger of tolerating sin in the church is spelled out again in the letters of the risen Christ to the seven churches. To Thyatira He says: “But I have this against you, that you tolerate your woman, Jezebel, who calls herself a prophetess. She teaches and seduces my servants to commit sexual immorality, and to eat things sacrificed to idols” (Revelation 2:20). Tolerance of persistent, unrepentant sin is not a virtue in the eyes of Christ. It is a charge He brings against His own church. At the same time, the gospel always holds the door open. Paul's second letter to Corinth includes the plea to forgive and comfort a man the church had disciplined, “lest by any means such a one should be swallowed up with his excessive sorrow” (2 Corinthians 2:7). Holiness and mercy are not opposites. They are the two hands of the same love."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Sexual immorality","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","strongs":"G4202","verses":"v. 1 (×2; and 6:13, 18; 7:2)","insight":"Strong's: “harlotry (including adultery and incest); figuratively, idolatry.” Πορνεία is the broad New Testament word for sexual activity outside of marriage between a man and a woman, and it is the root of our word pornography. It is related to πόρνη, a prostitute, and to πόρνος, the word in verses 9 to 11 for a sexually immoral person. The word covers far more than prostitution, as this chapter shows: here it describes incest. In 1 Corinthians it appears five times, more than in any other of Paul’s letters, because Corinth's culture treated sexual freedom as normal and some in the church had imported that view into their Christianity. Paul's answer in the next chapter will be that the body belongs to the Lord."},{"english":"Mourn / grieve","hebrew":"πενθέω","translit":"penthéō","strongs":"G3996","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “to grieve (the feeling or the act).” This is the word for mourning the dead, used by Jesus in the Beatitudes: “Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted” (Matthew 5:4). James uses it of repentance: “Lament, mourn, and weep” (James 4:9). Paul's charge against Corinth is that they did not mourn — they boasted. The word matters, because it defines the emotional centre of church discipline. Not anger, not superiority, not satisfaction at seeing someone exposed, but grief, as at a death. Every act of correction in the church should begin here. If you cannot mourn over a brother's sin, you are not ready to confront it."},{"english":"Yeast / leaven","hebrew":"ζύμη","translit":"zýmē","strongs":"G2219 (verb ζυμόω, zymóō, G2220; opposite ἄζυμος, ázymos, G106)","verses":"v. 6, 7, 8 (×2); “unleavened” v. 7, 8","insight":"Strong's: ζύμη is “ferment (as if boiling up),” and ἄζυμος, its opposite, “unleavened, i.e. (figuratively) uncorrupted.” In Scripture leaven is almost always a picture of corruption that spreads silently: the yeast of the Pharisees (hypocrisy), the yeast of Herod, the yeast of malice and wickedness. The key property is that a small amount affects the whole. You cannot isolate leaven once it is mixed in. That is why Paul's command is not “manage the leaven” but “purge it out.” And notice that he says the Corinthians already are ἄζυμοι, unleavened (v. 7). The command to clean out the leaven is a command to live consistently with what Christ has already made them."},{"english":"Passover","hebrew":"πάσχα","translit":"páscha","strongs":"G3957","verses":"v. 7","insight":"Strong's: “the Passover (the meal, the day, the festival or the special sacrifices connected with it).” The word comes from the Aramaic form of the Hebrew pesach, from the verb meaning to pass over — as the LORD passed over the houses marked with the blood of the lamb (Exodus 12:13). In verse 7 Paul uses it of the sacrificial lamb itself: “Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed.” In one word he connects the cross to the defining act of redemption in Israel's history. Just as the first Passover brought Israel out of slavery in Egypt, the death of Christ brings His people out of slavery to sin. And just as Israel ate the Passover with unleavened bread, the redeemed must keep their feast without the old leaven."},{"english":"Sincerity / purity","hebrew":"εἰλικρίνεια","translit":"eilikríneia","strongs":"G1505","verses":"v. 8","insight":"Strong's: “clearness, i.e. (by implication) purity (figuratively).” The adjective form, εἰλικρινής, is often explained as “judged by the sunlight” — something held up to the light to see whether it has any flaw or impurity. The etymology is debated, but the meaning is clear: transparency, nothing hidden, purity of motive. Paul uses it in 2 Corinthians 1:12 to describe how he conducted himself, and in 2 Corinthians 2:17 to contrast himself with those who peddle God's word for profit. The bread of the Christian feast is “sincerity and truth” — a life that can be held up to the light and examined without anything hidden. Leaven grows in the dark. Sincerity lives in the light."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the sin you have stopped grieving over, the leaven you have allowed to spread quietly through your life, and the way you have reversed God's order of judgment.","items":[{"title":"What Have You Stopped Mourning?","body":"There is something in your life or in your circle that once troubled you deeply and no longer does. A habit, a relationship, an attitude, a compromise. It has become normal. You may even have started to feel quietly proud of how unbothered you are by things that used to bother you, and to call it maturity or freedom. The Spirit wants to restore your capacity to grieve. Not with condemnation — with clarity. Ask Him today: “Lord, what have I become comfortable with that breaks Your heart?” And when He shows you, do not explain it away. Mourn. Grief over sin is not the end of joy. It is the beginning of a cleaner one."},{"title":"The Crumb in the Corner","body":"Leaven is never large when it begins. It is a crumb. A little bitterness, a small lie, a private fantasy, a minor dishonesty, a quiet resentment toward someone in the church. And it spreads — through your thoughts, your relationships, your prayers, your witness. The Spirit is holding the lamp like a father on the night before Passover, and He wants to search your house. Will you let Him into every room? The ones you show visitors and the ones you keep closed? Christ your Passover has been sacrificed. The feast has begun. You were never meant to keep it with old leaven in the cupboard."},{"title":"Loud Outside, Silent Inside","body":"It is easy to be outraged at the sins of the world — the culture, the government, the media, the people who do not know Christ. Paul says: what do I have to do with judging those outside? God judges them. Your concern is the people inside — and above all, yourself. The Spirit is asking you to reverse your instinct. Spend less energy condemning the world, which needs the gospel, and more energy dealing honestly with the sin among God's people, starting with your own. And when you see a brother or sister trapped in serious sin, do not gossip and do not ignore it. Go to them — privately, gently, in grief — as Jesus taught in Matthew 18. That is love."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Mourning Check","body":"Write down one sin in your own life that you have become comfortable with. Not the one you are already fighting — the one you have quietly made peace with. Ask: when did I stop grieving over this? What did I tell myself to make it acceptable? Then bring it to God with honest sorrow."},{"title":"The Leaven Check","body":"Is there a small thing in your heart toward another person — resentment, envy, suspicion, contempt — that is beginning to affect how you see them, talk about them or pray for them? Write their name and the attitude. Ask whether it has spread to anyone else through your words."},{"title":"The Two-Doors Check","body":"Count the unbelievers you have shared a meal or a real conversation with in the last month. Then ask whether you have been more critical of outsiders than of yourself and your church. Paul's pattern was open doors to the world and a guarded table in the church. Which direction is your life closer to?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Searching the House","body":"Tonight, do your own search by lamplight. Sit quietly with a sheet of paper and pray Psalm 139:23–24: “Search me, God, and know my heart.” Go room by room through your life: your thoughts, your relationships, your money, your speech, your private habits, your entertainment, your attitudes toward people in your church. Write down any leaven the Spirit shows you. Then, one by one, confess each item specifically, thank Christ your Passover for His sacrifice, and decide on one concrete step to remove it. Throw the paper away — burn it if you can — as a sign that the leaven is gone."},{"title":"Going Through the First Door","body":"This week, intentionally spend time with someone outside the church — a neighbour, colleague or acquaintance who does not know Christ. Share a meal or a coffee. Do not lecture them about their morality; Paul says that is not your job. Simply be present, listen, show the love of Christ, and if the opportunity comes, tell them what Christ your Passover has done for you. Follow the example of Jesus, who was called a friend of tax collectors and sinners and was not ashamed of it."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I come before You with a heart that has grown too comfortable. Forgive me for being puffed up when I should have mourned. Forgive me for the sins I have stopped grieving, for the compromises I have called freedom and the tolerance I have called love. Give me back a tender heart that weeps over what breaks Yours. Lord Jesus, You are my Passover. You were sacrificed in my place. Your blood is on the doorposts of my life, and death has passed over me. Thank You. Now, Holy Spirit, take the lamp and search my house. Go into every room — the ones I show people and the ones I keep closed. Find every crumb of old leaven — the malice, the bitterness, the hidden wickedness — and help me purge it out. I do not want to keep Your feast with old yeast in the cupboard. Let me be what You have already made me: unleavened, new, clean. Let my life be the bread of sincerity and truth, able to be held up to the light with nothing hidden. And Lord, teach me Your order. Make me slow to judge those outside and quick to deal honestly with myself and with Your church. Give me courage to go gently to a brother or sister trapped in sin, not with pride but with grief, so that they may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus. In His name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Achan and the Hidden Treasure","ref":"Joshua 7:1-26","relatedVerses":"v. 6-7","summary":"After the stunning victory at Jericho, Israel goes up against the small town of Ai and is routed. Joshua falls on his face before the ark, and God tells him the reason: Israel has sinned; someone has taken what was devoted to destruction and hidden it among his own things. One man, Achan, had taken a fine robe, silver and gold and buried them under his tent. One man's hidden sin had brought defeat on the whole nation. Only when it was exposed and dealt with could Israel go forward. This is the Old Testament picture of a little leaven leavening the whole lump. Sin is never merely private when you belong to God's people. What is hidden under one tent affects the whole camp."},{"title":"The Night the Lamb Was Slain","ref":"Exodus 12:1-28","relatedVerses":"v. 7-8","summary":"On the night God brought Israel out of Egypt, every household took a lamb without blemish, killed it at twilight, and painted its blood on the doorposts and lintel of the house. They ate the meat roasted with unleavened bread and bitter herbs, dressed for travel, staff in hand. And God commanded that for seven days no leaven be found in their houses. That night the LORD passed through Egypt, and wherever He saw the blood, He passed over. Paul says, “Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed in our place.” The church lives in that night forever. The blood has been applied, death has passed over, the chains are broken. And the redeemed people eat the feast with no leaven in the house, because they have been set free to belong to the God who saved them."},{"title":"Eli's Sons and the Father Who Only Scolded","ref":"1 Samuel 2:12-36; 3:11-14; 4:10-18","relatedVerses":"v. 1-2","summary":"Eli was the high priest at Shiloh, and his sons Hophni and Phinehas were priests too — and they were corrupt. They took the best portions of the sacrifices for themselves and slept with the women who served at the entrance of the tent of meeting. Eli heard about it and rebuked them with words, but he did nothing more, and they did not listen. God sent a prophet, and then the boy Samuel, to tell Eli that judgment was coming on his house because his sons made themselves vile and he did not restrain them. In the end both sons died in battle, the ark was captured, and Eli fell and died when he heard the news. Corinth had the same disease Eli had: it knew about the sin, it may even have disapproved, and it did nothing. Tolerating sin among God's people is not neutrality. It is a failure of love that ends in disaster."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":6,"chapterTitle":"Washed, Sanctified, Justified — Lawsuits, Liberty and the Body Bought With Blood","themeTitle":"“You Are Not Your Own”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Dare any of you, having a matter against his neighbor, go to law before the unrighteous, and not before the saints?","understanding":"Τολμάω — dare you? The verb carries outrage: how can you bring yourselves to do this? A believer with a πρᾶγμα, a dispute, a business matter, against another believer takes it to court “before the ἄδικοι” — the unrighteous, here meaning the pagan judges of Corinth — instead of before the saints. Civil courts in Roman Corinth were notoriously tilted toward the wealthy and well-connected, and lawsuits were a weapon the powerful used against those below them. Christians were using that weapon on each other.","words":[{"strongs":"G5111","hebrew":"τολμάω","translit":"tolmáō","english":"dare"},{"strongs":"G4229","hebrew":"πρᾶγμα","translit":"prâgma","english":"matter"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"go to law"},{"strongs":"G94","hebrew":"ἄδικος","translit":"ádikos","english":"unrighteous"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Don’t you know that the saints will judge the world? And if the world is judged by you, are you unworthy to judge the smallest matters?","understanding":"“Don’t you know?” — again. The saints will judge the world. Paul is drawing on a thread that runs from Daniel 7:22 (judgment given to the saints of the Most High) through Jesus' promise that the apostles would sit on thrones (Matthew 19:28). If you are destined to share in judging the world, how can you be unfit to settle κριτήρια ἐλάχιστα — the smallest cases?","words":[{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2922","hebrew":"κριτήριον","translit":"kritḗrion","english":"judge (matters)"},{"strongs":"G1646","hebrew":"ἐλάχιστος","translit":"eláchistos","english":"smallest"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Don’t you know that we will judge angels? How much more, things that pertain to this life?","understanding":"Even angels. Paul does not explain this, and it is wise not to over-explain it: presumably the fallen angels, whose judgment is certain (2 Peter 2:4; Jude 6). His point is an argument from the greater to the lesser. If you will share in judging angels, how much more can you handle βιωτικά — the ordinary affairs of daily life, property, contracts, money.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G982","hebrew":"βιωτικός","translit":"biōtikós","english":"things that pertain to this life"}]},{"v":4,"text":"If then, you have to judge things pertaining to this life, do you set them to judge who are of no account in the assembly?","understanding":"The Greek can be read as a statement or a question, and either way it stings. Either a question — do you appoint as judges people who have no standing in the church, that is, pagan magistrates? — or an ironic command: appoint the least respected members of the church as judges, since even they could do better than the courts. Ἐξουθενέω is the word of 1:28 — the despised things God chose. Paul's sarcasm turns their own status system upside down.","words":[{"strongs":"G982","hebrew":"βιωτικός","translit":"biōtikós","english":"things pertaining to this life"},{"strongs":"G1848","hebrew":"ἐξουθενέω","translit":"exouthenéō","english":"of no account"}]},{"v":5,"text":"I say this to move you to shame. Isn’t there even one wise man among you who would be able to decide between his brothers?","understanding":"Ἐντροπή — shame, confusion. Paul does intend to shame them now (contrast 4:14). This church, so proud of its σοφία, cannot produce one σοφός, one wise person, able to διακρίνω — to judge between, to arbitrate — a dispute between brothers. All their wisdom, and none of it usable for the simplest act of peacemaking.","words":[{"strongs":"G1791","hebrew":"ἐντροπή","translit":"entropḗ","english":"shame"},{"strongs":"G4680","hebrew":"σοφός","translit":"sophós","english":"wise"},{"strongs":"G1252","hebrew":"διακρίνω","translit":"diakrínō","english":"decide"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But brother goes to law with brother, and that before unbelievers!","understanding":"The tragedy in one line: brother against brother, “and that before ἄπιστοι” — unbelievers. The family airs its quarrels in the public square, before the very people it is supposed to be reaching with the gospel. What does a pagan judge conclude about Jesus when he watches two of His followers try to ruin each other?","words":[{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G571","hebrew":"ἄπιστος","translit":"ápistos","english":"unbelievers"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Therefore it is already altogether a defect in you, that you have lawsuits one with another. Why not rather be wronged? Why not rather be defrauded?","understanding":"Ἥττημα — a defeat, a loss. The very fact that you have κρίματα, lawsuits, against each other means you have already lost, whoever wins the case. Then two questions that go to the heart of the Sermon on the Mount: why not rather be ἀδικέω — wronged? Why not rather be ἀποστερέω — defrauded, robbed? Paul is asking whether the gospel has reached their wallets.","words":[{"strongs":"G2275","hebrew":"ἥττημα","translit":"hḗttēma","english":"defect (defeat)"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"lawsuits"},{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"be wronged"},{"strongs":"G650","hebrew":"ἀποστερέω","translit":"aposteréō","english":"defrauded"}]},{"v":8,"text":"No, but you yourselves do wrong, and defraud, and that against your brothers.","understanding":"Worse still: they are not merely refusing to suffer wrong; they are committing it. “You yourselves do wrong, and defraud” — the same two verbs, turned around — “and that against your brothers.” The plaintiffs and defendants in Corinth's courts were not innocent victims and wicked villains. They were believers cheating believers.","words":[{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"do wrong"},{"strongs":"G650","hebrew":"ἀποστερέω","translit":"aposteréō","english":"defraud"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Or don’t you know that the unrighteous will not inherit God’s Kingdom? Don’t be deceived. Neither the sexually immoral, nor idolaters, nor adulterers, nor male prostitutes, nor homosexuals,","understanding":"The link with verse 8 is the word ἄδικος: you do wrong (ἀδικέω), and the ἄδικοι — the wrongdoers — will not κληρονομέω, inherit, the kingdom of God. “Don’t be πλανάω” — deceived, led astray; some in Corinth evidently believed that grace made behaviour irrelevant. Then the list. Πόρνοι, the sexually immoral. Idolaters. Μοιχοί, adulterers. Then two words the WEB renders “male prostitutes” and “homosexuals.” Μαλακοί literally means soft, and in sexual contexts was used of the passive partner in male same-sex acts. Ἀρσενοκοῖται is a compound of “male” and “bed” that echoes the Greek wording of Leviticus 18:22 and 20:13. The strongest reading, given that echo, is that Paul has male same-sex intercourse in view generally, not only its exploitative forms. He places it on the same list as greed and slander, and verse 11 is written to people who used to be in every category.","words":[{"strongs":"G94","hebrew":"ἄδικος","translit":"ádikos","english":"unrighteous"},{"strongs":"G2816","hebrew":"κληρονομέω","translit":"klēronoméō","english":"inherit"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceived"},{"strongs":"G4205","hebrew":"πόρνος","translit":"pórnos","english":"sexually immoral"},{"strongs":"G1496","hebrew":"εἰδωλολάτρης","translit":"eidōlolátrēs","english":"idolaters"},{"strongs":"G3432","hebrew":"μοιχός","translit":"moichós","english":"adulterers"},{"strongs":"G3120","hebrew":"μαλακός","translit":"malakós","english":"male prostitutes"},{"strongs":"G733","hebrew":"ἀρσενοκοίτης","translit":"arsenokoítēs","english":"homosexuals"}]},{"v":10,"text":"nor thieves, nor covetous, nor drunkards, nor slanderers, nor extortionists, will inherit God’s Kingdom.","understanding":"The list continues, and it is deliberately broad. Κλέπται, thieves. Πλεονέκται, the greedy, those always wanting more. Μέθυσοι, drunkards. Λοίδοροι, verbal abusers, slanderers. Ἅρπαγες, swindlers. Notice which sins sit beside sexual ones: greed, a sharp tongue, cheating in business. A church that is fierce about sexual sin and relaxed about greed and slander has not read this verse. Paul's point is not that these sins disqualify a person from forgiveness, but that a life given over to them, without repentance, is not the life of someone who belongs to the kingdom.","words":[{"strongs":"G2812","hebrew":"κλέπτης","translit":"kléptēs","english":"thieves"},{"strongs":"G4123","hebrew":"πλεονέκτης","translit":"pleonéktēs","english":"covetous"},{"strongs":"G3183","hebrew":"μέθυσος","translit":"méthysos","english":"drunkards"},{"strongs":"G3060","hebrew":"λοίδορος","translit":"loídoros","english":"slanderers"},{"strongs":"G727","hebrew":"ἅρπαξ","translit":"hárpax","english":"extortionists"},{"strongs":"G2816","hebrew":"κληρονομέω","translit":"klēronoméō","english":"inherit"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Such were some of you, but you were washed. But you were sanctified. But you were justified in the name of the Lord Jesus, and in the Spirit of our God.","understanding":"One of the most hope-filled verses in the Bible. “Such were some of you.” Past tense. The Corinthian church was made of former adulterers, former idolaters, former thieves, former drunks, former swindlers, and people who had lived in same-sex relationships. And then three verbs, each preceded by a strong “but”: ἀπολούω — you were washed clean; ἁγιάζω — you were set apart as holy; δικαιόω — you were declared righteous. All three are completed actions, done “in the name of the Lord Jesus, and in the Spirit of our God.” Your past is not your identity. Your washing is.","words":[{"strongs":"G628","hebrew":"ἀπολούω","translit":"apoloúō","english":"washed"},{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctified"},{"strongs":"G1344","hebrew":"δικαιόω","translit":"dikaióō","english":"justified"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"}]},{"v":12,"text":"“All things are lawful for me,” but not all things are expedient. “All things are lawful for me,” but I will not be brought under the power of anything.","understanding":"Paul quotes a Corinthian slogan — the WEB rightly puts it in quotation marks — “All things are lawful for me,” ἔξεστιν. It probably began as a distortion of Paul's own teaching about freedom in Christ. He does not deny it outright; he qualifies it twice. Not all things συμφέρω — are beneficial, bring advantage. And “I will not be brought under the power of anything” — ἐξουσιάζω, a play on ἔξεστιν: “all things are in my power, but I will not be put in the power of any of them.” Freedom that ends in slavery is not freedom.","words":[{"strongs":"G1832","hebrew":"ἔξεστι","translit":"éxesti","english":"lawful"},{"strongs":"G4851","hebrew":"συμφέρω","translit":"symphérō","english":"expedient"},{"strongs":"G1850","hebrew":"ἐξουσιάζω","translit":"exousiázō","english":"be brought under the power"}]},{"v":13,"text":"“Foods for the belly, and the belly for foods,” but God will bring to nothing both it and them. But the body is not for sexual immorality, but for the Lord; and the Lord for the body.","understanding":"A second slogan: “Foods for the belly, and the belly for foods.” The logic of some Corinthians ran: the body is a temporary machine; food satisfies one appetite; sex satisfies another; both are natural and spiritually irrelevant. Paul answers: God will καταργέω — bring to nothing — both stomach and food. But the σῶμα, the body, is different: it is “not for sexual immorality, but for the Lord; and the Lord for the body.” Astonishing phrase: the Lord is for your body. He cares about it, and He has plans for it.","words":[{"strongs":"G1033","hebrew":"βρῶμα","translit":"brōma","english":"foods"},{"strongs":"G2836","hebrew":"κοιλία","translit":"koilía","english":"belly"},{"strongs":"G2673","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","english":"bring to nothing"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Now God raised up the Lord, and will also raise us up by his power.","understanding":"Here is why the body matters: God ἐγείρω — raised — the Lord, and will also raise us up by His power. The Greek world mostly regarded the body as a prison or a shell to be discarded. Christianity says the body has a future. What you do with it now is not spiritually neutral, because the same body will be raised. Chapter 15 will unpack this at length.","words":[{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Don’t you know that your bodies are members of Christ? Shall I then take the members of Christ, and make them members of a prostitute? May it never be!","understanding":"Your bodies are μέλη, members, limbs, of Christ. Think about what that means literally. Then Paul asks the unthinkable: shall I take a limb of Christ and make it a limb of a πόρνη — a prostitute? “May it never be!” — μὴ γένοιτο, Paul's strongest expression of horror. Sexual sin is not merely a private act. It drags Christ's own body into it.","words":[{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"bodies"},{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"members"},{"strongs":"G4204","hebrew":"πόρνη","translit":"pórnē","english":"prostitute"},{"strongs":"G1096","hebrew":"γίνομαι","translit":"gínomai","english":"may it never be"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Or don’t you know that he who is joined to a prostitute is one body? For, “The two”, he says, “will become one flesh.”","understanding":"Κολλάω — to glue, to stick fast. The one who is joined to a prostitute becomes one body with her. Paul proves it from Genesis 2:24, the foundational text on marriage: “The two will become one flesh.” Sex is never merely physical, even in a brothel. It creates a union that the Creator designed for marriage. That is why sexual sin is so destructive: it uses the deepest bonding act God made for something it was never meant for.","words":[{"strongs":"G2853","hebrew":"κολλάω","translit":"kolláō","english":"joined"},{"strongs":"G4204","hebrew":"πόρνη","translit":"pórnē","english":"prostitute"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"}]},{"v":17,"text":"But he who is joined to the Lord is one spirit.","understanding":"The same verb, κολλάω, now for something infinitely higher: “he who is joined to the Lord is one spirit” with Him. The believer is united to Christ in a bond deeper than any physical union. This is why sexual immorality is so incongruous for a Christian: you cannot be glued to the Lord and glued to a prostitute at the same time.","words":[{"strongs":"G2853","hebrew":"κολλάω","translit":"kolláō","english":"joined"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Flee sexual immorality! “Every sin that a man does is outside the body,” but he who commits sexual immorality sins against his own body.","understanding":"The command is one word: φεύγετε — flee! Present tense: keep fleeing, make it your habit. Not “negotiate with it,” not “manage it,” not “see how close you can get.” Run. Then a third slogan, probably Corinthian: “Every sin that a man does is outside the body.” Paul answers that sexual immorality is different: the immoral person sins “against his own body.” It is uniquely self-destructive because it involves the whole self in a union God designed to be holy.","words":[{"strongs":"G5343","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","english":"flee"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G265","hebrew":"ἁμάρτημα","translit":"hamártēma","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G1854","hebrew":"ἔξω","translit":"éxō","english":"outside"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":19,"text":"Or don’t you know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit who is in you, whom you have from God? You are not your own,","understanding":"“Or don’t you know” — the sixth time in this chapter. In 3:16 the church collectively was God's temple. Now the focus narrows: “your body” — singular — is the ναός, the inner sanctuary, of the Holy Spirit who is in you. The Holy of Holies has moved into your body. And then the sentence that overturns the whole philosophy of “my body, my business”: “You are not your own.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"}]},{"v":20,"text":"for you were bought with a price. Therefore glorify God in your body and in your spirit, which are God’s.","understanding":"Ἀγοράζω — to buy in the ἀγορά, the marketplace. Corinth had a slave market. A slave who was bought now belonged to a new master. “You were bought with a price” — τιμή, a price, a value paid. Peter tells you what the price was: the precious blood of Christ (1 Peter 1:19). Therefore δοξάζω — glorify, make visible the glory of — God “in your body.” Some later manuscripts add “and in your spirit, which are God’s,” as the WEB prints; the earliest manuscripts end with “in your body,” which is where Paul's whole argument has been heading.","words":[{"strongs":"G59","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","english":"bought"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"price"},{"strongs":"G1392","hebrew":"δοξάζω","translit":"doxázō","english":"glorify"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Two Brothers at the Bema","verseRef":"v. 1-8","image":"In the centre of Corinth's forum stood the βῆμα — a raised stone platform where Roman magistrates sat to hear cases. Paul himself had stood before it when Gallio threw out the accusation against him (Acts 18:12–17). Now picture two men from the same house church standing before that platform on a weekday morning. Last Sunday they shared the Lord's Supper. Today they are hurling accusations at each other over a debt or a property line, each with a hired advocate, while a crowd of pagans watches and a Roman official who couldn't care less about Jesus decides which Christian is the liar.","meaning":"Paul's outrage is not mainly about legal procedure. It is about witness and love. The church will one day share in judging the world and even angels; surely it can settle a dispute between two brothers. But the deeper question is verse 7: “Why not rather be wronged? Why not rather be defrauded?” The moment two believers drag each other into court, both have lost — ἥττημα, a defeat — whatever the verdict. Jesus said, “If anyone sues you to take away your coat, let him have your cloak also” (Matthew 5:40). This is not a blanket prohibition on every legal process; Paul himself appealed to Caesar to protect his rights (Acts 25:11). It is a challenge to the heart: is your money or your rights more important to you than your brother and the name of Christ?"},{"title":"Such Were Some of You","verseRef":"v. 9-11","image":"Imagine the Corinthian congregation gathered as this letter is read aloud for the first time. The reader reaches verse 9 and begins the list: the sexually immoral, idolaters, adulterers, men who slept with men, thieves, the greedy, drunkards, slanderers, swindlers. Around the room, people lower their eyes. A former temple prostitute. A man who used to drink himself unconscious every night at the docks. A merchant who had cheated his partners for years. A man who had lived with another man. A woman known in the market for her vicious tongue. Then the reader says: “Such were some of you.” And then: “But you were washed.”","meaning":"This verse does two things at once, and you need both. First, it is uncompromising: these behaviours, practised without repentance, are incompatible with the kingdom of God. Paul says “Don’t be deceived” because the Corinthians had convinced themselves otherwise. Do not soften what he says. But second, it is overwhelmingly hopeful: “such were some of you.” The church in Corinth was full of people from every category on that list, and every one of them had been washed, sanctified and justified. No sin on the list is beyond the reach of the blood of Christ. Your past is not your identity. Whatever you used to be — or whatever you are still fighting — the verbs that define you are washed, sanctified, justified. Live from them."},{"title":"Sold in the Agora","verseRef":"v. 19-20","image":"Picture the slave market in Corinth's agora on a busy morning. Men, women and children stand on a platform with placards around their necks describing their skills and origin. Buyers inspect them like livestock. Then a stranger steps forward and pays an enormous price for one ragged, worthless-looking slave — far more than he could ever be worth on the market. The slave is taken down from the platform. He no longer belongs to his old master. Everything he is — his time, his hands, his body — now belongs to the man who bought him. And the man who bought him intends to make him a son.","meaning":"Ἀγοράζω is literally to buy in the agora. “You were bought with a price.” The price was the blood of Christ (1 Peter 1:19; Revelation 5:9). That changes everything about how you see your body. The world says: my body, my choice, my business. The gospel says: “You are not your own.” Your body is not a possession you manage as you like; it is a temple where God the Holy Spirit lives, and it was purchased at infinite cost. That is not a threat; it is the most dignifying truth about your body ever spoken. You are worth the blood of the Son of God. Therefore glorify God in your body — in what you eat and drink, what you watch, where you go, who you touch, how you rest, what you do with your sexuality."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Lawsuits Between Believers (v. 1-8)","body":"Paul is outraged that believers take each other to pagan courts; the saints will judge the world and angels, so they can settle small disputes — and in any case it is better to be wronged than to defraud a brother."},{"label":"The Unrighteous Will Not Inherit the Kingdom (v. 9-11)","body":"Those who persist in a list of sins will not inherit God's kingdom — but some of the Corinthians had been exactly such people and had been washed, sanctified and justified in Jesus' name and by the Spirit."},{"label":"Corinthian Slogans Answered (v. 12-14)","body":"“All things are lawful” — but not all are beneficial, and I will not be mastered. “Food for the stomach” — but the body is for the Lord, and God will raise it."},{"label":"Members of Christ and the Prostitute (v. 15-18)","body":"Believers' bodies are members of Christ; uniting with a prostitute makes one body with her, while the one joined to the Lord is one spirit with Him — so flee sexual immorality."},{"label":"Temple of the Spirit, Bought With a Price (v. 19-20)","body":"The body is the temple of the Holy Spirit; you are not your own; you were bought with a price; therefore glorify God in your body."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Why Not Rather Be Wronged? The Gospel and Your Rights","paragraphs":["The first eight verses can seem like a detour from the sexual issues on either side of them, but they are not. The link is the word ἄδικος — unrighteous, wrongdoer — and the underlying issue is the same throughout the chapter: Corinthian believers were living as though belonging to Christ made no difference to their ordinary behaviour, whether in the bedroom or the courtroom. In Roman Corinth, civil litigation was largely a tool of the powerful. Judges and juries tended to favour people of higher social status, and lawsuits were often used to gain advantage, settle personal scores or shame rivals. Paul is not describing poor Christians seeking justice against oppressors. He is describing believers using the system to beat each other.","His argument moves in three steps. First, from the greater to the lesser: the saints will judge the world and even angels, so they are surely competent to settle βιωτικά — the ordinary disputes of daily life (vv. 2–3). Second, from shame: a church proud of its wisdom cannot find one wise man to arbitrate between brothers, and instead parades its quarrels before unbelievers (vv. 5–6). Third, and most piercing, from the cross: “Therefore it is already altogether a defect in you, that you have lawsuits one with another. Why not rather be wronged? Why not rather be defrauded?” (v. 7). The word ἥττημα means defeat. Whoever wins, both have lost — lost the testimony of the church, lost the love that should bind brothers, and lost the spirit of the One who did not defend His rights but laid them down.","It is important to read this carefully and honestly. Paul is not saying that the legal system is evil or that believers can never use it; Paul himself appealed to Caesar to protect his rights as a Roman citizen when his life was threatened (Acts 25:11), and Romans 13 calls civil authorities God's servants for justice. Nor is he telling victims of abuse or serious crime to stay silent; that would be to misuse this text grotesquely. He is addressing disputes between believers over βιωτικά — property, money, contracts, personal grievances — and saying that such disputes should be settled within the church, and that the willingness to suffer loss rather than to hurt a brother is the mark of a heart shaped by the cross. Jesus put it bluntly: “If anyone sues you to take away your coat, let him have your cloak also” (Matthew 5:40). The question is not whether you have rights. It is whether you love your rights more than your brother."]},{"heading":"Washed, Sanctified, Justified: The List and the Hope","paragraphs":["Verses 9 and 10 contain one of Paul's lists of behaviours that are incompatible with the kingdom of God, and verse 11 contains one of his most hopeful sentences. They must be read together, and neither must be softened. First, the warning. “Don’t be deceived” (v. 9) — the verb πλανάω suggests being led astray, and it tells us that some in Corinth had been persuaded that grace made behaviour irrelevant. Paul says flatly that the ἄδικοι — those whose lives are characterised by these behaviours without repentance — will not inherit the kingdom. The list is broad: sexual immorality, idolatry, adultery, two terms for male same-sex sexual activity, theft, greed, drunkenness, verbal abuse and swindling. Similar lists appear in Galatians 5:19–21 and Ephesians 5:5. Notice that greed, slander and drunkenness stand beside sexual sins. Paul does not rank them in a way that lets any reader feel safe.","The two terms in verse 9 that the WEB translates “male prostitutes” and “homosexuals” deserve honest attention, because they are debated in our time. Μαλακός literally means soft; in sexual contexts in Greek literature it described men who took the passive role in sex with other men. Ἀρσενοκοίτης is a compound of ἄρσην (male) and κοίτη (bed), and it appears to have been formed from the Greek translation of Leviticus 18:22 and 20:13, where those two words stand side by side in the prohibition of a man lying with a male. Some modern interpreters argue Paul meant only exploitative practices such as pederasty or prostitution. But given the direct echo of Leviticus, which prohibits the act itself without reference to age or exploitation, the strongest textual reading is that Paul is referring to male same-sex intercourse as such. Saying that plainly is not hatred. Paul's purpose in naming these behaviours is not to single out one group but to describe what the gospel rescues people from — and he immediately says that some of the Corinthians had been rescued from exactly this.","Because then comes verse 11, and you must not hurry past it. “Such were some of you, but you were washed. But you were sanctified. But you were justified in the name of the Lord Jesus, and in the Spirit of our God.” The tense is past: such were some of you. The church in Corinth contained former idolaters, former adulterers, former thieves, former drunkards, former swindlers and men who had lived in same-sex relationships. And each of them had been washed — ἀπολούω, cleansed thoroughly, perhaps alluding to baptism (compare Acts 22:16, “be baptized, and wash away your sins”); sanctified — set apart as God's own; and justified — declared righteous before God. Notice that justification comes last in the list. The Corinthians knew the doctrine; what they had forgotten was that it had changed who they were. The answer to sin in the Christian life is never “you are still what you were.” It is “you are no longer what you were — now live like it.”"]},{"heading":"The Lord Is for the Body: Why Sexual Holiness Matters","paragraphs":["From verse 12 Paul engages directly with the Corinthian slogans, and the WEB helpfully puts them in quotation marks. “All things are lawful for me” (v. 12) — probably a twisted version of Paul's own teaching that believers are not under the law. Paul answers twice: not all things are beneficial, and “I will not be brought under the power of anything.” There is a play on words in Greek: πάντα μοι ἔξεστιν, ἀλλ’ οὐκ ἐγὼ ἐξουσιασθήσομαι — “all things are in my power, but I will not be brought under the power of any.” The freedom the Corinthians were claiming was actually becoming slavery. Then a second slogan: “Foods for the belly, and the belly for foods” (v. 13). The underlying philosophy, common in the Greek world, was that the body is temporary and spiritually irrelevant. If the stomach wants food, feed it; if the body wants sex, satisfy it. Paul's reply turns on the resurrection: God will indeed do away with stomach and food, but the body is “for the Lord; and the Lord for the body,” because “God raised up the Lord, and will also raise us up by his power” (v. 14). Your body has a future. It is not a disposable shell.","Verses 15 to 17 then make an argument that no reader should skim. Your body is a member — a limb — of Christ. If you unite it to a prostitute, you are taking a limb of Christ and making it a limb of a prostitute. That is not hyperbole; it is Paul's theology of union with Christ applied literally. He supports it from Genesis 2:24: “The two will become one flesh.” Sex creates a one-flesh union, which God designed for the covenant of marriage between a man and a woman. To create that union outside marriage — even in a transaction with a prostitute — is to misuse the deepest bonding act God made. And it is to contradict a deeper union still: “he who is joined to the Lord is one spirit” (v. 17). The same verb, κολλάω, to glue, is used for both. You cannot be glued to the Lord and glued to sin at the same time.","So the command is short: “Flee sexual immorality!” (v. 18). Not resist it, not manage it, not debate it — run. Then a third saying, again probably a Corinthian slogan: “Every sin that a man does is outside the body.” Paul answers that sexual immorality is unique because it is a sin “against his own body.” It involves the whole person in a union God designed to be holy. And then the climax: “Or don’t you know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit who is in you, whom you have from God? You are not your own, for you were bought with a price. Therefore glorify God in your body” (vv. 19–20). In chapter 3 the church collectively was God's temple; here each believer's body is. The Holy of Holies has moved into you. And you were bought — ἀγοράζω, purchased in the marketplace like a slave — with the price of Christ's blood. The answer to “my body, my choice” is not a rule. It is a price tag written in blood."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["Verse 16 quotes Genesis 2:24, and so did Jesus, when the Pharisees tested Him about divorce: “Haven’t you read that he who made them from the beginning made them male and female, and said, ‘For this cause a man shall leave his father and mother, and shall be joined to his wife; and the two shall become one flesh?’ So that they are no more two, but one flesh. What therefore God has joined together, don’t let man tear apart” (Matthew 19:4–6). Jesus grounds the meaning of sex and marriage in creation itself, not in culture. Paul does the same. The one-flesh union is God's design, and it is holy because He designed it. Hebrews says the same in one sentence: “Let marriage be held in honor among all, and let the bed be undefiled: but God will judge the sexually immoral and adulterers” (Hebrews 13:4).","The language of “bought with a price” rings through the rest of the New Testament. In Revelation, the elders before the throne sing to the Lamb: “You are worthy to take the book, and to open its seals: for you were killed, and bought us for God with your blood, out of every tribe, language, people, and nation” (Revelation 5:9). The same verb, ἀγοράζω. And Peter specifies the price: “you were redeemed, not with corruptible things, with silver or gold... but with precious blood, as of a lamb without blemish or spot, the blood of Christ” (1 Peter 1:18–19). And Paul tells the Romans what a bought body does: “present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable to God, which is your spiritual service” (Romans 12:1). The body you were told was yours was bought, and the only fitting response is to give it back as a sacrifice.","And the command to flee echoes across Paul's later letters. He tells Timothy, “Flee from youthful lusts; but pursue righteousness, faith, love, and peace with those who call on the Lord out of a pure heart” (2 Timothy 2:22) — flee from, but also run toward, and do it in company. He tells the Thessalonians: “For this is the will of God: your sanctification, that you abstain from sexual immorality, that each one of you know how to control his own body in sanctification and honor, not in the passion of lust, even as the Gentiles who don’t know God” (1 Thessalonians 4:3–5). And Peter says it to scattered believers: “I beg you as foreigners and pilgrims, to abstain from fleshly lusts, which war against the soul” (1 Peter 2:11). The apostles agree completely: sexual holiness is not a minor matter of personal preference. It is the will of God for a body that belongs to Him."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Defraud / rob","hebrew":"ἀποστερέω","translit":"aposteréō","strongs":"G650","verses":"v. 7, 8 (and 7:5)","insight":"Strong's: “to despoil.” The word means to deprive someone of what is rightfully theirs — to cheat, rob, withhold. Jesus listed it among the commandments in Mark 10:19: “Do not defraud.” James uses it of employers who withhold workers' wages (James 5:4). Paul uses it twice here: why not rather be defrauded (v. 7)? Instead you defraud (v. 8). The question exposes the heart. Some Corinthians were not merely refusing to suffer loss; they were inflicting it on their brothers. Notice too that Paul uses the same verb in 7:5 of married couples depriving one another of sexual intimacy. Whether in business or marriage, to withhold from someone what love owes them is a form of fraud."},{"english":"Washed (fully)","hebrew":"ἀπολούω","translit":"apoloúō","strongs":"G628","verses":"v. 11","insight":"Strong's: “to wash fully, i.e. (figuratively) have remitted (reflexively).” The prefix ἀπο- intensifies it: washed off, washed away, washed clean. It occurs only twice in the New Testament — here and in Acts 22:16, where Ananias tells Saul, “Arise, be baptized, and wash away your sins, calling on the name of the Lord.” That connection suggests Paul may be alluding to baptism as the outward sign of an inward cleansing. But the power is not in the water; it is “in the name of the Lord Jesus, and in the Spirit of our God.” Whatever is on the list in verses 9 and 10 can be washed off. The stains of the past are not permanent dyes. They are dirt, and Jesus washes."},{"english":"Brought under the power of / mastered","hebrew":"ἐξουσιάζω","translit":"exousiázō","strongs":"G1850 (wordplay with ἔξεστι, éxesti, G1832)","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's defines ἐξουσιάζω as “to control,” and ἔξεστι, the slogan word “lawful,” as “it is right.” Paul plays them against each other: the slogan says πάντα μοι ἔξεστιν, everything is permitted to me; Paul answers οὐκ ἐγὼ ἐξουσιασθήσομαι, I will not be controlled by anything. The deepest danger of a freedom without limits is that it ends in a new slavery. Things that are permitted can still become masters — food, alcohol, screens, sex, money, approval. Paul's test of freedom is not “Is it allowed?” but “Is it ruling me?” A free man is not someone who can do anything. He is someone who is not owned by anything except Christ."},{"english":"Flee","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","strongs":"G5343","verses":"v. 18 (and 10:14)","insight":"Strong's: “to run away (literally or figuratively); by implication, to shun; by analogy, to vanish.” The word gives us the English “fugitive.” Paul uses it in the present imperative — keep fleeing, make running your habit. He uses it again in 10:14: “flee from idolatry.” And he tells Timothy to “flee from youthful lusts” (2 Timothy 2:22). In the moral life, some battles are won by standing firm (Ephesians 6:13). But with sexual temptation, the Bible's consistent strategy is distance. Joseph did not stay to argue with Potiphar's wife; he ran out of the house and left his garment in her hand (Genesis 39:12). The strongest believer is not the one who can stand closest to temptation without falling, but the one who runs soonest."},{"english":"Bought / purchased","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","strongs":"G59 (the price τιμή, timḗ, G5092)","verses":"v. 20 (again in 7:23)","insight":"Strong's: ἀγοράζω is “properly, to go to market, i.e. (by implication) to purchase; specially, to redeem,” and τιμή is “a value, i.e. money paid.” The word is built on ἀγορά, the marketplace, which in Corinth included the slave market. Paul's readers included slaves and former slaves who knew exactly what it meant to be bought: you belonged to a new master, totally. Paul uses the same phrase again in 7:23: “You were bought with a price. Don’t become bondservants of men.” Being bought by Christ frees you from every other owner. And the price of purchase is the measure of your worth. You were not bought cheaply. The price was His blood."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the rights you are holding tighter than your brother, the past you are still letting define you, and the part of your body you have been treating as your own.","items":[{"title":"Your Rights or Your Brother","body":"There is a grievance you are holding against another believer. Maybe it has gone to court; more likely it has gone to the court of your mind, where you have argued the case a thousand times and won it every time. They wronged you, cheated you, took what was yours. And you may be right. The Spirit is asking you the question Paul asked Corinth: “Why not rather be wronged?” Not because injustice does not matter, but because Christ is more important than your vindication, and your brother is worth more than your money or your reputation. What would it look like this week to release the grievance into the hands of the One who judges justly, and to seek peace with that person instead of victory over them?"},{"title":"Such Were You — But You Were Washed","body":"The enemy loves to tell you that you are still what you used to be. The addict, the adulterer, the liar, the thief, the person who did that thing you have never told anyone. And maybe you are still fighting some of it. The Spirit wants to give you a different voice to listen to. “Such were some of you. But you were washed. But you were sanctified. But you were justified.” Three past tense verbs. Done. Your identity is not the list in verses 9 and 10; it is the verbs in verse 11. That does not make the fight unnecessary — it makes the fight winnable, because you are fighting from cleanness, not toward it. Say it aloud today: “I was washed. I was sanctified. I was justified.”"},{"title":"The Room You Have Kept Locked","body":"Your body is the temple of the Holy Spirit. The Spirit lives in every part of it — your eyes, your hands, your mouth, your appetite, your sexuality. And yet there may be one area you have kept as your own. A habit you indulge in private. Images you look at. A relationship you have kept just over the line. Food or alcohol or a screen that has become a master. You have told yourself it is your business. The Spirit is saying gently but clearly: you are not your own. You were bought with a price. He is not asking for the area so He can shame you; He is asking for it because He lives there, and He wants to fill it with the glory of God. What room is it? Hand Him the key."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Grievance Check","body":"Is there a believer you are in conflict with — over money, property, an agreement, a hurt? Write their name. Then answer honestly: have I tried to resolve this within the church, privately and humbly? Have I been willing to be wronged rather than to win? Is there any way in which I have wronged them too (v. 8)?"},{"title":"The Identity Check","body":"Look at the list in verses 9 and 10. Which items describe your past? Which ones are you still struggling with? Write them down honestly. Then cross each one out and write over it: “Washed. Sanctified. Justified.” Ask: am I living from my old identity or my new one?"},{"title":"The Mastery Check","body":"Verse 12 says, “I will not be brought under the power of anything.” Name one permitted thing that has begun to master you — food, alcohol, a screen, social media, shopping, work, a substance, a habit. How often do you use it? What happens when you try to stop? If it controls you, it has become an idol, however lawful it is."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Making Peace Before Winning","body":"If you have a conflict with another believer, take one concrete step this week toward resolution within the body of Christ. Go to them privately and humbly. Do not start with your case; start by asking whether you have wronged them in any way, and apologise for anything you can honestly own. If you cannot resolve it together, ask a mature, trusted believer to help you both. And if the matter is about money or property, prayerfully consider whether you could let it go for the sake of your brother and the name of Christ."},{"title":"Dedicating the Temple","body":"Tonight, take ten minutes in a quiet place. Read verses 19 and 20 aloud slowly. Then dedicate each part of your body to God aloud, specifically: “Lord, my eyes belong to You — I will not use them to look at what defiles. My hands belong to You. My mouth belongs to You. My appetite belongs to You. My sexuality belongs to You.” If there is a specific area of temptation, name it and decide on one practical step to flee it this week: remove the app, end the conversation, change the route, install the filter, tell a trusted friend. Write the step down and do it within 24 hours. Fleeing is a decision made in advance."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, I come to You as one who was bought with a price. I am not my own. My body, my time, my money, my rights, my desires — all of them were purchased by Your blood, and all of them belong to You. Forgive me for living as though I owned myself. Father, I confess where I have held my rights tighter than my brothers and sisters. I have nursed grievances and argued my case and wanted to win more than I wanted peace. Teach me the way of the cross — to be willing to be wronged rather than to wrong another, and to love my brother more than my vindication. Thank You, Lord, that such were some of us — and such was I. But I was washed. I was sanctified. I was justified in the name of the Lord Jesus and by the Spirit of our God. Silence the voice that tells me I am still what I was. I am Yours. Holy Spirit, You live in my body. It is Your temple. I give You every room — my eyes, my hands, my mouth, my appetites, my sexuality. Where I have been mastered by anything other than Christ, break its power. Where I have been flirting with temptation, give me the wisdom and the speed to flee. And Lord, You are for my body. You will raise it. Let me glorify God in it today. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Abraham Lets Lot Choose","ref":"Genesis 13:1-18","relatedVerses":"v. 7","summary":"Abraham and his nephew Lot have both grown rich with flocks, and the land cannot support them together. Their herdsmen start quarrelling — in front of the Canaanites and Perizzites living in the land. Abraham could have insisted on his rights as the elder and the one to whom God had given the promise. Instead he says to Lot, let there be no strife between us, for we are brothers, and he gives Lot first choice of the land. Lot takes the well-watered plain near Sodom. Abraham takes what is left. And immediately afterward God speaks to Abraham and promises him all the land he can see, in every direction. Abraham chose to be wronged rather than to fight his brother in front of pagans — and God Himself defended his inheritance. That is verse 7 lived out: why not rather be wronged?"},{"title":"Samson and the Prostitute of Gaza","ref":"Judges 16:1-31","relatedVerses":"v. 15-18","summary":"Samson was set apart to God from birth, a Nazirite whose strength came from the Spirit of the LORD. But he never learned to flee. He went to Gaza and slept with a prostitute; then he loved Delilah, who was paid by the Philistines to find the secret of his strength. Day after day she pressed him, and instead of running he stayed, until he told her everything. His hair was cut, his strength left him, and the most dangerous line in his story follows: he did not know that the LORD had left him. The Philistines gouged out his eyes and set him grinding grain in prison. Samson's life is a portrait of a man who treated his body as his own and joined it to what destroyed him. God in His mercy still answered his final prayer — but the story is a warning written in blood: do not play with what you are commanded to flee."},{"title":"Hosea Buys Back His Wife","ref":"Hosea 3:1-5","relatedVerses":"v. 11, 19-20","summary":"God told the prophet Hosea to marry a woman who would be unfaithful to him, as a living picture of Israel's unfaithfulness to the LORD. Gomer left him for other lovers and ended up, it seems, in such a state that she had to be bought. And God told Hosea to go and love her again. So Hosea bought her back — for fifteen pieces of silver and a quantity of barley — and brought her home, telling her she would now be his alone. It is one of the most moving pictures of redemption in the Old Testament: a husband paying a price to buy back a wife who had sold herself to others. That is what Christ did for you. “You were bought with a price.” Not to be punished, but to be brought home and loved — and to belong to Him alone."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":7,"chapterTitle":"Married, Single, Widowed, Slave or Free — Living Undivided Before the Lord in Every Condition","themeTitle":"“Your Condition Is Not Your Calling — Christ Is”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Now concerning the things about which you wrote to me: it is good for a man not to touch a woman.","understanding":"“Now concerning” — the first of six times Paul uses this phrase to answer the Corinthians' letter. Then a sentence most scholars, rightly, read as a quotation of the Corinthians' own position: “it is good for a man not to touch a woman.” Ἅπτομαι, to touch, was a common euphemism for sexual relations. Having just corrected the libertines who said the body does not matter (chapter 6), Paul now faces the ascetics in the same church who said sex itself was unspiritual — even within marriage.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"wrote"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G680","hebrew":"ἅπτομαι","translit":"háptomai","english":"touch"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"}]},{"v":2,"text":"But, because of sexual immoralities, let each man have his own wife, and let each woman have her own husband.","understanding":"Paul's first qualification. Because πορνεῖαι — sexual immoralities, plural, the many forms available in Corinth — are a real danger, “let each man have his own wife, and let each woman have her own husband.” Ἔχω, to have, here means to have sexually in marriage. Note the perfect symmetry: each man, each woman, his own, her own. Monogamy, and mutuality.","words":[{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immoralities"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"have"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Let the husband give his wife the affection owed her, and likewise also the wife her husband.","understanding":"Ὀφειλή — a debt, what is owed. Husband and wife owe each other the intimacy of marriage, “and likewise also the wife her husband.” In a world where a man's sexual freedom was assumed and a wife's needs were rarely considered, Paul says the obligation runs equally in both directions.","words":[{"strongs":"G3782","hebrew":"ὀφειλή","translit":"opheilḗ","english":"affection owed"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husband"}]},{"v":4,"text":"The wife doesn’t have authority over her own body, but the husband. Likewise also the husband doesn’t have authority over his own body, but the wife.","understanding":"A revolutionary sentence in its culture. Ἐξουσιάζω — to have authority over. The wife does not have authority over her own body; the husband does. Every ancient reader would have expected that. But then: “likewise also the husband doesn’t have authority over his own body, but the wife.” No Greek or Roman moralist would have written the second half. In Christian marriage, each body belongs to the other.","words":[{"strongs":"G1850","hebrew":"ἐξουσιάζω","translit":"exousiázō","english":"have authority over"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Don’t deprive one another, unless it is by consent for a season, that you may give yourselves to fasting and prayer, and may be together again, that Satan doesn’t tempt you because of your lack of self-control.","understanding":"Ἀποστερέω — do not defraud one another, the same word as 6:7–8. Withholding marital intimacy is a form of robbery. The only exception: by mutual σύμφωνον — agreement, harmony, from which comes symphony — for a limited καιρός, so that you can σχολάζω, devote unhurried time, to prayer. Then come together again, so Satan does not πειράζω, tempt, you through ἀκρασία, lack of self-control. Paul is realistic: long abstinence in marriage is not a spiritual achievement; it is an opening for the enemy.","words":[{"strongs":"G650","hebrew":"ἀποστερέω","translit":"aposteréō","english":"deprive"},{"strongs":"G4859","hebrew":"σύμφωνος","translit":"sýmphōnos","english":"consent"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"season"},{"strongs":"G4980","hebrew":"σχολάζω","translit":"scholázō","english":"give yourselves"},{"strongs":"G4335","hebrew":"προσευχή","translit":"proseuchḗ","english":"prayer"},{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"tempt"},{"strongs":"G192","hebrew":"ἀκρασία","translit":"akrasía","english":"lack of self-control"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But this I say by way of concession, not of commandment.","understanding":"Συγγνώμη — a concession, a permission, as opposed to an ἐπιταγή, a command. What is the concession? Most naturally, the temporary abstinence of verse 5: Paul permits it but does not require it. He will not let the ascetics turn a voluntary season of prayer into a rule for every marriage.","words":[{"strongs":"G4774","hebrew":"συγγνώμη","translit":"syngnṓmē","english":"concession"},{"strongs":"G2003","hebrew":"ἐπιταγή","translit":"epitagḗ","english":"commandment"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Yet I wish that all men were like me. However each man has his own gift from God, one of this kind, and another of that kind.","understanding":"Paul is single and wishes everyone could share his freedom. But he immediately adds: “each man has his own χάρισμα from God.” Singleness is a grace-gift — the same word used for spiritual gifts in chapter 12 — and so, by implication, is marriage. One has this kind, another that kind. Neither is the superior spiritual path. Both are gifts to be received, not achievements to be proud of.","words":[{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"wish"},{"strongs":"G5486","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","english":"gift"}]},{"v":8,"text":"But I say to the unmarried and to widows, it is good for them if they remain even as I am.","understanding":"To the ἄγαμοι, the unmarried — the word may refer especially to widowers, since it is paired with widows — and the χῆραι, widows: it is good to remain as Paul is. Whether Paul had never married or was a widower himself, we do not know; Scripture does not say. What we know is that he was single as he wrote, and he found it good for ministry.","words":[{"strongs":"G22","hebrew":"ἄγαμος","translit":"ágamos","english":"unmarried"},{"strongs":"G5503","hebrew":"χήρα","translit":"chḗra","english":"widows"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"}]},{"v":9,"text":"But if they don’t have self-control, let them marry. For it’s better to marry than to burn.","understanding":"But if they do not ἐγκρατεύομαι — exercise self-control, the word an athlete used for disciplined training (9:25) — let them marry. “It’s better to marry than to πυρόω” — to burn, to be consumed with unfulfilled desire. Paul is not belittling marriage as a mere outlet. He is being realistic: God gave marriage as the right home for desire, and pretending to have the gift of singleness when you do not is not holiness.","words":[{"strongs":"G1467","hebrew":"ἐγκρατεύομαι","translit":"enkrateúomai","english":"have self-control"},{"strongs":"G1060","hebrew":"γαμέω","translit":"gaméō","english":"marry"},{"strongs":"G4448","hebrew":"πυρόω","translit":"pyróō","english":"burn"}]},{"v":10,"text":"But to the married I command—not I, but the Lord—that the wife not leave her husband","understanding":"Now to the married. Notice the careful distinction: “I command — not I, but the Lord.” Paul is citing Jesus' own teaching (Matthew 19:6; Mark 10:9–12). The wife is not to χωρίζω, separate from, her husband. In Greco-Roman society either spouse could end a marriage simply by leaving. Jesus' teaching, and Paul's, refuses that easy exit.","words":[{"strongs":"G3853","hebrew":"παραγγέλλω","translit":"parangéllō","english":"command"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G5563","hebrew":"χωρίζω","translit":"chōrízō","english":"leave"}]},{"v":11,"text":"(but if she departs, let her remain unmarried, or else be reconciled to her husband), and that the husband not leave his wife.","understanding":"A realistic parenthesis. If a separation has already happened, the wife must remain unmarried or be καταλλάσσω — reconciled — to her husband. That word is the great gospel word for God reconciling sinners to Himself (2 Corinthians 5:18–20). Christian marriage aims at reconciliation, not replacement. And the same applies to the husband: he is not to ἀφίημι, send away, divorce, his wife.","words":[{"strongs":"G5563","hebrew":"χωρίζω","translit":"chōrízō","english":"departs"},{"strongs":"G22","hebrew":"ἄγαμος","translit":"ágamos","english":"unmarried"},{"strongs":"G2644","hebrew":"καταλλάσσω","translit":"katallássō","english":"reconciled"},{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"leave"}]},{"v":12,"text":"But to the rest I—not the Lord—say, if any brother has an unbelieving wife, and she is content to live with him, let him not leave her.","understanding":"“To the rest I — not the Lord — say.” This is not Paul lowering his authority; he says in verse 40 that he has the Spirit of God, and in 14:37 that what he writes is the Lord's command. It means that Jesus had not addressed this specific situation during His earthly ministry, because it had not arisen yet: a marriage where one spouse becomes a believer and the other does not. If the unbelieving wife συνευδοκέω — consents, is willing — to stay, the believer must not divorce her.","words":[{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G571","hebrew":"ἄπιστος","translit":"ápistos","english":"unbelieving"},{"strongs":"G4909","hebrew":"συνευδοκέω","translit":"syneudokéō","english":"content"},{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"leave"}]},{"v":13,"text":"The woman who has an unbelieving husband, and he is content to live with her, let her not leave her husband.","understanding":"The same instruction exactly for a believing wife with an unbelieving husband. In the ancient world, a wife was expected to share her husband's gods; a woman who converted alone was taking a real risk. Paul does not tell her to leave. If he is willing to stay, she stays.","words":[{"strongs":"G571","hebrew":"ἄπιστος","translit":"ápistos","english":"unbelieving"},{"strongs":"G4909","hebrew":"συνευδοκέω","translit":"syneudokéō","english":"content"},{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"leave"}]},{"v":14,"text":"For the unbelieving husband is sanctified in the wife, and the unbelieving wife is sanctified in the husband. Otherwise your children would be unclean, but now they are holy.","understanding":"Why stay? Because the unbelieving spouse is ἁγιάζω — sanctified — in the believing one. This cannot mean saved; verse 16 shows the unbeliever still needs salvation. It means the marriage and the home are set apart, brought within the sphere of God's influence, through the believer. The Corinthians may have feared that a believer was defiled by an unbelieving spouse. Paul reverses it: holiness is more contagious than uncleanness. Your children are not ἀκάθαρτα, unclean, but ἅγια, holy.","words":[{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctified"},{"strongs":"G169","hebrew":"ἀκάθαρτος","translit":"akáthartos","english":"unclean"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Yet if the unbeliever departs, let there be separation. The brother or the sister is not under bondage in such cases, but God has called us in peace.","understanding":"But if the unbeliever χωρίζω — leaves — “let there be separation.” The believer is not δουλόω, enslaved, bound, in such cases. Many interpreters understand this as freedom to remarry when an unbelieving spouse permanently abandons the marriage; others read it more narrowly as freedom from the obligation to hold the marriage together. The text says the believer is not bound, and it grounds it in this: “God has called us in peace.” He does not require you to fight endlessly to keep someone who has chosen to go.","words":[{"strongs":"G5563","hebrew":"χωρίζω","translit":"chōrízō","english":"departs"},{"strongs":"G1402","hebrew":"δουλόω","translit":"doulóō","english":"under bondage"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For how do you know, wife, whether you will save your husband? Or how do you know, husband, whether you will save your wife?","understanding":"This verse can be read two ways. As hope: who knows, perhaps by staying you will save your spouse — so stay if they are willing. Or as release: you cannot know whether you will save them, so do not cling to a marriage they have abandoned on the theory that you must. Coming right after verse 15, the second reading fits the flow best. Either way, the salvation of a spouse is a hope to pray for, not a weight you can carry by force.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"save"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Only, as the Lord has distributed to each man, as God has called each, so let him walk. So I command in all the assemblies.","understanding":"The governing principle of the whole chapter, repeated three times (vv. 17, 20, 24): walk in the life the Lord μερίζω — apportioned — to you, in the situation in which God καλέω — called — you. Conversion does not require you to change your marital status, ethnicity or social position before you can serve God. “So I command in all the assemblies.” This is not a special rule for Corinth.","words":[{"strongs":"G3307","hebrew":"μερίζω","translit":"merízō","english":"distributed"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Was anyone called having been circumcised? Let him not become uncircumcised. Has anyone been called in uncircumcision? Let him not be circumcised.","understanding":"The first example: circumcision. Some Jewish men in the Greco-Roman world tried to hide their circumcision through a surgical procedure — ἐπισπάομαι, to draw over — so they could appear in the public baths and gymnasium without shame. Paul says: do not. And Gentiles should not get circumcised either. Your ethnic and religious background is not something you need to erase or adopt to belong to Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G4059","hebrew":"περιτέμνω","translit":"peritémnō","english":"circumcised"},{"strongs":"G1986","hebrew":"ἐπισπάομαι","translit":"epispáomai","english":"become uncircumcised"},{"strongs":"G203","hebrew":"ἀκροβυστία","translit":"akrobystía","english":"uncircumcision"}]},{"v":19,"text":"Circumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing, but the keeping of the commandments of God.","understanding":"A sentence that would have stunned every Jew who heard it: “Circumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing.” Circumcision was itself one of God's commandments! But Paul means that it no longer marks the boundary of God's people. What matters is τήρησις ἐντολῶν — keeping God's commands. In Galatians he puts it two other ways: what matters is “faith working through love” (5:6) and “a new creation” (6:15). The three together give the full picture: new life, expressed in faith, working itself out in obedient love.","words":[{"strongs":"G4061","hebrew":"περιτομή","translit":"peritomḗ","english":"circumcision"},{"strongs":"G203","hebrew":"ἀκροβυστία","translit":"akrobystía","english":"uncircumcision"},{"strongs":"G5084","hebrew":"τήρησις","translit":"tḗrēsis","english":"keeping"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Let each man stay in that calling in which he was called.","understanding":"The principle again. Κλῆσις here does not mean occupation, as later Christian usage sometimes took it. It means the situation you were in when God called you to Christ. Stay there, unless God moves you; your position is not an obstacle to your calling.","words":[{"strongs":"G2821","hebrew":"κλῆσις","translit":"klēsis","english":"calling"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"}]},{"v":21,"text":"Were you called being a bondservant? Don’t let that bother you, but if you get an opportunity to become free, use it.","understanding":"The second example: slavery, which touched a large proportion of the Corinthian church. Were you a δοῦλος when God called you? “Don’t let that bother you” — μὴ σοι μελέτω, do not let it trouble you. Your dignity before God is not reduced. Then a famously ambiguous phrase: either “if you can become free, take the opportunity,” as the WEB reads, or “even if you can become free, make use of your present condition.” The WEB's reading is the more natural one, and fits verse 23.","words":[{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bondservant"},{"strongs":"G3199","hebrew":"μέλω","translit":"mélō","english":"bother"},{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"free"},{"strongs":"G5530","hebrew":"χράομαι","translit":"chráomai","english":"use"}]},{"v":22,"text":"For he who was called in the Lord being a bondservant is the Lord’s free man. Likewise he who was called being free is Christ’s bondservant.","understanding":"The great reversal. The slave who is called in the Lord is the Lord's ἀπελεύθερος — freedman, a technical term for a slave who had been legally freed. And the free person who is called is Christ's δοῦλος, slave. Before God, everyone stands in the same place: freed from sin, owned by Christ. The social ladder of Corinth is flattened at the cross.","words":[{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bondservant"},{"strongs":"G558","hebrew":"ἀπελεύθερος","translit":"apeleútheros","english":"free man"},{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"free"}]},{"v":23,"text":"You were bought with a price. Don’t become bondservants of men.","understanding":"Paul repeats the phrase of 6:20 word for word: “You were bought with a price.” Then: “Don’t become bondservants of men.” You already belong to the Master who paid for you. Do not sell yourself to any other — not to human masters if you can avoid it, and not to human opinion, status or approval.","words":[{"strongs":"G59","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","english":"bought"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"price"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bondservants"}]},{"v":24,"text":"Brothers, let each man, in whatever condition he was called, stay in that condition with God.","understanding":"The third repetition of the principle, with two new words: stay in your condition “with God.” Not merely stay put; stay with God. The point is not resignation but presence: whatever your situation, it can be lived before God's face, in His company.","words":[{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":25,"text":"Now concerning virgins, I have no commandment from the Lord, but I give my judgment as one who has obtained mercy from the Lord to be trustworthy.","understanding":"“Now concerning” — the second topic from their letter: παρθένοι, virgins, here meaning young unmarried women, and perhaps engaged couples. Paul has no command from Jesus on this, so he gives his γνώμη, considered judgment, as one who has ἐλεέω — received mercy — to be πιστός, trustworthy. His authority rests on grace.","words":[{"strongs":"G3933","hebrew":"παρθένος","translit":"parthénos","english":"virgins"},{"strongs":"G2003","hebrew":"ἐπιταγή","translit":"epitagḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G1106","hebrew":"γνώμη","translit":"gnṓmē","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G1653","hebrew":"ἐλεέω","translit":"eleéō","english":"obtained mercy"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"trustworthy"}]},{"v":26,"text":"Therefore I think that because of the distress that is on us, that it’s good for a man to remain as he is.","understanding":"“The distress that is on us” — ἀνάγκη, pressure, constraint, and ἐνίστημι, present or impending. What was it? Paul does not say. It may have been a specific crisis in Corinth — there is evidence of grain shortages in the region in the 40s and 50s — or the general pressure of living in the last days before Christ's return, with persecution always possible. Either way, his advice is practical: in such times, it is good to remain as you are.","words":[{"strongs":"G318","hebrew":"ἀνάγκη","translit":"anánkē","english":"distress"},{"strongs":"G1764","hebrew":"ἐνίστημι","translit":"enístēmi","english":"on us (present)"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"}]},{"v":27,"text":"Are you bound to a wife? Don’t seek to be freed. Are you free from a wife? Don’t seek a wife.","understanding":"Two parallel commands. Δέω — are you bound to a wife? Do not seek λύσις, release. Are you free from a wife? Do not seek one. Notice this applies equally to married and single. Do not make your marital status the project of your life. Seek first the kingdom (Matthew 6:33).","words":[{"strongs":"G1210","hebrew":"δέω","translit":"déō","english":"bound"},{"strongs":"G3080","hebrew":"λύσις","translit":"lýsis","english":"freed"}]},{"v":28,"text":"But if you marry, you have not sinned. If a virgin marries, she has not sinned. Yet such will have oppression in the flesh, and I want to spare you.","understanding":"A vital clarification against the ascetics: “if you marry, you have not sinned.” Marriage is not second-class Christianity. But Paul is honest: married people will have θλῖψις — pressure, trouble — “in the flesh,” the ordinary burdens of family life in a hard world, and he would φείδομαι — spare — them if he could. He is giving pastoral advice, not a moral ranking.","words":[{"strongs":"G1060","hebrew":"γαμέω","translit":"gaméō","english":"marry"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sinned"},{"strongs":"G2347","hebrew":"θλῖψις","translit":"thlîpsis","english":"oppression"},{"strongs":"G5339","hebrew":"φείδομαι","translit":"pheídomai","english":"spare"}]},{"v":29,"text":"But I say this, brothers: the time is short, that from now on, both those who have wives may be as though they had none;","understanding":"The reason behind everything: “the time is short.” Συστέλλω — drawn together, compressed; the word was used of furling sails and of wrapping a body for burial. Time has been shortened by the coming of Christ. Then a series of “as though not” clauses, beginning with the most startling: those who have wives should live as though they had none. Not neglecting their wives, which would contradict verses 3 to 5, but not living as though marriage were ultimate.","words":[{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G4958","hebrew":"συστέλλω","translit":"systéllō","english":"short"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"have"}]},{"v":30,"text":"and those who weep, as though they didn’t weep; and those who rejoice, as though they didn’t rejoice; and those who buy, as though they didn’t possess;","understanding":"The series continues: those who κλαίω, weep, as though not weeping; those who χαίρω, rejoice, as though not rejoicing; those who buy, as though not κατέχω — holding on to it, possessing it permanently. Grief is real, joy is real, property is real — but none of them is final. The believer holds everything in this age with an open hand.","words":[{"strongs":"G2799","hebrew":"κλαίω","translit":"klaíō","english":"weep"},{"strongs":"G5463","hebrew":"χαίρω","translit":"chaírō","english":"rejoice"},{"strongs":"G59","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","english":"buy"},{"strongs":"G2722","hebrew":"κατέχω","translit":"katéchō","english":"possess"}]},{"v":31,"text":"and those who use the world, as not using it to the fullest. For the mode of this world passes away.","understanding":"Those who use the world, as not καταχράομαι — using it up, using it to the full, exhausting it. Then the reason: “the σχῆμα of this world passes away.” Σχῆμα is the outward form, the shape, the stage-set of the present world order — the word from which we get scheme. It is παράγω, passing by, like a scene changing in a theatre. Do not build your life on scenery.","words":[{"strongs":"G5530","hebrew":"χράομαι","translit":"chráomai","english":"use"},{"strongs":"G2710","hebrew":"καταχράομαι","translit":"katachráomai","english":"use to the fullest"},{"strongs":"G4976","hebrew":"σχῆμα","translit":"schēma","english":"mode"},{"strongs":"G3855","hebrew":"παράγω","translit":"parágō","english":"passes away"}]},{"v":32,"text":"But I desire to have you to be free from cares. He who is unmarried is concerned for the things of the Lord, how he may please the Lord;","understanding":"Paul wants them ἀμέριμνοι — free from anxious care. Then he contrasts two kinds of μεριμνάω, concern. The unmarried person is concerned for the things of the Lord, how to ἀρέσκω, please, the Lord. Paul is not condemning concern itself; he uses the same verb positively of caring for others in 12:25. He is noting where the concern naturally goes.","words":[{"strongs":"G275","hebrew":"ἀμέριμνος","translit":"amérimnos","english":"free from cares"},{"strongs":"G3309","hebrew":"μεριμνάω","translit":"merimnáō","english":"concerned"},{"strongs":"G700","hebrew":"ἀρέσκω","translit":"aréskō","english":"please"}]},{"v":33,"text":"but he who is married is concerned about the things of the world, how he may please his wife.","understanding":"The married man is concerned about “the things of the world, how he may please his wife.” This is not a criticism. Pleasing your spouse is exactly what a married person should do; Paul has just commanded it (vv. 3–5). He is simply observing that marriage legitimately divides a person's attention. The next verse's first word in many manuscripts is “and he is divided.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3309","hebrew":"μεριμνάω","translit":"merimnáō","english":"concerned"},{"strongs":"G700","hebrew":"ἀρέσκω","translit":"aréskō","english":"please"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wife"}]},{"v":34,"text":"There is also a difference between a wife and a virgin. The unmarried woman cares about the things of the Lord, that she may be holy both in body and in spirit. But she who is married cares about the things of the world—how she may please her husband.","understanding":"The same contrast for women. The unmarried woman and the παρθένος care about the things of the Lord, “that she may be holy both in body and in spirit” — wholly given to Him. The married woman cares how to please her husband. Again, not criticism, but realism. Married or single, the question is how to give the Lord the most undivided heart your situation allows.","words":[{"strongs":"G3307","hebrew":"μερίζω","translit":"merízō","english":"difference (divided)"},{"strongs":"G3933","hebrew":"παρθένος","translit":"parthénos","english":"virgin"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G700","hebrew":"ἀρέσκω","translit":"aréskō","english":"please"}]},{"v":35,"text":"This I say for your own profit; not that I may ensnare you, but for that which is appropriate, and that you may attend to the Lord without distraction.","understanding":"Paul's purpose: for their own σύμφορον, benefit — not to throw a βρόχος over them, a noose, a lasso to restrict them. He wants what is εὔσχημον — fitting, beautiful, appropriate — and that they may attend to the Lord ἀπερισπάστως, without distraction. That word describes exactly what Martha lacked and Mary had (Luke 10:40, where Martha is περιεσπᾶτο, “distracted”).","words":[{"strongs":"G4851","hebrew":"συμφέρω","translit":"symphérō","english":"profit"},{"strongs":"G1029","hebrew":"βρόχος","translit":"bróchos","english":"ensnare"},{"strongs":"G2158","hebrew":"εὐσχήμων","translit":"euschḗmōn","english":"appropriate"},{"strongs":"G563","hebrew":"ἀπερισπάστως","translit":"aperispástōs","english":"without distraction"}]},{"v":36,"text":"But if any man thinks that he is behaving inappropriately toward his virgin, if she is past the flower of her age, and if need so requires, let him do what he desires. He doesn’t sin. Let them marry.","understanding":"One of the hardest verses in the letter to interpret. Who is “any man” and who is “his virgin”? Two main views: a father deciding about his unmarried daughter, or a man and the woman he is engaged to. If he thinks he is ἀσχημονέω — acting improperly — and she is ὑπέρακμος — past the peak of her youth, or (if it refers to him) his passions are strong — and it must be, let them marry. “He doesn’t sin.” The pastoral point is clear even where the details are uncertain: marriage is honourable.","words":[{"strongs":"G807","hebrew":"ἀσχημονέω","translit":"aschēmonéō","english":"behaving inappropriately"},{"strongs":"G3933","hebrew":"παρθένος","translit":"parthénos","english":"virgin"},{"strongs":"G5230","hebrew":"ὑπέρακμος","translit":"hypérakmos","english":"past the flower of her age"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G1060","hebrew":"γαμέω","translit":"gaméō","english":"marry"}]},{"v":37,"text":"But he who stands steadfast in his heart, having no urgency, but has power over his own will, and has determined in his own heart to keep his own virgin, does well.","understanding":"But the one who stands ἑδραῖος — firm, settled, the word Paul uses of steadfastness in 15:58 — in his heart, under no ἀνάγκη, no compulsion, with control over his own θέλημα, will, and has decided to τηρέω, keep, his virgin (unmarried), does well. A settled, unpressured decision for singleness is a good decision.","words":[{"strongs":"G1476","hebrew":"ἑδραῖος","translit":"hedraîos","english":"steadfast"},{"strongs":"G318","hebrew":"ἀνάγκη","translit":"anánkē","english":"urgency"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"well"}]},{"v":38,"text":"So then both he who gives his own virgin in marriage does well, and he who doesn’t give her in marriage does better.","understanding":"The conclusion: both choices are good. Ἐκγαμίζω — to give in marriage — does well; not doing so does better, in the specific sense Paul has explained: freedom for undivided devotion in a time of pressure. “Better” here is not “holier.” It is “more advantageous for the work of the kingdom, for those who have the gift.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1547","hebrew":"ἐκγαμίζω","translit":"ekgamízō","english":"gives in marriage"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"well"}]},{"v":39,"text":"A wife is bound by law for as long as her husband lives; but if the husband is dead, she is free to be married to whomever she desires, only in the Lord.","understanding":"A final word on widows. A wife is δέω, bound, as long as her husband lives (compare Romans 7:2). But if he κοιμάω — falls asleep, the Christian euphemism for death — she is free to marry whomever she wishes, “only in the Lord.” That is, to a fellow believer. Remarriage is free; it is not unconditional.","words":[{"strongs":"G1210","hebrew":"δέω","translit":"déō","english":"bound"},{"strongs":"G2837","hebrew":"κοιμάω","translit":"koimáō","english":"dead (fallen asleep)"},{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"free"},{"strongs":"G1060","hebrew":"γαμέω","translit":"gaméō","english":"married"}]},{"v":40,"text":"But she is happier if she stays as she is, in my judgment, and I think that I also have God’s Spirit.","understanding":"But in Paul's γνώμη, judgment, she is μακαριωτέρα — happier, more blessed — if she remains as she is. And then, with a touch of irony toward those in Corinth who claimed special spiritual insight: “I think that I also have God’s Spirit.” Paul's “judgment” is not a mere opinion. It is Spirit-led counsel.","words":[{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"happier"},{"strongs":"G1106","hebrew":"γνώμη","translit":"gnṓmē","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Two Bodies, One Ledger","verseRef":"v. 3-5","image":"Picture an ancient account book kept by a household steward, with two columns facing each other. In one column, what the husband owes; in the other, what the wife owes. In most Greco-Roman homes, only one column would have had anything written in it: the wife owed her husband everything, and he owed her very little. Now picture Paul taking the pen and writing the same entries in both columns, line for line. The husband owes his wife. The wife owes her husband. Her body belongs to him; his body belongs to her. And then he writes underneath, in capitals: DO NOT DEFRAUD.","meaning":"In a culture where a husband's sexual freedom was largely taken for granted and a wife was treated as property, Paul's teaching was revolutionary. Christian marriage is radically mutual. Neither spouse owns their own body; each belongs to the other. That rules out both of Corinth's errors at once: the libertine who seeks sex outside marriage and the ascetic who withholds it within marriage as if holiness required it. The only permitted abstinence is mutual, temporary and devoted to prayer — and even then, Paul says, come back together, so that Satan does not get a foothold. Holiness in marriage does not mean less intimacy. It means faithful, generous, self-giving intimacy that reflects the covenant love of God."},{"title":"Furling the Sails","verseRef":"v. 29-31","image":"Picture a merchant ship coming into the harbour at Cenchreae at the end of a long voyage. As the port draws near, the captain gives the order and the sailors climb the rigging and begin to furl the great sails, drawing them in, binding them tight. The voyage is nearly over. Everything on deck changes: cargo is brought up, ropes are coiled, passengers gather their belongings. Nobody begins redecorating their cabin now. That image is in Paul's word in verse 29 — συστέλλω, drawn together, furled, compressed. “The time is short.”","meaning":"Because Christ has come and will come again, the present age has been compressed. That does not make marriage, grief, joy, possessions or work meaningless. But it makes none of them ultimate. Those who weep, as though not weeping; those who rejoice, as though not rejoicing; those who buy, as though not possessing — because “the mode of this world passes away.” The word σχῆμα describes a scene on a stage, scenery that is about to be changed. Many believers build their whole lives on the scenery — the house, the career, the relationship, the comfort. Paul says: hold it all with an open hand. Enjoy it, use it, grieve it, but do not cling to it. The ship is coming into port."},{"title":"Mary at His Feet","verseRef":"v. 32-35","image":"In a house in Bethany, Martha is moving fast — pots on the fire, bread to bake, guests to serve, her face flushed, her mind pulled in ten directions. The word Luke uses for her is περισπάω — dragged around, distracted (Luke 10:40). And at the feet of Jesus sits her sister Mary, still, listening, with nothing else in her hands. Jesus says to Martha: you are anxious and troubled about many things, but one thing is needed. Paul uses the opposite of Martha's word in verse 35: ἀπερισπάστως — undistracted, not dragged around.","meaning":"This is Paul's goal in the whole chapter. Not to rank marriage below singleness, and not to put a noose on anyone, but that every believer, married or single, would attend to the Lord without distraction. The single person has a particular opportunity for undivided devotion, and Paul wants them to see it as a gift, not a lack. The married person has legitimate concerns for spouse and family, and Paul wants them to fulfil those concerns faithfully without letting them crowd out the Lord. The question for each of us is not, “Which state is better?” It is, “What is dragging me away from His feet, and how can I give Him the most undivided heart my situation allows?”"}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Marriage and Sexual Intimacy (v. 1-7)","body":"Against the Corinthian slogan that sexual relations are unspiritual, Paul affirms marriage as the remedy for immorality and insists on mutual, generous intimacy; abstinence is only by agreement, for a season, for prayer. Singleness and marriage are each a gift from God."},{"label":"The Unmarried and Widows (v. 8-9)","body":"It is good to remain single as Paul is, but if they lack self-control, they should marry."},{"label":"Believers Married to Believers (v. 10-11)","body":"On the authority of Jesus' own teaching: no divorce; if separated, remain unmarried or be reconciled."},{"label":"Believers Married to Unbelievers (v. 12-16)","body":"Do not leave a willing unbelieving spouse — the home is sanctified through the believer; but if the unbeliever leaves, the believer is not bound, for God has called us to peace."},{"label":"Remain in Your Calling (v. 17-24)","body":"The governing principle: live the life God assigned you when He called you. Circumcision, uncircumcision, slavery and freedom do not determine your standing with God; you were bought with a price — stay with God."},{"label":"Virgins and the Shortness of Time (v. 25-35)","body":"Because of the present distress and because the time is short, Paul advises remaining as you are — not as a command but for undivided devotion; marriage is not sin, but it brings legitimate cares."},{"label":"Engaged Couples and Widows (v. 36-40)","body":"Marrying does well; remaining single with a settled will does better for undivided service; a widow is free to remarry, only in the Lord, though Paul judges her happier if she stays as she is."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Against Both Errors: The Goodness of Sexual Intimacy in Marriage","paragraphs":["Chapter 7 begins the second half of the letter, in which Paul answers questions the Corinthians had written to him, each marked by the phrase “Now concerning” (7:1, 25; 8:1; 12:1; 16:1, 12). The first question is about sex and marriage, and the first sentence is almost certainly a quotation of the Corinthians' own view: “it is good for a man not to touch a woman.” The verb ἅπτομαι, to touch, was a familiar euphemism for sexual intercourse. In chapter 6 Paul corrected the libertines who said the body did not matter and they could do as they pleased. Here he corrects the opposite group in the same church: ascetics who believed that because the spiritual was everything and the body was nothing, true spirituality meant abstaining from sex — even, apparently, within marriage.","Paul's response is a careful and beautiful balance. He grants that celibacy can be good (vv. 7–8), but he denies that marriage or marital intimacy is unspiritual. Because of the danger of sexual immorality, each man should have his own wife and each woman her own husband (v. 2). Then comes a series of statements that would have been startling in the ancient world for their mutuality. The husband owes his wife sexual intimacy, “and likewise also the wife her husband” (v. 3). The wife does not have authority over her own body, but the husband does — and “likewise also the husband doesn’t have authority over his own body, but the wife” (v. 4). In a world where a husband was legally free to visit prostitutes and a wife was often treated as property, Paul writes both columns of the ledger identically. Christian marriage is a covenant of mutual self-giving.","Verse 5 then forbids ἀποστερέω — defrauding — the same word used of believers cheating each other in court (6:7–8). To withhold marital intimacy is a form of robbery. The only exception is mutual agreement, for a limited time, for the purpose of devoted prayer, and even then the couple should come together again, “that Satan doesn’t tempt you because of your lack of self-control.” Paul is a realist about human nature and about the enemy. Long abstinence in marriage is not a mark of spiritual maturity; it is a door left open. And then verse 7: “each man has his own gift from God, one of this kind, and another of that kind.” The word is χάρισμα, the same word used for the spiritual gifts in chapters 12 to 14. Singleness is a grace-gift; so, by implication, is marriage. Neither one makes you more spiritual. Both are given."]},{"heading":"Divorce, Mixed Marriages and the Peace of God","paragraphs":["In verses 10 and 11 Paul addresses married believers, and he does so with the highest authority available: “I command — not I, but the Lord.” He is citing the teaching of Jesus, who said, “What therefore God has joined together, don’t let man tear apart” (Matthew 19:6), and “Whoever divorces his wife, and marries another, commits adultery against her” (Mark 10:11). In Corinth, as in the Roman world generally, divorce was simple: either spouse could end a marriage by walking out. Paul says the Christian ideal is permanence, and that if separation does occur, the goal is reconciliation, not remarriage (v. 11). He uses the word καταλλάσσω — the same verb he uses in 2 Corinthians 5:18–20 for God reconciling the world to Himself. The gospel of reconciliation shapes the Christian approach to a broken marriage. (Jesus Himself named one exception in Matthew 19:9 — sexual immorality — which Paul does not discuss here because he is stating the general rule.)","Verses 12 to 16 then address a new situation Jesus had not spoken to, because it had not yet arisen: a marriage where one spouse came to faith and the other did not. “To the rest I — not the Lord — say” is not a lower level of authority; Paul claims the Spirit's guidance in verse 40 and in 14:37 calls his writing the Lord's command. It simply means there is no recorded saying of Jesus on this point. The Corinthians apparently feared that a believer was defiled by remaining married to an unbeliever. Paul says the opposite: “the unbelieving husband is sanctified in the wife” (v. 14). This does not mean the unbeliever is saved — verse 16 shows he still needs salvation. It means the marriage is set apart for God, the home is under the influence of His holiness, and the children are not unclean but holy. Holiness, in the new covenant, is more contagious than defilement. Peter gives the same counsel to wives with unbelieving husbands: they “may be won by the behavior of their wives without a word” (1 Peter 3:1).","But if the unbeliever leaves, “let there be separation. The brother or the sister is not under bondage in such cases, but God has called us in peace” (v. 15). The interpretation of “not under bondage” is honestly debated. Many careful interpreters, both ancient and modern, understand it to mean that a believer who has been permanently abandoned by an unbelieving spouse is free to remarry; others think it means only that the believer is not obliged to fight to preserve a marriage the other has ended. The text clearly says the believer is not bound, and it grounds this in the peace of God. Verse 16 then probably means: you cannot know whether staying would lead to your spouse's salvation, so do not cling to a marriage that has been abandoned on the theory that you must. God does not call you to endless conflict. He calls you to peace."]},{"heading":"Bloom Where You Were Planted — Because the Time Is Short","paragraphs":["The centre of the chapter is verses 17 to 24, where Paul steps back and states the principle behind his advice, repeating it three times: “as God has called each, so let him walk” (v. 17); “Let each man stay in that calling in which he was called” (v. 20); “let each man, in whatever condition he was called, stay in that condition with God” (v. 24). He applies it to two areas beyond marriage. Ethnic and religious background: the circumcised should not try to undo it, and the uncircumcised should not try to acquire it, because “Circumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing, but the keeping of the commandments of God” (v. 19). And social status: a slave should not be troubled by his condition, because before God he is the Lord's freedman, while the free man is Christ's slave (v. 22). In the Corinthian church, which included both wealthy households and many slaves, this was enormously important. Your standing with God does not depend on your status in society.","Two things guard this principle from misuse. First, it is not a command to accept injustice. Paul explicitly says that if a slave can gain freedom, he should take it (v. 21, on the most natural reading), and verse 23 adds: “You were bought with a price. Don’t become bondservants of men.” Paul's letter to Philemon shows him working for the freedom of a slave he loved. The point is not that circumstances do not matter but that they do not define you. You can serve God fully in any condition, and you need not wait for your circumstances to change before you begin. Second, the principle is not resignation but relationship: “stay in that condition with God” (v. 24). Every situation can be lived before God's face.","From verse 25 the reason for Paul's preference for singleness becomes clear: “the distress that is on us” (v. 26) and “the time is short” (v. 29). The distress may have been a local crisis, such as the food shortages known in the region in this period, or the general pressure on believers in the last days; Paul does not specify. But the theology of verses 29 to 31 applies in every generation. Time has been compressed by the coming of Christ. The present form of the world is passing away like a scene on a stage. Those who marry, weep, rejoice, buy or use the world should do so “as though not” — fully engaged, but holding nothing as ultimate. That is why Paul says marriage is not sin (v. 28), but that the single person can give the Lord undivided attention (vv. 32–35). His goal is not to burden anyone — “not that I may ensnare you” — but “that you may attend to the Lord without distraction” (v. 35). Married or single, slave or free, Jew or Gentile, the one thing needed is the same: an undivided heart."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["Paul's teaching on singleness as a gift (v. 7) echoes the teaching of Jesus. When Jesus forbade divorce, His disciples said, “If this is the case of the man with his wife, it is not expedient to marry.” And Jesus replied, “Not all men can receive this saying, but those to whom it is given. For there are eunuchs who were born that way from their mother’s womb, and there are eunuchs who were made eunuchs by men; and there are eunuchs who made themselves eunuchs for the Kingdom of Heaven’s sake. He who is able to receive it, let him receive it” (Matthew 19:10–12). Note the phrase “those to whom it is given” — the same idea as Paul's χάρισμα. Celibacy for the kingdom is a gift, not a requirement, and not a higher spiritual class. And Paul warns Timothy that those who forbid marriage are teaching “doctrines of demons” (1 Timothy 4:1–3). The New Testament honours both singleness and marriage and forbids anyone to despise either.","Paul's teaching on the permanence of marriage stands in an unbroken line from the prophets through Jesus. Malachi, the last of the prophets, rebuked men who were abandoning the wives of their youth: “Yahweh has been witness between you and the wife of your youth, against whom you have dealt treacherously, though she is your companion, and the wife of your covenant” (Malachi 2:14). And God gives His own verdict: “For I hate divorce” (Malachi 2:16). Jesus returns to creation itself: the two become one flesh, and what God has joined, man must not separate (Matthew 19:4–6). And Paul, in Ephesians, gives marriage its deepest meaning: “Husbands, love your wives, even as Christ also loved the assembly, and gave himself up for it” (Ephesians 5:25). Christian marriage is a living picture of the covenant between Christ and His church. That is why its faithfulness matters so much.","And the theme of verses 29 to 31, that “the mode of this world passes away,” is echoed by John: “The world is passing away with its lusts, but he who does God’s will remains forever” (1 John 2:17). Jesus taught that in the resurrection “they neither marry, nor are given in marriage, but are like God’s angels in heaven” (Matthew 22:30). Marriage is a good gift for this age, but it is not the final reality; it points beyond itself to the marriage supper of the Lamb (Revelation 19:7–9). Living with that end in view does not cheapen marriage or singleness. It frees both from the impossible burden of being everything. Jesus said, “seek first God’s Kingdom, and his righteousness; and all these things will be given to you as well” (Matthew 6:33). Undivided devotion to Him is the one thing needed, in every condition of life."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Deprive / defraud","hebrew":"ἀποστερέω","translit":"aposteréō","strongs":"G650","verses":"v. 5 (see 6:7–8)","insight":"Strong's: “to despoil.” Paul uses the same verb he used in 6:7–8 for believers cheating each other in court. Within marriage, withholding sexual intimacy is a form of fraud — depriving your spouse of what the covenant owes them. This is not a licence for one spouse to demand or coerce; verse 4 makes both bodies belong to each other, which rules out selfishness on either side. The point is that marital intimacy is a debt of love, freely and mutually given. The only exception Paul allows is mutual consent, for a time, for prayer — and even then, he says, come back together."},{"english":"Gift (of grace)","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","strongs":"G5486","verses":"v. 7","insight":"Strong's: “a (divine) gratuity, i.e. deliverance (from danger or passion); (specially), a (spiritual) endowment.” This is the word used for spiritual gifts in 1:7, 12:4, 12:9, 12:28 and 12:31. By using it of singleness — and by implication of marriage — Paul elevates both. Neither is a default or a failure. Each is a gift of God's grace, given to particular people for His purposes. Notice the phrase “each man has his own gift.” You do not choose which gift you have; you discover it. And the person who has the gift of singleness should not envy the married, nor the married despise the single. Both have received grace."},{"english":"Called","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","strongs":"G2564 (noun, calling κλῆσις, klēsis, G2821)","verses":"v. 15, 17, 18 (×2), 20 (×2), 21, 22 (×2), 24","insight":"Strong's: “to ‘call’ (properly, aloud, but used in a variety of applications, directly or otherwise).” The verb appears nine times in verses 15 to 24, making it the key word of the passage. In Paul it almost always refers to God's effective call to salvation (1:9, 26). The noun κλῆσις, calling, in verse 20 does not mean a job or career, as later Christian usage sometimes took it, but the circumstances in which God's call found you. The great truth of this section is that God's call reaches people in every situation — married, single, slave, free, Jew, Gentile — and that you can answer His call fully without first changing your circumstances."},{"english":"Shortened / compressed","hebrew":"συστέλλω","translit":"systéllō","strongs":"G4958","verses":"v. 29","insight":"Strong's: “to send (draw) together, i.e. enwrap (enshroud a corpse for burial), contract (an interval).” The word was used of furling sails as a ship came into harbour and, in Acts 5:6, of the young men wrapping the body of Ananias for burial. “The time is short” means that the time remaining has been drawn together, compressed — by the coming of Christ, the present age has been put on a short schedule. That is not a reason for panic but for perspective. When the sails are being furled, you prepare to land. You do not start rearranging the deck chairs."},{"english":"Without distraction","hebrew":"ἀπερισπάστως","translit":"aperispástōs","strongs":"G563","verses":"v. 35","insight":"Strong's: “undistractedly, i.e. free from (domestic) solicitude.” It is built from a negative prefix and the verb περισπάω, to drag around, the word Luke uses of Martha, who “was distracted with much serving” (Luke 10:40). Paul's whole aim in the chapter is summed up in this one word. He does not want anyone dragged around by anxious concern. He wants every believer — married or single — to be able to attend to the Lord with an undivided heart. Marriage and singleness are not competing paths to holiness. They are different settings in which the same goal must be pursued: sitting at His feet, undistracted."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the way you have made your marital status your identity, the circumstances you are waiting to change before you serve God fully, and the scenery you have been building your life on.","items":[{"title":"Your Status Is Not Your Story","body":"If you are single, you may have come to see your singleness as a waiting room, a problem to be solved, or evidence that something is wrong with you. If you are married, you may have made your marriage the centre of your world, or you may be quietly unhappy in it and wondering whether God has failed you. The Spirit says to both: each has his own gift from God. Your marital status is not your identity, and it is not your calling. Christ is. If you are single, receive it as a gift and use its freedom for undivided devotion. If you are married, love your spouse generously, and do not let even a good marriage crowd out the Lord. And if your marriage is broken, know that God has called you in peace."},{"title":"Stay With God Where You Are","body":"You have been waiting. Waiting for the better job, the healed relationship, the move, the promotion, the spouse, the end of a hard season — and telling yourself that then you will really serve God. Three times in this chapter Paul says: remain in the condition in which you were called, “with God.” Not resignation — presence. The Spirit wants you to stop postponing obedience until your circumstances improve. Where you are right now is where God has called you to walk with Him. Ask: how can I serve Christ fully in this situation, today, as it is? And if a door to freedom opens, walk through it — but do not wait for it to begin."},{"title":"The Scenery Is Changing","body":"“The mode of this world passes away.” The career, the house, the reputation, the comfort, even the good relationships of this age — all of it is scenery on a stage that is about to change. The Spirit is not asking you to despise these things. He is asking you not to cling to them. What are you holding so tightly that losing it would destroy you? What have you built your sense of security on that is part of the passing scene? The time is short. The sails are being furled. Let the Spirit loosen your grip, so that you can weep without despair, rejoice without idolatry, and buy without being owned by what you buy."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Marriage Check (for the married)","body":"Are you giving your spouse the affection, attention and intimacy you owe them (vv. 3–5), freely and generously? Or have you been withholding — physically, emotionally, or with your time — for reasons that have nothing to do with prayer? Write down one area where you have been defrauding your spouse, and one area where you can give more."},{"title":"The Gift Check (for the single)","body":"Do you see your singleness as a gift from God (v. 7) or as a lack? What opportunities for undivided devotion does it give you right now that you are not using? Write down one way you could use your freedom this month to serve the Lord in a way a married person could not."},{"title":"The Open-Hand Check (for everyone)","body":"Read verses 29 to 31 slowly. Then write down the one thing in this world you would find hardest to lose. Ask honestly: am I holding this “as though not,” or has it become ultimate? What would it look like to hold it with an open hand?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Undistracted Time","body":"Paul's goal is that you would “attend to the Lord without distraction” (v. 35). This week, set aside one hour — married or single — to sit at the feet of Jesus like Mary. Turn off your phone. Go somewhere quiet. Bring only your Bible and a notebook. Spend the first fifteen minutes simply naming the things that usually drag you around, and handing each one to God. Spend the rest reading slowly, praying, and listening. If you are married, consider doing this by mutual agreement as a couple, and then sharing with each other what God showed you."},{"title":"Serving Where You Stand","body":"Identify the condition you are “called in” right now — your work, your marital status, your family situation, your health, your limitations. Write it at the top of a page. Then write underneath: “Lord, how can I serve You fully here, today, as it is?” List three specific ways. Choose one and do it this week, without waiting for your circumstances to change. Then pray verse 24 over your situation: “Lord, I stay in this condition — with You.”"}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, You have given each of us our own gift — one of this kind, another of that kind. Thank You for the gift You have given me. Forgive me for envying others' gifts and despising my own, and forgive me for making my status — married or single — into my identity, when my identity is in Christ. If I am married, teach me to love my spouse with generous, faithful, self-giving love; let me never defraud them of what love owes; and let our home be set apart for You. If I am single, teach me to receive my singleness as grace and to use its freedom for undivided devotion to You. Lord, You called me in the condition I was in, and You have not asked me to wait for my circumstances to change before I serve You. Help me to walk with You here, today, as I am. I was bought with a price; I will not become a slave of men or of anything in this passing world. Lord, the time is short. The form of this world is passing away. Loosen my grip on everything that is only scenery. Let me weep without despair, rejoice without idolatry, buy without being owned. And above all, give me an undivided heart — to sit at Your feet without distraction, like Mary, choosing the one thing that will not be taken away. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Anna, Eighty-Four Years Undistracted","ref":"Luke 2:36-38","relatedVerses":"v. 8, 32-35, 40","summary":"Anna had been married for only seven years when her husband died. Then she lived as a widow for decades — Luke says about eighty-four years — and she never left the temple, worshipping God with fasting and prayer night and day. She did not treat widowhood as an empty waiting room. She used its freedom for undivided devotion. And so when Mary and Joseph brought the infant Jesus into the temple, Anna was there at that very hour, and she recognised Him. She gave thanks to God and spoke about the child to everyone who was waiting for the redemption of Jerusalem. Anna is a living illustration of 1 Corinthians 7:34–35: a woman who cared for the things of the Lord, holy in body and spirit, attending to Him without distraction, and positioned to see the Messiah when He came."},{"title":"Ruth Marries Only in the Lord","ref":"Ruth 1:16-17; 3:1-13; 4:9-17","relatedVerses":"v. 39","summary":"Ruth was a Moabite widow who chose to follow her mother-in-law Naomi back to Israel, saying that Naomi's people would be her people and Naomi's God her God. In Bethlehem she gleaned in the fields of Boaz, a godly relative of her late husband. When the time came for her to remarry, she did not seek just anyone. She went to Boaz, a man of faith, and asked him to act as her kinsman-redeemer. Boaz praised her because she had not gone after young men, rich or poor. He redeemed the family property, married her before the elders at the gate, and from their marriage came Obed, the grandfather of David — and the line of the Messiah. Ruth shows the freedom and the boundary of verse 39: a widow is free to remarry whom she wishes, only in the Lord."},{"title":"Onesimus, the Lord's Freedman","ref":"Philemon 1:8-21","relatedVerses":"v. 21-23","summary":"Onesimus was a slave who ran away from his master, Philemon, a believer in Colossae. Somehow he met Paul in prison, and there he came to Christ. Paul sent him back to Philemon with a short letter that is now part of Scripture. In it Paul asks Philemon to receive Onesimus no longer as a slave but as more than a slave, a beloved brother, and to welcome him as he would welcome Paul himself. Paul even offers to pay whatever Onesimus owes. Onesimus had been called in the Lord while still a slave, and Paul treated him as the Lord's freedman and his own son. And Paul worked, with careful pastoral wisdom, for his freedom. That is 1 Corinthians 7:21–23 in a real friendship: your status does not define your worth in Christ — and if freedom can be gained, take it."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":8,"chapterTitle":"Knowledge Puffs Up, Love Builds Up — The Brother for Whom Christ Died","themeTitle":"“Being Right Is Not the Same as Being Loving”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Now concerning things sacrificed to idols: We know that we all have knowledge. Knowledge puffs up, but love builds up.","understanding":"“Now concerning” — the third topic from their letter: εἰδωλόθυτα, things sacrificed to idols. In Corinth, much of the meat available came from animals offered in pagan temples; part was burned, part eaten by the worshippers, and the rest often sold in the market. Then Paul quotes a Corinthian slogan, “We all have knowledge,” and immediately puts it in its place: γνῶσις φυσιοῖ — knowledge inflates, like a bellows — but ἀγάπη οἰκοδομεῖ — love builds a house. One fills you with air. The other builds something that lasts.","words":[{"strongs":"G1494","hebrew":"εἰδωλόθυτον","translit":"eidōlóthyton","english":"things sacrificed to idols"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G5448","hebrew":"φυσιόω","translit":"physióō","english":"puffs up"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3618","hebrew":"οἰκοδομέω","translit":"oikodoméō","english":"builds up"}]},{"v":2,"text":"But if anyone thinks that he knows anything, he doesn’t yet know as he ought to know.","understanding":"The person who thinks he has arrived at knowledge has shown that he hasn't. The perfect tense “knows” suggests someone who believes he has finished learning. True knowledge always includes an awareness of how much remains unknown. The Corinthians' confidence in their knowledge was the very proof that it was incomplete.","words":[{"strongs":"G1380","hebrew":"δοκέω","translit":"dokéō","english":"thinks"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knows"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"}]},{"v":3,"text":"But if anyone loves God, the same is known by him.","understanding":"A stunning reversal. You expect Paul to say, “if anyone loves God, he knows God.” Instead: “the same is known by him.” The decisive thing is not how much you know about God but that God knows you — and the evidence of being known by Him is that you love Him. Knowledge begins with being known (compare Galatians 4:9).","words":[{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loves"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"known"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Therefore concerning the eating of things sacrificed to idols, we know that no idol is anything in the world, and that there is no other God but one.","understanding":"Now the theology the “strong” in Corinth were relying on, and Paul agrees with it. “No idol is anything in the world” — an εἴδωλον has no real existence as a god. And “there is no other God but one” — the heart of Israel's faith, the Shema of Deuteronomy 6:4. The strong were right about this. The question is what they did with being right.","words":[{"strongs":"G1497","hebrew":"εἴδωλον","translit":"eídōlon","english":"idol"},{"strongs":"G3762","hebrew":"οὐδείς","translit":"oudeís","english":"nothing"},{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"}]},{"v":5,"text":"For though there are things that are called “gods”, whether in the heavens or on earth; as there are many “gods” and many “lords”;","understanding":"Paul acknowledges the religious landscape of Corinth: there are so-called gods and so-called lords everywhere — in the heavens (sun, moon, planets, the Olympian gods) and on earth (local deities, emperor cults, household gods). Corinth had temples to Apollo, Aphrodite, Asclepius, Demeter and many more. Paul puts “gods” and “lords” in a qualifier: “things that are called.” They are many in name. They are not real as gods.","words":[{"strongs":"G3004","hebrew":"λέγω","translit":"légō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"gods"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"lords"}]},{"v":6,"text":"yet to us there is one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we for him; and one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom are all things, and we live through him.","understanding":"One of the most important Christological statements in the New Testament. Paul takes the Shema — “The LORD our God, the LORD is one” — and splits it between the Father and the Son without breaking its oneness. “One God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we for him; and one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom are all things, and we live through him.” The title Lord, κύριος, is the word the Greek Old Testament used for Yahweh. Jesus is included inside the confession of the one God of Israel, as the One through whom all things were made.","words":[{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]},{"v":7,"text":"However, that knowledge isn’t in all men. But some, with consciousness of the idol until now, eat as of a thing sacrificed to an idol, and their conscience, being weak, is defiled.","understanding":"But “that knowledge isn’t in all men.” Some believers, converted from paganism, had eaten idol meat all their lives as an act of worship. Their συνείδησις — conscience, moral awareness — still associated the meat with the idol. When they ate, it felt like worship, and their conscience, being ἀσθενής (weak), was μολύνω — soiled, stained. The problem was not the meat. It was what the act meant to them.","words":[{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weak"},{"strongs":"G3435","hebrew":"μολύνω","translit":"molýnō","english":"defiled"}]},{"v":8,"text":"But food will not commend us to God. For neither, if we don’t eat, are we the worse; nor, if we eat, are we the better.","understanding":"Probably another Corinthian slogan, and Paul agrees: food will not παρίστημι — present us, bring us near — to God. Eating does not make you better; abstaining does not make you worse. Food is spiritually neutral. That is exactly why it is not worth destroying a brother over.","words":[{"strongs":"G1033","hebrew":"βρῶμα","translit":"brōma","english":"food"},{"strongs":"G3936","hebrew":"παρίστημι","translit":"parístēmi","english":"commend"},{"strongs":"G4052","hebrew":"περισσεύω","translit":"perisseúō","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G5302","hebrew":"ὑστερέω","translit":"hysteréō","english":"worse"}]},{"v":9,"text":"But be careful that by no means does this liberty of yours become a stumbling block to the weak.","understanding":"The turn. “Be careful that by no means does this ἐξουσία of yours” — this right, this freedom, this authority you are proud of — “become a πρόσκομμα to the weak.” A πρόσκομμα is something you strike your foot against and stumble. Your freedom is real. It can also become a rock in your brother's path.","words":[{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"be careful"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"liberty"},{"strongs":"G4348","hebrew":"πρόσκομμα","translit":"próskomma","english":"stumbling block"},{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weak"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For if a man sees you who have knowledge sitting in an idol’s temple, won’t his conscience, if he is weak, be emboldened to eat things sacrificed to idols?","understanding":"The scene: a “strong” believer κατάκειμαι — reclining, as at a banquet — in an εἰδωλεῖον, an idol's temple. Excavations at Corinth have uncovered dining rooms at the sanctuary of Asclepius and many more at the sanctuary of Demeter and Kore on the slopes of the Acrocorinth. A weak believer walks past and sees him. And the dark irony is in the verb: the weak brother's conscience will be οἰκοδομέω — “built up,” the very word for love's work in verse 1 — built up to do what it cannot do in faith.","words":[{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G2621","hebrew":"κατάκειμαι","translit":"katákeimai","english":"sitting (reclining)"},{"strongs":"G1493","hebrew":"εἰδωλεῖον","translit":"eidōleîon","english":"idol's temple"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G3618","hebrew":"οἰκοδομέω","translit":"oikodoméō","english":"be emboldened (built up)"}]},{"v":11,"text":"And through your knowledge, he who is weak perishes, the brother for whose sake Christ died.","understanding":"Ἀπόλλυμι — he is destroyed, ruined. Not merely offended; damaged in his faith, perhaps drawn back into the idolatry he was saved from. And then the phrase that should stop every “strong” Christian in his tracks: “the brother for whose sake Christ died.” Christ thought this weak brother worth dying for. You will not give up a steak for him?","words":[{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weak"},{"strongs":"G622","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","english":"perishes"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"died"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Thus, sinning against the brothers, and wounding their conscience when it is weak, you sin against Christ.","understanding":"Three escalating verbs. You ἁμαρτάνω — sin — against your brothers. You τύπτω — strike, beat — their weak conscience; the word is used of physical blows. And so you sin “against Christ.” Wound the body, and you wound the Head. This is exactly what the risen Jesus said to Saul: “why do you persecute me?”","words":[{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sinning"},{"strongs":"G5180","hebrew":"τύπτω","translit":"týptō","english":"wounding"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weak"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Therefore if food causes my brother to stumble, I will eat no meat forever more, that I don’t cause my brother to stumble.","understanding":"Paul's personal resolution, in the strongest possible terms. If food σκανδαλίζω — trips up — my brother, I will never eat κρέας, meat, again, εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα, forever. He does not tell the strong what to do; he tells them what he will do. Love does not ask, “What am I allowed to do?” It asks, “What will help my brother?”","words":[{"strongs":"G1033","hebrew":"βρῶμα","translit":"brōma","english":"food"},{"strongs":"G4624","hebrew":"σκανδαλίζω","translit":"skandalízō","english":"causes to stumble"},{"strongs":"G2907","hebrew":"κρέας","translit":"kréas","english":"meat"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Bellows and the Builder","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a blacksmith's forge in the lower city of Corinth. In one corner, a boy is working the bellows — a leather bag that swells enormous with every pull and collapses empty with every push. It is big, it is noisy, and there is nothing in it but air. In the street outside, a mason is laying stone on stone, slowly, with mortar and patience, building a house that will stand for a hundred years. Paul says: knowledge is the bellows. Love is the mason.","meaning":"Paul is not against knowledge. He agrees with the Corinthians' theology — there is one God, idols are nothing. But knowledge on its own φυσιοῖ — it inflates you. It makes you big and empty. Love οἰκοδομεῖ — it builds others up into something solid. And verse 3 goes deeper still: the real issue is not whether you know God but whether God knows you, and the sign that He knows you is that you love Him — and, as the rest of the chapter shows, love your brother. You can win every theological argument and still be nothing but air. Being right is not the same as being loving."},{"title":"Dinner in the Temple Dining Room","verseRef":"v. 7-12","image":"On the slope below the Acrocorinth, at the sanctuary of Demeter, archaeologists have found dozens of small dining rooms with stone benches along the walls where worshippers reclined to eat sacrificial meals. Picture one of them on a feast day. A wealthy Corinthian believer, well-educated, confident in his knowledge that idols are nothing, accepts an invitation from a business associate and reclines at the table. The meat comes from the altar outside. He eats without a second thought. Through the doorway, a poorer believer — a former worshipper of Demeter who wept with relief the day he turned from idols — sees him. He stands there, confused. Then he thinks: if my brother can do this, maybe it doesn't matter. And he goes in.","meaning":"The strong man's theology was correct, and his brother was ruined by it. That is the tragedy of this chapter. Paul's word in verse 10 is bitterly ironic: the weak brother's conscience is “built up” — the very word for what love does in verse 1 — built up to sin. The strong believer's example did not strengthen his brother's faith; it pressured him to act against his conscience, which for him was a return to idolatry. And then Paul names who this brother is: the one “for whose sake Christ died.” If Jesus thought this person was worth dying for, how can you treat him as an acceptable loss for the sake of a meal? To wound his conscience is to sin against Christ Himself."},{"title":"One God, One Lord","verseRef":"v. 4-6","image":"Every morning, a devout Jewish man in Corinth would recite the Shema, the confession Israel had prayed for more than a thousand years: “Hear, Israel: Yahweh is our God. Yahweh is one.” He would say it surrounded by temples — to Apollo, to Aphrodite, to Poseidon, to the deified emperors — in a city with many “gods” and many “lords.” Now picture Paul, a Pharisee who prayed the Shema from childhood, taking that ancient sentence and opening it up: one God, the Father; one Lord, Jesus Christ. Not two gods. One God — and the Son is inside the confession.","meaning":"Verse 6 is one of the earliest and most remarkable statements of Christian faith about Jesus. Paul takes the two key words of the Shema, God and Lord, and assigns “God” to the Father and “Lord” — the Greek title used for Yahweh in Israel's Scriptures — to Jesus. And he gives Jesus the role of Creator: “through whom are all things.” This is not a later development imposed on Christianity. It is here, in a letter written barely twenty-five years after the crucifixion, stated as something the Corinthians already knew. And notice the purpose clause: “we for him” and “we live through him.” The one God is not an abstract idea. He is the source and goal of your existence, and Jesus is the one through whom you live."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Knowledge and Love (v. 1-3)","body":"The Corinthians claim knowledge; Paul answers that knowledge inflates but love builds up, and that what matters is being known by God, which shows itself in loving Him."},{"label":"One God, One Lord (v. 4-6)","body":"Paul affirms the theology of the strong: idols are nothing, and there is one God, the Father, and one Lord, Jesus Christ — the Shema confessed with the Son inside it."},{"label":"The Weak Conscience (v. 7-8)","body":"Not everyone has this knowledge; former idolaters still feel that eating idol meat is worship, and their weak conscience is defiled. Food itself neither commends nor condemns."},{"label":"Freedom That Wounds (v. 9-12)","body":"The freedom of the strong must not become a stumbling block; if a weak brother sees a strong one eating in an idol's temple, he may be drawn into sin and ruined — and to sin against such a brother is to sin against Christ."},{"label":"Paul's Resolution (v. 13)","body":"If food causes his brother to stumble, Paul will never eat meat again."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Problem of Idol Meat in a City of Temples","paragraphs":["To understand chapters 8 to 10 you have to understand how deeply idolatry was woven into ordinary life in Corinth. Most meat in the ancient world was associated with sacrifice. An animal would be brought to a temple, killed on the altar, part of it burned for the god, part given to the priests, and the rest returned to the worshipper to eat — often in dining rooms attached to the temple — or sold in the market. Archaeologists at Corinth have found dining facilities at the sanctuary of Asclepius and many small dining rooms at the sanctuary of Demeter and Kore on the slopes of the Acrocorinth. Trade guilds held their banquets in temples. Birthdays, weddings and business deals were celebrated there. To refuse every invitation that involved idol meat was to withdraw from much of social and economic life.","So the Corinthians had written to Paul with a question, and it seems clear that a confident group in the church — the “strong,” as they are usually called — had already decided the answer. Their reasoning went like this: “We all have knowledge” (v. 1). We know that idols are nothing, because there is only one God (v. 4). Food is spiritually neutral; it does not bring us closer to God or push us away (v. 8). Therefore we are free to eat idol meat, even in a temple dining room. Paul agrees with every one of their premises. He says yes, there is one God; yes, idols are nothing; yes, food is neutral. But he refuses their conclusion. Because their whole argument has left out one thing: the brother.","Notice how Paul begins. Before he answers the question about meat, he addresses the question about knowledge. “Knowledge puffs up, but love builds up” (v. 1). The strong were proud of their theological understanding, and Paul does not dispute that it was correct. He disputes that correctness is enough. Knowledge, on its own, φυσιόω — it inflates, like a bellows filled with air. Love οἰκοδομέω — it builds, like a mason laying stone. And then verse 3 turns the whole matter around: the decisive thing is not that you know God but that you are known by Him, and the evidence of that is love. A Christian who knows everything and loves nobody has not understood the first thing about God."]},{"heading":"The Shema With the Son Inside It","paragraphs":["Verse 6 deserves a careful look, because it is one of the most important theological sentences Paul ever wrote, and he slips it into a discussion about meat. “Yet to us there is one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we for him; and one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom are all things, and we live through him.” Every Jewish reader would have recognised the background immediately. It is the Shema, Deuteronomy 6:4, the central confession of Israel's faith, recited twice daily by devout Jews: “Hear, Israel: Yahweh is our God. Yahweh is one.” In the Greek Old Testament, the divine name Yahweh was rendered κύριος, Lord. So the Shema in Greek reads: “The Lord our God, the Lord is one.”","What Paul does is extraordinary. He keeps the Shema's oneness — “one God... one Lord” — and he assigns its two key terms to two persons: “God” to the Father, and “Lord” to Jesus Christ. And he gives Jesus the role of Creator, “through whom are all things,” which in Jewish thought belonged to God alone. This is not polytheism; Paul has just insisted that there is no God but one. It is the Christian confession that the one God of Israel has revealed Himself as Father and Son, and that Jesus is included in the divine identity. John says the same in his Gospel: “All things were made through him. Without him was not anything made that has been made” (John 1:3). And Paul says it again to the Colossians: “all things have been created through him, and for him” (Colossians 1:16).","Why does Paul put this here, in a chapter about food? Because it is the foundation of everything he is about to say. The strong were right: there is one God, and the idols are nothing. But the One God they confessed includes the Lord Jesus Christ, who died for the weak brother (v. 11). The same confession that frees you from idols binds you to your brother. You cannot use the doctrine of Christ's lordship as a reason to ignore the people for whom He died. And the purpose clauses in verse 6 point the same way: “we for him” and “we live through him.” Your freedom is not for you. It is for Him, and His concern is for the weak."]},{"heading":"When Your Freedom Becomes Your Brother's Stumbling Block","paragraphs":["Verses 7 to 12 introduce the weak brother, and it is essential to understand who he is. He is not a legalist trying to impose rules on others. He is a former idol-worshipper whose conscience still associates idol meat with the worship of the idol (v. 7). For him, eating it is not neutral; it feels like going back to the god he renounced. His conscience is “weak” in the sense that it is not yet fully informed by the truth that idols are nothing. But it is still his conscience, and to act against it is sin for him (compare Romans 14:23, “whatever is not of faith is sin”). The problem is not that he is easily offended in the modern sense of being annoyed. The problem is that he might be drawn into doing something he believes is idolatry.","Now picture what happens. The strong believer, confident in his knowledge, reclines at a meal in a temple dining room (v. 10). The weak believer sees him. And his conscience is “emboldened” — the Greek word is οἰκοδομέω, literally “built up” — the very word Paul used for love's work in verse 1. This is bitter irony: the strong believer's freedom does “build up” his brother, but it builds him up to sin. He eats against his conscience, and he is “destroyed” — ἀπόλλυμι, the word used of perishing in 1:18. Whether Paul means final ruin or serious spiritual damage, the stakes are high. And then he names who this weak person is: “the brother for whose sake Christ died” (v. 11).","That phrase changes everything. Christ valued this weak, confused, under-informed believer so highly that He died for him. And the strong believer is willing to risk his ruin for the sake of a meal. Verse 12 makes the consequences explicit: “Thus, sinning against the brothers, and wounding their conscience when it is weak, you sin against Christ.” The word τύπτω means to strike, to beat — it is used of physical blows in the Gospels. To wound a brother's conscience is to strike Christ. That is because the brother is a member of His body, as the risen Lord told Saul: “why do you persecute me?” And so Paul ends not with a rule for others but with a resolution for himself: “if food causes my brother to stumble, I will eat no meat forever more” (v. 13). Love does not ask, “What am I allowed to do?” It asks, “What will build up my brother?” Chapter 9 will show that this was not a hypothetical for Paul; it was the pattern of his whole life."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["Paul applies the same principle in Romans, written a few years later from Corinth: “Therefore let’s not judge one another any more, but judge this rather, that no man put a stumbling block in his brother’s way, or an occasion for falling” (Romans 14:13). And again: “Yet if because of food your brother is grieved, you walk no longer in love. Don’t destroy with your food him for whom Christ died” (Romans 14:15). And he states the conclusion plainly: “It is good to not eat meat, drink wine, nor do anything by which your brother stumbles, is offended, or is made weak” (Romans 14:21). In Romans 15 he grounds it in the example of Jesus: “Now we who are strong ought to bear the weaknesses of the weak, and not to please ourselves... For even Christ didn’t please himself” (Romans 15:1, 3).","The identification of a brother with Christ in verse 12 comes straight from the words of Jesus. On the Damascus road, the risen Lord did not ask Saul, “Why do you persecute my followers?” He asked, “Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?” — and when Saul asked who He was, the answer came: “I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting” (Acts 9:4–5). Paul never forgot it. What you do to the members of Christ's body, you do to Christ. Jesus taught the same in His parable of the judgment: “Most certainly I tell you, because you did it to one of the least of these my brothers, you did it to me” (Matthew 25:40). And He gave a terrifying warning about causing believers to stumble: “whoever causes one of these little ones who believe in me to stumble, it would be better for him that a huge millstone should be hung around his neck, and that he should be sunk in the depths of the sea” (Matthew 18:6).","The issue of idol meat itself appears elsewhere in the New Testament. The Jerusalem council asked Gentile believers to “abstain from things sacrificed to idols” (Acts 15:29), largely for the sake of fellowship with Jewish believers and to avoid any appearance of idolatry. And the risen Christ rebuked the church in Pergamum because some there held “the teaching of Balaam, who taught Balak to throw a stumbling block before the children of Israel, to eat things sacrificed to idols, and to commit sexual immorality” (Revelation 2:14). Paul's careful distinction will become clearer in chapter 10: meat sold in the market can be eaten with thanksgiving, but participating in a temple meal is participating in idolatry. In chapter 8 he does not yet make that argument. He makes a more fundamental one. Even if you are right, love may require you to give up your rights."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Knowledge","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","strongs":"G1108","verses":"v. 1 (×2), 7, 10, 11","insight":"Strong's: “knowing (the act), i.e. (by implication) knowledge.” Γνῶσις was one of the Corinthians' favourite words, and Paul uses it ten times in this letter — more than in any other. He thanked God that they were enriched in all knowledge (1:5), and he will say that knowledge without love is nothing (13:2) and that knowledge will pass away (13:8). In this chapter he contrasts it with love. Later Christian movements known as Gnostics, from this word, claimed secret spiritual knowledge that set them above ordinary believers. The seeds of that attitude are visible in Corinth. Paul's answer is not anti-intellectualism; it is love. Knowledge is a tool. Love is the builder who uses it."},{"english":"Build up / edify","hebrew":"οἰκοδομέω","translit":"oikodoméō","strongs":"G3618","verses":"v. 1, 10","insight":"Strong's: “to be a house-builder, i.e. construct or (figuratively) confirm.” The word combines οἶκος, house, and δέμω, to build. Paul uses it as the great test for Christian behaviour: does it build up? It appears in 8:1, 10:23 and repeatedly in chapter 14, where he tells the Corinthians to seek gifts that build up the church. In verse 1 love builds up. In verse 10, with dark irony, the strong believer's example “builds up” the weak brother's conscience — to sin. The same word can describe construction or destruction, depending on what is being built. The question is never only whether you are influencing others, but what you are building in them."},{"english":"Conscience","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","strongs":"G4893","verses":"v. 7, 10, 12","insight":"Strong's: “co-perception, i.e. moral consciousness.” The word means literally “knowing with” — the inner witness that knows along with you what you are doing and judges whether it is right. Paul uses it more than any other New Testament writer, and three times in this chapter. A conscience can be “weak” — not fully informed by truth — and still be binding on the person who has it, because to act against it is to act against what you believe is right. Paul's teaching is twofold: inform your own conscience by the truth, and do not pressure another person to violate theirs. A conscience is not infallible, but it is precious. To wound it is to strike Christ."},{"english":"Stumbling block","hebrew":"πρόσκομμα","translit":"próskomma","strongs":"G4348 (verb, cause to stumble σκανδαλίζω, skandalízō, G4624)","verses":"v. 9 (verb σκανδαλίζω in v. 13 ×2)","insight":"Strong's defines πρόσκομμα as “a stub, i.e. (figuratively) occasion of apostasy” — something you strike your foot against — and σκανδαλίζω as “to entrap, i.e. trip up.” Both words describe causing someone to fall. Paul uses the noun in Romans 14:13, 20 and the verb here. The important point is that the stumbling in view is not mere annoyance. It is falling into sin — being drawn into doing something one believes to be wrong, or being led back into a former way of life. To be a stumbling block is not to offend someone's preferences. It is to put an obstacle in their path to Christ."},{"english":"Perish / be destroyed","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","strongs":"G622","verses":"v. 11","insight":"Strong's: “to destroy fully (reflexively, to perish, or lose), literally or figuratively.” This is a strong word. It is the same word John 3:16 uses of those who “perish,” and the word Paul used in 1:18 of “those who are dying.” Some interpreters think Paul means final spiritual ruin; others think he means serious damage to the brother's faith and Christian walk. Either way, he is not describing hurt feelings. He is describing the potential destruction of a person's spiritual life. And he places it in the starkest possible contrast with the cross: this brother is “the brother for whose sake Christ died.” Christ died to save him from perishing. You are risking his perishing for a meal."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the pride hidden in your correct theology, the freedoms you exercise without thinking about who is watching, and the people you have quietly decided are not worth adjusting your life for.","items":[{"title":"Right and Inflated","body":"You may be right. About doctrine, about practice, about the freedom you have in Christ, about the weaknesses of other believers. The Spirit is not asking you to stop being right. He is asking what your rightness is building. Is it building others up — or is it inflating you? Do you use your knowledge to serve the weak or to look down on them? When someone is less informed than you, do you feel compassion or contempt? Paul's test is stark: “if anyone thinks that he knows anything, he doesn’t yet know as he ought to know.” The person who has truly understood God loves. If your knowledge has made you harder, colder or more superior, it has made you less like Christ, not more."},{"title":"Who Is Watching Through the Doorway?","body":"There is someone watching your life who is younger in the faith, more fragile, still fighting battles you have won. They watch what you drink, what you watch, what you joke about, where you go, how you spend your money, what you post. You may be completely free in Christ to do those things. But what do they conclude when they see you? The Spirit is asking you to look through the doorway and see the person standing there. Your freedom is real, and it is precious. But it was given to you for love, not for self-display. What freedom are you exercising that might be a stumbling block to someone else? Are you willing to give it up for their sake?"},{"title":"The Brother for Whom Christ Died","body":"There is a believer you find tiresome. Weak, scrupulous, sensitive, immature, behind you in understanding. You have quietly decided they are not worth adjusting your life for. The Spirit wants you to see them with the four words of verse 11: “for whose sake Christ died.” Jesus did not consider this person an acceptable loss. He thought them worth His blood. So what is your steak, your glass of wine, your opinion, your freedom, your preference, compared to their soul? Paul said he would give up meat forever rather than cause his brother to stumble. What are you willing to give up?"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Bellows Check","body":"Think of the last theological disagreement you had with another believer. Write down honestly: was I more concerned to be right or to build them up? Did I leave them strengthened in Christ or simply defeated in argument? Did my knowledge make me more loving or more proud?"},{"title":"The Doorway Check","body":"List three freedoms you exercise that some believers would be uncomfortable with — drinks, entertainment, activities, language, spending. For each one, ask: is there anyone in my life who might be drawn into sin, against their conscience, by watching me do this? If so, write their name beside it."},{"title":"The Weak-Brother Check","body":"Name one believer you tend to dismiss as weak, immature or overly sensitive. Write beside their name: “the brother (or sister) for whose sake Christ died.” Then ask: how have I treated them? What would love do for them this week?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Laying Down a Right","body":"Choose one freedom from your Doorway Check that might be causing someone to stumble. For the next thirty days, voluntarily give it up — not because it is wrong in itself, but as an act of love for the weaker brother or sister you named. Each time you feel the loss, pray for that person by name. At the end of the thirty days, ask God whether this should become a permanent change. Write down what you learn about love from the experience."},{"title":"Building Instead of Inflating","body":"This week, find one believer who is less mature or less informed than you in some area. Instead of correcting them, invest in building them up. Ask them about their walk with God. Listen. Share something God has taught you in your own weakness. Pray with them. Encourage something genuine you see in their faith. Do not mention the area where you think they are weak. Let love do the building, and see what God does."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I confess that I have been puffed up with knowledge. I have been proud of being right, and I have used my understanding to look down on others instead of building them up. Forgive me. Teach me that if I think I know anything, I do not yet know as I ought to know. And thank You that what matters most is not that I know You, but that I am known by You. Let the evidence of that be love. Lord God, You are the one God, the Father, from whom are all things, and I exist for You. Lord Jesus Christ, You are the one Lord, through whom all things were made, and I live through You. Every idol in my life is nothing; I renounce them all. But Lord, do not let my freedom from idols make me careless of my brothers. Show me who is watching me through the doorway. Show me where my liberty has become a stumbling block to someone weaker. Give me the love to lay down my rights for their sake, as You laid down Your life for mine. Help me to see every weak, confused, struggling believer as the brother or sister for whom Christ died. Forgive me for every time I have wounded someone's conscience and so wounded You. And make me like Paul — willing to give up anything, forever, rather than cause one of Your little ones to stumble. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Daniel's Resolve at the King's Table","ref":"Daniel 1:1-21","relatedVerses":"v. 1, 7-8","summary":"When Daniel and his friends were taken to Babylon, they were enrolled in the king's training programme and assigned a daily portion of the king's food and wine — food that had almost certainly been offered to Babylon's gods or that did not meet the requirements of God's law. Daniel resolved not to defile himself. He did not stage a protest or condemn the Babylonians. He asked the official politely for permission to eat vegetables and water for ten days, and trusted God with the result. At the end of the test, Daniel and his friends looked healthier than all the others, and God gave them knowledge and wisdom beyond everyone in the kingdom. Daniel shows that faithfulness in matters of food and idolatry can be quiet, humble and wise, and that God honours a conscience kept clean before Him."},{"title":"David Pours Out the Water From Bethlehem","ref":"2 Samuel 23:13-17","relatedVerses":"v. 13","summary":"During a war with the Philistines, David was hiding in a cave while an enemy garrison occupied his hometown of Bethlehem. He said, almost wistfully, that he wished someone would bring him a drink from the well by Bethlehem's gate. Three of his mighty men broke through the Philistine lines, drew water from the well and brought it back to him. But David would not drink it. He poured it out on the ground before the LORD, saying that it was the blood of the men who had risked their lives for it. David had every right to drink that water. It was a gift, freely given. But when he understood what it had cost, he chose to give it up as an offering. That is the spirit of 1 Corinthians 8:13: some things you are free to enjoy become impossible to enjoy once you see the cost to others."},{"title":"Paul Circumcises Timothy","ref":"Acts 16:1-5","relatedVerses":"v. 9, 13","summary":"Paul was the great apostle of freedom from the law, who would write to the Galatians that circumcision is nothing. And yet, when he met a young disciple named Timothy in Lystra, whose mother was Jewish and whose father was Greek, Paul circumcised him before taking him on his missionary journey. Why? Luke says it was because of the Jews who lived in those areas, who all knew that Timothy's father was a Greek. Paul did not believe circumcision was necessary for salvation; he fought fiercely against that idea. But he was willing to lay aside his freedom so that Timothy's ministry would not be a stumbling block to the very Jews they were trying to reach. Paul practised what he preached in 1 Corinthians 8 and 9: freedom used for love, not for self."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":9,"chapterTitle":"The Rights I Refused — All Things to All Men, and a Crown That Never Withers","themeTitle":"“The Freest Man in the Room Made Himself Everyone’s Servant”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Am I not free? Am I not an apostle? Haven’t I seen Jesus Christ, our Lord? Aren’t you my work in the Lord?","understanding":"Four questions, each expecting “Yes.” Am I not ἐλεύθερος — free? Am I not an apostle? Have I not ὁράω — seen — Jesus our Lord? (An apostle had to be a witness of the risen Christ; Paul saw Him on the Damascus road, 15:8.) Are you not my ἔργον, my work, in the Lord? Chapter 9 is not a change of subject from chapter 8. Paul has just said he would give up meat forever for his brother. Now he shows that he has already given up far more: rights he unquestionably had.","words":[{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"free"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"}]},{"v":2,"text":"If to others I am not an apostle, yet at least I am to you; for you are the seal of my apostleship in the Lord.","understanding":"Some outside Corinth questioned Paul's apostleship. But the Corinthians themselves are his σφραγίς — his seal, the wax impression that authenticates a document. A church that exists because of his preaching is the proof that his commission was real. You do not need a certificate when the building is standing in front of you.","words":[{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seal"},{"strongs":"G651","hebrew":"ἀποστολή","translit":"apostolḗ","english":"apostleship"}]},{"v":3,"text":"My defense to those who examine me is this.","understanding":"Ἀπολογία — a formal defence in court, the root of our word apologetics. And those who ἀνακρίνω — examine, cross-examine — him are the critics in Corinth. Ironically, the defence he is about to give is not a defence of his rights. It is a defence of his decision not to use them.","words":[{"strongs":"G627","hebrew":"ἀπολογία","translit":"apología","english":"defense"},{"strongs":"G350","hebrew":"ἀνακρίνω","translit":"anakrínō","english":"examine"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Have we no right to eat and to drink?","understanding":"Ἐξουσία — the same word as “liberty” in 8:9. Paul is deliberately using the vocabulary of the strong. Have we no right to eat and drink at the church's expense? Of course we do. The strong in Corinth were insisting on their ἐξουσία to eat idol meat. Paul is about to show them a man who had real rights and laid them down.","words":[{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G2068","hebrew":"ἐσθίω","translit":"esthíō","english":"eat"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Have we no right to take along a wife who is a believer, even as the rest of the apostles, and the brothers of the Lord, and Cephas?","understanding":"Ἀδελφὴν γυναῖκα — a sister as a wife, a believing wife. The other apostles, the Lord's brothers (James, Jude and the others named in Mark 6:3), and Cephas all travelled with their wives and were supported by the churches. This verse tells us Peter was married and that his wife shared his ministry — a detail confirmed by Jesus healing Peter's mother-in-law (Mark 1:30).","words":[{"strongs":"G4013","hebrew":"περιάγω","translit":"periágō","english":"take along"},{"strongs":"G79","hebrew":"ἀδελφή","translit":"adelphḗ","english":"a believer (sister)"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wife"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Or have only Barnabas and I no right to not work?","understanding":"Barnabas, like Paul, supported himself by working. This is a quiet tribute to Barnabas, Paul's first partner (Acts 13), and it tells us that despite their later disagreement (Acts 15:39), Paul still held him in honour. Neither man was obliged to work; both chose to.","words":[{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G2038","hebrew":"ἐργάζομαι","translit":"ergázomai","english":"work"}]},{"v":7,"text":"What soldier ever serves at his own expense? Who plants a vineyard, and doesn’t eat of its fruit? Or who feeds a flock, and doesn’t drink from the flock’s milk?","understanding":"Three pictures from ordinary life. No soldier στρατεύομαι — serves in a campaign — paying his own ὀψώνια, rations and wages. No farmer plants an ἀμπελών, a vineyard, and refuses to eat the grapes. No shepherd ποιμαίνω — tends — a ποίμνη, a flock, without drinking its milk. Soldier, farmer, shepherd: all are standard New Testament pictures of ministry. And all of them live from their work.","words":[{"strongs":"G4754","hebrew":"στρατεύομαι","translit":"strateúomai","english":"serves"},{"strongs":"G3800","hebrew":"ὀψώνιον","translit":"opsṓnion","english":"expense"},{"strongs":"G290","hebrew":"ἀμπελών","translit":"ampelṓn","english":"vineyard"},{"strongs":"G4165","hebrew":"ποιμαίνω","translit":"poimaínō","english":"feeds"},{"strongs":"G4167","hebrew":"ποίμνη","translit":"poímnē","english":"flock"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Do I speak these things according to the ways of men? Or doesn’t the law also say the same thing?","understanding":"Paul anticipates the objection: these are merely human examples. So he turns to Scripture. “Doesn’t the law also say the same thing?”","words":[{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"}]},{"v":9,"text":"For it is written in the law of Moses, “You shall not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain.” Is it for the oxen that God cares,","understanding":"He quotes Deuteronomy 25:4. An ox walking in circles on the threshing floor, trampling the grain, was not to be φιμόω — muzzled — so that it could eat as it worked. Then Paul asks: “Is it for the oxen that God cares?” Of course God cares for oxen (Jonah 4:11 ends with God's concern for the cattle of Nineveh). But Paul's point is that the law's deeper purpose is about people. If God provides for the ox, how much more for the worker in His field.","words":[{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G5392","hebrew":"φιμόω","translit":"phimóō","english":"muzzle"},{"strongs":"G1016","hebrew":"βοῦς","translit":"boûs","english":"ox"},{"strongs":"G248","hebrew":"ἀλοάω","translit":"aloáō","english":"treads out the grain"},{"strongs":"G3199","hebrew":"μέλω","translit":"mélō","english":"cares"}]},{"v":10,"text":"or does he say it assuredly for our sake? Yes, it was written for our sake, because he who plows ought to plow in hope, and he who threshes in hope should partake of his hope.","understanding":"“Yes, it was written for our sake.” The ploughman ἀροτριάω — ploughs — in ἐλπίς, hope, of a harvest. The thresher threshes in hope of sharing the grain. Every kind of labour is sustained by the expectation of sharing its fruit. Paul reads Scripture as written for the church (compare Romans 15:4).","words":[{"strongs":"G722","hebrew":"ἀροτριόω","translit":"arotrióō","english":"plows"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G248","hebrew":"ἀλοάω","translit":"aloáō","english":"threshes"}]},{"v":11,"text":"If we sowed to you spiritual things, is it a great thing if we reap your fleshly things?","understanding":"Σπείρω and θερίζω — sowing and reaping. If we sowed πνευματικά, spiritual things — the gospel, the Spirit, eternal life — is it a big thing if we reap σαρκικά, material things — food, shelter, support? The comparison is deliberately lopsided. What Paul gave them is infinitely more valuable than anything they could give him.","words":[{"strongs":"G4687","hebrew":"σπείρω","translit":"speírō","english":"sowed"},{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual things"},{"strongs":"G2325","hebrew":"θερίζω","translit":"therízō","english":"reap"},{"strongs":"G4559","hebrew":"σαρκικός","translit":"sarkikós","english":"fleshly things"}]},{"v":12,"text":"If others partake of this right over you, don’t we yet more? Nevertheless we did not use this right, but we bear all things, that we may cause no hindrance to the Good News of Christ.","understanding":"Others — perhaps Apollos or Peter — had received support from Corinth. Paul had a greater claim. “Nevertheless we did not use this right.” Instead we στέγω — the word means to cover with a roof, and so to bear, to endure, to put up with — all things, so as not to put any ἐγκοπή in the way of the gospel. Ἐγκοπή was a cut in a road, a trench dug to block an advancing army. Paul would rather go hungry than dig a ditch across the gospel's path.","words":[{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G4722","hebrew":"στέγω","translit":"stégō","english":"bear"},{"strongs":"G1464","hebrew":"ἐγκοπή","translit":"enkopḗ","english":"hindrance"},{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Don’t you know that those who serve around sacred things eat from the things of the temple, and those who wait on the altar have their portion with the altar?","understanding":"“Don’t you know?” Those who serve in the temple eat from the ἱερά, the sacred things; those who προσεδρεύω — attend constantly — at the θυσιαστήριον, the altar, συμμερίζομαι, share in its offerings. This was true in Israel's temple, where priests and Levites lived from the sacrifices (Numbers 18), and it was common knowledge in pagan Corinth too.","words":[{"strongs":"G2413","hebrew":"ἱερός","translit":"hierós","english":"sacred things"},{"strongs":"G4332","hebrew":"προσεδρεύω","translit":"prosedreúō","english":"wait on"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"},{"strongs":"G4829","hebrew":"συμμερίζομαι","translit":"symmerízomai","english":"have their portion"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Even so the Lord ordained that those who proclaim the Good News should live from the Good News.","understanding":"The climax of the argument: “the Lord ordained.” Διατάσσω — gave orders, commanded. Paul is citing Jesus' own instruction when He sent out His disciples: “the laborer is worthy of his wages” (Luke 10:7). Those who καταγγέλλω — proclaim — the gospel should live from the gospel. It is not merely permitted. It is the Lord's arrangement.","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1299","hebrew":"διατάσσω","translit":"diatássō","english":"ordained"},{"strongs":"G2605","hebrew":"καταγγέλλω","translit":"katangéllō","english":"proclaim"},{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"}]},{"v":15,"text":"But I have used none of these things, and I don’t write these things that it may be done so in my case; for I would rather die, than that anyone should make my boasting void.","understanding":"Having proved his right beyond doubt, Paul says he has used none of it — and he is not writing to ask for it now. Then a broken, passionate sentence: he would rather die than let anyone κενόω — empty, make void — his καύχημα, his ground for boasting. What is the boast? Not that he preaches; verse 16 says that is no ground for boasting at all. It is that he preaches free.","words":[{"strongs":"G5530","hebrew":"χράομαι","translit":"chráomai","english":"used"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"die"},{"strongs":"G2745","hebrew":"καύχημα","translit":"kaúchēma","english":"boasting"},{"strongs":"G2758","hebrew":"κενόω","translit":"kenóō","english":"make void"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For if I preach the Good News, I have nothing to boast about; for necessity is laid on me; but woe is to me, if I don’t preach the Good News.","understanding":"Preaching itself is no ground for boasting, because ἀνάγκη — necessity, compulsion — is laid on him. Paul did not choose this vocation; it chose him on the Damascus road. “Woe is to me, if I don’t preach the Good News.” Οὐαί is the cry of the prophets — Isaiah's “Woe is me!” in the presence of God's holiness (Isaiah 6:5). Paul has been seized by a commission he cannot put down.","words":[{"strongs":"G2097","hebrew":"εὐαγγελίζω","translit":"euangelízō","english":"preach"},{"strongs":"G2745","hebrew":"καύχημα","translit":"kaúchēma","english":"boast"},{"strongs":"G318","hebrew":"ἀνάγκη","translit":"anánkē","english":"necessity"},{"strongs":"G3759","hebrew":"οὐαί","translit":"ouaí","english":"woe"}]},{"v":17,"text":"For if I do this of my own will, I have a reward. But if not of my own will, I have a stewardship entrusted to me.","understanding":"A difficult verse. If he preached ἑκών — voluntarily, of his own initiative — he would have a μισθός, a wage. But he does not: he is under orders. He has been entrusted with an οἰκονομία — a stewardship, the same family as the “stewards” of 4:1–2. A slave-steward does not get paid for doing his duty. So where is Paul's reward?","words":[{"strongs":"G1635","hebrew":"ἑκών","translit":"hekṓn","english":"of my own will"},{"strongs":"G3408","hebrew":"μισθός","translit":"misthós","english":"reward"},{"strongs":"G3622","hebrew":"οἰκονομία","translit":"oikonomía","english":"stewardship"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"entrusted"}]},{"v":18,"text":"What then is my reward? That, when I preach the Good News, I may present the Good News of Christ without charge, so as not to abuse my authority in the Good News.","understanding":"Here is the answer, and it is beautiful. Paul's “reward” is the privilege of offering the gospel ἀδάπανος — without cost to anyone. His pay is to work for no pay. That is the one thing in his ministry that is truly his own gift to God, beyond the call of duty. And it keeps him from καταχράομαι — over-using, abusing — his ἐξουσία, his right in the gospel.","words":[{"strongs":"G3408","hebrew":"μισθός","translit":"misthós","english":"reward"},{"strongs":"G77","hebrew":"ἀδάπανος","translit":"adápanos","english":"without charge"},{"strongs":"G2710","hebrew":"καταχράομαι","translit":"katachráomai","english":"abuse"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"}]},{"v":19,"text":"For though I was free from all, I brought myself under bondage to all, that I might gain the more.","understanding":"The paradox of Christian freedom in one sentence. Ἐλεύθερος ἐκ πάντων — free from all — yet πᾶσιν ἐμαυτὸν ἐδούλωσα — I enslaved myself to all. The freest man in the room made himself everyone's slave. Why? “That I might κερδαίνω — gain, win — the more.” Freedom is not the right to please yourself. It is the power to serve without being forced.","words":[{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"free"},{"strongs":"G1402","hebrew":"δουλόω","translit":"doulóō","english":"brought under bondage"},{"strongs":"G2770","hebrew":"κερδαίνω","translit":"kerdaínō","english":"gain"}]},{"v":20,"text":"To the Jews I became as a Jew, that I might gain Jews; to those who are under the law, as under the law, that I might gain those who are under the law;","understanding":"To Jews he became as a Jew — keeping Jewish customs, circumcising Timothy (Acts 16:3), taking a Nazirite-type vow in Jerusalem (Acts 21:23–26). To those under the law, as under the law — ‘not being myself under the law,’ as many early manuscripts add here. Paul's identity was not shaped by the law. But he would adopt its customs to remove obstacles for those who were.","words":[{"strongs":"G2453","hebrew":"Ἰουδαῖος","translit":"Ioudaîos","english":"Jew"},{"strongs":"G2770","hebrew":"κερδαίνω","translit":"kerdaínō","english":"gain"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"}]},{"v":21,"text":"to those who are without law, as without law (not being without law toward God, but under law toward Christ), that I might win those who are without law.","understanding":"To those without the law — Gentiles — as one without the law. Then a crucial parenthesis that guards against misunderstanding: “not being without law toward God, but ἔννομος Χριστοῦ” — under the law of Christ, literally “in-lawed to Christ.” Paul is never lawless. He is bound to Christ and His command of love. Flexibility in culture; no flexibility in obedience.","words":[{"strongs":"G459","hebrew":"ἄνομος","translit":"ánomos","english":"without law"},{"strongs":"G1772","hebrew":"ἔννομος","translit":"énnomos","english":"under law"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G2770","hebrew":"κερδαίνω","translit":"kerdaínō","english":"win"}]},{"v":22,"text":"To the weak I became as weak, that I might gain the weak. I have become all things to all men, that I may by all means save some.","understanding":"The climax of the list, and it connects straight back to chapter 8: “To the weak I became as weak.” Paul did for the weak brother exactly what he asked the strong to do — he limited his own freedom. Then the famous summary: “I have become all things to all men, that I may by all means save some.” Not all men — some. Paul never imagined everyone would respond. But for the sake of some, he would adapt everything except the gospel itself.","words":[{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weak"},{"strongs":"G2770","hebrew":"κερδαίνω","translit":"kerdaínō","english":"gain"},{"strongs":"G3956","hebrew":"πᾶς","translit":"pâs","english":"all things"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"save"}]},{"v":23,"text":"Now I do this for the sake of the Good News, that I may be a joint partaker of it.","understanding":"The motive behind all of it: “for the sake of the Good News, that I may be a συγκοινωνός of it” — a co-sharer, a fellow participant. Paul wants to share in the blessings of the gospel alongside those he reaches. He is not standing outside the gospel distributing it. He is inside it, running with everyone else.","words":[{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"},{"strongs":"G4791","hebrew":"συγκοινωνός","translit":"synkoinōnós","english":"joint partaker"}]},{"v":24,"text":"Don’t you know that those who run in a race all run, but one receives the prize? Run like that, that you may win.","understanding":"Now an image every Corinthian knew. The Isthmian Games, second only to the Olympics, were held every two years a few miles from the city. Paul may even have been in Corinth during the games of AD 51. In the στάδιον — the stadium, the race course, about 600 feet long — all run, but only one receives the βραβεῖον, the prize. “Run like that” — not lazily, not as a spectator, but to καταλαμβάνω, to seize the prize.","words":[{"strongs":"G4712","hebrew":"στάδιον","translit":"stádion","english":"race"},{"strongs":"G5143","hebrew":"τρέχω","translit":"tréchō","english":"run"},{"strongs":"G1017","hebrew":"βραβεῖον","translit":"brabeîon","english":"prize"},{"strongs":"G2638","hebrew":"καταλαμβάνω","translit":"katalambánō","english":"win"}]},{"v":25,"text":"Every man who strives in the games exercises self-control in all things. Now they do it to receive a corruptible crown, but we an incorruptible.","understanding":"Every athlete who ἀγωνίζομαι — competes, from which comes our word agony — ἐγκρατεύομαι, exercises self-control in everything: diet, sleep, training, pleasure. Athletes at the major games swore that they had trained for ten months. And for what? A φθαρτός στέφανος — a perishable wreath. At the Isthmian Games the victor's crown was a wreath of pine, or in some periods of withered celery. It would be brown within a week. “But we an ἄφθαρτος” — an incorruptible one.","words":[{"strongs":"G75","hebrew":"ἀγωνίζομαι","translit":"agōnízomai","english":"strives"},{"strongs":"G1467","hebrew":"ἐγκρατεύομαι","translit":"enkrateúomai","english":"exercises self-control"},{"strongs":"G5349","hebrew":"φθαρτός","translit":"phthartós","english":"corruptible"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crown"},{"strongs":"G862","hebrew":"ἄφθαρτος","translit":"áphthartos","english":"incorruptible"}]},{"v":26,"text":"I therefore run like that, not aimlessly. I fight like that, not beating the air,","understanding":"Two athletic pictures. A runner who does not run ἀδήλως — aimlessly, without a clear finish line in view. A boxer, πυκτεύω, who does not throw punches at the ἀήρ, the air — shadowboxing, swinging wildly without landing a blow. Paul's life is not a show workout. Every stride is aimed; every blow lands.","words":[{"strongs":"G5143","hebrew":"τρέχω","translit":"tréchō","english":"run"},{"strongs":"G84","hebrew":"ἀδήλως","translit":"adḗlōs","english":"aimlessly"},{"strongs":"G4438","hebrew":"πυκτέω","translit":"pyktéō","english":"fight (box)"},{"strongs":"G109","hebrew":"ἀήρ","translit":"aḗr","english":"air"}]},{"v":27,"text":"but I beat my body and bring it into submission, lest by any means, after I have preached to others, I myself should be rejected.","understanding":"Ὑπωπιάζω — to strike under the eye, to give a black eye; a boxer's word. Paul punches his own body and δουλαγωγέω — leads it as a slave, brings it into submission. He is not despising the body (he has just said it is the Lord's temple, 6:19). He is refusing to let it rule him. And the reason is sobering: “lest… after I have κηρύσσω — preached, heralded — to others, I myself should be ἀδόκιμος” — disqualified, failing the test. The herald who announced the rules of the games could himself be disqualified if he broke them.","words":[{"strongs":"G5299","hebrew":"ὑπωπιάζω","translit":"hypōpiázō","english":"beat"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G1396","hebrew":"δουλαγωγέω","translit":"doulagōgéō","english":"bring into submission"},{"strongs":"G2784","hebrew":"κηρύσσω","translit":"kērýssō","english":"preached"},{"strongs":"G96","hebrew":"ἀδόκιμος","translit":"adókimos","english":"rejected"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Ox on the Threshing Floor","verseRef":"v. 9-11","image":"Picture a threshing floor outside a village in Israel: a circle of hard-packed earth on a hilltop where the wind blows. Sheaves of wheat are spread across it, and an ox walks round and round over them, its hooves breaking the heads of grain loose from the stalks. All day it walks, trampling the harvest. And every so often it lowers its head and takes a mouthful. No one stops it. The law of Moses said, “You shall not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain.” The animal that does the work shares in what it produces.","meaning":"Paul uses this small law to make a large point: those who labour in the gospel have a right to be supported by those they serve. Jesus said it directly — “the laborer is worthy of his wages” (Luke 10:7) — and Paul calls it something “the Lord ordained” (v. 14). Generous support of those who teach the Word is not charity; it is obedience. But notice the deeper turn in the chapter. Paul proves the right beyond doubt, and then refuses to use it. He would rather go hungry than let anyone say the gospel came with a price tag. Both lessons stand. If you are taught, support those who teach you (Galatians 6:6). If you teach, hold your rights loosely for the sake of the gospel."},{"title":"The Trench Across the Road","verseRef":"v. 12","image":"In ancient warfare, when a defending army wanted to slow an advancing enemy, it would dig a deep trench across the road — an ἐγκοπή, a cutting-in. The marching column would grind to a halt at the edge, unable to move forward until the ditch was filled. Paul uses that word in verse 12. He would rather “bear all things” — hunger, weariness, insult, poverty — than dig a trench across the path of the gospel. Picture him at night in his rented room above the tentmaker's shop, hands blistered from the needle and awl, refusing the money that would have made life easier, because he knew someone in Corinth would use it to call him a fraud.","meaning":"Corinth was full of travelling teachers who charged fees and lived well off their audiences. Paul refused to look like one of them. He was willing to give up his legitimate rights — food, a wife's companionship on his travels, financial support — so that nothing about him would become an obstacle to someone hearing the gospel. That is the question this verse asks of you: what in your life might be digging a trench across someone else's road to Christ? Your rights, your comforts, your reputation, your preferences, even good things you are entitled to — are any of them hindering the gospel? Love asks not, “What am I owed?” but, “What is in the way?”"},{"title":"The Withered Wreath","verseRef":"v. 24-27","image":"It is the spring of the Isthmian Games, a few miles from Corinth. The crowds pack the stadium. The runners have trained for months — strict diets, early mornings, no wine, no late nights, every muscle disciplined. The starting signal sounds and they explode down the track. One man crosses the line first, his chest heaving, and the crowd roars as a herald places on his head the prize: a wreath of pine, or in some years of withered celery. He wears it proudly. In a week it will be brown. In a month, dust. And down in the crowd, perhaps, a tentmaker is watching, thinking: they do all this for a crown that dies.","meaning":"Paul's point is not that Christians should be athletes but that they should have at least as much purpose and discipline as athletes. Every competitor “exercises self-control in all things” — for a crown that wilts. Believers run for a crown that never fades. And so Paul refuses to run aimlessly or to box the air. He punches his own body and makes it serve him rather than rule him, “lest by any means, after I have preached to others, I myself should be rejected.” The herald who announced the rules could himself be disqualified. If the apostle Paul took that seriously, so should you. Discipline is not legalism. It is love for the prize."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Paul's Credentials (v. 1-3)","body":"Paul is free, an apostle, a witness of the risen Lord, and the Corinthians are the seal of his apostleship — this is his defence to his critics."},{"label":"The Right to Support (v. 4-14)","body":"Paul proves his right to be supported from human example (soldier, farmer, shepherd), from the law (the ox), from fairness (sowing spiritual things, reaping material things), from the temple, and from the command of the Lord Himself."},{"label":"The Right Refused (v. 15-18)","body":"Paul has used none of these rights; preaching itself is a necessity laid on him, so his reward is to preach the gospel free of charge."},{"label":"All Things to All Men (v. 19-23)","body":"Though free from all, Paul made himself a slave to all — to Jews, to those under the law, to those without the law, to the weak — so that by all means he might save some, for the sake of the gospel."},{"label":"Run to Win (v. 24-27)","body":"Like athletes in the Isthmian Games, believers must run with purpose and self-control for an imperishable crown; Paul disciplines his body so that he will not be disqualified after preaching to others."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Proving a Right in Order to Lay It Down","paragraphs":["Chapter 9 can look like a digression — Paul suddenly defending his apostleship in the middle of a discussion about idol meat. But it is the heart of the argument. In chapter 8 the strong in Corinth were insisting on their ἐξουσία, their right, to eat idol meat. Paul answered that love might require them to give up that right for the sake of a weaker brother. Now he shows them that he is not asking them to do anything he has not already done himself, on a much larger scale. He uses exactly their vocabulary: ἐξουσία, right, occurs six times in this chapter (vv. 4, 5, 6, 12 twice, 18). And he spends fourteen verses proving that his right was real before he tells them he did not use it.","The argument for support is one of the most thorough in his letters. He appeals to ordinary life — no soldier serves at his own expense, no farmer plants a vineyard without eating the grapes, no shepherd tends a flock without drinking the milk (v. 7). He appeals to the law of Moses — “You shall not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain” (v. 9, quoting Deuteronomy 25:4) — and insists that it was written for the sake of people. He appeals to justice — if we sowed spiritual things, is it too much to reap material things (v. 11)? He appeals to the temple, where priests lived from the offerings (v. 13). And finally he appeals to the highest authority of all: “Even so the Lord ordained that those who proclaim the Good News should live from the Good News” (v. 14). Paul is almost certainly referring to Jesus' instructions to the seventy-two: “the laborer is worthy of his wages” (Luke 10:7). He quotes both texts together in 1 Timothy 5:18.","Then comes the turn: “But I have used none of these things” (v. 15). Paul's refusal of support was not because he thought it wrong. It was a strategic decision for the gospel's sake. In Corinth, travelling philosophers and teachers were often paid by wealthy patrons, which put the teacher under obligation and made him something like a household dependant. Others charged fees, and the public suspected them of teaching for profit. Paul wanted nothing to suggest that the gospel could be bought, or that he could be owned. He worked with his hands as a tentmaker (Acts 18:3) and accepted gifts from other churches when he was in need (2 Corinthians 11:8–9; Philippians 4:15–16), but he refused to be supported by the Corinthians themselves. Later, his rivals would twist even this against him. But his principle stood: “we bear all things, that we may cause no hindrance to the Good News of Christ” (v. 12)."]},{"heading":"Free From All, Slave to All: The Shape of Gospel Flexibility","paragraphs":["Verses 19 to 23 are among the most quoted in Paul's letters, and among the most misunderstood. “For though I was free from all, I brought myself under bondage to all, that I might gain the more.” This is the paradox of Christian freedom. Paul is the freest man in the room — free from the law as a means of righteousness, free from human masters, free from the opinions of critics. And he uses his freedom to make himself everyone's slave. Freedom, for Paul, is not the right to please yourself. It is the power to serve others without being forced to.","Then he gives examples. To Jews he became as a Jew — circumcising Timothy (Acts 16:3), observing Jewish customs, joining in purification rites at the temple (Acts 21:23–26). To Gentiles, those “without law,” he became as one without the law — eating with them, speaking in their categories, quoting their poets (Acts 17:28). To the weak he became weak — limiting his own freedom so as not to trouble their consciences, exactly as he urged the strong to do in chapter 8. And he sums it up: “I have become all things to all men, that I may by all means save some” (v. 22).","But there are two guardrails here that must not be missed. The first is in verse 21: Paul is never “without law toward God, but under law toward Christ.” He is ἔννομος Χριστοῦ — literally “in-lawed to Christ.” His flexibility is in matters of culture, custom and personal freedom, never in matters of obedience or truth. He would eat with Gentiles, but he would not join their idolatry (chapter 10 makes this explicit). He would keep Jewish customs, but he would never say that keeping them was necessary for salvation (Galatians 2 shows him fighting fiercely on that point). The second guardrail is the goal: “that I may by all means save some.” Paul adapts himself, not the gospel. He changes everything about himself that can be changed, so that nothing but the cross itself remains as an offence. A Christianity that adapts the message to make it comfortable has misunderstood this verse completely. Paul's message never changed. His preferences did."]},{"heading":"The Herald Who Could Be Disqualified","paragraphs":["The chapter ends with an image the Corinthians knew intimately. The Isthmian Games were held every two years at the sanctuary of Poseidon on the isthmus, a few miles from Corinth. They were second in prestige only to the Olympic Games, and athletes came from all over the Greek world. If Paul was in Corinth from about AD 50 to 52, he may well have been there during the games of 51, perhaps even selling tents to the crowds. So when he writes, “Don’t you know that those who run in a race all run, but one receives the prize?” (v. 24), every reader could picture the stadium. And when he says, “Every man who strives in the games exercises self-control in all things” (v. 25), they knew the rigorous training athletes endured — and the prize they won: a wreath of pine at the Isthmian Games, or in some periods withered celery, that would be brown within days.","Paul's point is the contrast between the crowns. The athletes discipline themselves for “a corruptible crown, but we an incorruptible.” If people will give up comfort and pleasure for months for a wreath that dies, how much more should believers be willing to discipline themselves for a crown that lasts forever? And that discipline is directly connected to what Paul has been saying about rights. The strong in Corinth wanted to exercise every freedom they had. Paul says the Christian life is more like an athlete's training than a banquet: you voluntarily give up good things for the sake of the prize. Self-control is not the enemy of freedom. It is freedom used well.","And then verse 27, one of the most sobering verses Paul ever wrote: “I beat my body and bring it into submission, lest by any means, after I have preached to others, I myself should be rejected.” The verb ὑπωπιάζω is a boxer's word — to strike under the eye, to give a black eye. Paul is not despising his body; he has just called it the temple of the Spirit. He is refusing to let its appetites run his life. And he names his fear: that after acting as a κῆρυξ — a herald, the official who announced the contestants and the rules at the games — he himself might be ἀδόκιμος, disqualified, failing the test. Interpreters differ on whether Paul means loss of his salvation or loss of his reward and usefulness. The word is used elsewhere of people whose faith proves counterfeit (2 Corinthians 13:5; Titus 1:16), and chapter 10 will immediately warn about Israelites who were baptised and fed by God and still fell in the wilderness. At the very least, Paul refused to assume that preaching to others guaranteed his own faithfulness. Neither should you."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["The principle of supporting gospel workers, which Paul says “the Lord ordained” (v. 14), comes from Jesus' instructions when He sent out the seventy-two: “Remain in that same house, eating and drinking the things they give, for the laborer is worthy of his wages” (Luke 10:7). Paul later quotes both this saying and the ox law together when instructing Timothy about honouring elders: “Let the elders who rule well be counted worthy of double honor, especially those who labor in the word and in teaching. For the Scripture says, ‘You shall not muzzle the ox when it treads out the grain.’ And, ‘The laborer is worthy of his wages’” (1 Timothy 5:17–18). And he tells the Galatians: “But let him who is taught in the word share all good things with him who teaches” (Galatians 6:6). Yet in his own practice, both in Thessalonica and Ephesus, he chose to work: “not because we don’t have the right, but to make ourselves an example to you, that you should imitate us” (2 Thessalonians 3:9). He reminded the Ephesian elders of the words of Jesus, “It is more blessed to give than to receive” (Acts 20:35).","The athletic imagery of verses 24 to 27 runs through Paul's letters to the very end of his life. To the Philippians he writes: “Forgetting the things which are behind, and stretching forward to the things which are before, I press on toward the goal for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus” (Philippians 3:13–14). And in his final letter, waiting for execution in Rome, he looks back on the race: “I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (2 Timothy 4:7). The man who feared being disqualified in 1 Corinthians 9 finished. And the writer of Hebrews gives the whole church the same picture: “let us run with perseverance the race that is set before us, looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith” (Hebrews 12:1–2).","And the imperishable crown of verse 25 is promised by several New Testament writers under different names. James: “Blessed is the man who endures temptation, for when he has been approved, he will receive the crown of life, which the Lord promised to those who love him” (James 1:12). Peter, to elders who shepherd God's flock willingly and not for gain: “When the chief Shepherd is revealed, you will receive the crown of glory that doesn’t fade away” (1 Peter 5:4). And the risen Christ to the suffering church in Smyrna: “Be faithful to death, and I will give you the crown of life” (Revelation 2:10). The wreaths of the Isthmian Games withered in a week. The crown Christ gives does not fade."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Right / authority / liberty","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","strongs":"G1849","verses":"v. 4, 5, 6, 12 (×2), 18 (and 8:9)","insight":"Strong's: “privilege, i.e. (subjectively) force, capacity, competency, freedom, or (objectively) mastery.” It is the key word linking chapters 8 and 9. In 8:9 the Corinthians' ἐξουσία — their liberty to eat idol meat — threatened to become a stumbling block. In chapter 9 Paul uses the same word six times for his own rights as an apostle: to eat and drink at the church's expense, to take a wife on his travels, to stop working, to be supported. He proves them all. Then he refuses them all. The lesson is that a right is not a command. Having the authority to do something does not mean you must do it. Mature freedom includes the freedom not to use your freedom."},{"english":"Hindrance / obstacle","hebrew":"ἐγκοπή","translit":"enkopḗ","strongs":"G1464","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “a hindrance.” The word comes from a verb meaning to cut into, and it was used of a trench or obstruction cut across a road to stop an advancing army. This is its only appearance in the New Testament. Paul says he would rather bear all things — hunger, poverty, humiliation, criticism — than put such an obstacle in the way of the gospel. That is a radical standard for ministry. It means asking not merely whether something is permissible, but whether it could slow down someone's journey to Christ. A preacher's lifestyle, a church's spending, a Christian's habits can all become trenches across the road."},{"english":"Gain / win","hebrew":"κερδαίνω","translit":"kerdaínō","strongs":"G2770","verses":"v. 19, 20 (×2), 21, 22","insight":"Strong's: “to gain (literally or figuratively).” It is a commercial word, used of making a profit in trade — as in Jesus' parable of the talents, where servants “gained” more (Matthew 25:16–17). Paul uses it five times in four verses for winning people to Christ. And Jesus uses it of restoring a brother: “you have gained back your brother” (Matthew 18:15). It reveals what Paul counted as profit. Not money, not comfort, not his rights — people. He gave up everything else in order to gain them. Ask yourself what you are trying to gain with your life. Paul's answer was souls."},{"english":"Exercise self-control","hebrew":"ἐγκρατεύομαι","translit":"enkrateúomai","strongs":"G1467","verses":"v. 25 (and 7:9)","insight":"Strong's: “to exercise self-restraint (in diet and chastity).” The word comes from κράτος, strength or power, and means to have power over oneself. It was the standard term for an athlete's rigorous training regime: controlled diet, abstinence from wine and sexual activity, early sleep, disciplined exercise. Paul uses the same verb in 7:9 of sexual self-control. The related noun ἐγκράτεια appears in the fruit of the Spirit in Galatians 5:23. Self-control is not grim self-hatred. It is power over yourself for the sake of something you love more than the immediate pleasure. The athlete wants the crown more than the wine. The believer wants Christ more than anything."},{"english":"Rejected / disqualified","hebrew":"ἀδόκιμος","translit":"adókimos","strongs":"G96","verses":"v. 27","insight":"Strong's: “unapproved, i.e. rejected; by implication, worthless (literally or morally).” It is the negative of δόκιμος, approved — the word for metal that passes the assayer's test. An ἀδόκιμος coin is counterfeit; an ἀδόκιμος athlete is disqualified. Paul uses it of minds given up to sin (Romans 1:28), of people whose faith is not genuine (2 Corinthians 13:5–7), and of those who are “unfit for any good work” (Titus 1:16). Here he applies it to himself as a possibility to be guarded against. Not because he doubted God's faithfulness, but because he refused to presume on his own. The herald who announces the rules is not exempt from them."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the rights you are holding onto that may be hindering the gospel, the cultural comforts you are unwilling to leave for the sake of others, and the lack of discipline that could disqualify you.","items":[{"title":"What Are You Owed?","body":"You have rights. Some are legal, some are moral, some are just the reasonable expectations of a person who works hard and serves faithfully. And you may have been insisting on them — in your church, your family, your workplace. Paul had more right than anyone to be paid, honoured and comfortable, and he said: I have used none of these things. Not because the rights were wrong, but because he cared more about the gospel than about what he was owed. The Spirit is asking: what right are you clutching that might be digging a trench across someone else's road to Christ? Could you let it go? Freedom is not getting what you deserve. It is being free enough not to need it."},{"title":"Who Have You Refused to Become Like?","body":"Paul became as a Jew to Jews, as one without law to Gentiles, as weak to the weak. He crossed every cultural line he could cross without compromising Christ. And you? There are people you find hard to be around — a different generation, a different class, a different culture, a different style of faith. You have expected them to come to you, on your terms, in your comfortable world. The Spirit is asking: what would it look like for you to go to them? To learn their language, eat their food, enter their world, lay aside your preferences — all without changing the gospel — so that you might by all means save some?"},{"title":"Shadowboxing","body":"You may be busy. Active in church, serving, even preaching to others. But Paul warns about two kinds of wasted effort: running aimlessly and boxing the air. Is your spiritual life aimed at a clear goal, or are you just keeping busy? Is it disciplined, or is it driven by moods? And is there an appetite in your body — food, sleep, sex, screens, comfort — that has been running you instead of serving you? The Spirit is not condemning you. He is calling you into training. Athletes give up good things for a crown that withers. What are you willing to give up for a crown that never fades?"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Rights Check","body":"List three rights you have insisted on recently — at home, at church, at work. Beside each, ask: if I gave this up, could it help someone see Christ more clearly? Is my insistence on it hindering the gospel in any way? Circle one you could lay down."},{"title":"The Bridge Check","body":"Name one person or group you have made little effort to understand because they are culturally different from you. What would it cost you to become “as” them — to enter their world without compromising Christ? What is stopping you?"},{"title":"The Training Check","body":"Write down your spiritual disciplines over the past month: prayer, Scripture, fasting, giving, service, rest. Then write down one bodily appetite that has been ruling you. Ask honestly: am I running with purpose, or boxing the air?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Supporting Those Who Sow","body":"Verse 14 says the Lord ordained that those who proclaim the gospel should live from it. This week, do something concrete to support someone who teaches you the Word — your pastor, a missionary, a Bible teacher, a small group leader. Give financially if you can. Write them a specific note of thanks for what you have reaped from their sowing. Pray for them by name. Do not wait for them to ask; Paul's whole point is that the best teachers often will not."},{"title":"A Training Plan for an Imperishable Crown","body":"Athletes train for months with a plan. Write a simple thirty-day spiritual training plan. Choose one discipline to strengthen (for example: twenty minutes in the Word every morning before your phone). Choose one appetite to bring under control (for example: no screens after 10 p.m., or one day a week of fasting). Write verse 25 at the top: “they do it to receive a corruptible crown, but we an incorruptible.” Tell one trusted friend about your plan and ask them to check on you weekly. Run with purpose."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You were free from all, and You made Yourself the servant of all. You had every right in heaven and on earth, and You laid them down for me. Teach me to do the same. Father, I confess that I have clung to my rights. I have insisted on what I am owed, and I have not asked whether my insistence was hindering the gospel. Show me the trenches I have dug across other people's roads to You, and give me grace to fill them in. Let me bear all things rather than put an obstacle in the way of the Good News. Make me all things to all people. Give me the humility to enter the world of people who are different from me, to learn their language, to set aside my preferences — without ever changing the message — so that by all means I might save some. And Lord, keep me under the law of Christ, flexible in culture and immovable in obedience. Holy Spirit, put me in training. I do not want to run aimlessly or box the air. Give me self-control in all things. Where my body has been ruling me, help me bring it into submission, not out of hatred for it, but out of love for the prize. Guard me, so that after preaching to others, I myself am not disqualified. And fix my eyes on the crown that will never fade. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Nehemiah Refuses the Governor's Allowance","ref":"Nehemiah 5:14-19","relatedVerses":"v. 12, 15-18","summary":"Nehemiah was appointed governor of Judah, and the governor was entitled to a food allowance paid for by taxing the people. Earlier governors had used that right and more, laying heavy burdens on the people and letting their servants lord it over them. For twelve years Nehemiah refused the allowance. He fed a hundred and fifty officials at his own table out of his own resources, and he worked on the wall himself. His reason was simple: the burden on the people was already heavy, and he feared God. Nehemiah had a legitimate right, and he refused it so that the work of God would not be hindered by resentment. His prayer at the end of the passage is exactly Paul's heart in verses 15 to 18: remember me, my God, for good, according to all I have done for this people."},{"title":"Abram Refuses the Spoils of Sodom","ref":"Genesis 14:17-24","relatedVerses":"v. 15","summary":"After Abram rescued Lot and defeated the kings who had plundered Sodom, the king of Sodom offered him all the recovered goods. By the customs of war, Abram had every right to them. But Abram had just been blessed by Melchizedek, priest of God Most High, and he refused to take so much as a thread or a sandal strap, so that the king of Sodom could never say, “I have made Abram rich.” He allowed his allies to take their share, but he would not let anyone boast that his wealth came from a pagan king rather than from God. Paul's passion in verse 15 — that no one should make his boasting void — is the same instinct. Some rights are not worth taking, because of what others would say about the God you serve."},{"title":"King Saul, Anointed and Rejected","ref":"1 Samuel 10:1-11; 15:10-26","relatedVerses":"v. 26-27","summary":"Saul began with every advantage. He was anointed by Samuel, the Spirit of God came on him, and he even prophesied among the prophets, so that people asked whether Saul was also among them. But he never learned to bring himself under God's command. He offered a sacrifice he was not authorised to offer because he was impatient. He spared the best of the Amalekite spoil when God had commanded total destruction, then claimed it was for sacrifice. And Samuel told him that to obey is better than sacrifice, and that because he had rejected the word of the LORD, the LORD had rejected him as king. Saul is the Old Testament picture of 1 Corinthians 9:27: a man who started in the Spirit, ministered to others, and was disqualified because he would not discipline his own will."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":10,"chapterTitle":"Warnings From the Wilderness — The Way of Escape, the Table of the Lord, and the Glory of God","themeTitle":"“Let Him Who Thinks He Stands Be Careful That He Doesn’t Fall”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Now I would not have you ignorant, brothers, that our fathers were all under the cloud, and all passed through the sea;","understanding":"Paul turns from the athlete who might be disqualified (9:27) to a whole nation that was. “I would not have you ἀγνοέω — ignorant.” And notice he calls Israel “our fathers” — to a largely Gentile church. In Christ, the Corinthians have been grafted into Israel's story. Then the word “all” begins: all under the νεφέλη, the cloud of God's presence; all through the θάλασσα, the Red Sea. Every one of them experienced God's deliverance.","words":[{"strongs":"G50","hebrew":"ἀγνοέω","translit":"agnoéō","english":"ignorant"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"fathers"},{"strongs":"G3507","hebrew":"νεφέλη","translit":"nephélē","english":"cloud"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"}]},{"v":2,"text":"and were all baptized into Moses in the cloud and in the sea;","understanding":"“All baptized into Moses.” Paul sees the Red Sea crossing as a kind of baptism: passing through water, under the cloud, into a new identity as Moses' people. Corinthian believers had been baptized into Christ and thought that guaranteed their safety whatever they did. Paul says: Israel had a baptism too.","words":[{"strongs":"G907","hebrew":"βαπτίζω","translit":"baptízō","english":"baptized"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G3507","hebrew":"νεφέλη","translit":"nephélē","english":"cloud"}]},{"v":3,"text":"and all ate the same spiritual food;","understanding":"“All ate the same spiritual food” — the manna, bread from heaven. Πνευματικός here means given by the Spirit, supernatural in origin, and perhaps pointing beyond itself. Jesus would later say that He is the true bread from heaven (John 6:32–35). The Corinthians had the Lord's Supper. Israel had the manna.","words":[{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual"},{"strongs":"G1033","hebrew":"βρῶμα","translit":"brōma","english":"food"}]},{"v":4,"text":"and all drank the same spiritual drink. For they drank of a spiritual rock that followed them, and the rock was Christ.","understanding":"“All drank the same spiritual drink” — water from the rock (Exodus 17; Numbers 20). Then a remarkable phrase: “a spiritual rock that followed them, and the rock was Christ.” Later Jewish tradition told of a rock or well that travelled with Israel through the wilderness, and a form of that story is already found in a first-century Jewish writing. Paul's point goes deeper than the legend: the One who sustained Israel was the pre-existent Christ Himself. He was present with His people long before Bethlehem.","words":[{"strongs":"G4188","hebrew":"πόμα","translit":"póma","english":"drink"},{"strongs":"G4095","hebrew":"πίνω","translit":"pínō","english":"drank"},{"strongs":"G4073","hebrew":"πέτρα","translit":"pétra","english":"rock"},{"strongs":"G190","hebrew":"ἀκολουθέω","translit":"akolouthéō","english":"followed"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"}]},{"v":5,"text":"However with most of them, God was not well pleased, for they were overthrown in the wilderness.","understanding":"Then the shock. “With most of them, God was not well pleased” — the understatement is deliberate. Of the adult generation that left Egypt, only Joshua and Caleb entered the land. They were καταστρώννυμι — strewn, scattered like corpses — across the ἔρημος, the wilderness. Every privilege, and most of them died outside the promise.","words":[{"strongs":"G2106","hebrew":"εὐδοκέω","translit":"eudokéō","english":"well pleased"},{"strongs":"G2693","hebrew":"καταστρώννυμι","translit":"katastrṓnnymi","english":"overthrown"},{"strongs":"G2048","hebrew":"ἔρημος","translit":"érēmos","english":"wilderness"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Now these things were our examples, to the intent we should not lust after evil things, as they also lusted.","understanding":"Ταῦτα δὲ τύποι — these things became τύποι, types, patterns, examples. A τύπος was the mark left by a blow, a stamp impressed in wax. Israel's history is stamped with lessons for us. The first and root warning: not to be ἐπιθυμηταί — cravers — of evil, as they craved. Every sin that follows begins with desire.","words":[{"strongs":"G5179","hebrew":"τύπος","translit":"týpos","english":"examples"},{"strongs":"G1938","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμητής","translit":"epithymētḗs","english":"lust after"},{"strongs":"G1937","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμέω","translit":"epithyméō","english":"lusted"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Don’t be idolaters, as some of them were. As it is written, “The people sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to play.”","understanding":"Warning one: idolatry. Paul quotes Exodus 32:6 from the golden calf story: the people sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to παίζω — to play, to revel. The word suggests dancing and celebration that slid into sexual immorality. Notice: eating and drinking in the presence of an idol. That is exactly what the strong in Corinth were doing at temple meals.","words":[{"strongs":"G1496","hebrew":"εἰδωλολάτρης","translit":"eidōlolátrēs","english":"idolaters"},{"strongs":"G2068","hebrew":"ἐσθίω","translit":"esthíō","english":"eat"},{"strongs":"G4095","hebrew":"πίνω","translit":"pínō","english":"drink"},{"strongs":"G3815","hebrew":"παίζω","translit":"paízō","english":"play"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Let us not commit sexual immorality, as some of them committed, and in one day twenty-three thousand fell.","understanding":"Warning two: sexual immorality. The background is Numbers 25, where Israelite men were drawn into sexual immorality with Moabite women and into the worship of Baal of Peor. Numbers 25:9 gives the number who died in the plague as 24,000; Paul says 23,000 “in one day.” Paul may be citing the number who fell on the single day, or rounding; the slight difference does not touch his point. Idolatry and sexual sin went together in the wilderness, and they went together in Corinth's temples.","words":[{"strongs":"G4203","hebrew":"πορνεύω","translit":"porneúō","english":"commit sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Let us not test Christ, as some of them tested, and perished by the serpents.","understanding":"Warning three: testing the Lord. Ἐκπειράζω — to put to the test thoroughly, to push to see how far you can go. In Numbers 21 the people complained against God and Moses, and fiery serpents came among them. Many manuscripts read “test Christ”; others read “the Lord.” If “Christ” is original, as many think, it is another identification of Israel's God with Jesus. The strong in Corinth were testing God by walking right up to the edge of idolatry.","words":[{"strongs":"G1598","hebrew":"ἐκπειράζω","translit":"ekpeirázō","english":"test"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G3789","hebrew":"ὄφις","translit":"óphis","english":"serpents"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Don’t grumble, as some of them also grumbled, and perished by the destroyer.","understanding":"Warning four: grumbling. Γογγύζω is an onomatopoeic word, the sound of muttering. Israel grumbled constantly in the wilderness (Numbers 14; 16:41). The ὀλοθρευτής, the destroyer, recalls the destroying angel of Passover (Exodus 12:23). Grumbling sounds small. In Scripture it is treated as a symptom of unbelief.","words":[{"strongs":"G1111","hebrew":"γογγύζω","translit":"gongýzō","english":"grumble"},{"strongs":"G622","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","english":"perished"},{"strongs":"G3644","hebrew":"ὀλοθρευτής","translit":"olothreutḗs","english":"destroyer"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Now all these things happened to them by way of example, and they were written for our admonition, on whom the ends of the ages have come.","understanding":"All of this happened τυπικῶς — as a pattern — and was written down πρὸς νουθεσίαν ἡμῶν, for our warning, our admonition. And we are the people “on whom the τέλη of the ages have come.” The ends of the ages have arrived; the climax of history is now. That raises the stakes, not lowers them.","words":[{"strongs":"G5179","hebrew":"τύπος","translit":"týpos","english":"example"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G3559","hebrew":"νουθεσία","translit":"nouthesía","english":"admonition"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"ends"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"ages"},{"strongs":"G2658","hebrew":"καταντάω","translit":"katantáō","english":"come"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Therefore let him who thinks he stands be careful that he doesn’t fall.","understanding":"The application in one sentence. “Let him who thinks he ἵστημι — stands — be careful that he doesn’t πίπτω — fall.” The strong in Corinth thought they were standing firm because of their knowledge and their sacraments. The greatest danger in the Christian life is not weakness but overconfidence.","words":[{"strongs":"G1380","hebrew":"δοκέω","translit":"dokéō","english":"thinks"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stands"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"be careful"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fall"}]},{"v":13,"text":"No temptation has taken you except what is common to man. God is faithful, who will not allow you to be tempted above what you are able, but will with the temptation also make the way of escape, that you may be able to endure it.","understanding":"One of the most loved promises in the Bible, and it comes in the middle of a warning. Πειρασμός — testing, temptation, trial. No temptation has seized you except what is ἀνθρώπινος — human, common to everyone. You are not uniquely tempted. Then: “God is faithful” (as in 1:9). He will not ἐάω — permit — you to be tempted beyond your ability, and with the temptation He will also make the ἔκβασις — the way out, the exit, a mountain pass out of a valley — so that you may be able ὑποφέρω — to bear up under it. The promise is not that you will never be tempted. It is that there will always be a door, and God will put it there.","words":[{"strongs":"G3986","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","english":"temptation"},{"strongs":"G442","hebrew":"ἀνθρώπινος","translit":"anthrṓpinos","english":"common to man"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G1439","hebrew":"ἐάω","translit":"eáō","english":"allow"},{"strongs":"G1545","hebrew":"ἔκβασις","translit":"ékbasis","english":"way of escape"},{"strongs":"G5297","hebrew":"ὑποφέρω","translit":"hypophérō","english":"endure"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Therefore, my beloved, flee from idolatry.","understanding":"“Therefore” — because God provides a way of escape — “flee from εἰδωλολατρία.” The way of escape from idolatry is not to stand near it and resist; it is to run. Paul uses the same verb as 6:18 for sexual immorality: φεύγετε. And he calls them “my beloved” — the warning is from a father's heart.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G5343","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","english":"flee"},{"strongs":"G1495","hebrew":"εἰδωλολατρεία","translit":"eidōlolatreía","english":"idolatry"}]},{"v":15,"text":"I speak as to wise men. Judge what I say.","understanding":"Paul appeals to their own judgment: “I speak as to φρόνιμοι — sensible people. κρίνω — judge for yourselves.” There may be a trace of irony; they prided themselves on their wisdom. But it is also respect: he wants them to see the logic, not just obey the rule.","words":[{"strongs":"G5429","hebrew":"φρόνιμος","translit":"phrónimos","english":"wise men"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judge"}]},{"v":16,"text":"The cup of blessing which we bless, isn’t it a sharing of the blood of Christ? The bread which we break, isn’t it a sharing of the body of Christ?","understanding":"“The cup of blessing which we bless” — a phrase very probably drawn from the Jewish meal liturgy, where the cup over which a thanksgiving was said after the meal was called the cup of blessing; at Passover it was the third cup. Is it not a κοινωνία, a sharing, a participation, in the blood of Christ? The bread we κλάω, break — is it not a participation in His body? The Lord's Supper is not just a memorial meal. It is communion — a real sharing in Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G4221","hebrew":"ποτήριον","translit":"potḗrion","english":"cup"},{"strongs":"G2129","hebrew":"εὐλογία","translit":"eulogía","english":"blessing"},{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"bless"},{"strongs":"G2842","hebrew":"κοινωνία","translit":"koinōnía","english":"sharing"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G740","hebrew":"ἄρτος","translit":"ártos","english":"bread"},{"strongs":"G2806","hebrew":"κλάω","translit":"kláō","english":"break"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Because there is one loaf of bread, we, who are many, are one body; for we all partake of the one loaf of bread.","understanding":"One ἄρτος — one loaf — so we, who are many, are one body, because we all μετέχω, partake, of the one loaf. The picture is a single loaf of bread broken and shared around the room. Every person eating from it is joined to the others. This is why division at the Lord's Table (chapter 11) is so serious.","words":[{"strongs":"G740","hebrew":"ἄρτος","translit":"ártos","english":"loaf of bread"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G3348","hebrew":"μετέχω","translit":"metéchō","english":"partake"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Consider Israel according to the flesh. Don’t those who eat the sacrifices participate in the altar?","understanding":"Paul points to Israel's sacrificial system. Those who ate the sacrifices were κοινωνοί τοῦ θυσιαστηρίου — partners with the altar. To eat a sacrificial meal was to share in the worship it expressed. Eating is never just eating when it happens at an altar.","words":[{"strongs":"G2068","hebrew":"ἐσθίω","translit":"esthíō","english":"eat"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifices"},{"strongs":"G2844","hebrew":"κοινωνός","translit":"koinōnós","english":"participate"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"}]},{"v":19,"text":"What am I saying then? That a thing sacrificed to idols is anything, or that an idol is anything?","understanding":"Paul anticipates the strong's objection: are you saying an idol is real after all? No. He has already said in 8:4 that an idol is nothing. The meat is just meat. The statue is just stone. But that is not the whole truth, as the next verse shows.","words":[{"strongs":"G1494","hebrew":"εἰδωλόθυτον","translit":"eidōlóthyton","english":"thing sacrificed to idols"},{"strongs":"G1497","hebrew":"εἴδωλον","translit":"eídōlon","english":"idol"}]},{"v":20,"text":"But I say that the things which the Gentiles sacrifice, they sacrifice to demons, and not to God, and I don’t desire that you would have fellowship with demons.","understanding":"The other half of the truth: what pagans sacrifice, they sacrifice δαιμονίοις — to demons — and not to God. Paul is echoing Deuteronomy 32:17. The idol is nothing, but behind it are real spiritual powers that receive the worship. And Paul does not want them to become κοινωνοί τῶν δαιμονίων — partners with demons. The theology of the strong was half right, and the missing half was deadly.","words":[{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"Gentiles"},{"strongs":"G2380","hebrew":"θύω","translit":"thýō","english":"sacrifice"},{"strongs":"G1140","hebrew":"δαιμόνιον","translit":"daimónion","english":"demons"},{"strongs":"G2844","hebrew":"κοινωνός","translit":"koinōnós","english":"fellowship"}]},{"v":21,"text":"You can’t both drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons. You can’t both partake of the table of the Lord, and of the table of demons.","understanding":"Two cups, two tables, and you cannot have both. The τράπεζα κυρίου, the table of the Lord, and the τράπεζα δαιμονίων, the table of demons. In the ancient world, invitations to temple banquets sometimes read “to dine at the table of the lord Serapis.” Paul uses the same language. You cannot sit at both tables. Exclusive loyalty is the nature of covenant.","words":[{"strongs":"G4221","hebrew":"ποτήριον","translit":"potḗrion","english":"cup"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1140","hebrew":"δαιμόνιον","translit":"daimónion","english":"demons"},{"strongs":"G3348","hebrew":"μετέχω","translit":"metéchō","english":"partake"},{"strongs":"G5132","hebrew":"τράπεζα","translit":"trápeza","english":"table"}]},{"v":22,"text":"Or do we provoke the Lord to jealousy? Are we stronger than he?","understanding":"Two sharp questions echoing Deuteronomy 32:21, where God says Israel provoked Him to jealousy with what is not God. Do we παραζηλόω — provoke — the Lord to jealousy? Are we ἰσχυρότεροι — stronger — than He is? A direct jab at the “strong.” If you think your strength allows you to flirt with idolatry, are you stronger than God?","words":[{"strongs":"G3863","hebrew":"παραζηλόω","translit":"parazēlóō","english":"provoke to jealousy"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"stronger"}]},{"v":23,"text":"“All things are lawful for me,” but not all things are profitable. “All things are lawful for me,” but not all things build up.","understanding":"The Corinthian slogan again (as in 6:12): “All things are lawful for me.” Paul's two answers now are not all things συμφέρω — benefit — and not all things οἰκοδομέω — build up. In chapter 6 the test was, will it master me? Here the test is, will it help others? The question shifts from self to neighbour.","words":[{"strongs":"G1832","hebrew":"ἔξεστι","translit":"éxesti","english":"lawful"},{"strongs":"G4851","hebrew":"συμφέρω","translit":"symphérō","english":"profitable"},{"strongs":"G3618","hebrew":"οἰκοδομέω","translit":"oikodoméō","english":"build up"}]},{"v":24,"text":"Let no one seek his own, but each one his neighbor’s good.","understanding":"The principle beneath the whole discussion. Μηδεὶς τὸ ἑαυτοῦ ζητείτω — let no one seek his own — ἀλλὰ τὸ τοῦ ἑτέρου — but that of the other. Love does not ask what is good for me, but what is good for him. It is 13:5 in advance: love “doesn’t seek its own way.”","words":[{"strongs":"G2212","hebrew":"ζητέω","translit":"zētéō","english":"seek"}]},{"v":25,"text":"Whatever is sold in the butcher shop, eat, asking no question for the sake of conscience,","understanding":"Now practical guidance. Meat sold in the μάκελλον — the provision market; a Latin inscription mentioning Corinth's macellum has been found — you may eat without asking questions for conscience' sake. Some of it would have come from temple sacrifices; much of it would not. Paul tells believers not to investigate. Once meat is in the market, it is just food.","words":[{"strongs":"G3111","hebrew":"μάκελλον","translit":"mákellon","english":"butcher shop"},{"strongs":"G4453","hebrew":"πωλέω","translit":"pōléō","english":"sold"},{"strongs":"G2068","hebrew":"ἐσθίω","translit":"esthíō","english":"eat"},{"strongs":"G350","hebrew":"ἀνακρίνω","translit":"anakrínō","english":"asking question"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"}]},{"v":26,"text":"for “the earth is the Lord’s, and its fullness.”","understanding":"The reason, from Psalm 24:1, which Jewish families recited as a blessing before meals: “the earth is the Lord’s, and its fullness.” Everything belongs to God. Food is His gift, whatever pagans may have done with it. An idol cannot steal what God made.","words":[{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4138","hebrew":"πλήρωμα","translit":"plḗrōma","english":"fullness"}]},{"v":27,"text":"But if one of those who don’t believe invites you to a meal, and you are inclined to go, eat whatever is set before you, asking no questions for the sake of conscience.","understanding":"If an unbeliever invites you to dinner at his home and you want to go, go. Eat what is παρατίθημι — set before you — without asking questions. Paul is not telling believers to withdraw from social life with pagans. He wants them at those tables, as Jesus ate with sinners.","words":[{"strongs":"G571","hebrew":"ἄπιστος","translit":"ápistos","english":"who don't believe"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"invites"},{"strongs":"G3908","hebrew":"παρατίθημι","translit":"paratíthēmi","english":"set before"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"}]},{"v":28,"text":"But if anyone says to you, “This was offered to idols,” don’t eat it for the sake of the one who told you, and for the sake of conscience. For “the earth is the Lord’s, and all its fullness.”","understanding":"But if someone μηνύω — points out, discloses — “This was offered to idols,” then do not eat. Why? For the sake of the one who told you, and for conscience. Someone has drawn attention to the meat's religious meaning; now eating it would be a statement. (The repeated quotation of Psalm 24:1 at the end of this verse in the WEB is found in later manuscripts; the earliest omit it.)","words":[{"strongs":"G1494","hebrew":"εἰδωλόθυτον","translit":"eidōlóthyton","english":"offered to idols"},{"strongs":"G3377","hebrew":"μηνύω","translit":"mēnýō","english":"says (points out)"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"}]},{"v":29,"text":"Conscience, I say, not your own, but the other’s conscience. For why is my liberty judged by another conscience?","understanding":"“Conscience, I say, not your own, but the other’s.” Your own conscience is clear; you know the idol is nothing. But the other person's conscience is now involved. Then a question in the voice of the strong: why should my freedom be judged by someone else's conscience? Paul's answer is in the next verses: it should not be judged — but it should be limited by love.","words":[{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G1657","hebrew":"ἐλευθερία","translit":"eleuthería","english":"liberty"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"}]},{"v":30,"text":"If I partake with thankfulness, why am I denounced for something I give thanks for?","understanding":"“If I partake with χάρις — with thanks — why am I βλασφημέω — slandered — over something I give thanks for?” Paul defends the freedom to eat with gratitude. But he is also saying: why give someone the opportunity to speak evil of what I receive with thanks? Thanksgiving is the believer's way of eating, and it should not become an occasion for slander.","words":[{"strongs":"G3348","hebrew":"μετέχω","translit":"metéchō","english":"partake"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"thankfulness"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"denounced"},{"strongs":"G2168","hebrew":"εὐχαριστέω","translit":"eucharistéō","english":"give thanks"}]},{"v":31,"text":"Whether therefore you eat, or drink, or whatever you do, do all to the glory of God.","understanding":"The great summary of the whole section, and one of the broadest commands in the New Testament. Eating, drinking, “or whatever you do” — πάντα εἰς δόξαν θεοῦ ποιεῖτε, do everything for the glory of God. The most ordinary acts of life — a meal, a drink — are to be done to make God's worth visible. There is no secular corner of a Christian's life.","words":[{"strongs":"G2068","hebrew":"ἐσθίω","translit":"esthíō","english":"eat"},{"strongs":"G4095","hebrew":"πίνω","translit":"pínō","english":"drink"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]},{"v":32,"text":"Give no occasion for stumbling, either to Jews, or to Greeks, or to the assembly of God;","understanding":"Ἀπρόσκοποι γίνεσθε — become people who cause no one to stumble. Three groups: Jews, Greeks, and the church of God. Paul's concern reaches outward to unbelievers of every background, and inward to fellow believers. Your freedom must not become a stumbling block for anyone.","words":[{"strongs":"G677","hebrew":"ἀπρόσκοπος","translit":"apróskopos","english":"no occasion for stumbling"},{"strongs":"G2453","hebrew":"Ἰουδαῖος","translit":"Ioudaîos","english":"Jews"},{"strongs":"G1672","hebrew":"Ἕλλην","translit":"Héllēn","english":"Greeks"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"}]},{"v":33,"text":"even as I also please all men in all things, not seeking my own profit, but the profit of the many, that they may be saved.","understanding":"Paul's own example closes the argument: “I please all men in all things” — not by compromising truth but by setting aside his own preferences. “Not seeking my own σύμφορον — profit, advantage — but the profit of the many, that they may be saved.” The goal of Christian freedom, like everything else, is the salvation of others. And 11:1 follows: “Be imitators of me, even as I also am of Christ.”","words":[{"strongs":"G700","hebrew":"ἀρέσκω","translit":"aréskō","english":"please"},{"strongs":"G2212","hebrew":"ζητέω","translit":"zētéō","english":"seeking"},{"strongs":"G4851","hebrew":"συμφέρω","translit":"symphérō","english":"profit"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saved"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Bones in the Wilderness","verseRef":"v. 1-5","image":"Picture the people of Israel on the far shore of the Red Sea. Behind them, the water has closed over Pharaoh's army. Above them, the pillar of cloud. Every one of them — men, women, children — has walked through the sea on dry ground. Every morning, manna lies on the ground like frost. When they are thirsty, water pours from a rock. They have seen more miracles in a month than most people see in a lifetime. And now follow them forward forty years. Picture the trail they left across the Sinai desert: grave after grave, the bones of an entire generation scattered across the sand. Of all the adults who walked through the sea, only two — Joshua and Caleb — set foot in the promised land.","meaning":"Paul repeats the word “all” five times in four verses: all under the cloud, all through the sea, all baptized, all ate, all drank. Then: “with most of them, God was not well pleased.” The Corinthians thought their baptism and the Lord's Supper made them safe, whatever they did at the temple banquets. Paul says Israel had their own baptism and their own spiritual food and drink — and it did not save them from the consequences of idolatry, immorality, testing God and grumbling. Spiritual privilege is not the same as spiritual safety. Experiences of God's power are not a substitute for faithful obedience. Many have walked through the sea and died in the desert."},{"title":"The Mountain Pass","verseRef":"v. 13","image":"Picture an army surrounded in a valley. Mountains rise on every side; the enemy holds every ridge. The soldiers are exhausted and the situation looks hopeless. Then a scout returns with news: there is a narrow pass through the mountains, hidden from the enemy's view. It is steep and difficult, but it is there. The army follows it, single file, and comes out on the other side. That narrow pass is the ἔκβασις of verse 13 — an exit, a way out of a closed place. The word was used for a passage out of a valley or a landing place out of the sea.","meaning":"This verse is not a promise that you will never face temptation, or that temptation will be easy. It is a promise about God's faithfulness inside temptation. First, your temptation is “common to man” — you are not uniquely cursed, and others have faced it and won. Second, God limits it: He will not allow you to be tempted beyond what you are able. Third, He provides the way out, together with the temptation itself — not after you have fallen, but with it. Fourth, the purpose is that you “may be able to endure it.” And notice what follows immediately: “Therefore, my beloved, flee from idolatry.” Sometimes the way of escape is not a mysterious rescue. It is a door you have to choose to run through."},{"title":"Two Tables","verseRef":"v. 16-21","image":"On a Sunday evening in Corinth, a group of believers gather in a house. One loaf of bread is broken and passed from hand to hand; one cup is blessed and shared. They are remembering the Lord's death, and they are participating in His body and blood. Now picture the next evening, a mile away, in the dining hall of a pagan temple. An invitation card has gone out: “You are invited to dine at the table of the lord Serapis.” The meat comes from the altar. The meal begins with an offering to the god. And among the guests, reclining with a cup in his hand, is one of the believers from last night's gathering, telling himself that the idol is nothing.","meaning":"Paul agrees that the idol is nothing (v. 19). But he insists that something real is happening at that altar: “the things which the Gentiles sacrifice, they sacrifice to demons, and not to God” (v. 20). To eat a sacrificial meal is to become a κοινωνός, a partner, with whatever is being worshipped, just as eating the Lord's Supper is a κοινωνία in the body and blood of Christ. You cannot sit at both tables. Today the altars look different — but the principle is the same. Anything that receives the worship, loyalty and devotion that belong to God is an idol, and behind it are powers that are not neutral. Ask yourself what other tables you have been sitting at."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Israel's Privileges (v. 1-4)","body":"All the Israelites were under the cloud, passed through the sea, were baptized into Moses, and ate and drank spiritual food and drink from the rock that was Christ."},{"label":"Israel's Fall and Its Lessons (v. 5-11)","body":"Yet most of them died in the wilderness because of craving evil, idolatry, sexual immorality, testing the Lord and grumbling — and these things were written as examples for us, on whom the ends of the ages have come."},{"label":"Warning and Promise (v. 12-13)","body":"Whoever thinks he stands should take care not to fall; but no temptation is beyond the ordinary, and God is faithful to provide the way of escape."},{"label":"Flee Idolatry: The Two Tables (v. 14-22)","body":"The Lord's Supper is a participation in Christ's body and blood and makes believers one body; pagan sacrifices are offered to demons, and believers cannot share both the Lord's table and the table of demons."},{"label":"Freedom, Conscience and the Glory of God (v. 23-33)","body":"Not everything lawful is beneficial or builds up; eat market meat and meals in unbelievers' homes freely, but abstain if someone points out it was offered to idols, for the sake of their conscience; do everything to the glory of God and seek the good of others so that they may be saved."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Baptized and Fed — and Still Overthrown","paragraphs":["Chapter 10 follows directly from 9:27, where Paul said he disciplined his body so that he would not be disqualified. Now he gives an entire nation as an example of disqualification. The argument is aimed at the strong in Corinth, who apparently believed that their baptism and participation in the Lord's Supper made them spiritually secure, so they could attend temple banquets without danger. Paul says: look at Israel. “Our fathers were all under the cloud, and all passed through the sea; and were all baptized into Moses in the cloud and in the sea; and all ate the same spiritual food; and all drank the same spiritual drink” (vv. 1–4). The repetition of “all” is deliberate — five times. Every single one of them experienced God's saving power and provision. And then verse 5: “However with most of them, God was not well pleased, for they were overthrown in the wilderness.”","Verse 4 contains one of the most striking statements about Christ in Paul's letters: “they drank of a spiritual rock that followed them, and the rock was Christ.” Later Jewish tradition developed a legend that the rock or well that gave water at the start of the wilderness journey travelled with Israel for forty years; an early form of this tradition appears in a Jewish work from about Paul's time known as Pseudo-Philo. Whether or not Paul has the legend in mind, his theological point is clear and bold: the One who sustained Israel in the wilderness was the pre-existent Christ. Moses' rock was a sign of a deeper reality. Jesus was present with His people long before His incarnation. And if Israel could sin against Christ in the wilderness and fall, so could the Corinthians.","Then come four specific warnings, each drawn from a wilderness story. Idolatry: the golden calf, where “the people sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to play” (v. 7, Exodus 32:6) — notice, eating and drinking at an idol feast, exactly the issue in Corinth. Sexual immorality: the incident at Baal-peor (Numbers 25), where idolatry and immorality went together, as they often did in Corinth's temples. (Numbers 25:9 says 24,000 died; Paul says 23,000 “in one day” — he may be counting those who fell on a single day, or using a round figure; the point is unaffected.) Testing the Lord: the complaint in Numbers 21 that brought the fiery serpents. Grumbling: the repeated murmuring that brought judgment (Numbers 14, 16). And then the purpose: “these things happened to them by way of example, and they were written for our admonition” (v. 11). The Old Testament is not ancient history to Paul. It is a warning written for the church."]},{"heading":"Overconfidence and the Faithfulness of God","paragraphs":["Verse 12 distils the warning into one sentence: “Therefore let him who thinks he stands be careful that he doesn’t fall.” The danger Paul is addressing is not weakness. It is overconfidence. The strong in Corinth were sure of their standing. They had knowledge, they had gifts, they had sacraments. And precisely because they were sure, they were walking into situations — temple banquets — that put them at risk of falling into idolatry. The most dangerous moment in the Christian life is often not when you feel weak, but when you feel strong enough not to need caution.","But Paul does not leave them with fear. Verse 13 is one of the great promises of Scripture, and its placement is important: it comes right in the middle of a warning about falling. “No temptation has taken you except what is common to man. God is faithful, who will not allow you to be tempted above what you are able, but will with the temptation also make the way of escape, that you may be able to endure it.” Four truths are packed into it. First, your temptation is ἀνθρώπινος — human, common. You are not the only one, and you are not facing something no one else has faced. Second, God is faithful — the same declaration as 1:9. Third, God sets limits: He will not permit temptation beyond your ability. Fourth, He provides the ἔκβασις, the way out, together with the temptation itself. The purpose is not to remove the trial but to enable you to “endure it” — to bear up under it and come through.","Then notice how verse 14 begins: “Therefore, my beloved, flee from idolatry.” The way of escape from temptation is very often to run. God makes the exit; you must take it. Many believers pray for deliverance while walking toward the very situation that tempts them, and then wonder why the escape did not appear. The Corinthians were praying, in effect, for protection while reclining at the table of an idol. Paul's answer is: God has given you a way out. It is the door. Use it. And James adds the necessary clarification: God does not tempt anyone; each one is tempted “when he is drawn away by his own lust, and enticed” (James 1:14). God tests to strengthen. Temptation comes from our desire and the enemy's design. But God is faithful in both."]},{"heading":"The Lord's Table, the Demons' Table, and a Life for God's Glory","paragraphs":["Verses 14 to 22 give Paul's decisive answer to the question of eating in pagan temples, which chapter 8 left open. In chapter 8 he argued from love for the weak brother. Here he argues from the nature of worship itself. The Lord's Supper is a κοινωνία — a sharing, a participation — in the blood and body of Christ (v. 16). The single loaf shared by all makes the many into one body (v. 17). And Israel's sacrificial meals made the eaters partners with the altar (v. 18). Eating at an altar is never just eating. It is participation in the worship that altar represents.","Then Paul completes the theology the strong had only half right. Yes, an idol is nothing (v. 19). But “the things which the Gentiles sacrifice, they sacrifice to demons, and not to God” (v. 20). He is echoing Deuteronomy 32:17, which says Israel “sacrificed to demons, not God.” Behind the empty statue are real spiritual powers that receive the worship given to it. To share in a temple meal is to become a κοινωνός of demons. “You can’t both drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons. You can’t both partake of the table of the Lord, and of the table of demons” (v. 21). Invitations survive from the ancient world that read, “to dine at the table of the lord Serapis” — Paul uses the same language. And he warns, echoing Deuteronomy 32:21, that to do so is to provoke the Lord to jealousy. “Are we stronger than he?”","Verses 23 to 33 then give practical guidance and a beautiful summary. Meat sold in the market can be eaten freely, without investigation, “for ‘the earth is the Lord’s, and its fullness’” (v. 26, Psalm 24:1). An unbeliever's dinner invitation can be accepted, and whatever is served eaten — unless someone points out that it was offered to idols, in which case abstain, for the sake of that person's conscience (vv. 27–29). This is not legalism; it is love. And then comes the principle that governs everything: “Whether therefore you eat, or drink, or whatever you do, do all to the glory of God” (v. 31). No part of life is spiritually neutral. A meal, a drink, a purchase, a conversation — all can make God's worth visible. And the measure is always others: “Give no occasion for stumbling, either to Jews, or to Greeks, or to the assembly of God; even as I also please all men in all things, not seeking my own profit, but the profit of the many, that they may be saved” (vv. 32–33)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["Paul's reading of the wilderness as a warning for the church is shared by the writer of Hebrews: “With whom was he displeased forty years? Wasn’t it with those who sinned, whose bodies fell in the wilderness? To whom did he swear that they wouldn’t enter into his rest, but to those who were disobedient? We see that they were not able to enter in because of unbelief” (Hebrews 3:17–19). And Paul states the principle behind his use of these stories in Romans: “For whatever things were written before were written for our learning, that through perseverance and through encouragement of the Scriptures we might have hope” (Romans 15:4). The wilderness stories were written for us. And Jesus Himself saw His own death in one of them: “As Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of Man be lifted up, that whoever believes in him should not perish, but have eternal life” (John 3:14–15). The very episode Paul uses as a warning in verse 9 became, in Jesus' hands, a picture of salvation.","Paul's identification of Christ with the spiritual food and drink of Israel (vv. 3–4) is developed by Jesus in John's Gospel. When the crowd reminded Him that “Our fathers ate the manna in the wilderness,” Jesus replied, “it wasn’t Moses who gave you the bread out of heaven, but my Father gives you the true bread out of heaven... I am the bread of life. He who comes to me will not be hungry, and he who believes in me will never be thirsty” (John 6:31–35). And at the Feast of Tabernacles, which commemorated the wilderness journey, Jesus stood and cried, “If anyone is thirsty, let him come to me and drink!” (John 7:37). The rock that followed Israel has a name. And the Lord's Supper of verse 16 goes back to the night Jesus took bread and said, “Take, eat; this is my body,” and the cup, “this is my blood of the new covenant, which is poured out for many for the remission of sins” (Matthew 26:26, 28).","The promise of verse 13 finds its foundation in Christ's own experience. Hebrews says, “For we don’t have a high priest who can’t be touched with the feeling of our infirmities, but one who has been in all points tempted like we are, yet without sin” (Hebrews 4:15), and “in that he himself has suffered being tempted, he is able to help those who are tempted” (Hebrews 2:18). The faithful God who provides the way of escape does so through a Saviour who walked through every temptation Himself. Peter adds, “the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptation” (2 Peter 2:9). And the command of verse 31 to do everything for God's glory echoes through the apostles' letters: “Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God the Father, through him” (Colossians 3:17); “that in all things God may be glorified through Jesus Christ” (1 Peter 4:11)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Example / type / pattern","hebrew":"τύπος","translit":"týpos","strongs":"G5179","verses":"v. 6 (and the adverb in v. 11)","insight":"Strong's: “a die (as struck), i.e. (by implication) a stamp or scar; by analogy, a shape, i.e. a statue, (figuratively) style or resemblance; specially, a sampler (‘type’), i.e. a model (for imitation) or instance (for warning).” The word comes from τύπτω, to strike — the same verb used in 8:12 for wounding a weak conscience. A τύπος is the mark left by a blow: the print of the nails in Jesus' hands (John 20:25), the impression stamped into a coin. Israel's experiences in the wilderness left a mark on history that serves as a pattern for the church. When Paul says these things are “examples,” he means they are stamped with lessons that apply directly to us."},{"english":"Temptation / testing / trial","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","strongs":"G3986","verses":"v. 13 (×2)","insight":"Strong's: “a putting to proof (by experiment (of good), experience (of evil), solicitation, discipline or provocation); by implication, adversity.” The same word can mean a trial that tests and strengthens faith (James 1:2, 12) or an enticement to sin (Matthew 6:13). Both meanings may be present here: the situations in Corinth were both tests of loyalty and temptations to idolatry. The key truth is not that you can avoid πειρασμός — Jesus Himself faced it — but that God sets its limits and provides the way through. Every trial that comes into a believer's life has passed through the hands of a faithful God who has measured it against your strength."},{"english":"Way of escape / exit","hebrew":"ἔκβασις","translit":"ékbasis","strongs":"G1545","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's: “an exit (literally or figuratively).” The word combines ἐκ (out of) and βαίνω (to go) — a going-out. It was used of a way out of a valley through the mountains, a landing place from the sea, or the outcome of a situation. Its only other New Testament use is Hebrews 13:7, of the “outcome” of the faith of former leaders. In 1 Corinthians 10:13 the article is significant: “the way of escape” — a specific exit designed for that specific temptation. God does not merely leave a door open somewhere; He makes the exit with the temptation. Your responsibility is to look for it and walk through it."},{"english":"Sharing / participation / communion","hebrew":"κοινωνία","translit":"koinōnía","strongs":"G2842 (related noun, partner κοινωνός, koinōnós, G2844)","verses":"v. 16 (×2); κοινωνός in v. 18, 20","insight":"Strong's defines κοινωνία as “partnership, i.e. (literally) participation, or (social) intercourse,” and κοινωνός as “a sharer, i.e. associate.” This is the word from which the church gets “communion.” It appeared in 1:9 — called into the fellowship of His Son. Here it describes what happens at the Lord's Table: a real participation in the body and blood of Christ, and a real union with one another. Then Paul uses the related noun to describe what happens at a pagan altar: becoming partners with demons. Worship is never a spectator activity. Whatever you worship, you share in. That is why the table you sit at matters so much."},{"english":"Glory","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","strongs":"G1391","verses":"v. 31","insight":"Strong's: “glory (as very apparent), in a wide application (literal or figurative, objective or subjective).” In the Old Testament, the Hebrew word for glory carries the sense of weight — God's heaviness, His substance and worth. In Greek, δόξα originally meant opinion or reputation and came to mean splendour and honour. To do something “to the glory of God” is to do it in a way that makes His worth visible and enhances His reputation in the eyes of others. Verse 31 extends that to eating and drinking — the most ordinary acts of life. There is nothing so small that it cannot either display or obscure the glory of God."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the false security you have built on past experiences of God, the door of escape you keep walking past, and the other tables you have been sitting at.","items":[{"title":"You Walked Through the Sea — Will You Die in the Desert?","body":"You have experienced God. You were baptized. You have felt His presence, received His provision, seen His answers to prayer. Perhaps you have even seen miracles. And the enemy wants you to believe that because of all that, you are safe whatever you do. Paul says: all of Israel walked through the sea, and most of them died in the wilderness. The Spirit is not trying to frighten you out of assurance. He is warning you out of presumption. Past experiences of God are not a licence for present compromise. Where have you been relying on yesterday's encounter while drifting today? “Let him who thinks he stands be careful that he doesn’t fall.”"},{"title":"The Door You Keep Walking Past","body":"There is a temptation you keep falling into, and you have half-convinced yourself that it is beyond your power to resist. The Spirit wants you to hear verse 13 as a personal word: this temptation is common to man; God is faithful; He has not allowed it beyond what you can bear; and He has made a way of escape. The question is not whether the door exists. It is whether you are looking for it — and whether you are willing to run through it when you see it. Often the door appears early: a moment before the click, the drink, the conversation, the look. Ask the Spirit to show you where the door is in your specific temptation, and decide now to take it next time."},{"title":"The Other Table","body":"You sit at the Lord's Table. You take the bread and the cup. And there may be another table you have been sitting at too — something that receives the devotion, the trust, the loyalty that belong to God. Money. Approval. A relationship. Your career. Your image. An addiction. Something you go to for comfort and identity that is not Christ. You tell yourself it is nothing, just as the strong in Corinth did. But Paul says that behind idols are powers that are not neutral, and that you cannot sit at both tables. The Spirit is asking: what is the other table in your life? Will you get up from it?"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Overconfidence Check","body":"Write down one area of your life where you feel spiritually secure enough to take risks other believers would avoid. Then ask honestly: is this freedom, or is it presumption? Am I standing — or do I only think I am standing? What would Israel's story say to me here?"},{"title":"The Escape Check","body":"Name your most frequent temptation. Then trace backwards the last time you gave in to it. At what moment did the way of escape appear? What would it have looked like to take it? Write it down specifically — the time, the place, the choice."},{"title":"The Two-Tables Check","body":"List the three things that most compete with God for your trust, comfort and devotion. For each one, ask: if it were taken away, would I feel I had lost my security, my identity, or my joy? If so, it may have become a table you are sitting at alongside the Lord's."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Mapping Your Exits","body":"Take a sheet of paper and write your top two temptations at the top. Under each, write down: (1) the situations, times or emotions that usually lead to it; (2) the earliest point at which you could turn away; (3) one specific action you will take at that point — a door you will walk through (for example: leave the room, call a friend, close the app, go for a walk, pray aloud). Write verse 13 at the bottom. Keep the page somewhere you will see it. Next time the temptation comes, look for the door you mapped, and take it. Then thank God for His faithfulness."},{"title":"An Ordinary Day to the Glory of God","body":"Choose one ordinary day this week. From the moment you wake up, deliberately do every task “to the glory of God” (v. 31) — eating, working, driving, cleaning, talking, resting. Before each activity, pause briefly and say, “Lord, let this show Your worth.” Before every meal, thank God aloud that the earth is the Lord's and its fullness. At the end of the day, write down what changed in how you did ordinary things, and where you noticed God in places you usually overlook."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I thank You for every time You have brought me through the sea, covered me with Your cloud, and fed me with spiritual food and drink. Thank You that the Rock who followed Israel is Christ, and that He has followed me too. But Lord, I do not want to be one who walked through the sea and died in the desert. Forgive me for the times I have presumed on Your grace, relying on yesterday's experiences while drifting today. Keep me from craving evil, from idolatry, from sexual immorality, from testing You, and from grumbling. Let me learn from the examples You have written for me. Lord, I confess that I have thought I was standing when I was close to falling. Give me humility and holy caution. And thank You that You are faithful. Thank You that no temptation I face is beyond what is common to man, that You have measured every trial against my strength, and that You always make a way of escape. Open my eyes to see the door, and give me the courage to run through it. Jesus, I come to Your table. I share in Your body and blood. I renounce every other table — every idol that has taken the devotion that belongs to You. You alone are my Lord. And let me do everything — eating, drinking, working, resting, whatever I do — for the glory of God, seeking not my own good but the good of many, that they may be saved. In Your name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Golden Calf","ref":"Exodus 32:1-35","relatedVerses":"v. 7","summary":"While Moses was on the mountain receiving the law, the people grew impatient and asked Aaron to make them gods. He collected their gold earrings, cast a calf, and they declared it the god who brought them out of Egypt. Aaron built an altar and announced a feast to the LORD. The next morning they offered sacrifices, sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to play. These were people who had seen the plagues, crossed the sea, eaten manna, and heard God's voice from the mountain only weeks before. And they turned to an idol and called it worship. Paul quotes this story because the Corinthians were doing something similar: eating and drinking in the presence of idols while claiming to belong to the true God. Spiritual experience does not make you immune to idolatry. It can happen in the shadow of the mountain."},{"title":"The Bronze Serpent","ref":"Numbers 21:4-9","relatedVerses":"v. 9","summary":"On the long journey around Edom the people grew impatient and spoke against God and Moses, complaining about the manna and asking why they had been brought into the wilderness to die. They were testing the Lord. And fiery serpents came among them, and many died. When the people confessed their sin, God told Moses to make a bronze serpent and set it on a pole, and everyone who had been bitten and looked at it lived. Paul uses the first half of the story as a warning in 1 Corinthians 10:9: do not test the Lord. Jesus uses the second half as a picture of the gospel in John 3:14: as Moses lifted up the serpent, the Son of Man must be lifted up, so that whoever believes in Him should not perish. The same story contains both warning and salvation."},{"title":"David on the Rooftop","ref":"2 Samuel 11:1-27; 12:1-13","relatedVerses":"v. 12-13","summary":"In the spring, when kings went out to battle, David stayed home in Jerusalem. One evening he got up from his bed and walked on the roof of his palace, and he saw a woman bathing. At that moment there were several ways of escape: he could have turned away, gone back inside, or gone out to join his army. Instead he sent to find out who she was. Even when he learned she was the wife of Uriah, one of his loyal soldiers, he could have stopped. He did not. He sent for her, slept with her, and then arranged her husband's death to cover his sin. David was a man after God's own heart, a man who thought he was standing. He fell because he walked past one door after another. When the prophet Nathan confronted him, David repented, and God forgave him — but the consequences followed his family for the rest of his life. The way of escape was there every step of the way."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":11,"chapterTitle":"Order in Worship and the Lord's Supper — Proclaiming His Death Until He Comes","themeTitle":"“You Cannot Honour the Body on the Cross While Humiliating the Body at the Table”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Be imitators of me, even as I also am of Christ.","understanding":"This verse belongs with chapter 10 — the chapter division here is unfortunate. Having described how he sets aside his own advantage for the salvation of others (10:33), Paul says: μιμηταί μου γίνεσθε — become imitators of me — “even as I also am of Christ.” His authority to be copied is derived. He is worth imitating only insofar as he is imitating Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G3402","hebrew":"μιμητής","translit":"mimētḗs","english":"imitators"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Now I praise you, brothers, that you remember me in all things, and hold firm the traditions, even as I delivered them to you.","understanding":"A rare word of praise before correction. They remember Paul and hold firm the παραδόσεις — the traditions, the teachings handed over — “even as I παραδίδωμι, delivered, them to you.” Christian truth is a deposit handed from person to person, not invented fresh by each congregation. The same verb appears in verse 23 for the Lord's Supper and in 15:3 for the gospel itself.","words":[{"strongs":"G1867","hebrew":"ἐπαινέω","translit":"epainéō","english":"praise"},{"strongs":"G3415","hebrew":"μνάομαι","translit":"mnáomai","english":"remember"},{"strongs":"G2722","hebrew":"κατέχω","translit":"katéchō","english":"hold firm"},{"strongs":"G3862","hebrew":"παράδοσις","translit":"parádosis","english":"traditions"},{"strongs":"G3860","hebrew":"παραδίδωμι","translit":"paradídōmi","english":"delivered"}]},{"v":3,"text":"But I would have you know that the head of every man is Christ, and the head of the woman is man, and the head of Christ is God.","understanding":"Κεφαλή — head. What does Paul mean by it? Interpreters debate whether the primary sense is authority (the one who leads) or source (the one from whom another comes). Verses 8 and 9 show that source is in Paul's mind, and the wider usage of Scripture shows that leadership is too (Ephesians 5:23). The strongest reading holds both. And notice the third clause, which governs the other two: “the head of Christ is God.” The Son is fully equal with the Father, and yet He willingly honours the Father's headship. Headship and equality are not opposites.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Every man praying or prophesying, having his head covered, dishonors his head.","understanding":"A man who prays or prophesies with something hanging down over his head (the Greek is literally “having down the head”) dishonours his head — both his physical head and, Paul implies, Christ his head. In Roman religious practice, men often covered their heads with their togas when offering sacrifice. Paul may be telling Christian men not to adopt that pagan practice in worship.","words":[{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"praying"},{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesying"},{"strongs":"G2617","hebrew":"καταισχύνω","translit":"kataischýnō","english":"dishonors"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"}]},{"v":5,"text":"But every woman praying or prophesying with her head uncovered dishonors her head. For it is one and the same thing as if she were shaved.","understanding":"Notice the assumption first: women pray and prophesy in the assembly. Paul does not forbid it; he regulates how it is done. A woman doing so ἀκατακάλυπτος — unveiled — dishonours her head. In first-century Greek and Roman society, a respectable married woman typically covered her head in public; to appear uncovered could signal immodesty or rejection of her husband. Paul says it is the same as having her head ξυράω — shaved — a mark of public disgrace.","words":[{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"praying"},{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesying"},{"strongs":"G177","hebrew":"ἀκατακάλυπτος","translit":"akatakályptos","english":"uncovered"},{"strongs":"G2617","hebrew":"καταισχύνω","translit":"kataischýnō","english":"dishonors"},{"strongs":"G3587","hebrew":"ξυράω","translit":"xyráō","english":"shaved"}]},{"v":6,"text":"For if a woman is not covered, let her hair also be cut off. But if it is shameful for a woman to have her hair cut off or be shaved, let her be covered.","understanding":"A logical argument from shame. If a woman refuses to κατακαλύπτω — cover — herself, she might as well have her hair κείρω — cut short. But if that is αἰσχρόν, shameful, as everyone in Corinth agreed it was, then let her be covered. Paul is appealing to a standard of decency they already shared.","words":[{"strongs":"G2619","hebrew":"κατακαλύπτω","translit":"katakalýptō","english":"covered"},{"strongs":"G2751","hebrew":"κείρω","translit":"keírō","english":"cut off"},{"strongs":"G149","hebrew":"αἰσχρόν","translit":"aischrón","english":"shameful"},{"strongs":"G3587","hebrew":"ξυράω","translit":"xyráō","english":"shaved"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For a man indeed ought not to have his head covered, because he is the image and glory of God, but the woman is the glory of the man.","understanding":"Now an argument from creation. The man is the εἰκών καὶ δόξα θεοῦ — the image and glory of God (Genesis 1:26–27) — and so should not cover his head in worship. The woman is the δόξα of the man — the one in whom his honour is displayed. Paul is not denying that woman also bears God's image; Genesis 1:27 says plainly that both do. He is speaking of how each relates to the other in God's created order.","words":[{"strongs":"G2619","hebrew":"κατακαλύπτω","translit":"katakalýptō","english":"covered"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]},{"v":8,"text":"For man is not from woman, but woman from man;","understanding":"The basis of verse 7 in Genesis 2: the woman was taken from the man. This is the “source” sense of headship.","words":[{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"}]},{"v":9,"text":"for man wasn’t created for the woman, but woman for the man.","understanding":"Again from Genesis 2:18 — the woman was made as a helper suitable for the man. The Hebrew word for helper there is used elsewhere of God Himself helping Israel; it implies no inferiority. Paul draws from the creation account a pattern of relationship, not of worth.","words":[{"strongs":"G2936","hebrew":"κτίζω","translit":"ktízō","english":"created"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For this cause the woman ought to have authority over her own head, because of the angels.","understanding":"One of the most puzzling verses in Paul. Literally, “the woman ought to have authority on her head, because of the angels.” Most likely the covering is a sign of her authority to pray and prophesy within God's order. And “because of the angels” probably refers to angels as present at Christian worship and watching it (compare 4:9; Ephesians 3:10; 1 Peter 1:12). Honest readers admit uncertainty here; Paul does not explain it, and the Corinthians apparently understood what we do not.","words":[{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Nevertheless, neither is the woman independent of the man, nor the man independent of the woman, in the Lord.","understanding":"A crucial balancing statement: πλήν — nevertheless, however. “In the Lord,” woman is not χωρίς — apart from, independent of — man, nor man of woman. Whatever order exists, it is mutual dependence, not domination. In Christ, they need each other.","words":[{"strongs":"G4133","hebrew":"πλήν","translit":"plḗn","english":"nevertheless"},{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"independent"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]},{"v":12,"text":"For as woman came from man, so a man also comes through a woman; but all things are from God.","understanding":"The balancing argument from birth. Woman originally came from man (Genesis 2), but every man since has come through a woman. Neither can boast over the other. And then the final perspective: τὰ δὲ πάντα ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ — all things are from God. Both man and woman owe their existence to Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Judge for yourselves. Is it appropriate that a woman pray to God unveiled?","understanding":"“Judge for yourselves.” Paul again appeals to their own sense of what is πρέπον — fitting, proper, what stands out as appropriate. Is it fitting for a woman to pray to God uncovered? In their cultural setting, everyone knew the answer.","words":[{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judge"},{"strongs":"G4241","hebrew":"πρέπω","translit":"prépō","english":"appropriate"},{"strongs":"G177","hebrew":"ἀκατακάλυπτος","translit":"akatakályptos","english":"unveiled"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Doesn’t even nature itself teach you that if a man has long hair, it is a dishonor to him?","understanding":"An argument from φύσις — nature. It is generally thought that Paul means the natural sense of things as it was perceived in his culture, or the general tendency that women's hair grows longer than men's. If a man κομάω — wears long, flowing hair like a woman — it is an ἀτιμία, a dishonour, to him. The point is the visible distinction between men and women.","words":[{"strongs":"G5449","hebrew":"φύσις","translit":"phýsis","english":"nature"},{"strongs":"G2863","hebrew":"κομάω","translit":"komáō","english":"has long hair"},{"strongs":"G819","hebrew":"ἀτιμία","translit":"atimía","english":"dishonor"}]},{"v":15,"text":"But if a woman has long hair, it is a glory to her, for her hair is given to her for a covering.","understanding":"But a woman's long hair is a δόξα to her, for it is given ἀντὶ περιβολαίου — as a covering, a wrap, a mantle. Nature itself has already given her a covering, which the custom of veiling reflects. Some read this as meaning her hair is the covering Paul has been talking about; most read it as a natural analogy supporting the custom.","words":[{"strongs":"G2863","hebrew":"κομάω","translit":"komáō","english":"has long hair"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G4018","hebrew":"περιβόλαιον","translit":"peribólaion","english":"covering"}]},{"v":16,"text":"But if any man seems to be contentious, we have no such custom, neither do God’s assemblies.","understanding":"The closing word. If anyone wants to be φιλόνεικος — contentious, fond of winning arguments — “we have no such custom, neither do God’s assemblies.” Paul means: we have no other practice than what I have described. He appeals to the common practice of all the churches. Corinth was not to invent its own.","words":[{"strongs":"G1380","hebrew":"δοκέω","translit":"dokéō","english":"seems"},{"strongs":"G5380","hebrew":"φιλόνεικος","translit":"philóneikos","english":"contentious"},{"strongs":"G4914","hebrew":"συνήθεια","translit":"synḗtheia","english":"custom"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"}]},{"v":17,"text":"But in giving you this command, I don’t praise you, that you come together not for the better but for the worse.","understanding":"The tone changes sharply. In verse 2 Paul praised them. Now: “I don’t praise you.” Their gatherings are not making them κρείσσων — better — but ἥσσων — worse. Their worship was doing harm. It is possible for a church to meet regularly and become worse by it.","words":[{"strongs":"G3853","hebrew":"παραγγέλλω","translit":"parangéllō","english":"command"},{"strongs":"G1867","hebrew":"ἐπαινέω","translit":"epainéō","english":"praise"},{"strongs":"G4905","hebrew":"συνέρχομαι","translit":"synérchomai","english":"come together"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G2276","hebrew":"ἥττον","translit":"hḗtton","english":"worse"}]},{"v":18,"text":"For first of all, when you come together in the assembly, I hear that divisions exist among you, and I partly believe it.","understanding":"“First of all” — σχίσματα again, the same tearing word of 1:10. But this time the divisions are not about preachers. They are social and economic, and they appear at the Lord's Table itself. Paul “partly believes” the reports — he is cautious, but he knows Corinth well enough to believe it.","words":[{"strongs":"G4905","hebrew":"συνέρχομαι","translit":"synérchomai","english":"come together"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G4978","hebrew":"σχίσμα","translit":"schísma","english":"divisions"}]},{"v":19,"text":"For there also must be factions among you, that those who are approved may be revealed among you.","understanding":"Biting irony. There “must be” αἱρέσεις — factions, parties, the root of our word heresy — so that the δόκιμοι, the approved, the genuine, may become φανεροί, evident. Divisions expose who is truly faithful. God uses even the church's sin to reveal the real thing.","words":[{"strongs":"G139","hebrew":"αἵρεσις","translit":"haíresis","english":"factions"},{"strongs":"G1384","hebrew":"δόκιμος","translit":"dókimos","english":"approved"},{"strongs":"G5318","hebrew":"φανερός","translit":"phanerós","english":"revealed"}]},{"v":20,"text":"When therefore you assemble yourselves together, it is not the Lord’s supper that you eat.","understanding":"The devastating verdict: when you come together, “it is not the Lord’s supper that you eat.” Κυριακὸν δεῖπνον — the supper belonging to the Lord. They called it the Lord's Supper, but the way they ate it turned it into something else. Ritual does not make a meal holy if the meal denies what the ritual means.","words":[{"strongs":"G4905","hebrew":"συνέρχομαι","translit":"synérchomai","english":"assemble"},{"strongs":"G2960","hebrew":"κυριακός","translit":"kyriakós","english":"Lord's"},{"strongs":"G1173","hebrew":"δεῖπνον","translit":"deîpnon","english":"supper"}]},{"v":21,"text":"For in your eating each one takes his own supper first. One is hungry, and another is drunken.","understanding":"The scene: each one προλαμβάνω — goes ahead with — his own supper. In early Christianity the Lord's Supper was part of a full fellowship meal. Wealthier members, who could arrive early and brought plenty of food, ate and drank before the poorer members — many of them slaves who could only come after work — arrived. The result: “one is hungry, and another is drunken.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4301","hebrew":"προλαμβάνω","translit":"prolambánō","english":"takes first"},{"strongs":"G1173","hebrew":"δεῖπνον","translit":"deîpnon","english":"supper"},{"strongs":"G3983","hebrew":"πεινάω","translit":"peináō","english":"hungry"},{"strongs":"G3184","hebrew":"μεθύω","translit":"methýō","english":"drunken"}]},{"v":22,"text":"What, don’t you have houses to eat and to drink in? Or do you despise God’s assembly, and put them to shame who don’t have enough? What shall I tell you? Shall I praise you? In this I don’t praise you.","understanding":"Four furious questions. Don't you have houses to eat and drink in? Or do you καταφρονέω — despise, look down on — the church of God, and καταισχύνω — humiliate — those who have nothing? What shall I say? Shall I praise you? “In this I don’t praise you.” To shame the poor at the Lord's Table is to despise the church itself.","words":[{"strongs":"G3614","hebrew":"οἰκία","translit":"oikía","english":"houses"},{"strongs":"G2706","hebrew":"καταφρονέω","translit":"kataphronéō","english":"despise"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G2617","hebrew":"καταισχύνω","translit":"kataischýnō","english":"put to shame"},{"strongs":"G1867","hebrew":"ἐπαινέω","translit":"epainéō","english":"praise"}]},{"v":23,"text":"For I received from the Lord that which also I delivered to you, that the Lord Jesus on the night in which he was betrayed took bread.","understanding":"The earliest written account of the Lord's Supper in existence — written before any of the Gospels. Παραλαμβάνω and παραδίδωμι are the technical words for receiving and passing on a sacred tradition. “I received from the Lord” — through the apostolic witness, ultimately from Christ Himself. And note the poignant detail: “on the night in which he was παραδίδωμι” — the same verb: handed over, betrayed. The Lord's Supper was instituted on the very night He was handed over to death.","words":[{"strongs":"G3880","hebrew":"παραλαμβάνω","translit":"paralambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G3860","hebrew":"παραδίδωμι","translit":"paradídōmi","english":"delivered"},{"strongs":"G3571","hebrew":"νύξ","translit":"nýx","english":"night"},{"strongs":"G3860","hebrew":"παραδίδωμι","translit":"paradídōmi","english":"betrayed"},{"strongs":"G740","hebrew":"ἄρτος","translit":"ártos","english":"bread"}]},{"v":24,"text":"When he had given thanks, he broke it, and said, “Take, eat. This is my body, which is broken for you. Do this in memory of me.”","understanding":"Εὐχαριστέω — having given thanks; the word from which the church gets “Eucharist.” He broke the bread and said, “This is my body, which is for you” — the WEB adds “broken,” following later manuscripts; the earliest read simply “for you.” Then: εἰς τὴν ἐμὴν ἀνάμνησιν — for my remembrance. In Jewish thought, remembrance was not merely recalling the past but bringing its saving reality into the present, as at Passover.","words":[{"strongs":"G2168","hebrew":"εὐχαριστέω","translit":"eucharistéō","english":"given thanks"},{"strongs":"G2806","hebrew":"κλάω","translit":"kláō","english":"broke"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G364","hebrew":"ἀνάμνησις","translit":"anámnēsis","english":"memory"}]},{"v":25,"text":"In the same way he also took the cup, after supper, saying, “This cup is the new covenant in my blood. Do this, as often as you drink, in memory of me.”","understanding":"“After supper” — the cup came after the meal, consistent with the Passover setting. “This cup is the καινὴ διαθήκη — the new covenant — in my blood.” Jeremiah 31:31–34 had promised a new covenant in which God would write His law on hearts and remember sins no more. Jesus says it is established by His blood.","words":[{"strongs":"G4221","hebrew":"ποτήριον","translit":"potḗrion","english":"cup"},{"strongs":"G1173","hebrew":"δεῖπνον","translit":"deîpnon","english":"supper"},{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G364","hebrew":"ἀνάμνησις","translit":"anámnēsis","english":"memory"}]},{"v":26,"text":"For as often as you eat this bread and drink this cup, you proclaim the Lord’s death until he comes.","understanding":"Paul's own comment: every time you eat this bread and drink this cup, you καταγγέλλω — proclaim, announce publicly — the Lord's death “until he comes.” The Supper is a sermon without words. It looks back to the cross and forward to the return. And it is spoken to the whole room: the way you treat each other at the Table either proclaims the Lord's death or contradicts it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2068","hebrew":"ἐσθίω","translit":"esthíō","english":"eat"},{"strongs":"G740","hebrew":"ἄρτος","translit":"ártos","english":"bread"},{"strongs":"G4221","hebrew":"ποτήριον","translit":"potḗrion","english":"cup"},{"strongs":"G2605","hebrew":"καταγγέλλω","translit":"katangéllō","english":"proclaim"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"comes"}]},{"v":27,"text":"Therefore whoever eats this bread or drinks the Lord’s cup in a way unworthy of the Lord will be guilty of the body and the blood of the Lord.","understanding":"Ἀναξίως — in an unworthy manner. The adverb describes how one eats, not who eats. Nobody is worthy of the Lord's Table. The question is whether you come in a manner consistent with what it means. In Corinth, eating while the poor went hungry was an unworthy manner. Such a person is ἔνοχος — liable, guilty — of the body and blood of the Lord: he sins against the very death the Supper proclaims.","words":[{"strongs":"G371","hebrew":"ἀναξίως","translit":"anaxíōs","english":"in a way unworthy"},{"strongs":"G1777","hebrew":"ἔνοχος","translit":"énochos","english":"guilty"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"}]},{"v":28,"text":"But let a man examine himself, and so let him eat of the bread, and drink of the cup.","understanding":"Δοκιμάζω — let him test himself, examine himself, the assayer's word of 3:13. Not to find out whether he is good enough — he is not — but to make sure he is coming in the right way: with repentance, faith and love for the body. “And so let him eat.” The goal of self-examination is not to keep people away but to bring them rightly.","words":[{"strongs":"G1381","hebrew":"δοκιμάζω","translit":"dokimázō","english":"examine"},{"strongs":"G2068","hebrew":"ἐσθίω","translit":"esthíō","english":"eat"}]},{"v":29,"text":"For he who eats and drinks in an unworthy way eats and drinks judgment to himself, if he doesn’t discern the Lord’s body.","understanding":"Whoever eats and drinks without διακρίνω — discerning, recognising — “the body” eats and drinks κρίμα, judgment, on himself. What body? In context, the church as Christ's body is strongly in view (10:17 — one loaf, one body; and the whole problem here is despising fellow members). Many also hear a reference to the Lord's body given on the cross. The two cannot be separated: you cannot honour the body on the cross while humiliating the body at the table.","words":[{"strongs":"G2068","hebrew":"ἐσθίω","translit":"esthíō","english":"eats"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G1252","hebrew":"διακρίνω","translit":"diakrínō","english":"discern"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":30,"text":"For this cause many among you are weak and sickly, and not a few sleep.","understanding":"A sobering statement. “For this cause many among you are ἀσθενεῖς — weak — and ἄρρωστοι — sick — and not a few κοιμάω — sleep,” the Christian word for death. Paul attributes illness and even death in the church to God's discipline for abusing the Lord's Supper. This is not a general rule that all sickness is caused by sin; Scripture rejects that idea (John 9:3). It is a specific word about a specific sin in a specific church.","words":[{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weak"},{"strongs":"G732","hebrew":"ἄῤῥωστος","translit":"árrhōstos","english":"sickly"},{"strongs":"G2837","hebrew":"κοιμάω","translit":"koimáō","english":"sleep"}]},{"v":31,"text":"For if we discerned ourselves, we wouldn’t be judged.","understanding":"If we διακρίνω — judged, discerned — ourselves rightly, we would not be κρίνω — judged — by God. The same root, played on: honest self-judgment now prevents divine judgment later.","words":[{"strongs":"G1252","hebrew":"διακρίνω","translit":"diakrínō","english":"discerned"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"}]},{"v":32,"text":"But when we are judged, we are punished by the Lord, that we may not be condemned with the world.","understanding":"But even when God judges His people, His purpose is παιδεύω — to discipline, to train as a child — “that we may not be κατακρίνω, condemned, with the world.” God's discipline of believers is the opposite of condemnation. It is a Father's correction to keep His children from the fate of the world.","words":[{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"},{"strongs":"G3811","hebrew":"παιδεύω","translit":"paideúō","english":"punished (disciplined)"},{"strongs":"G2632","hebrew":"κατακρίνω","translit":"katakrínō","english":"condemned"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":33,"text":"Therefore, my brothers, when you come together to eat, wait for one another.","understanding":"The practical solution is simple: when you come together to eat, ἐκδέχομαι — wait for, welcome — one another. Do not start until everyone, including the poorest slave, has arrived. The Lord's Table is a family table.","words":[{"strongs":"G4905","hebrew":"συνέρχομαι","translit":"synérchomai","english":"come together"},{"strongs":"G2068","hebrew":"ἐσθίω","translit":"esthíō","english":"eat"},{"strongs":"G1551","hebrew":"ἐκδέχομαι","translit":"ekdéchomai","english":"wait for"}]},{"v":34,"text":"But if anyone is hungry, let him eat at home, lest your coming together be for judgment. The rest I will set in order whenever I come.","understanding":"If anyone is so hungry that he cannot wait, let him eat at home first, so that the gathering does not bring κρίμα, judgment. The church meal is not about satisfying appetite; it is about communion. Paul will set the rest in order — διατάσσω — when he comes.","words":[{"strongs":"G3983","hebrew":"πεινάω","translit":"peináō","english":"hungry"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"home"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G1299","hebrew":"διατάσσω","translit":"diatássō","english":"set in order"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Dining Room and the Courtyard","verseRef":"v. 17-22","image":"Picture a large villa in Corinth on a Sunday evening — the kind of house wealthy believers like Gaius owned. A villa excavated near Corinth has often been used to illustrate the scene: an elegant dining room, the triclinium, with couches for perhaps nine or ten guests, and a larger open courtyard, the atrium, beyond. The host's friends — people of means who did not have to work late — arrive early and recline on the couches. The best food is served to them, and the wine flows. Hours later, as darkness falls, the dockworkers and slaves arrive, tired and hungry from a full day's labour. They crowd into the courtyard. By the time they get there, the good food is gone, and some of the people on the couches are drunk. Then everyone takes the bread and the cup together, and calls it the Lord's Supper.","meaning":"Paul's verdict is devastating: “it is not the Lord’s supper that you eat.” They had all the right words and actions, but the way they ate denied what the meal meant. The Lord's Supper proclaims the death of the One who gave Himself for the poor, the slave and the outcast — and the Corinthians were using it to reinforce the social hierarchy that the cross had abolished. That is why Paul says they despise the church of God and humiliate those who have nothing. James warned against the same sin: honouring the man in fine clothes and telling the poor man to sit on the floor (James 2:1–4). Every time a church divides along lines of wealth, class, race or status at the Lord's Table, it eats something other than the Lord's Supper."},{"title":"On the Night He Was Handed Over","verseRef":"v. 23-26","image":"An upper room in Jerusalem, lit by oil lamps. Thirteen men reclining around a low table for the Passover meal. Outside, the city is full of pilgrims; somewhere in the darkness, plans are being made. One of the thirteen has already arranged to betray his master for thirty pieces of silver. And the master knows. He takes a flat loaf of unleavened bread, gives thanks, breaks it, and hands it to them: this is my body, which is for you. After supper He takes a cup of wine: this cup is the new covenant in my blood. Do this in remembrance of me. A few hours later He is arrested in a garden.","meaning":"Paul notes that the Lord's Supper was instituted “on the night in which he was betrayed” — the same Greek verb as “delivered”: handed over. Jesus gave His friends a meal to remember Him by on the night He was handed over to death by a friend. And every time the church eats it, Paul says, “you proclaim the Lord’s death until he comes.” The Supper is a sermon. It announces that Jesus died for sinners, that a new covenant has been established in His blood, and that He is coming again. That sermon is either confirmed or contradicted by how the church treats its members at the table. You cannot proclaim the self-giving death of Christ while grabbing the best for yourself."},{"title":"Discerning the Body","verseRef":"v. 27-32","image":"Picture a believer in Corinth holding the bread in his hand. He looks at it and thinks of Christ's body broken on the cross. That is right, but Paul asks him to look further. He wants him to lift his eyes from the bread and look around the room — at the slave standing hungry in the courtyard, at the widow who arrived late, at the poor man whose plate is empty. They too are the body. “Because there is one loaf of bread, we, who are many, are one body” (10:17). To discern the body is to see Christ in the bread and Christ's people in the room, and to refuse to separate the two.","meaning":"This passage has sometimes frightened sensitive believers away from the Lord's Table, as if they had to be perfect to partake. But Paul's word is ἀναξίως, unworthily — an adverb describing the manner of eating, not the worthiness of the eater. No one is worthy. The question is whether you come in a way that honours what the Supper means. Self-examination is not about proving you are good enough; it is about coming in repentance, faith and love. “Let a man examine himself, and so let him eat.” The goal is to eat. And even God's discipline, Paul says, is a Father's correction “that we may not be condemned with the world.” Come to the Table — but come with eyes open to the cross and to your brothers and sisters."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Imitate Me as I Imitate Christ (v. 1-2)","body":"Paul's call to imitation closes chapter 10; he then praises the Corinthians for holding to the traditions he delivered."},{"label":"Head Coverings and Creation Order (v. 3-16)","body":"Arguing from headship (Christ, man, God), creation, the angels, nature and church practice, Paul instructs men to pray and prophesy uncovered and women to pray and prophesy covered — while insisting that in the Lord man and woman are interdependent, and all things are from God."},{"label":"Divisions at the Lord's Supper (v. 17-22)","body":"Their gatherings do more harm than good: the wealthy eat their own meals first while the poor go hungry, so what they eat is not the Lord's Supper, and they despise God's church."},{"label":"The Tradition of the Lord's Supper (v. 23-26)","body":"Paul repeats the words of Jesus on the night He was betrayed: the bread is His body for us, the cup is the new covenant in His blood; eating and drinking proclaim the Lord's death until He comes."},{"label":"Eating Worthily and Discerning the Body (v. 27-34)","body":"Eating in an unworthy manner makes one guilty of the body and blood of the Lord; each should examine himself and discern the body; God's discipline has fallen on some, but it is to save them from condemnation; wait for one another."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Head Coverings: What Is Clear and What Is Not","paragraphs":["Verses 2 to 16 are among the most difficult in Paul's letters, and honesty requires saying so plainly. Paul gives several interwoven arguments — from headship (v. 3), shame (vv. 5–6), creation (vv. 7–9), the angels (v. 10), nature (vv. 14–15) and church practice (v. 16) — and some of his references were clear to the Corinthians in ways they are not to us. But the core is not hard to see, and it is important not to let the difficult details obscure the clear ones. First, and often overlooked: women prayed and prophesied in the Corinthian assembly (v. 5), and Paul does not forbid it. He regulates how it is done. The church of Pentecost was one where “your daughters will prophesy” (Acts 2:17), and Philip had four daughters who prophesied (Acts 21:9).","Second, the cultural setting. In the Greco-Roman world, a respectable married woman normally covered her head in public — usually by drawing her outer garment, or a shawl, over her hair. To appear uncovered in a public gathering could signal immodesty, rejection of her marriage, or association with certain pagan cults where women let their hair down in ecstatic worship. For men, by contrast, Roman practice sometimes involved covering the head with the toga when offering sacrifice. Paul wants Christian worship to be distinct from both: men should not adopt a religious head-covering, and women should not abandon theirs. Some Corinthian women may have concluded that because “there is neither male nor female; for you are all one in Christ Jesus” (Galatians 3:28), all visible distinctions should go. Paul says no. Equality in Christ does not erase the God-given distinction between men and women.","Third, the theology. Verse 3 sets out a pattern of headship: Christ is the head of every man, the man is the head of the woman, and God is the head of Christ. Whether κεφαλή means primarily authority or source is debated; verses 8 and 9 show that the idea of source from the creation account is central here, while the broader biblical pattern (Ephesians 5:23) shows that it also includes leadership. Notice, though, that Paul includes “the head of Christ is God” — and no orthodox Christian believes the Son is inferior to the Father. Headship and equality are not opposites. And Paul immediately guards against any notion of male superiority: “Nevertheless, neither is the woman independent of the man, nor the man independent of the woman, in the Lord. For as woman came from man, so a man also comes through a woman; but all things are from God” (vv. 11–12). How should believers today apply this? Christians differ on whether the specific practice of head-covering is binding across cultures. What is binding is the principle beneath it: men and women are equal in worth and both called to minister, and they are to honour, not erase, the distinctions God built into creation — and to do it in a way that brings honour, not shame, to their head."]},{"heading":"Not the Lord's Supper: When the Table Divides the Body","paragraphs":["From verse 17 Paul turns to a far more serious problem, and the praise of verse 2 gives way to rebuke: “I don’t praise you, that you come together not for the better but for the worse.” In the early church, the Lord's Supper was celebrated as part of a full fellowship meal, sometimes called the love feast (Jude 12). Churches met in homes, and in Corinth that meant the homes of wealthier members, since only they had space for a whole congregation. A well-known reconstruction, based on a villa excavated near Corinth, suggests that the dining room might seat perhaps nine to twelve people, while the rest would gather in the courtyard. The host's social equals would be honoured with places in the dining room, often with better food — a common practice at Roman banquets, where guests were served according to their rank.","The result was a disaster. Wealthy members, free to arrive early, began eating and drinking their own lavish suppers. Poorer members — slaves, labourers, people who had to work until evening — arrived late to find little or nothing left. “One is hungry, and another is drunken” (v. 21). The social divisions of Corinth were being reproduced, even intensified, at the very meal that proclaimed their abolition. That is why Paul says, “it is not the Lord’s supper that you eat” (v. 20). They had the bread and the cup and the right words, but the way they ate contradicted the meaning of the meal. They despised the church of God and humiliated the poor (v. 22).","Verse 19 contains a note of bitter irony: “there also must be factions among you, that those who are approved may be revealed among you.” Paul is not saying divisions are good. He is saying that God uses even the church's sin to expose what is real. When the table divides along lines of class and status, it becomes clear who truly understands the gospel and who does not. This is not merely a first-century problem. Every church that allows divisions of wealth, race, education, class or status to determine who is honoured and who is overlooked when it gathers is in danger of eating something other than the Lord's Supper. James saw the same sin in his churches (James 2:1–4). The cross levels the ground. The Table must do the same."]},{"heading":"Proclaiming His Death and Discerning His Body","paragraphs":["Paul's answer to the abuse of the Supper is not a new rule but a reminder of what the Supper is. Verses 23 to 26 are the earliest written account of the Lord's Supper that we have — earlier than any of the Gospels, written perhaps twenty-five years after the event. Paul uses the technical words of tradition: “I received... that which also I delivered.” This is not his invention; it is the church's sacred memory, going back to Jesus Himself. And it is remarkably close to Luke's account: “This is my body which is given for you. Do this in memory of me... This cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for you” (Luke 22:19–20). Notice the detail Paul chooses to include: “on the night in which he was betrayed.” The verb παραδίδωμι is the same as “delivered” in the previous line. The Lord handed the bread to His disciples on the night He was handed over to death. The Supper is born in self-giving.","Then Paul adds his own comment in verse 26: “For as often as you eat this bread and drink this cup, you proclaim the Lord’s death until he comes.” The Supper looks back to the cross and forward to the return, and in between it preaches. Every time the church eats, it announces that Jesus died for sinners and that He is coming again. And the way the church eats is part of that announcement. If the rich feast while the poor go hungry, the sermon is contradicted. That is why verse 27 says that whoever eats “in a way unworthy of the Lord” is guilty of His body and blood. The word ἀναξίως is an adverb describing the manner of eating, not the worthiness of the person. No one is worthy of the Table; that is the point of grace. The question is whether you come in a manner consistent with what Christ did.","Verses 28 and 29 then call for self-examination and for “discerning the body.” In context, “the body” very likely includes the church — Paul has just said in 10:17 that “we, who are many, are one body; for we all partake of the one loaf,” and the entire problem here is the despising of fellow members. Many readers also hear a reference to the Lord's body given on the cross, represented in the bread. The two belong together: to discern the body is to recognise Christ in the bread and Christ's people around the table, and to refuse to separate them. Paul's warning in verse 30 — that some in Corinth were weak, sick and had even died — is sobering, and it should not be generalised into a claim that all sickness is caused by sin, which Jesus explicitly rejected (John 9:3). It is a specific word about a specific abuse. But the goal of God's discipline is stated plainly: “we are punished by the Lord, that we may not be condemned with the world” (v. 32). It is the discipline of a Father who loves His children too much to let them go on destroying themselves. And the practical solution is simple and beautiful: “when you come together to eat, wait for one another” (v. 33)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["Paul's account of the Last Supper stands beside the Gospel accounts, and is closest to Luke's: “He took bread, and when he had given thanks, he broke, and gave it to them, saying, ‘This is my body which is given for you. Do this in memory of me.’ Likewise, he took the cup after supper, saying, ‘This cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for you’” (Luke 22:19–20). Matthew records the purpose of the blood: “for this is my blood of the new covenant, which is poured out for many for the remission of sins” (Matthew 26:28). And from the very beginning, the church gathered around this meal: “They continued steadfastly in the apostles’ teaching and fellowship, in the breaking of bread, and prayer” (Acts 2:42), and “breaking bread at home, they took their food with gladness and singleness of heart” (Acts 2:46). Notice “singleness of heart” — the opposite of the Corinthian divisions.","The meaning of eating Christ's body and blood had already been opened up by Jesus in His discourse at Capernaum: “unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink his blood, you don’t have life in yourselves. He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood has eternal life, and I will raise him up at the last day... He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood lives in me, and I in him” (John 6:53–54, 56). Whatever one's understanding of how Christ is present in the Supper, the New Testament is united that it is a real communion with Him — “a sharing of the blood of Christ” and “a sharing of the body of Christ” (10:16) — and that it is meant to bind His people to Him and to each other.","The idea that God disciplines His children in love, which Paul applies in verses 30 to 32, is developed in Hebrews: “My son, don’t take lightly the chastening of the Lord, nor faint when you are reproved by him; For whom the Lord loves, he chastens” (Hebrews 12:5–6). And: “he for our profit, that we may be partakers of his holiness. All chastening seems for the present to be not joyous but grievous; yet afterward it yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness to those who have been exercised thereby” (Hebrews 12:10–11). The risen Christ says the same to the church in Laodicea: “As many as I love, I reprove and chasten. Be zealous therefore, and repent” (Revelation 3:19). God's discipline of His church is never a sign that He has abandoned it. It is the proof that He has not."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Head","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","strongs":"G2776","verses":"v. 3 (×3), 4 (×2), 5 (×2), 7, 10","insight":"Strong's: “the head (as the part most readily taken hold of), literally or figuratively.” In this passage Paul uses κεφαλή both literally (the physical head that is covered or uncovered) and figuratively (Christ, man and God as “head”). The figurative meaning is debated: some scholars argue for “authority over,” others for “source” or “origin.” Both meanings appear in Greek literature, and both seem present here: verses 8 and 9 stress source (woman from man), while Ephesians 5:23 links headship to leadership and loving self-sacrifice. The decisive interpretive key is the third clause of verse 3: “the head of Christ is God.” The Son is equal to the Father in deity and yet honours the Father's headship. Headship in God's design is never superiority. It is an ordered relationship of honour."},{"english":"Traditions / teachings handed down","hebrew":"παράδοσις","translit":"parádosis","strongs":"G3862 (verb, deliver παραδίδωμι, paradídōmi, G3860; verb, receive παραλαμβάνω, paralambánō, G3880)","verses":"v. 2 (verbs in v. 2, 23)","insight":"Strong's defines παράδοσις as “transmission, i.e. (concretely) a precept; specially, the Jewish traditionary law,” παραδίδωμι as “to surrender, i.e yield up, intrust, transmit,” and παραλαμβάνω as “to receive near.” These were the technical terms for passing on and receiving sacred teaching. Jesus condemned human traditions that nullified God's word (Mark 7:8–13). But Paul uses the same word positively for the apostolic teaching that came from Christ. The Christian faith is not invented by each generation; it is received and handed on. Paul received the Lord's Supper (v. 23) and the gospel (15:3) and delivered them. Every believer stands in a chain of faithful transmission that goes back to Jesus. Your job is to receive it faithfully and hand it on intact."},{"english":"Remembrance / memorial","hebrew":"ἀνάμνησις","translit":"anámnēsis","strongs":"G364","verses":"v. 24, 25","insight":"Strong's: “recollection.” In biblical thought, remembrance is not just mental recall of a past event. When Israel remembered the Passover, they relived it — “It is because of that which Yahweh did for me when I came out of Egypt” (Exodus 13:8). The past saving event was made present to each generation. Jesus' command, “Do this in memory of me,” calls the church to that kind of remembrance: not simply thinking about the cross, but bringing its saving reality into the present gathering. Hebrews 10:3 uses the same word for the annual remembrance of sins in the old sacrifices. In the Lord's Supper, the church remembers not its sins but the One who took them away."},{"english":"Covenant","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","strongs":"G1242","verses":"v. 25","insight":"Strong's: “properly, a disposition, i.e. (specially) a contract (especially a devisory will).” In the Greek Old Testament it translates the Hebrew word for covenant — the solemn, binding relationship God establishes with His people. “The new covenant” points directly to Jeremiah 31:31–34, where God promises a new covenant not like the one at Sinai: He will put His law within them, write it on their hearts, be their God, and remember their sin no more. Jesus says that covenant is inaugurated “in my blood.” Every time you take the cup, you are drinking to the terms of a covenant in which God has promised to forgive you and to make you His. Hebrews 8 and 9 explore this at length."},{"english":"Discern / distinguish / judge rightly","hebrew":"διακρίνω","translit":"diakrínō","strongs":"G1252","verses":"v. 29, 31","insight":"Strong's: “to separate thoroughly, i.e. (literally and reflexively) to withdraw from, or (by implication) oppose; figuratively, to discriminate (by implication, decide), or (reflexively) hesitate.” Paul plays on words from the same root throughout verses 29 to 32: διακρίνω (discern), κρίμα (judgment), κρίνω (judge), κατακρίνω (condemn). To discern the body is to recognise it for what it is and treat it accordingly — to distinguish the Lord's Supper from an ordinary meal, and the people of God from a social hierarchy. If we discern ourselves rightly now, we will not fall under God's judgment. Spiritual discernment is not only about doctrine; it is about seeing Christ in His people and acting accordingly."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the way you honour or dishonour God's design for men and women, the divisions you tolerate at the Lord's Table, and the way you come to the bread and the cup.","items":[{"title":"Honouring the Design","body":"Whatever you conclude about head coverings, the Spirit is asking a deeper question: do you honour the way God made men and women? Our culture swings between two errors — erasing the distinctions altogether, or using them to dominate and diminish. Paul rejects both. Men and women are equal before God and interdependent in the Lord; both are called to pray and prophesy; and the distinctions God built into creation are to be honoured, not erased or abused. If you are a man, are you using your position to serve or to dominate? If you are a woman, are you embracing your God-given identity or resenting it? And all of us: are we honouring our head — Christ — in the way we worship?"},{"title":"Who Is in the Courtyard?","body":"Every church has its dining room and its courtyard. The people who are honoured and the people who are overlooked. The ones with influence and the ones who arrive late, tired, and find the good things already taken. The Spirit is asking you to look around your church and notice who is in the courtyard. The poor, the newcomers, the single parents, the elderly, the ones who do not fit, the ones from a different background. Are they welcomed at the table or merely tolerated at the edges? Have you been eating your own supper while they go hungry — for food, for friendship, for honour? “Wait for one another.” Go and sit with someone in the courtyard."},{"title":"Coming to the Table With Open Eyes","body":"You may have come to the Lord's Table many times without really seeing it. Or you may have stayed away, afraid you were not worthy. The Spirit wants to say two things. First: nobody is worthy. That is why Christ's body was broken and His blood poured out. Come. Second: come with your eyes open. Examine yourself honestly — not to prove you are good enough, but to confess what needs confessing. Then look at the bread and see His body given for you. Look around the room and see His body, the church. Is there someone in that body you need to forgive, reconcile with or honour before you eat? Then eat, and proclaim His death until He comes."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Imitation Check","body":"Paul said, “Be imitators of me, even as I also am of Christ.” Who are you imitating in your faith? Write down one or two names. Then ask: am I copying them because they look like Christ, or because they are impressive? And who is imitating you?"},{"title":"The Courtyard Check","body":"Think about your church gatherings. Write down the names of three people you rarely speak to or who seem to be on the edges. What do they have in common? Is there any way you have — even unintentionally — contributed to their being overlooked?"},{"title":"The Table Check","body":"Before the next time you take the Lord's Supper, ask: is there any unconfessed sin I need to bring to the Lord? Is there anyone in the body of Christ I am refusing to forgive, despising or ignoring? Am I coming as a spectator or as someone proclaiming the Lord's death until He comes?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Preparing for the Table","body":"Set aside fifteen minutes before your next Communion service. Read 1 Corinthians 11:23–32 slowly. Then pray through three questions: (1) Lord, what do I need to confess? (2) Lord, who in Your body do I need to make peace with or honour? (3) Lord, how can I proclaim Your death this week? Write down your answers. If there is someone you need to reconcile with, do it before you come, if possible (compare Matthew 5:23–24). Then come to the Table with gratitude, not fear."},{"title":"Waiting for One Another","body":"This month, practise verse 33 literally and figuratively. At your next church gathering, make a point of welcoming and sitting with someone who usually arrives late, sits alone, or is new. If your church shares meals, wait until everyone is served before you eat, and make sure the people who are usually last are well served. If you have the means, invite someone who could never repay you to share a meal at your home. Let the way you eat proclaim the Lord's death."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, on the night You were handed over, You took bread, gave thanks, broke it and said, ‘This is my body, which is for you.’ You took the cup and said, ‘This cup is the new covenant in my blood.’ Thank You. Thank You for Your body broken and Your blood poured out for me. Every time I eat and drink, let me proclaim Your death until You come. Father, I confess that I have not always discerned the body. I have come to Your Table carelessly, and I have overlooked brothers and sisters for whom Christ died. Forgive me for every time I have honoured the dining room and ignored the courtyard. Open my eyes to see Your people as You see them, and teach me to wait for one another. Lord, I submit to Your design for men and women. Where I have dishonoured it, by erasing what You made or abusing it, forgive me. Help me to honour my head, which is Christ, in the way I live and worship. And Father, thank You that when You discipline me, it is so that I will not be condemned with the world. I would rather judge myself honestly now than fall under Your judgment later. Search me, correct me, and bring me to Your Table with open eyes and a clean heart. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Night of the First Passover","ref":"Exodus 12:1-14; 13:3-10","relatedVerses":"v. 23-26","summary":"God commanded Israel to eat the Passover meal on the night He would bring them out of Egypt: a lamb without blemish, its blood on the doorposts, unleavened bread and bitter herbs. And He commanded them to keep it as a memorial forever, so that every generation would tell their children what the LORD had done. When Jesus gathered His disciples on the night He was handed over, He took the elements of that very meal and gave them a new meaning: the bread is His body, the cup is the new covenant in His blood. The Lord's Supper is the Passover fulfilled. Just as Israel remembered the night of their deliverance from slavery, the church remembers the night that led to its deliverance from sin — and proclaims it until He comes."},{"title":"Mephibosheth at the King's Table","ref":"2 Samuel 9:1-13","relatedVerses":"v. 20-22, 33","summary":"After David became king, he asked whether anyone was left from the house of Saul to whom he could show kindness for Jonathan's sake. He was told of Mephibosheth, Jonathan's son, who was lame in both feet and living in obscurity. David sent for him. Mephibosheth fell on his face and called himself a dead dog. But David restored to him all of Saul's land and said that he would eat at the king's table always, like one of the king's sons. A crippled man from a fallen family, with nothing to offer, was given a permanent seat at the royal table. That is what the Lord's Table is meant to be: a place where the weak and the overlooked are honoured as sons. The Corinthians had turned it into a place where the poor were humiliated. David's table shows what grace looks like when it sets a place for the least."},{"title":"Uzzah and the Holy Things","ref":"2 Samuel 6:1-11; 1 Chronicles 15:11-15","relatedVerses":"v. 27-32","summary":"David set out with great celebration to bring the ark of God to Jerusalem, but instead of having the Levites carry it on poles as the law required, they placed it on a new cart. When the oxen stumbled, Uzzah reached out his hand to steady the ark, and God struck him down. David was angry and afraid, and he left the ark at the house of Obed-edom, who was blessed for the next three months. Later David realised that they had not handled the holy things as God had commanded, and the ark was brought up the right way, with rejoicing. The story is a sobering reminder that holy things must be approached in the way God intends. The Corinthians had treated the Lord's Supper carelessly, and some had become sick and died. God's holiness is not a threat to those who come humbly. It is a danger to those who come carelessly."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":12,"chapterTitle":"One Spirit, Many Gifts, One Body — No Member Too Small to Matter","themeTitle":"“The Eye Can’t Tell the Hand, ‘I Have No Need for You’”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Now concerning spiritual things, brothers, I don’t want you to be ignorant.","understanding":"“Now concerning” — the fourth topic from their letter: τὰ πνευματικά, which can mean “spiritual things,” “spiritual gifts,” or “spiritual people.” The ambiguity may be deliberate; the Corinthians were proud of being πνευματικοί. Chapters 12 to 14 form one long argument, and chapter 13 stands at its centre. Paul does not want them to be ἀγνοέω — uninformed — about the one thing they thought they understood best.","words":[{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual things"},{"strongs":"G50","hebrew":"ἀγνοέω","translit":"agnoéō","english":"ignorant"}]},{"v":2,"text":"You know that when you were heathen, you were led away to those mute idols, however you might be led.","understanding":"A reminder of where they came from. When they were ἔθνη — pagans — they were ἀπάγω, led away, carried off, to ἄφωνα idols — voiceless, mute. Pagan religion in Corinth included ecstatic experiences, frenzied worship and trance-like states. Paul's point: being “carried away” in a religious experience is not in itself a sign of the Holy Spirit. They had been carried away before, by dead idols that could not speak.","words":[{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"heathen"},{"strongs":"G880","hebrew":"ἄφωνος","translit":"áphōnos","english":"mute"},{"strongs":"G1497","hebrew":"εἴδωλον","translit":"eídōlon","english":"idols"},{"strongs":"G520","hebrew":"ἀπάγω","translit":"apágō","english":"led away"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Therefore I make known to you that no man speaking by God’s Spirit says, “Jesus is accursed.” No one can say, “Jesus is Lord,” but by the Holy Spirit.","understanding":"Therefore the test of genuine spiritual experience is not intensity but confession. No one speaking by God's Spirit says, “Jesus is ἀνάθεμα” — accursed. And no one can truly say “Κύριος Ἰησοῦς — Jesus is Lord” except by the Holy Spirit. This was the earliest Christian confession (Romans 10:9). In a world where people were expected to say “Caesar is Lord,” it was dangerous. Decades later, officials urged the elderly bishop Polycarp simply to say “Lord Caesar” and offer incense; he refused and was burned. The Spirit's first work is not spectacle; it is to make Jesus Lord.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G331","hebrew":"ἀνάθεμα","translit":"anáthema","english":"accursed"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Now there are various kinds of gifts, but the same Spirit.","understanding":"Now three parallel statements, and a hidden Trinity. Διαιρέσεις — distributions, varieties — of χαρίσματα, grace-gifts — but the same Spirit. The word χάρισμα reminds them that every gift is grace, not achievement.","words":[{"strongs":"G1243","hebrew":"διαίρεσις","translit":"diaíresis","english":"various kinds"},{"strongs":"G5486","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","english":"gifts"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"}]},{"v":5,"text":"There are various kinds of service, and the same Lord.","understanding":"Varieties of διακονίαι — services, ministries, the word for waiting tables — but the same Lord. Every gift is a form of service, and every service is rendered to the one Lord Jesus.","words":[{"strongs":"G1243","hebrew":"διαίρεσις","translit":"diaíresis","english":"various kinds"},{"strongs":"G1248","hebrew":"διακονία","translit":"diakonía","english":"service"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]},{"v":6,"text":"There are various kinds of workings, but the same God, who works all things in all.","understanding":"Varieties of ἐνεργήματα — workings, effects, operations — but the same God who ἐνεργέω — energises, works — all of them in everyone. Spirit, Lord, God: verses 4 to 6 are one of the clearest early statements of the Trinity at work in the church. Gifts are from the Spirit, as service to the Lord, by the power of God the Father.","words":[{"strongs":"G1755","hebrew":"ἐνέργημα","translit":"enérgēma","english":"workings"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1754","hebrew":"ἐνεργέω","translit":"energéō","english":"works"}]},{"v":7,"text":"But to each one is given the manifestation of the Spirit for the profit of all.","understanding":"The governing principle of all spiritual gifts. To each one is given — δίδοται, a divine passive: God gives it — the φανέρωσις, the manifestation, the making-visible, of the Spirit, πρὸς τὸ συμφέρον — for the common good, for the benefit of all. Every believer has a gift. And no gift is given for personal display. It is given to build up others.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G5321","hebrew":"φανέρωσις","translit":"phanérōsis","english":"manifestation"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G4851","hebrew":"συμφέρω","translit":"symphérō","english":"profit"}]},{"v":8,"text":"For to one is given through the Spirit the word of wisdom, and to another the word of knowledge, according to the same Spirit;","understanding":"The list begins. A λόγος σοφίας — word of wisdom — perhaps a Spirit-given insight into how to apply God's truth in a situation. A λόγος γνώσεως — word of knowledge — perhaps Spirit-given understanding of God's truth or of a hidden matter. Paul does not define these precisely, and honest interpreters admit we cannot be certain of the exact boundaries. Notice that wisdom and knowledge, the gifts Corinth prized most, are listed first — and given by the same Spirit as all the rest.","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"}]},{"v":9,"text":"to another faith, by the same Spirit; and to another gifts of healings, by the same Spirit;","understanding":"Πίστις — faith. Not saving faith, which all believers have, but a special gift of faith to trust God for extraordinary things — the faith that “removes mountains” (13:2). Χαρίσματα ἰαμάτων — gifts of healings; both words are plural, suggesting many different gifts for many kinds of healing, not a single permanent power.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5486","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","english":"gifts"},{"strongs":"G2386","hebrew":"ἴαμα","translit":"íama","english":"healings"}]},{"v":10,"text":"and to another workings of miracles; and to another prophecy; and to another discerning of spirits; to another different kinds of languages; and to another the interpretation of languages.","understanding":"Ἐνεργήματα δυνάμεων — workings of powers, miracles. Προφητεία — prophecy, Spirit-given speech for the strengthening, encouragement and comfort of the church (14:3). Διακρίσεις πνευμάτων — the ability to distinguish between spirits, to discern whether a message or manifestation is from the Holy Spirit, a human spirit or an evil one. Γένη γλωσσῶν — kinds of tongues, languages. Ἑρμηνεία γλωσσῶν — interpretation of tongues. Note that tongues, the gift Corinth valued most highly, comes near the end of the list.","words":[{"strongs":"G1755","hebrew":"ἐνέργημα","translit":"enérgēma","english":"workings"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"miracles"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"},{"strongs":"G1253","hebrew":"διάκρισις","translit":"diákrisis","english":"discerning"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G2058","hebrew":"ἑρμηνεία","translit":"hermēneía","english":"interpretation"}]},{"v":11,"text":"But the one and the same Spirit produces all of these, distributing to each one separately as he desires.","understanding":"The sovereignty of the Spirit. One and the same Spirit ἐνεργέω — works — all of these, διαιρέω — distributing, apportioning — to each one individually “as he desires.” Βούλομαι is a word of deliberate will. The Spirit is not a force; He is a Person who chooses. You do not choose your gift. He does. That ends all boasting and all envy at once.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1754","hebrew":"ἐνεργέω","translit":"energéō","english":"produces"},{"strongs":"G1244","hebrew":"διαιρέω","translit":"diairéō","english":"distributing"},{"strongs":"G1014","hebrew":"βούλομαι","translit":"boúlomai","english":"desires"}]},{"v":12,"text":"For as the body is one, and has many members, and all the members of the body, being many, are one body; so also is Christ.","understanding":"Now the great image. The body is one and has many μέλη, members, limbs; all the members, many as they are, are one body — “so also is Christ.” Notice: not “so also is the church,” but “so also is Christ.” The church is so united to Christ that Paul can call it by His name.","words":[{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"},{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"members"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"}]},{"v":13,"text":"For in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body, whether Jews or Greeks, whether bond or free; and were all given to drink into one Spirit.","understanding":"How did we become one body? “In one Spirit we were all baptized into one body.” Every believer, without exception — “all” — has been immersed into the body of Christ by the Spirit. Jews and Greeks, slaves and free: the deepest divisions of the ancient world were dissolved. And we were all given one Spirit to ποτίζω — drink. The same Spirit poured into every believer.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G907","hebrew":"βαπτίζω","translit":"baptízō","english":"baptized"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bond"},{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"free"},{"strongs":"G4222","hebrew":"ποτίζω","translit":"potízō","english":"given to drink"}]},{"v":14,"text":"For the body is not one member, but many.","understanding":"The simplest possible sentence, and the foundation of everything that follows: the body is not one member, but many. Diversity is not a problem to be solved. It is the design.","words":[{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"member"}]},{"v":15,"text":"If the foot would say, “Because I’m not the hand, I’m not part of the body,” it is not therefore not part of the body.","understanding":"The first absurdity: the inferiority complex. A πούς, foot, says: because I am not a χείρ, hand, I don't belong to the body. Feet in the ancient world were regarded as lowly; hands were skilled and honoured. Some in Corinth felt that because they did not have the prized gifts, they did not really matter. Paul says: you are still part of the body.","words":[{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"foot"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":16,"text":"If the ear would say, “Because I’m not the eye, I’m not part of the body,” it’s not therefore not part of the body.","understanding":"The same absurdity with the ear and the eye. The οὖς, ear, envies the ὀφθαλμός, eye — the organ most associated with understanding and perception in Greek thought. Wishing to be a different member does not make you less a member.","words":[{"strongs":"G3775","hebrew":"οὖς","translit":"oûs","english":"ear"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eye"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":17,"text":"If the whole body were an eye, where would the hearing be? If the whole were hearing, where would the smelling be?","understanding":"Imagine a body that was only an eye — a giant eyeball rolling down the street. Where would the ἀκοή, the hearing, be? If everything were hearing, where would the ὄσφρησις, the sense of smell, be? A church where everyone had the same gift would be a monstrosity.","words":[{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eye"},{"strongs":"G189","hebrew":"ἀκοή","translit":"akoḗ","english":"hearing"},{"strongs":"G3750","hebrew":"ὄσφρησις","translit":"ósphrēsis","english":"smelling"}]},{"v":18,"text":"But now God has set the members, each one of them, in the body, just as he desired.","understanding":"The answer to both the inferiority complex and the superiority complex: God τίθημι — placed, set — each of the members in the body καθὼς ἠθέλησεν, just as He wanted. Your place in the body is not an accident. It is God's design. That is why comparison is so foolish.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5087","hebrew":"τίθημι","translit":"títhēmi","english":"set"},{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"members"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"desired"}]},{"v":19,"text":"If they were all one member, where would the body be?","understanding":"If all were a single member, “where would the body be?” There would be no body at all. The body exists precisely in the many-ness of its members.","words":[{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"member"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":20,"text":"But now they are many members, but one body.","understanding":"The summary: many members, one body. Unity is not uniformity. Diversity is not division.","words":[{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"members"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":21,"text":"The eye can’t tell the hand, “I have no need for you,” or again the head to the feet, “I have no need for you.”","understanding":"The second absurdity: the superiority complex. The eye cannot say to the hand, “I have no χρεία — no need — of you,” nor the head to the feet. Some in Corinth, proud of their spectacular gifts, looked down on those with ordinary ones. Paul says the most exalted member still needs the humblest.","words":[{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eye"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G5532","hebrew":"χρεία","translit":"chreía","english":"need"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"}]},{"v":22,"text":"No, much rather, those members of the body which seem to be weaker are necessary.","understanding":"On the contrary — πολλῷ μᾶλλον, much more — the members that seem ἀσθενέστερα, weaker, are ἀναγκαῖα — necessary, indispensable. Think of the internal organs: the heart, the liver, the kidneys. You never see them. They do not perform in public. And you cannot live a day without them.","words":[{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"members"},{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weaker"},{"strongs":"G316","hebrew":"ἀναγκαῖος","translit":"anankaîos","english":"necessary"}]},{"v":23,"text":"Those parts of the body which we think to be less honorable, on those we bestow more abundant honor; and our unpresentable parts have more abundant propriety;","understanding":"The parts we think less ἔντιμα, honourable, we clothe with more abundant τιμή, honour; and our ἀσχήμονα — our unpresentable, private parts — are treated with greater εὐσχημοσύνη, modesty, decorum. We instinctively protect and cover the parts of the body that are most vulnerable.","words":[{"strongs":"G820","hebrew":"ἄτιμος","translit":"átimos","english":"less honorable"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G4055","hebrew":"περισσότερος","translit":"perissóteros","english":"more abundant"},{"strongs":"G809","hebrew":"ἀσχήμων","translit":"aschḗmōn","english":"unpresentable"},{"strongs":"G2157","hebrew":"εὐσχημοσύνη","translit":"euschēmosýnē","english":"propriety"}]},{"v":24,"text":"whereas our presentable parts have no such need. But God composed the body together, giving more abundant honor to the inferior part,","understanding":"Our presentable parts need no special treatment. But God συγκεράννυμι — mixed together, blended, composed — the body, giving more abundant honour to the part that ὑστερέω, lacks it. The same verb as 1:7 (“you come behind in no gift”). God's design deliberately honours the members the world would overlook.","words":[{"strongs":"G2157","hebrew":"εὐσχημοσύνη","translit":"euschēmosýnē","english":"presentable (propriety)"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4786","hebrew":"συγκεράννυμι","translit":"synkeránnymi","english":"composed together"},{"strongs":"G5302","hebrew":"ὑστερέω","translit":"hysteréō","english":"inferior"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"}]},{"v":25,"text":"that there should be no division in the body, but that the members should have the same care for one another.","understanding":"The purpose: that there be no σχίσμα — the word of 1:10 and 11:18 — in the body, but that the members have the same μεριμνάω, care, for one another. The word for anxious care in 7:32–34 is used here positively: members should be as concerned for one another as they are for themselves.","words":[{"strongs":"G4978","hebrew":"σχίσμα","translit":"schísma","english":"division"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"members"},{"strongs":"G3309","hebrew":"μεριμνάω","translit":"merimnáō","english":"care"}]},{"v":26,"text":"When one member suffers, all the members suffer with it. Or when one member is honored, all the members rejoice with it.","understanding":"Συμπάσχω — when one member suffers, all suffer with it. Συγχαίρω — when one member is honoured, all rejoice with it. This is the body at its healthiest: a stubbed toe makes the whole body wince; good news for one part makes the whole body glad. Envy is impossible in a body that rejoices with the honoured member.","words":[{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffers"},{"strongs":"G4841","hebrew":"συμπάσχω","translit":"sympáschō","english":"suffer with"},{"strongs":"G1392","hebrew":"δοξάζω","translit":"doxázō","english":"honored"},{"strongs":"G4796","hebrew":"συγχαίρω","translit":"synchaírō","english":"rejoice with"}]},{"v":27,"text":"Now you are the body of Christ, and members individually.","understanding":"The direct application: “Now you are the body of Christ, and members individually.” Not “a” body; the body of Christ in Corinth. And μέλη ἐκ μέρους — members, each with your own part. You are not the whole body. You are not nothing. You are a member.","words":[{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"members"}]},{"v":28,"text":"God has set some in the assembly: first apostles, second prophets, third teachers, then miracle workers, then gifts of healings, helps, governments, and various kinds of languages.","understanding":"A second list, this time partly ranked: first ἀπόστολοι, second προφῆται, third διδάσκαλοι — the foundational ministries of the word that build up the church — then other gifts. Notice two gifts that are easy to overlook: ἀντιλήψεις, helps, acts of assistance and support, and κυβερνήσεις — administration, guidance, the word for a ship's pilot steering through the harbour. And “kinds of tongues” is again listed last.","words":[{"strongs":"G5087","hebrew":"τίθημι","translit":"títhēmi","english":"set"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostles"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G1320","hebrew":"διδάσκαλος","translit":"didáskalos","english":"teachers"},{"strongs":"G484","hebrew":"ἀντίληψις","translit":"antílēpsis","english":"helps"},{"strongs":"G2941","hebrew":"κυβέρνησις","translit":"kybérnēsis","english":"governments"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"}]},{"v":29,"text":"Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are all teachers? Are all miracle workers?","understanding":"A series of questions, each in Greek expecting the answer “No.” Are all apostles? No. Are all prophets? No. Are all teachers? No. Are all workers of miracles? No. The diversity of the body is God's intention.","words":[{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostles"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G1320","hebrew":"διδάσκαλος","translit":"didáskalos","english":"teachers"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"miracle workers"}]},{"v":30,"text":"Do all have gifts of healings? Do all speak with various languages? Do all interpret?","understanding":"Do all have gifts of healings? Do all speak in tongues? Do all interpret? No. The grammar expects “No” every time. Whatever Corinth believed, Paul says that no single gift — including tongues — is given to every believer or required as proof of spirituality.","words":[{"strongs":"G5486","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","english":"gifts"},{"strongs":"G2386","hebrew":"ἴαμα","translit":"íama","english":"healings"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G1329","hebrew":"διερμηνεύω","translit":"diermēneúō","english":"interpret"}]},{"v":31,"text":"But earnestly desire the best gifts. Moreover, I show a most excellent way to you.","understanding":"Ζηλόω — strive for, desire earnestly — the greater gifts; in chapter 14 he will show that these are the gifts that build up others most, especially prophecy. “Moreover, I show you a καθ’ ὑπερβολήν way” — a way beyond all measure, a way that surpasses everything. Not another gift, but the way in which all gifts must be exercised. The next chapter is that way.","words":[{"strongs":"G2206","hebrew":"ζηλόω","translit":"zēlóō","english":"earnestly desire"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"best"},{"strongs":"G5236","hebrew":"ὑπερβολή","translit":"hyperbolḗ","english":"most excellent"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"way"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Clay Hands and Feet at the Shrine of Asclepius","verseRef":"v. 14-21","image":"On the northern edge of ancient Corinth stood a sanctuary to Asclepius, the god of healing. Archaeologists excavating there found a remarkable collection of offerings: terracotta models of human body parts — hands, feet, legs, arms, eyes, ears — left by worshippers who had prayed to be healed of a specific ailment. Picture them on the shelves of the shrine: a clay foot here, a clay ear there, a pair of eyes, a hand, each one separate, lifeless, cut off from any body. Every Corinthian knew those shelves. Now hear Paul describe a foot saying it does not belong because it is not a hand, an ear saying it does not belong because it is not an eye, an eye telling the hand it is not needed.","meaning":"Whether or not Paul had the Asclepius offerings in mind — and some scholars think he may have — the image fits his point perfectly. A body part on its own is just a clay model on a shelf: lifeless, useless, a sign of sickness. A body part in the body is alive and essential. The Corinthians were acting like separated limbs — the foot jealous of the hand, the eye despising the hand. Paul says: God has placed each member in the body just as He wanted (v. 18). You are not the whole body, and you are not a clay limb on a shelf. You belong. And you need the members you think you do not need."},{"title":"The Organs You Never See","verseRef":"v. 22-24","image":"Think about your own body for a moment. The parts people notice are your face, your hands, your voice, your eyes. But right now, out of sight, your heart is beating about seventy times a minute without your permission. Your liver is filtering your blood. Your kidneys are balancing your fluids. Your lungs are drawing breath. You will never see these organs, and nobody will ever compliment you on them. And if any one of them stopped for long, you would die, however beautiful your face or skilled your hands.","meaning":"“Much rather, those members of the body which seem to be weaker are necessary” (v. 22). In every church there are people whose gifts are public and visible — preaching, leading worship, healing, prophesying — and people whose gifts are hidden: praying in secret, cleaning the building, caring for children, visiting the sick, managing the finances, giving quietly. The world honours the face and ignores the kidneys. God does the opposite: He has composed the body “giving more abundant honor to the inferior part” (v. 24). The Roman world used the image of the body to tell the lower classes to stay in their place; Paul uses it to give the overlooked members special honour. If your gift is hidden, it is not small. And if your gift is visible, do not imagine you could live without the members no one sees."},{"title":"The Stubbed Toe","verseRef":"v. 25-26","image":"You walk across a dark room at night and strike your little toe against the leg of a bed. Instantly your whole body reacts. You gasp. Your hands fly to your foot. Your eyes water. Your face twists. You hop on the other leg. Your mind can think of nothing else for a few seconds. No part of your body says, “That's just the toe's problem.” Now picture the opposite: someone hands you a gift you have longed for, and the joy floods your whole body — your face lights up, your hands reach out, your heart races.","meaning":"That is what Paul means: “When one member suffers, all the members suffer with it. Or when one member is honored, all the members rejoice with it” (v. 26). This is the healthy body of Christ. It feels the pain of its weakest member as its own pain. And it celebrates the honour of any member as its own honour. That makes jealousy impossible, because your brother's success is your success. And it makes indifference impossible, because your sister's grief is your grief. Paul tells the Romans the same: “Rejoice with those who rejoice. Weep with those who weep” (Romans 12:15). Ask yourself: when someone in your church is honoured, do you rejoice — or do you wince?"}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Test of the Spirit (v. 1-3)","body":"The Corinthians had been carried away by mute idols; the true mark of the Spirit is not ecstasy but the confession “Jesus is Lord.”"},{"label":"One God, Many Gifts (v. 4-11)","body":"Varieties of gifts, services and workings come from the same Spirit, Lord and God; each believer receives a manifestation of the Spirit for the common good; the Spirit distributes as He wills."},{"label":"One Body, Many Members (v. 12-20)","body":"As the body is one with many members, so is Christ; all were baptized by one Spirit into one body; no member can say it does not belong because it is not another member; God has arranged the members as He chose."},{"label":"The Honour of the Weaker Members (v. 21-26)","body":"No member can say it does not need another; the weaker members are indispensable, and God gives greater honour to those that lack it, so that there will be no division and all members will suffer and rejoice together."},{"label":"You Are the Body of Christ (v. 27-31)","body":"God has appointed apostles, prophets, teachers and other gifts in the church; not all have the same gift; seek the greater gifts — and now Paul will show a more excellent way."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Real Test of Spirituality: Who Is Lord?","paragraphs":["Chapters 12 to 14 deal with the most visible and exciting aspect of Corinthian church life: spiritual gifts, especially speaking in tongues. And Paul begins in a surprising place. Before he discusses any gift, he reminds the Corinthians of their pagan past: “when you were heathen, you were led away to those mute idols, however you might be led” (v. 2). The verb suggests being carried away, swept along. Pagan worship in Corinth included frenzied, ecstatic experiences — trances, prophetic utterances, emotional highs — in the cults of Dionysus, Cybele and others. The point is crucial. Spiritual intensity is not, in itself, proof of the Holy Spirit. The Corinthians had experienced religious ecstasy before they knew Christ, and it came from idols that could not even speak.","So what is the test? Verse 3: “no man speaking by God’s Spirit says, ‘Jesus is accursed.’ No one can say, ‘Jesus is Lord,’ but by the Holy Spirit.” We do not know exactly why anyone in Corinth would have said “Jesus is accursed” — perhaps it was a hypothetical, perhaps something said in a trance, perhaps something heard in the synagogue, where a crucified man could be regarded as cursed (Deuteronomy 21:23). But the positive point is clear. The decisive mark of the Spirit's work is not how spectacular your experience is, but whether it exalts Jesus as Lord. Κύριος Ἰησοῦς — “Jesus is Lord” — was the earliest Christian creed (Romans 10:9; Philippians 2:11). In the Roman Empire it was a costly confession; citizens were expected to acknowledge Caesar as lord, and some Christians later died for refusing.","This test applies to every age. A ministry, a meeting, a manifestation or a prophecy is not proved genuine by its emotional power, its size or its supernatural effects. It is proved by whether it leads people to bow to Jesus Christ as Lord — in their confession and in their lives. Jesus Himself said that the Spirit “will testify about me” (John 15:26) and “He will glorify me” (John 16:14). The Spirit's first and greatest work is not to draw attention to Himself, or to the person through whom He works, but to Jesus. Any spirituality that makes much of the gift and little of the Giver has already failed the test of verse 3."]},{"heading":"Gifts From the Triune God, for the Common Good","paragraphs":["Verses 4 to 6 are a quiet masterpiece. “Now there are various kinds of gifts, but the same Spirit. There are various kinds of service, and the same Lord. There are various kinds of workings, but the same God, who works all things in all.” Spirit, Lord, God: one of the earliest and clearest statements in the New Testament of the Trinity working together in the church. Each statement also teaches something about the gifts. They are χαρίσματα — gifts of grace, not achievements. They are διακονίαι — forms of service, not status symbols. And they are ἐνεργήματα — workings of God's power, not human abilities. Every spiritual gift is grace received, service rendered, and power from God.","Then verse 7 states the principle that governs everything in chapters 12 to 14: “to each one is given the manifestation of the Spirit for the profit of all.” Two things are clear. First, every believer has a gift — “to each one.” There are no spectators in the body of Christ. Second, every gift is given “for the profit of all” — for the common good, not for private display. The Corinthians treated their gifts as personal achievements that proved their spirituality. Paul says they are gifts given to build up others. The list that follows in verses 8 to 10 is not exhaustive; other lists in Romans 12:6–8, Ephesians 4:11 and 1 Peter 4:10–11 include gifts not mentioned here, such as teaching, encouragement, giving, leadership, mercy, evangelism and pastoring. Paul is illustrating diversity, not producing a complete catalogue.","Verse 11 then grounds it all in the sovereignty of the Spirit: “the one and the same Spirit produces all of these, distributing to each one separately as he desires.” Βούλομαι is a word of deliberate choice. The Spirit is not an impersonal force dispensing power; He is a Person who decides. That truth has two great implications. It ends boasting, because you did not earn your gift. And it ends envy, because you did not choose your gift and neither did anyone else. Many Christians today debate whether some of the gifts listed here, such as tongues, prophecy and healings, continue in the church until Christ returns or ceased with the apostolic age. Paul does not address that question in this chapter; what he does make plain is that whatever gifts the Spirit gives, He gives them as He wills and for the good of all."]},{"heading":"The Body: Against Inferiority and Against Superiority","paragraphs":["The image of the body was common in the ancient world, and that makes Paul's use of it all the more striking. Roman writers used it to justify social hierarchy. The historian Livy tells of Menenius Agrippa persuading rebellious plebeians to return to Rome with a fable: the limbs of the body rebelled against the belly, refusing to feed it, and the whole body wasted away — so the lower classes should serve the upper classes for the good of all. That was the political use of the body image: know your place. Paul takes the same image and turns it inside out. Yes, every member is needed. But the conclusion is not that the weak should serve the strong. It is that “those members of the body which seem to be weaker are necessary” (v. 22), and that God has given “more abundant honor to the inferior part” (v. 24).","Paul addresses two opposite errors. The first is inferiority (vv. 15–20). The foot says it does not belong because it is not a hand; the ear says it does not belong because it is not an eye. Some Corinthians apparently felt that because they did not have the prized gifts — tongues especially — they were second-class members. Paul's answer is that God has placed every member in the body “just as he desired” (v. 18). You may wish you were a different member, but wishing does not change your belonging, and the body would be a monstrosity if everyone were the same: “If the whole body were an eye, where would the hearing be?” (v. 17). The second error is superiority (vv. 21–26). The eye says to the hand, “I have no need for you.” The head says to the feet, “I have no need for you.” Some Corinthians with spectacular gifts despised those with ordinary ones. Paul says the most visible members cannot survive without the hidden ones.","And then he tells them God's purpose in designing the body this way: “that there should be no division in the body, but that the members should have the same care for one another. When one member suffers, all the members suffer with it. Or when one member is honored, all the members rejoice with it” (vv. 25–26). This is the opposite of Corinth's competition and envy. In a healthy body, there is no rivalry between members. There is shared pain and shared joy. Verse 27 drives it home: “Now you are the body of Christ, and members individually.” Verses 28 to 30 then list ministries and gifts and ask a series of questions that in Greek all expect the answer “No”: Are all apostles? Do all speak in tongues? Do all interpret? No single gift is for everyone. And yet Paul ends with an invitation: “earnestly desire the best gifts” — the gifts that build up the most people — and then: “I show a most excellent way to you” (v. 31). The way is not another gift. It is love."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["Paul develops the body image again in Romans, written a few years later from Corinth: “For even as we have many members in one body, and all the members don’t have the same function, so we, who are many, are one body in Christ, and individually members one of another. Having gifts differing according to the grace that was given to us, if prophecy, let us prophesy according to the proportion of our faith; or service, let us give ourselves to service; or he who teaches, to his teaching” (Romans 12:4–7). And in Ephesians he adds the head: Christ “is the head... from whom all the body, being fitted and knit together through that which every joint supplies, according to the working in measure of each individual part, makes the body increase to the building up of itself in love” (Ephesians 4:15–16). Notice the final words: “in love.” Ephesians, like 1 Corinthians, insists that the gifts build up the body only when exercised in love.","Verse 13 — “in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body, whether Jews or Greeks, whether bond or free” — echoes Paul's words to the Galatians: “For as many of you as were baptized into Christ have put on Christ. There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free man, there is neither male nor female; for you are all one in Christ Jesus” (Galatians 3:27–28). And it fulfils the promise of Pentecost, when the Spirit was poured out on all who believed, and they “began to speak with other languages, as the Spirit gave them the ability to speak” (Acts 2:4). The unity of the body is not created by human organisation. It is created by the Spirit, who joins every believer to Christ and therefore to every other believer.","Peter applies the same principle of gifts given for others: “As each has received a gift, employ it in serving one another, as good managers of the grace of God in its various forms. If anyone speaks, let it be as it were the very words of God. If anyone serves, let it be as of the strength which God supplies, that in all things God may be glorified through Jesus Christ” (1 Peter 4:10–11). Notice the purpose: that God may be glorified. And the confession of verse 3 reaches its fulfilment in Philippians: “at the name of Jesus every knee should bow... and that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father” (Philippians 2:10–11). The Spirit who enables the believer to say “Jesus is Lord” now will one day bring every tongue in the universe to say it."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Gift (of grace)","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","strongs":"G5486","verses":"v. 4, 9, 28, 30, 31","insight":"Strong's: “a (divine) gratuity, i.e. deliverance (from danger or passion); (specially), a (spiritual) endowment, i.e. (subjectively) religious qualification, or (objectively) miraculous faculty.” The word is formed from χάρις, grace, with the suffix -μα indicating the result of an action: a χάρισμα is grace in concrete form, grace that has landed somewhere. Paul uses the same word for singleness and marriage (7:7) and for the gift of eternal life (Romans 6:23). The Corinthians called their abilities πνευματικά — spiritual things — which emphasised the experience. Paul calls them χαρίσματα, which emphasises the Giver. You cannot be proud of a gift. You can only be grateful and faithful with it."},{"english":"Manifestation / making visible","hebrew":"φανέρωσις","translit":"phanérōsis","strongs":"G5321","verses":"v. 7","insight":"Strong's: “exhibition, i.e. (figuratively) expression, (by extension) a bestowment.” It is related to φανερός, visible, and φαίνω, to shine. A spiritual gift is a φανέρωσις of the Spirit — the invisible Spirit made visible in the life of a believer, as light becomes visible when it falls on an object. The only other New Testament use is 2 Corinthians 4:2, of “the manifestation of the truth.” This changes how you see your gift. It is not primarily about you. It is the Spirit making Himself visible through you. And verse 7 says why: “for the profit of all.” The Spirit shows Himself through you so that others can be built up."},{"english":"Member / limb","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","strongs":"G3196","verses":"v. 12 (×2), 14, 18, 19, 20, 22, 25, 26 (×3), 27","insight":"Strong's: “a limb or part of the body.” Paul uses it twelve times in this chapter, more than in any other passage of Scripture. In 6:15 he used it to show that sexual immorality takes a member of Christ and joins it to a prostitute. Here he uses it to show that every believer is a functioning part of Christ's body. Notice that μέλος means a living limb, not a loose part. A hand cut off is no longer a μέλος; it is a dead thing. Your life as a believer cannot be lived detached from the body. You are a member, not an individual who occasionally visits the body. And no member is too small to matter."},{"english":"Suffer with","hebrew":"συμπάσχω","translit":"sympáschō","strongs":"G4841 (paired with, rejoice with συγχαίρω, synchaírō, G4796)","verses":"v. 26","insight":"Strong's defines συμπάσχω as “to experience pain jointly or of the same kind (specially, persecution; to ‘sympathize’),” and συγχαίρω as “to sympathize in gladness, congratulate.” Both are formed with σύν, with. This is the vocabulary of shared life. Paul uses συμπάσχω in Romans 8:17 for suffering with Christ, and συγχαίρω in Philippians 2:17–18 for rejoicing together with the Philippians. In the body of Christ, there is no such thing as private pain or private joy. Your brother's suffering is your suffering, and your sister's honour is your honour. Jealousy cannot survive in a heart that has learned συγχαίρω."},{"english":"Administration / guidance / steering","hebrew":"κυβέρνησις","translit":"kybérnēsis","strongs":"G2941","verses":"v. 28","insight":"Strong's: “pilotage, i.e. (figuratively) directorship (in the church).” The related word κυβερνήτης means a ship's pilot or helmsman (Acts 27:11; Revelation 18:17), and English gets “govern” from the same root through Latin. Corinth was a city of two harbours, and every reader knew what a pilot did: he steered a large ship safely through narrow waters into port. This is a gift easily overlooked in a church dazzled by tongues and prophecy. But Paul lists it as a gift of God, alongside “helps” (ἀντιλήψεις). The person who plans, organises, steers and keeps things on course is exercising a gift of the Spirit as surely as the person who prophesies."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the way you measure spirituality, the gift you have buried because it seems small, and the members of the body you have quietly decided you do not need.","items":[{"title":"What Makes You Spiritual?","body":"You may have measured spirituality — yours and others' — by intensity: the emotion of worship, the spectacle of gifts, the volume of prayer, the size of a ministry. The Spirit wants to reset the measure. The first and greatest work of the Holy Spirit is to make Jesus Lord. Not in your vocabulary only, but in your decisions, your money, your relationships, your private life. Ask yourself: if someone watched my life for a week, would they conclude that Jesus is Lord? A believer with no spectacular gifts who quietly obeys Christ in everything is more spiritual than a gifted performer who does not. Let the Spirit do His first work in you."},{"title":"The Gift You Buried","body":"You have a gift. Verse 7 says “to each one is given.” But you may have concluded that your gift is not important — that you are a foot, not a hand; an ear, not an eye. You see others preaching, leading, prophesying, and you think the body would not miss you. The Spirit wants you to hear verse 18: God placed you in the body “just as he desired.” And verse 22: the parts that seem weaker are indispensable. Your hidden gift — the prayer no one hears, the help no one notices, the administration no one thanks you for, the encouragement you give in private — is not small. It is necessary. What gift have you buried? Dig it up and use it."},{"title":"I Have No Need for You","body":"There are people in your church you have quietly decided you do not need. Perhaps they are less gifted, less educated, less spiritual-seeming, less interesting. Perhaps they slow things down or make you uncomfortable. The Spirit is asking you to hear yourself saying, “I have no need for you.” Paul says that is as absurd as an eye saying it does not need a hand. Worse, God has deliberately given more abundant honour to the members you overlook. Who are they? Name them. Then ask God to show you what He has given them that you need — and go and receive it."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Lordship Check","body":"Write down three areas of your life: one where Jesus is clearly Lord, one where you are still negotiating, and one where you have not yet surrendered at all. Be honest. The confession “Jesus is Lord” is the Spirit's first work. Where is He still waiting to do it?"},{"title":"The Gift Check","body":"What do you believe your spiritual gift or gifts are? If you do not know, ask three mature believers who know you well what they see God doing through you. Then ask: am I using this gift for “the profit of all,” or for my own reputation? Am I using it at all?"},{"title":"The Body Check","body":"When was the last time you suffered with a member of your church who was suffering, or rejoiced with one who was honoured? Name one person in each situation right now. If you cannot think of anyone, ask why. Have you been living as a member, or as a spectator?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Honouring a Hidden Member","body":"This week, identify someone in your church whose ministry is hidden — the person who prays faithfully, cleans the building, runs the sound, cares for the children, manages the finances, visits the sick. Give them “more abundant honor” (v. 23). Write them a specific note of thanks naming exactly what they do and why it matters. If appropriate, thank them publicly or tell a leader about their faithfulness. Pray for them by name. Let them know they are not a clay limb on a shelf, but a vital member of Christ's body."},{"title":"Suffering and Rejoicing Together","body":"Practise verse 26 deliberately this month. Find one member of your church who is suffering — illness, grief, loss, hardship — and do something concrete to share their burden: a meal, a visit, a call, help with a practical need, sustained prayer. Then find one member who has recently been honoured or blessed — a promotion, a birth, an answered prayer, a success in ministry — and celebrate with them sincerely, without comparison or envy. Notice what happens in your own heart as you do both."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Holy Spirit, thank You that I can say, ‘Jesus is Lord,’ only because You have opened my eyes. Do Your first work in me again today. Make Jesus Lord in every part of my life — not just in my words but in my decisions, my money, my body, my relationships. Father, thank You for the gift You have given me. I did not earn it and I did not choose it; You distributed it as You willed. Forgive me for burying it because it seemed small, and forgive me for boasting in it as though I had made it myself. Show me how to use it for the profit of all. Lord Jesus, I am a member of Your body. Forgive me for every time I have said, ‘Because I am not that member, I don’t belong.’ And forgive me for every time I have said to another member, ‘I have no need of you.’ Teach me to honour the hidden members, to see the weaker parts as indispensable, and to give more abundant honour where the world gives none. Make me a member who suffers with those who suffer and rejoices with those who rejoice. Heal every division in Your body, beginning in me. And now, Lord, show me the more excellent way. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Aaron and Hur Hold Up Moses' Hands","ref":"Exodus 17:8-13","relatedVerses":"v. 21-22","summary":"When the Amalekites attacked Israel at Rephidim, Joshua led the army into battle while Moses stood on a hilltop with the staff of God in his hand. As long as Moses held up his hands, Israel prevailed; when he lowered them, Amalek prevailed. But Moses' hands grew heavy. So Aaron and Hur brought a stone for him to sit on and held up his hands, one on each side, until sunset — and Joshua won the battle. The great leader could not do it alone. Two men whose role seemed small — just holding up a tired man's arms — were indispensable to the victory. That is 1 Corinthians 12:21–22: the head cannot say to the hands, “I have no need for you,” and the members that seem weaker are necessary."},{"title":"Each Family Builds Its Section of the Wall","ref":"Nehemiah 3:1-32","relatedVerses":"v. 12-20, 27","summary":"Nehemiah chapter 3 can look like a tedious list of names, but it is one of the most beautiful pictures of the body of Christ in the Old Testament. The wall of Jerusalem was rebuilt by priests and goldsmiths, perfume makers and merchants, rulers of districts, Levites, men from surrounding towns, and even one man working with his daughters. Each group took a section, often the section next to their own house. Some built gates, some repaired long stretches, some fixed a small portion. Nobody built the whole wall, and nobody's section was unnecessary. When they were finished, the wall was complete in fifty-two days. That is the church: many members, each with its own part, together building one city for God."},{"title":"Would That All the LORD's People Were Prophets","ref":"Numbers 11:16-29","relatedVerses":"v. 4-11","summary":"Moses was overwhelmed by the burden of leading Israel alone, so God told him to gather seventy elders, and He took some of the Spirit that was on Moses and put it on them, and they prophesied. Two of the elders, Eldad and Medad, had stayed in the camp, but the Spirit rested on them too, and they prophesied there. A young man ran to tell Moses, and Joshua urged him to stop them. Moses replied that he wished all the LORD's people were prophets and that the LORD would put His Spirit on them all. Moses did not want a monopoly on the Spirit. He wanted the burden shared. God distributed His Spirit as He chose, even to those who were not where people expected. That is the heart of 1 Corinthians 12: the Spirit gives gifts to each one as He wills, for the good of all."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":13,"chapterTitle":"The Most Excellent Way — Love That Never Fails","themeTitle":"“Not a Wedding Poem but a Scalpel: Everything Without Love Is Noise”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"If I speak with the languages of men and of angels, but don’t have love, I have become sounding brass, or a clanging cymbal.","understanding":"This chapter was not written for weddings. It was written as a rebuke to gifted people who were using their gifts to compete, and it sits between two chapters about spiritual gifts like a scalpel in the middle of an operation. “If I speak with the γλῶσσαι — the tongues, the languages — of men and of angels” — the very gift Corinth prized most, perhaps believing it was the speech of heaven — “but don’t have ἀγάπη, I have become” noise. Χαλκὸς ἠχῶν — echoing bronze; Corinthian bronze was famous across the empire. Κύμβαλον ἀλαλάζον — a clanging, shrieking cymbal, the kind used in the frenzied worship of pagan cults. Without love, the most spiritual-sounding speech is just a gong.","words":[{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G5475","hebrew":"χαλκός","translit":"chalkós","english":"brass"},{"strongs":"G2278","hebrew":"ἠχέω","translit":"ēchéō","english":"sounding"},{"strongs":"G2950","hebrew":"κύμβαλον","translit":"kýmbalon","english":"cymbal"},{"strongs":"G214","hebrew":"ἀλαλάζω","translit":"alalázō","english":"clanging"}]},{"v":2,"text":"If I have the gift of prophecy, and know all mysteries and all knowledge; and if I have all faith, so as to remove mountains, but don’t have love, I am nothing.","understanding":"Now the gifts of revelation and power, stacked to their maximum. All prophecy. All μυστήρια, mysteries. All γνῶσις, knowledge. All πίστις, faith, “so as to remove mountains” — almost certainly an echo of Jesus' own words (Matthew 17:20). Every gift Corinth admired, at its highest possible level. “But don’t have love, I am οὐδέν — nothing.” Not “I have nothing.” I am nothing. A man can be spectacularly gifted and spiritually zero.","words":[{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"},{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mysteries"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountains"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3762","hebrew":"οὐδείς","translit":"oudeís","english":"nothing"}]},{"v":3,"text":"If I give away all my goods to feed the poor, and if I give my body to be burned, but don’t have love, it profits me nothing.","understanding":"Now the gifts of sacrifice, also at their maximum. If I ψωμίζω — dole out, feed out in morsels — all my ὑπάρχοντα, my possessions, to the poor. If I hand over my body to be burned — the earliest manuscripts read “that I may boast,” a single letter's difference in Greek; either way, the ultimate act of self-sacrifice. “But don’t have love, it ὠφελέω — profits — me nothing.” It is possible to give everything away and die a martyr's death for the wrong reason. Generosity and martyrdom without love are just impressive ways of serving yourself.","words":[{"strongs":"G5595","hebrew":"ψωμίζω","translit":"psōmízō","english":"give away (feed)"},{"strongs":"G5224","hebrew":"ὑπάρχοντα","translit":"hypárchonta","english":"goods"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G2545","hebrew":"καίω","translit":"kaíō","english":"burned"},{"strongs":"G5623","hebrew":"ὠφελέω","translit":"ōpheléō","english":"profits"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Love is patient and is kind; love doesn’t envy. Love doesn’t brag, is not proud,","understanding":"Now love is described — not by abstract definition but by fifteen verbs. Love is something you do. Μακροθυμεῖ — love is long-tempered, slow to boil; it endures wrong for a long time without retaliating. Χρηστεύεται — love is kind, actively useful, looking for ways to do good. Then the negatives begin, and each one is a portrait of Corinth: love οὐ ζηλοῖ — does not envy, does not burn with jealousy; οὐ περπερεύεται — does not brag, does not show off, does not play the windbag; οὐ φυσιοῦται — is not puffed up, the bellows-word Paul has used five times already against them.","words":[{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3114","hebrew":"μακροθυμέω","translit":"makrothyméō","english":"is patient"},{"strongs":"G5541","hebrew":"χρηστεύομαι","translit":"chrēsteúomai","english":"is kind"},{"strongs":"G2206","hebrew":"ζηλόω","translit":"zēlóō","english":"envy"},{"strongs":"G4068","hebrew":"περπερεύομαι","translit":"perpereúomai","english":"brag"},{"strongs":"G5448","hebrew":"φυσιόω","translit":"physióō","english":"proud"}]},{"v":5,"text":"doesn’t behave itself inappropriately, doesn’t seek its own way, is not provoked, takes no account of evil;","understanding":"Οὐκ ἀσχημονεῖ — love does not behave disgracefully, rudely, shamefully; the word of 7:36. Οὐ ζητεῖ τὰ ἑαυτῆς — does not seek its own, the principle of 10:24 and 10:33. Οὐ παροξύνεται — is not provoked, not exasperated, not easily irritated; the word gives us paroxysm. Οὐ λογίζεται τὸ κακόν — does not keep a ledger of wrongs. Λογίζομαι is an accounting word, the word Paul uses in Romans 4 for God crediting righteousness. Love does not keep a running account of how it has been wronged.","words":[{"strongs":"G807","hebrew":"ἀσχημονέω","translit":"aschēmonéō","english":"behave inappropriately"},{"strongs":"G2212","hebrew":"ζητέω","translit":"zētéō","english":"seek"},{"strongs":"G3947","hebrew":"παροξύνω","translit":"paroxýnō","english":"provoked"},{"strongs":"G3049","hebrew":"λογίζομαι","translit":"logízomai","english":"takes account"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"}]},{"v":6,"text":"doesn’t rejoice in unrighteousness, but rejoices with the truth;","understanding":"Love does not χαίρω — rejoice — at ἀδικία, wrongdoing, injustice; the Corinthians had been puffed up over a man's sexual sin (5:2). Instead love συγχαίρω — rejoices together with — the truth. Love and truth are partners. Love that celebrates sin or ignores it is not biblical love; it is sentimentality.","words":[{"strongs":"G5463","hebrew":"χαίρω","translit":"chaírō","english":"rejoice"},{"strongs":"G93","hebrew":"ἀδικία","translit":"adikía","english":"unrighteousness"},{"strongs":"G4796","hebrew":"συγχαίρω","translit":"synchaírō","english":"rejoices with"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":7,"text":"bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things.","understanding":"Four times πάντα — all things. Love στέγει — covers, roofs over, bears — all things; the word of 9:12, a roof that protects, or a vessel that holds without leaking. Πιστεύει — believes all things: not gullibility, but a refusal to give up faith in what God can do in a person. Ἐλπίζει — hopes all things: refuses to write anyone off. Ὑπομένει — endures all things: stays under the load and does not run. This is not a description of a doormat. It is a description of Jesus.","words":[{"strongs":"G4722","hebrew":"στέγω","translit":"stégō","english":"bears"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believes"},{"strongs":"G1679","hebrew":"ἐλπίζω","translit":"elpízō","english":"hopes"},{"strongs":"G5278","hebrew":"ὑπομένω","translit":"hypoménō","english":"endures"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Love never fails. But where there are prophecies, they will be done away with. Where there are various languages, they will cease. Where there is knowledge, it will be done away with.","understanding":"Ἡ ἀγάπη οὐδέποτε πίπτει — love never falls, never collapses, never fails. Then the contrast: prophecies will καταργέω — be rendered inoperative, done away with; tongues will παύω — cease, stop; knowledge will be done away with. The gifts Corinth was proud of are temporary tools for this age. Love is the one thing that will survive into eternity unchanged.","words":[{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fails"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecies"},{"strongs":"G2673","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","english":"done away with"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G3973","hebrew":"παύω","translit":"paúō","english":"cease"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"}]},{"v":9,"text":"For we know in part, and we prophesy in part;","understanding":"Ἐκ μέρους — in part. All our knowledge and all our prophesying are partial, fragmentary, incomplete. Even the most gifted Christian sees only a portion of the truth. This should humble every theologian and every prophet.","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3313","hebrew":"μέρος","translit":"méros","english":"in part"},{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesy"}]},{"v":10,"text":"but when that which is complete has come, then that which is partial will be done away with.","understanding":"“When τὸ τέλειον — that which is complete, perfect, mature — has come,” the partial will be done away with. What is “the perfect”? Some have argued it is the completed New Testament. But verse 12 defines it: “then face to face… then I will know fully, even as I was also fully known.” That describes the return of Christ and the face-to-face knowledge of God in the age to come, not the closing of the canon. This is the strongest reading of the text.","words":[{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"complete"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G3313","hebrew":"μέρος","translit":"méros","english":"partial"},{"strongs":"G2673","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","english":"done away with"}]},{"v":11,"text":"When I was a child, I spoke as a child, I felt as a child, I thought as a child. Now that I have become a man, I have put away childish things.","understanding":"An illustration from growing up. When I was a νήπιος — an infant, the word of 3:1 — I spoke, thought and reasoned like an infant. When I became a man, I καταργέω — did away with, put out of operation — the things of childhood. Childish things were right for a child. The point is not that the gifts are childish, but that the present age compared with the coming one is like childhood compared with adulthood. And there may be a sting: Corinth, so proud of its spiritual maturity, was being told it was still in the nursery (3:1).","words":[{"strongs":"G3516","hebrew":"νήπιος","translit":"nḗpios","english":"child"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoke"},{"strongs":"G5426","hebrew":"φρονέω","translit":"phronéō","english":"felt"},{"strongs":"G3049","hebrew":"λογίζομαι","translit":"logízomai","english":"thought"},{"strongs":"G2673","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","english":"put away"}]},{"v":12,"text":"For now we see in a mirror, dimly, but then face to face. Now I know in part, but then I will know fully, even as I was also fully known.","understanding":"Ἔσοπτρον — a mirror. Ancient mirrors were made of polished bronze, the kind of goods Corinth's famous bronze industry produced, and even the best gave a dim, distorted image. Ἐν αἰνίγματι — in a riddle, an enigma. Numbers 12:8 says God spoke to Moses “mouth to mouth, even plainly, and not in riddles” — Paul may be echoing it. Now we see God as in a dim bronze mirror; then πρόσωπον πρὸς πρόσωπον — face to face. Now I know in part; then I will ἐπιγινώσκω — know fully — even as I have been fully known. God already knows you completely. One day you will know Him without the mirror.","words":[{"strongs":"G2072","hebrew":"ἔσοπτρον","translit":"ésoptron","english":"mirror"},{"strongs":"G135","hebrew":"αἴνιγμα","translit":"aínigma","english":"dimly (in a riddle)"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G1921","hebrew":"ἐπιγινώσκω","translit":"epiginṓskō","english":"know fully"}]},{"v":13,"text":"But now faith, hope, and love remain—these three. The greatest of these is love.","understanding":"Νυνὶ δὲ μένει — but now remains — faith, hope, love, these three. They abide; they are the permanent things beneath the passing gifts. And “the greatest of these is love.” Why greatest? Because faith will become sight, and hope will become possession, but love will simply go on, because God is love (1 John 4:8). In heaven you will no longer need to believe or hope. You will never stop loving.","words":[{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remain"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3187","hebrew":"μείζων","translit":"meízōn","english":"greatest"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Bronze Gong in the Temple Courtyard","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Walk into the courtyard of a pagan temple in Corinth during a festival. The noise hits you first: clashing cymbals, booming bronze gongs, shrieking pipes and the ecstatic cries of worshippers in a frenzy. In the cults of Dionysus and Cybele, cymbals and drums were used to whip worshippers into a trance. The sound is enormous, impressive, overwhelming — and when the festival is over, it fades into silence and leaves nothing behind. Now walk into a Christian house church on Sunday evening. Someone is speaking in tongues, loudly, eloquently. Someone else is prophesying. Someone is explaining deep mysteries. And in the courtyard, a poor slave who arrived late sits hungry and ignored.","meaning":"Paul says: if I speak with the tongues of men and angels, but have not love, I am just like that bronze gong in the temple courtyard. The comparison would have been devastating to the Corinthians: their most prized spiritual gift, exercised without love, was no different from the noise of idol worship. Then he raises the stakes. Prophecy, knowledge of all mysteries, mountain-moving faith — without love, I am nothing. Giving away everything, even martyrdom — without love, I gain nothing. The gifts are real. But love is not one gift among many; it is the medium in which every gift must operate. Without it, the most impressive spirituality in the world is just noise."},{"title":"Love With a Face","verseRef":"v. 4-7","image":"Read verses 4 to 7 again, but this time replace the word “love” with the name “Jesus.” Jesus is patient and is kind; Jesus doesn't envy. Jesus doesn't brag, is not proud, doesn't behave inappropriately, doesn't seek His own way, is not provoked, takes no account of evil; doesn't rejoice in unrighteousness, but rejoices with the truth; bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things. Every line fits perfectly. Now picture Him at the foot-washing in John 13, kneeling with a towel. At the cross, praying for those who drove the nails. On the beach with Peter after the denial, cooking breakfast. Now try reading it with your own name.","meaning":"Paul does not define love with an abstract statement. He describes it with fifteen verbs — love is something it does. And every one of the negatives is a mirror held up to Corinth: they envied, they bragged, they were puffed up, they behaved disgracefully at the Lord's Supper, they sought their own advantage, they were easily provoked into factions, they kept a ledger of grievances, they rejoiced in sin. The chapter is not sentimental. It is surgical. And it is the portrait of Christ. When you put your own name in place of “love,” you discover how far you have to go — and you also discover exactly what the Spirit is forming in you. This is not a standard you reach by trying harder. It is the fruit of the Spirit (Galatians 5:22)."},{"title":"The Bronze Mirror and the Face","verseRef":"v. 12","image":"Picture a Corinthian woman in the morning, holding up a polished bronze mirror — the finest kind available, perhaps from one of the city's bronze workshops. She can see her face, but only dimly: the colours are wrong, the edges blur, and any scratch or tarnish on the metal distorts the image. She knows roughly what she looks like. She does not see herself clearly. Now picture someone she loves, who has been away for years, walking through the door. She drops the mirror. She does not need it anymore. She is looking into a face.","meaning":"“For now we see in a mirror, dimly, but then face to face.” Everything we know of God in this age — through Scripture, through the Spirit, through prophecy, through knowledge, through experience — is true but partial, like an image in a bronze mirror. Even the greatest prophet and the most learned theologian sees in part. That should make us humble about our knowledge and patient with others whose knowledge is partial too. But it also gives an extraordinary hope: one day the mirror will be dropped. We will see Him face to face. And the most astonishing line comes last: “I will know fully, even as I was also fully known.” God already knows you completely — every thought, every failure — and loves you. One day you will know Him the same way."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Gifts Without Love Are Worthless (v. 1-3)","body":"Tongues of men and angels, prophecy, all knowledge, mountain-moving faith, total generosity and even martyrdom amount to noise, nothing and no gain if love is absent."},{"label":"What Love Does and Does Not Do (v. 4-7)","body":"Fifteen verbs describe love: patient and kind; not envious, boastful, proud, rude, self-seeking, easily provoked or resentful; not rejoicing in wrong but in truth; bearing, believing, hoping and enduring all things."},{"label":"Love Never Fails; the Gifts Are Temporary (v. 8-12)","body":"Prophecy, tongues and knowledge will pass away when the complete comes, because they are partial; now we see dimly as in a mirror, but then face to face and will know fully as we are fully known."},{"label":"The Three That Remain (v. 13)","body":"Faith, hope and love remain, and the greatest of these is love."}],"sections":[{"heading":"A Scalpel, Not a Valentine","paragraphs":["First Corinthians 13 is probably the most famous chapter Paul ever wrote, and it is read at countless weddings. That is not wrong — it is a description of love that every marriage needs. But it is important to see that Paul did not write it for a wedding. He wrote it in the middle of a sharp correction of a church that was using its spiritual gifts to compete, to show off and to look down on one another. Chapter 12 ended with the call to “earnestly desire the best gifts” and the promise, “I show a most excellent way to you” (12:31). Chapter 14 will return to tongues and prophecy and give practical instructions for worship. Chapter 13 stands between them as the key to both. Love is not a gift alongside the others. It is the way all the gifts must be used.","The first three verses build a staircase of spiritual achievement — and then knock it down. Tongues of men and angels: the most prized gift in Corinth, which some may have believed was the very language of heaven. Without love, it is “sounding brass, or a clanging cymbal” (v. 1). The image would have been sharp in Corinth: cymbals and bronze instruments were associated with the noisy, ecstatic worship of pagan cults. Prophecy, all mysteries, all knowledge, mountain-moving faith: without love, “I am nothing” (v. 2). Notice — not “I have nothing,” but “I am nothing.” Giving away everything I own, handing over my body to be burned (or, as the earliest manuscripts read, “that I may boast”): without love, “it profits me nothing” (v. 3).","This is one of the most searching passages in all of Scripture. It means that it is possible to be gifted, knowledgeable, generous, theologically correct and even willing to die for the faith — and still be spiritually empty. Jesus warned of the same thing: “Many will tell me in that day, ‘Lord, Lord, didn’t we prophesy in your name, in your name cast out demons, and in your name do many mighty works?’ Then I will tell them, ‘I never knew you’” (Matthew 7:22–23). Gifts are not proof of godliness. The Corinthians had confused spiritual power with spiritual maturity. Paul tells them that the measure of a Christian is not how much power flows through him, but how much love."]},{"heading":"Fifteen Verbs: The Portrait of Love — and of Corinth","paragraphs":["Verses 4 to 7 are written almost entirely with verbs. Paul does not define love; he shows it in action. And each phrase, especially the negatives, is aimed directly at Corinth. Love is patient — μακροθυμεῖ, slow to anger, long-suffering — while the Corinthians were quick to divide and quick to sue. Love is kind — χρηστεύεται, actively good to others — while they left the poor hungry at the Lord's Supper. Love does not envy — but they were full of ζῆλος (3:3). Love does not brag — περπερεύεται, a rare word for being a windbag, showing off — while they paraded their gifts. Love is not puffed up — φυσιοῦται, the bellows word used five times already in the letter (4:6, 18, 19; 5:2; 8:1). Love does not behave inappropriately — while they disgraced the Lord's Table. Love does not seek its own — while they insisted on their rights.","Love is not provoked — οὐ παροξύνεται, not easily exasperated or irritated — while they were provoked into factions. Love takes no account of evil — οὐ λογίζεται τὸ κακόν — an accounting term: love does not keep a ledger of wrongs to be settled later. That is exactly what you do when you take your brother to court. Love does not rejoice in unrighteousness — while they were proud of tolerating sexual sin (5:2) — but rejoices with the truth. And then four great affirmations: love bears all things — στέγει, covers like a roof, protects, or holds like a watertight vessel; believes all things — refuses to give up on what God can do in a person; hopes all things — refuses to write anyone off; endures all things — stays under the weight and does not run away.","Two misunderstandings need to be avoided. First, “believes all things” and “bears all things” do not mean naivety or tolerance of abuse. Love “rejoices with the truth” (v. 6); it does not pretend evil is good, and it does not enable harm. The same letter commands the removal of an unrepentant sinner from the church (5:13) — an act of love aimed at his salvation. Biblical love can say no, confront and set boundaries. What it cannot do is stop caring, stop hoping or stop seeking the other person's good. Second, this is not a standard you reach by gritting your teeth. Read the fifteen verbs with “Jesus” in place of “love,” and they fit perfectly. That is because this is the portrait of Christ, and it is formed in believers by His Spirit: “the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness” (Galatians 5:22). You cannot manufacture it. You can surrender to the One who produces it."]},{"heading":"When the Mirror Is Dropped: Why Love Outlasts Everything","paragraphs":["Verse 8 turns from what love does to how long it lasts. “Love never fails.” The verb πίπτει means to fall, to collapse. Love never falls down. By contrast, the gifts the Corinthians were proud of are temporary. Prophecies will be done away with. Tongues will cease. Knowledge will be done away with. Why? “For we know in part, and we prophesy in part; but when that which is complete has come, then that which is partial will be done away with” (vv. 9–10). The gifts are tools for this age, for a church that sees only partially and needs help to grow. When the complete comes, the tools will be put away, just as a man puts away childish things when he grows up (v. 11).","What is “that which is complete”? Some interpreters have argued that it refers to the completion of the New Testament canon, and that therefore gifts like prophecy and tongues ceased once Scripture was finished. But the text itself defines “the complete” in verse 12: “For now we see in a mirror, dimly, but then face to face. Now I know in part, but then I will know fully, even as I was also fully known.” Face-to-face sight of God and full knowledge equal to God's knowledge of us do not describe the situation of Christians reading a completed Bible; they describe the return of Christ and the age to come. That is the strongest reading of the text. Whatever one concludes from other evidence about the continuation of particular gifts today, this passage places their ending at the coming of the perfect — when Jesus returns.","And then the mirror. Ancient mirrors were polished metal, usually bronze, and even the best gave a dim, imperfect image. Corinth was famous for its bronze, so the picture would have been vivid. “Now we see in a mirror, dimly” — literally ἐν αἰνίγματι, in a riddle. The phrase may echo Numbers 12:8, where God says He speaks with Moses “mouth to mouth, even plainly, and not in riddles.” In this age even our best knowledge of God is like a reflection in bronze: real, but blurred. But a day is coming when we will see Him face to face, and will know Him as fully as He already knows us. That is why love is the greatest. “But now faith, hope, and love remain — these three. The greatest of these is love” (v. 13). In that day faith will become sight, and hope will become possession. But love will not be replaced by anything. It will simply be perfected. Love is the one thing you do now that you will go on doing forever, because God is love."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["The supremacy of love in 1 Corinthians 13 goes back to the teaching of Jesus Himself. Asked which commandment was greatest, Jesus answered: “‘You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind.’ This is the first and great commandment. A second likewise is this, ‘You shall love your neighbor as yourself.’ The whole law and the prophets depend on these two commandments” (Matthew 22:37–40). And on the night before He died, He gave His disciples a new commandment: “A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another. Just as I have loved you, you also love one another. By this everyone will know that you are my disciples, if you have love for one another” (John 13:34–35). Notice the test: not gifts, not knowledge, but love. That is exactly Paul's point to Corinth.","Paul repeats the principle in Romans, written from Corinth: “Owe no one anything, except to love one another; for he who loves his neighbor has fulfilled the law... Love doesn’t harm a neighbor. Love therefore is the fulfillment of the law” (Romans 13:8, 10). And to the Colossians: “Above all these things, walk in love, which is the bond of perfection” (Colossians 3:14). Peter: “above all things be earnest in your love among yourselves, for love covers a multitude of sins” (1 Peter 4:8) — a close parallel to “bears all things.” And the source of this love is God Himself: “God’s love has been poured out into our hearts through the Holy Spirit who was given to us” (Romans 5:5), and “God commends his own love toward us, in that while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us” (Romans 5:8).","John gives the deepest foundation of all: “Beloved, let us love one another, for love is of God; and everyone who loves has been born of God, and knows God. He who doesn’t love doesn’t know God, for God is love” (1 John 4:7–8). That is why love is greater than faith and hope: faith and hope are our response to God, but love is God's own nature. And John also completes the hope of verse 12: “Beloved, now we are children of God, and it is not yet revealed what we will be. But we know that, when he is revealed, we will be like him; for we will see him just as he is” (1 John 3:2). Paul's “face to face” and John's “we will see him just as he is” describe the same day. On that day the gifts will fall silent, the mirror will be laid aside, and love — which never fails — will be all in all."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Love","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","strongs":"G26","verses":"v. 1, 2, 3, 4 (×3), 8, 13 (×2)","insight":"Strong's: “love, i.e. affection or benevolence; specially (plural) a love-feast.” Ἀγάπη was a relatively uncommon word in classical Greek; the New Testament writers took it up and filled it with new meaning. Greek had other words for love: ἔρως for desire, φιλία for friendship, στοργή for family affection. Ἀγάπη became the word for the self-giving love that seeks the good of the other regardless of their worthiness — the love God showed in sending His Son (John 3:16; 1 John 4:10). It is not primarily a feeling but a commitment expressed in action, which is why Paul describes it with verbs. Strong's note that the plural could mean a “love-feast” is telling: the early church's fellowship meal was called the ἀγάπη — exactly the meal Corinth had turned into a display of division (11:20–22)."},{"english":"Is patient / long-suffering","hebrew":"μακροθυμέω","translit":"makrothyméō","strongs":"G3114","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “to be long-spirited, i.e. (objectively) forbearing or (subjectively) patient.” The word combines μακρός, long, and θυμός, passion or anger: love has a long fuse. It is used of God's patience with sinners (Romans 2:4; 2 Peter 3:9, “The Lord... is patient with us”) and of the farmer waiting for his crop (James 5:7). It is the first quality Paul lists, and it is specifically patience with people, not with circumstances. Love absorbs wrong for a long time without striking back. In a church that rushed to lawsuits and factions, this was the first lesson they needed."},{"english":"Takes account of / reckons","hebrew":"λογίζομαι","translit":"logízomai","strongs":"G3049","verses":"v. 5 (“takes no account of evil”), 11 (“thought”)","insight":"Strong's: “to take an inventory, i.e. estimate (literally or figuratively).” It is a bookkeeping word, used for entering items into a ledger. Paul uses it eleven times in Romans 4 for God “crediting” righteousness to Abraham and “not counting” sin against the forgiven: “Blessed is the man whom the Lord will by no means charge with sin” (Romans 4:8). In 2 Corinthians 5:19 God was “not reckoning” the world's trespasses against them. So when Paul says love “takes no account of evil,” he is saying that love does to others what God has done for us in Christ: it refuses to keep a ledger of wrongs to be settled later. Forgiveness is love closing the account book."},{"english":"Bears / covers","hebrew":"στέγω","translit":"stégō","strongs":"G4722","verses":"v. 7 (and 9:12)","insight":"Strong's: “to roof over, i.e. (figuratively) to cover with silence (endure patiently).” The noun στέγη means a roof. Love “roofs over” all things — it protects others, covers their weaknesses rather than exposing them, shelters them from the storm. The word was also used of a vessel that holds water without leaking: love contains what is poured into it without spilling it out in gossip or complaint. Paul used the same verb in 9:12: “we bear all things, that we may cause no hindrance to the Good News.” This is not covering up wrongdoing — love “rejoices with the truth” (v. 6) — but refusing to broadcast others' failures and choosing to protect rather than to expose."},{"english":"Mirror","hebrew":"ἔσοπτρον","translit":"ésoptron","strongs":"G2072 (paired with, riddle αἴνιγμα, aínigma, G135)","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's defines ἔσοπτρον as “a mirror (for looking into),” and αἴνιγμα as “an obscure saying (‘enigma’), i.e. (abstractly) obscureness.” Ancient mirrors were usually polished bronze, and even a good one gave a dim and slightly distorted image. James uses the same word for a man who looks at his face in a mirror and forgets what he looks like (James 1:23). Paul's point is not that what we see now is false, but that it is indirect and incomplete — like trying to make out a face in tarnished metal, or to understand a riddle. Our knowledge of God in this age is real but partial. One day the mirror will be set aside, and we will see face to face."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the gifts you have been relying on as proof of your spirituality, the specific ways you are failing to love, and the eternal perspective that puts every present thing in its place.","items":[{"title":"Gifted and Empty?","body":"You may be gifted. You may know a great deal, serve faithfully, give generously and even suffer for Christ. And the Spirit wants to ask you the question this chapter asks: is it love? Is your knowledge used to build others up or to win? Is your service done for the people or for the applause? Is your giving an act of love or a way of feeling good about yourself? It is possible to do everything right and be nothing. Do not hear this as condemnation. Hear it as an invitation. The Spirit wants to take everything you do and fill it with the one thing that makes it count forever. Ask Him today: “Lord, where am I a clanging cymbal?”"},{"title":"Put Your Name in the Verses","body":"Read verses 4 to 7 aloud with your own name in place of the word “love.” Slowly. Where do you stumble? Where do you know it is not true? “I am patient.” “I don't envy.” “I don't keep a record of wrongs.” “I bear all things.” The places where your voice catches are the places the Spirit is putting His finger on. Do not be discouraged; this is the portrait of Jesus, and no one reads it about themselves without wincing. But each wince is a door. Ask the Spirit to produce in you the specific fruit you lack. He does not ask you to manufacture love. He asks you to let Him grow it in you."},{"title":"What Will Last?","body":"Prophecies will be done away with. Tongues will cease. Knowledge will pass away. The things you are most proud of in your spiritual life may be among the things that will one day be put away like childhood toys. But love never fails. The Spirit is inviting you to reorder your priorities by what will last. How much of your energy goes into things that will be done away with — your reputation, your achievements, your knowledge, your platform — and how much into love, the only thing you will take into eternity unchanged? The mirror will be dropped. You will see Him face to face. What will you wish you had spent your life on?"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Cymbal Check","body":"List your spiritual gifts, strengths or areas of service. Beside each one, write honestly: am I doing this in love — for the good of others — or for my own satisfaction, reputation or sense of importance? Circle any that are noise without love."},{"title":"The Fifteen-Verb Check","body":"Write out the fifteen verbs of verses 4 to 7. Beside each one, give yourself an honest rating from 1 to 5. Then pick the lowest two. For each, write down one specific relationship or situation where you are failing to love in that way."},{"title":"The Ledger Check","body":"Love “takes no account of evil.” Is there a ledger in your heart? A list of wrongs someone has done to you that you are keeping for later? Write the person's name. Then ask: am I willing to close the account, as God closed mine in Christ?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Closing the Account","body":"Take the name from your Ledger Check. Tonight, on a sheet of paper, write down the wrongs you have been holding against them. Then read 2 Corinthians 5:19 and Romans 4:8 aloud — God is not counting your sins against you. Pray, specifically: “Lord, as You have closed my account, I close theirs. I release them from this debt.” Then tear up or burn the paper. This does not mean pretending the wrongs did not happen or that trust is instantly restored. It means you will no longer keep the ledger. If appropriate, take one step this week to show kindness to that person."},{"title":"A Week of Love in Action","body":"Choose three of the fifteen verbs that you most need to grow in. For the next seven days, each morning, pray: “Holy Spirit, make me patient (or kind, or not easily provoked) today, and show me one person to love in this way.” Then look for that person and that opportunity during the day. Each evening, write down what happened — where you loved and where you failed. At the end of the week, read 1 Corinthians 13 again and thank God for what He has begun to grow in you."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, You are love. And everything You have given me — every gift, every ability, every piece of knowledge, every opportunity to serve — is meant to be soaked in love. Forgive me for the times I have been a clanging cymbal: loud, impressive and empty. Forgive me for using my gifts to be seen, my knowledge to win, my service to feel important. Without love, I am nothing. Lord Jesus, You are the face of this chapter. You are patient and kind. You do not envy or boast. You were never proud, never rude, never self-seeking. You were not easily provoked, and You kept no record of wrongs — You went to the cross to close my account. You bear all things, believe all things, hope all things, endure all things. Make me like You. Holy Spirit, pour the love of God into my heart. Grow in me the fruit I cannot produce. Where I am impatient, make me patient. Where I am harsh, make me kind. Where I keep a ledger, help me tear it up. Where I give up on people, teach me to hope. And Lord, lift my eyes. Now I see in a mirror dimly, but one day I will see You face to face. Let me spend my life on what will last. Faith, hope and love remain — and the greatest is love. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Jonathan Gives David His Robe","ref":"1 Samuel 18:1-4; 20:1-42; 23:16-18","relatedVerses":"v. 4-5","summary":"Jonathan was the son of King Saul and the natural heir to Israel's throne. Then David killed Goliath, and Jonathan's soul was knit to David's, and he loved him as his own soul. He took off his robe, his armour, his sword, his bow and his belt and gave them to David — symbols of his royal position. When Saul tried to kill David, Jonathan protected him at the risk of his own life. And later, in the wilderness, Jonathan found David and strengthened his hand in God, telling him that he would be king over Israel and that Jonathan would be second to him. Jonathan had every reason to envy David. Instead he rejoiced in God's choice of his friend over himself. That is love that does not envy, does not seek its own, and rejoices with the truth."},{"title":"Joseph Weeps Over His Brothers","ref":"Genesis 45:1-15; 50:15-21","relatedVerses":"v. 5, 7","summary":"Joseph's brothers had hated him, thrown him into a pit and sold him into slavery. Years later, when they came to Egypt to buy grain, he was the second most powerful man in the land, and they did not recognise him. He could have kept a ledger and settled every account. Instead he wept so loudly that the Egyptians heard it, revealed himself, embraced his brothers, and told them not to be distressed, because God had sent him ahead to preserve life. After their father died, the brothers feared he would finally take revenge. Joseph wept again, told them that they had meant evil against him but God had meant it for good, and promised to provide for them and their children. That is love that takes no account of evil, bears all things and hopes all things."},{"title":"The Father Who Ran","ref":"Luke 15:11-32","relatedVerses":"v. 4, 7","summary":"A younger son demanded his inheritance, left home and wasted everything in reckless living. When famine came he ended up feeding pigs, starving, and he decided to go back and ask to be a hired servant. But while he was still far off, his father saw him, was moved with compassion, ran, fell on his neck and kissed him. Before the son could finish his speech, the father called for the best robe, a ring and sandals, and a feast. He had never stopped watching the road. He believed all things and hoped all things. Then the older brother refused to come in, angry and keeping score: these many years I served you. The father went out to him too and pleaded with him. Love was patient with both sons — the one who had run away and the one who stayed home keeping a ledger."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":14,"chapterTitle":"Five Words With Understanding — Tongues, Prophecy and Worship That Builds Up","themeTitle":"“God Is Not a God of Confusion, but of Peace”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Follow after love, and earnestly desire spiritual gifts, but especially that you may prophesy.","understanding":"The bridge from chapter 13. Διώκετε τὴν ἀγάπην — pursue love, chase it, hunt it down; the verb is used for persecution and for running after a prize. Love is the way. And within that way, ζηλοῦτε — be eager for — spiritual gifts, “but especially that you may prophesy.” Love determines which gifts are most to be desired: the ones that build others up.","words":[{"strongs":"G1377","hebrew":"διώκω","translit":"diṓkō","english":"follow after"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2206","hebrew":"ζηλόω","translit":"zēlóō","english":"earnestly desire"},{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual gifts"},{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesy"}]},{"v":2,"text":"For he who speaks in another language speaks not to men, but to God; for no one understands; but in the Spirit he speaks mysteries.","understanding":"Speaking in a tongue is directed to God, not to people, because no one ἀκούω — hears, in the sense of understands — what is said. The speaker utters μυστήρια — mysteries — “in the Spirit” (or “in his spirit”). Paul does not despise this. It is real communication with God. But in a gathering, it communicates nothing to anyone else.","words":[{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speaks"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"language"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"understands"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mysteries"}]},{"v":3,"text":"But he who prophesies speaks to men for their edification, exhortation, and consolation.","understanding":"Prophecy, by contrast, speaks to people, and Paul gives the clearest New Testament description of what it does: οἰκοδομή — building up, edification; παράκλησις — encouragement, exhortation, the comfort-word of 2 Corinthians 1; παραμυθία — consolation, gentle comfort, the word used for comforting the grieving. New Testament prophecy is not mainly about predicting the future. It is Spirit-given speech that strengthens, urges and comforts the church.","words":[{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesies"},{"strongs":"G3619","hebrew":"οἰκοδομή","translit":"oikodomḗ","english":"edification"},{"strongs":"G3874","hebrew":"παράκλησις","translit":"paráklēsis","english":"exhortation"},{"strongs":"G3889","hebrew":"παραμυθία","translit":"paramythía","english":"consolation"}]},{"v":4,"text":"He who speaks in another language edifies himself, but he who prophesies edifies the assembly.","understanding":"The contrast in one sentence. The tongue-speaker builds up ἑαυτόν — himself. The prophet builds up the ἐκκλησία — the assembly. Paul does not say self-edification is wrong; there is a place for it in private. But in public worship, the test of chapter 12 applies: the gift is given “for the profit of all” (12:7).","words":[{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"language"},{"strongs":"G3618","hebrew":"οἰκοδομέω","translit":"oikodoméō","english":"edifies"},{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesies"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Now I desire to have you all speak with other languages, but rather that you would prophesy. For he is greater who prophesies than he who speaks with other languages, unless he interprets, that the assembly may be built up.","understanding":"Paul is not against tongues — “I desire to have you all speak with other languages” — but he wants even more that they would prophesy. The one who prophesies is μείζων, greater — not as a person but in usefulness to the church — than the one who speaks in tongues, “unless he διερμηνεύω — interprets — that the assembly may be built up.” Interpreted tongues build up the church just as prophecy does.","words":[{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesy"},{"strongs":"G3187","hebrew":"μείζων","translit":"meízōn","english":"greater"},{"strongs":"G1329","hebrew":"διερμηνεύω","translit":"diermēneúō","english":"interprets"},{"strongs":"G3618","hebrew":"οἰκοδομέω","translit":"oikodoméō","english":"built up"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But now, brothers, if I come to you speaking with other languages, what would I profit you, unless I speak to you either by way of revelation, or of knowledge, or of prophesying, or of teaching?","understanding":"Paul puts himself in the scene. If he arrived in Corinth and spoke only in tongues, what would he ὠφελέω — benefit — them? Nothing, unless he brought ἀποκάλυψις (revelation), γνῶσις (knowledge), προφητεία (prophecy) or διδαχή (teaching) — something the mind can receive.","words":[{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G5623","hebrew":"ὠφελέω","translit":"ōpheléō","english":"profit"},{"strongs":"G602","hebrew":"ἀποκάλυψις","translit":"apokálypsis","english":"revelation"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophesying"},{"strongs":"G1322","hebrew":"διδαχή","translit":"didachḗ","english":"teaching"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Even things without life, giving a voice, whether pipe or harp, if they didn’t give a distinction in the sounds, how would it be known what is piped or harped?","understanding":"An analogy from music. Even ἄψυχα — lifeless instruments — like the αὐλός (flute, pipe) or the κιθάρα (lyre, harp), must give a διαστολή — a distinction in the notes. A flute that plays random sounds produces noise, not a tune. No one can recognise what is being played.","words":[{"strongs":"G895","hebrew":"ἄψυχος","translit":"ápsychos","english":"things without life"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G836","hebrew":"αὐλός","translit":"aulós","english":"pipe"},{"strongs":"G2788","hebrew":"κιθάρα","translit":"kithára","english":"harp"},{"strongs":"G1293","hebrew":"διαστολή","translit":"diastolḗ","english":"distinction"}]},{"v":8,"text":"For if the trumpet gave an uncertain sound, who would prepare himself for war?","understanding":"A military analogy. If the σάλπιγξ — the war trumpet — gives an ἄδηλος sound, an indistinct signal, who will παρασκευάζω — get ready — for πόλεμος, battle? In ancient armies, different trumpet calls signalled advance, retreat or assembly. An unclear call could get an army killed.","words":[{"strongs":"G4536","hebrew":"σάλπιγξ","translit":"sálpinx","english":"trumpet"},{"strongs":"G82","hebrew":"ἄδηλος","translit":"ádēlos","english":"uncertain"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G3903","hebrew":"παρασκευάζω","translit":"paraskeuázō","english":"prepare"},{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"}]},{"v":9,"text":"So also you, unless you uttered by the tongue words easy to understand, how would it be known what is spoken? For you would be speaking into the air.","understanding":"The application. Unless your tongue utters εὔσημος — clear, easily recognised — speech, how will anyone know what is being said? “For you would be speaking into the air.” The word is ἀήρ — the same image as the boxer beating the air in 9:26. Unintelligible speech in public worship is a punch that lands on nothing.","words":[{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"tongue"},{"strongs":"G2154","hebrew":"εὔσημος","translit":"eúsēmos","english":"easy to understand"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G109","hebrew":"ἀήρ","translit":"aḗr","english":"air"}]},{"v":10,"text":"There are, it may be, so many kinds of sounds in the world, and none of them is without meaning.","understanding":"There are many γένη — kinds — of φωναί, voices or languages, in the world, and none of them is ἄφωνος — without meaning. Every language communicates to those who know it. Paul's argument rests on the fact that language exists to be understood.","words":[{"strongs":"G1085","hebrew":"γένος","translit":"génos","english":"kinds"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"sounds"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G880","hebrew":"ἄφωνος","translit":"áphōnos","english":"without meaning"}]},{"v":11,"text":"If then I don’t know the meaning of the sound, I would be to him who speaks a foreigner, and he who speaks would be a foreigner to me.","understanding":"If I do not know the δύναμις — the force, the meaning — of the sound, I am a βάρβαρος to the speaker, and he to me. Βάρβαρος was the Greek word for a non-Greek speaker, imitating the “bar-bar” sound of a language you cannot understand. In a city as proud of its Greek culture as Corinth, the word stung. Tongues without interpretation turn brothers into foreigners.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"meaning"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G915","hebrew":"βάρβαρος","translit":"bárbaros","english":"foreigner"}]},{"v":12,"text":"So also you, since you are zealous for spiritual gifts, seek that you may abound to the building up of the assembly.","understanding":"Paul turns their zeal in the right direction. They are ζηλωταί — zealots — for πνεύματα, spiritual manifestations. Fine: then seek to περισσεύω — overflow, abound — in what builds up the church. Paul never tells them to stop being passionate. He tells them where to point their passion.","words":[{"strongs":"G2207","hebrew":"ζηλωτής","translit":"zēlōtḗs","english":"zealous"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spiritual gifts"},{"strongs":"G4052","hebrew":"περισσεύω","translit":"perisseúō","english":"abound"},{"strongs":"G3619","hebrew":"οἰκοδομή","translit":"oikodomḗ","english":"building up"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Therefore let him who speaks in another language pray that he may interpret.","understanding":"Therefore, the one who speaks in a tongue should pray for the ability to interpret. The gift of tongues is not to be suppressed but completed — brought into the service of the whole church.","words":[{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"language"},{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"pray"},{"strongs":"G1329","hebrew":"διερμηνεύω","translit":"diermēneúō","english":"interpret"}]},{"v":14,"text":"For if I pray in another language, my spirit prays, but my understanding is unfruitful.","understanding":"If I pray in a tongue, my πνεῦμα — my spirit — prays, but my νοῦς — my mind, my understanding — is ἄκαρπος, unfruitful, barren. Something real is happening in my spirit, but my mind is not engaged, and nobody else benefits.","words":[{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"pray"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"language"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"understanding"},{"strongs":"G175","hebrew":"ἄκαρπος","translit":"ákarpos","english":"unfruitful"}]},{"v":15,"text":"What is it then? I will pray with the spirit, and I will pray with the understanding also. I will sing with the spirit, and I will sing with the understanding also.","understanding":"Paul's conclusion is not either/or but both/and. I will pray with the spirit and with the mind. I will ψάλλω — sing praise, originally to pluck a stringed instrument — with the spirit and with the mind. Christian worship engages the whole person. Mindless emotion and emotionless thought are both less than God deserves.","words":[{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"pray"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"understanding"},{"strongs":"G5567","hebrew":"ψάλλω","translit":"psállō","english":"sing"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Otherwise if you bless with the spirit, how will he who fills the place of the unlearned say the “Amen” at your giving of thanks, seeing he doesn’t know what you say?","understanding":"The practical problem. If you εὐλογέω — bless, give thanks — only in the spirit, how can the ἰδιώτης — the outsider, the ordinary person, the one without this gift — say the ἀμήν at your εὐχαριστία, your thanksgiving? In the synagogue and early church, the congregation answered prayers with a spoken “Amen” — firm, true, I agree. You cannot say Amen to what you do not understand.","words":[{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"bless"},{"strongs":"G2399","hebrew":"ἰδιώτης","translit":"idiṓtēs","english":"unlearned"},{"strongs":"G281","hebrew":"ἀμήν","translit":"amḗn","english":"Amen"},{"strongs":"G2169","hebrew":"εὐχαριστία","translit":"eucharistía","english":"giving of thanks"}]},{"v":17,"text":"For you most certainly give thanks well, but the other person is not built up.","understanding":"“You most certainly give thanks καλῶς — well.” Paul grants that the tongue-speaker's prayer is genuine and beautiful. “But the other person is not built up.” Good worship is not measured only by what happens between you and God, but also by what happens to your brother.","words":[{"strongs":"G2168","hebrew":"εὐχαριστέω","translit":"eucharistéō","english":"give thanks"},{"strongs":"G2573","hebrew":"καλῶς","translit":"kalōs","english":"well"},{"strongs":"G3618","hebrew":"οἰκοδομέω","translit":"oikodoméō","english":"built up"}]},{"v":18,"text":"I thank my God, I speak with other languages more than you all.","understanding":"A surprising confession that should stop anyone from reading this chapter as anti-tongues. Paul thanks God that he speaks in tongues more than all of them. The apostle is not criticising a gift he lacks. He is regulating a gift he uses more than anyone.","words":[{"strongs":"G2168","hebrew":"εὐχαριστέω","translit":"eucharistéō","english":"thank"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"}]},{"v":19,"text":"However in the assembly I would rather speak five words with my understanding, that I might instruct others also, than ten thousand words in another language.","understanding":"“However in the assembly” — that is the decisive qualifier — he would rather speak five words with his νοῦς, his mind, to κατηχέω — instruct, teach; the word gives us catechism — others, than μύριοι, ten thousand, the largest number in Greek, in a tongue. Five against ten thousand. In church, intelligibility wins every time.","words":[{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"understanding"},{"strongs":"G2727","hebrew":"κατηχέω","translit":"katēchéō","english":"instruct"},{"strongs":"G3463","hebrew":"μύριοι","translit":"mýrioi","english":"ten thousand"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"language"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Brothers, don’t be children in thoughts, yet in malice be babies, but in thoughts be mature.","understanding":"“Brothers, don’t be παιδία — children — in your φρένες, your thinking.” The fascination with spectacular gifts for their own sake was childish. “In malice, νηπιάζω — be infants” — be innocent of evil like a baby. “But in thoughts be τέλειοι” — mature, grown-up. The same contrast as 3:1–2 and 13:11.","words":[{"strongs":"G3813","hebrew":"παιδίον","translit":"paidíon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G5424","hebrew":"φρήν","translit":"phrḗn","english":"thoughts"},{"strongs":"G2549","hebrew":"κακία","translit":"kakía","english":"malice"},{"strongs":"G3515","hebrew":"νηπιάζω","translit":"nēpiázō","english":"be babies"},{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"mature"}]},{"v":21,"text":"In the law it is written, “By men of strange languages and by the lips of strangers I will speak to this people. Not even thus will they hear me, says the Lord.”","understanding":"Paul quotes Isaiah 28:11–12 — “the law” here means the Scriptures generally. In Isaiah, God warned rebellious Israel that because they would not listen to His prophets in their own language, He would speak to them through the foreign tongue of Assyrian invaders. Unintelligible foreign speech was a sign of judgment on those who refused to hear.","words":[{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G2084","hebrew":"ἑτερόγλωσσος","translit":"heteróglōssos","english":"strange languages"},{"strongs":"G5491","hebrew":"χεῖλος","translit":"cheîlos","english":"lips"},{"strongs":"G1522","hebrew":"εἰσακούω","translit":"eisakoúō","english":"hear"}]},{"v":22,"text":"Therefore other languages are for a sign, not to those who believe, but to the unbelieving; but prophesying is for a sign, not to the unbelieving, but to those who believe.","understanding":"Therefore tongues are a σημεῖον — a sign — not to believers but to unbelievers. This is a difficult verse, and verses 23 to 25 explain it. Uninterpreted tongues function as a sign of judgment to unbelievers: they hear only incomprehensible sounds and are confirmed in their unbelief, as in Isaiah's day. Prophecy, on the other hand, is a sign for believers — evidence of God's presence among His people — and, as the next verses show, it can bring unbelievers to faith.","words":[{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G4592","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","english":"sign"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believe"},{"strongs":"G571","hebrew":"ἄπιστος","translit":"ápistos","english":"unbelieving"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophesying"}]},{"v":23,"text":"If therefore the whole assembly is assembled together and all speak with other languages, and unlearned or unbelieving people come in, won’t they say that you are crazy?","understanding":"The scenario. The whole church gathers, and everyone speaks in tongues at once. Ἰδιῶται — outsiders, inquirers — or ἄπιστοι, unbelievers, walk in. Will they not say μαίνεσθε — you are mad, raving? Corinth knew the frenzied worship of the pagan mystery cults. A church that looked the same would convince no one.","words":[{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G4905","hebrew":"συνέρχομαι","translit":"synérchomai","english":"assembled together"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G2399","hebrew":"ἰδιώτης","translit":"idiṓtēs","english":"unlearned"},{"strongs":"G571","hebrew":"ἄπιστος","translit":"ápistos","english":"unbelieving"},{"strongs":"G3105","hebrew":"μαίνομαι","translit":"maínomai","english":"crazy"}]},{"v":24,"text":"But if all prophesy, and someone unbelieving or unlearned comes in, he is reproved by all, and he is judged by all.","understanding":"But if all prophesy — in turn, intelligibly — and an unbeliever comes in, he is ἐλέγχω — convicted, exposed, brought under conviction — by all, and ἀνακρίνω — examined, called to account — by all. The intelligible word of God, spoken in the power of the Spirit, does what Jesus said the Spirit would do: convict the world of sin (John 16:8).","words":[{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesy"},{"strongs":"G571","hebrew":"ἄπιστος","translit":"ápistos","english":"unbelieving"},{"strongs":"G1651","hebrew":"ἐλέγχω","translit":"elénchō","english":"reproved"},{"strongs":"G350","hebrew":"ἀνακρίνω","translit":"anakrínō","english":"judged"}]},{"v":25,"text":"And thus the secrets of his heart are revealed. So he will fall down on his face and worship God, declaring that God is among you indeed.","understanding":"The result: the κρυπτά — the hidden things — of his heart become φανερά, exposed. He falls on his face and προσκυνέω — worships — God, ἀπαγγέλλω — declaring openly — that “God is among you indeed.” This echoes Isaiah 45:14, where the nations confess that surely God is in Israel. The goal of Spirit-filled worship is not that visitors say, “What an amazing experience,” but that they say, “God is really here.”","words":[{"strongs":"G2927","hebrew":"κρυπτός","translit":"kryptós","english":"secrets"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G5318","hebrew":"φανερός","translit":"phanerós","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G518","hebrew":"ἀπαγγέλλω","translit":"apangéllō","english":"declaring"},{"strongs":"G3689","hebrew":"ὄντως","translit":"óntōs","english":"indeed"}]},{"v":26,"text":"What is it then, brothers? When you come together, each one of you has a psalm, has a teaching, has a revelation, has another language, has an interpretation. Let all things be done to build each other up.","understanding":"A precious window into early Christian worship. When you gather, each one has something: a ψαλμός (a song), a διδαχή (a teaching), an ἀποκάλυψις (a revelation), a tongue, an interpretation. Worship was participatory; many members contributed. And the governing rule: πάντα πρὸς οἰκοδομὴν γινέσθω — let everything be done for building up.","words":[{"strongs":"G4905","hebrew":"συνέρχομαι","translit":"synérchomai","english":"come together"},{"strongs":"G5568","hebrew":"ψαλμός","translit":"psalmós","english":"psalm"},{"strongs":"G1322","hebrew":"διδαχή","translit":"didachḗ","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G602","hebrew":"ἀποκάλυψις","translit":"apokálypsis","english":"revelation"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"language"},{"strongs":"G2058","hebrew":"ἑρμηνεία","translit":"hermēneía","english":"interpretation"},{"strongs":"G3619","hebrew":"οἰκοδομή","translit":"oikodomḗ","english":"build up"}]},{"v":27,"text":"If any man speaks in another language, let it be two, or at the most three, and in turn; and let one interpret.","understanding":"Practical regulation for tongues: two, or at most three, ἀνὰ μέρος — in turn, one at a time — and one must interpret. Order, not suppression.","words":[{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"language"},{"strongs":"G3313","hebrew":"μέρος","translit":"méros","english":"in turn"},{"strongs":"G1329","hebrew":"διερμηνεύω","translit":"diermēneúō","english":"interpret"}]},{"v":28,"text":"But if there is no interpreter, let him keep silent in the assembly, and let him speak to himself, and to God.","understanding":"If there is no διερμηνευτής — no interpreter — the tongue-speaker should σιγάω, keep silent, in the assembly, and speak to himself and to God. The gift is not forbidden, but in public it must serve the public.","words":[{"strongs":"G1328","hebrew":"διερμηνευτής","translit":"diermēneutḗs","english":"interpreter"},{"strongs":"G4601","hebrew":"σιγάω","translit":"sigáō","english":"keep silent"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speak"}]},{"v":29,"text":"Let the prophets speak, two or three, and let the others discern.","understanding":"Regulation for prophecy: two or three prophets should speak, and the others διακρίνω — weigh, evaluate, discern — what is said. New Testament prophecy is not received uncritically. It must be tested (1 Thessalonians 5:20–21; 1 John 4:1). This is a crucial difference from Old Testament prophecy and from Scripture itself: a prophecy in the Corinthian church could be partly right, partly mistaken, and had to be weighed.","words":[{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speak"},{"strongs":"G1252","hebrew":"διακρίνω","translit":"diakrínō","english":"discern"}]},{"v":30,"text":"But if a revelation is made to another sitting by, let the first keep silent.","understanding":"If a revelation comes to someone sitting nearby, the first speaker should σιγάω — fall silent. No one monopolises the floor, even with a genuine word from God.","words":[{"strongs":"G602","hebrew":"ἀποκάλυψις","translit":"apokálypsis","english":"revelation"},{"strongs":"G4601","hebrew":"σιγάω","translit":"sigáō","english":"keep silent"}]},{"v":31,"text":"For you all can prophesy one by one, that all may learn, and all may be exhorted.","understanding":"All can prophesy, καθ’ ἕνα — one by one — so that all may μανθάνω, learn, and all may be encouraged. The purpose of order is not to restrict the Spirit but to make sure everyone benefits.","words":[{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesy"},{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learn"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhorted"}]},{"v":32,"text":"The spirits of the prophets are subject to the prophets,","understanding":"A vital principle: the spirits of the prophets are ὑποτάσσω — subject to — the prophets. The Holy Spirit does not override a person's self-control. Unlike the frenzy of pagan ecstasy, Christian prophecy can wait its turn. “I couldn’t help it; the Spirit made me” is not an excuse for disorder.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirits"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"subject"}]},{"v":33,"text":"for God is not a God of confusion, but of peace, as in all the assemblies of the saints.","understanding":"The theological reason for everything in this chapter: “God is not a God of ἀκαταστασία” — disorder, confusion, chaos, the word used of political upheaval and riots — “but of εἰρήνη,” peace. Worship reflects the character of the God being worshipped. A chaotic God would be worshipped chaotically. The God of peace is worshipped in ordered freedom. “As in all the assemblies of the saints” probably belongs with this sentence, or begins the next.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G181","hebrew":"ἀκαταστασία","translit":"akatastasía","english":"confusion"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"}]},{"v":34,"text":"Let the wives be quiet in the assemblies, for it has not been permitted for them to be talking except in submission, as the law also says,","understanding":"One of the most debated verses in the letter. “Let the wives” — γυναῖκες can mean women or wives; verse 35's “their own husbands” suggests wives — “be quiet in the assemblies.” It cannot mean total silence, because Paul has already assumed that women pray and prophesy in the assembly (11:5). The most likely meaning, in context, is that wives should not disrupt the weighing of prophecy (v. 29) by questioning or challenging — perhaps their own husbands' prophecies — in public. Σιγάω is the same verb used of the tongue-speaker (v. 28) and the prophet (v. 30): it means stop speaking in a particular situation, not permanent silence. They are to be ὑποτάσσω — in an ordered relationship — “as the law also says,” probably the creation order of Genesis 2.","words":[{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wives"},{"strongs":"G4601","hebrew":"σιγάω","translit":"sigáō","english":"be quiet"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"},{"strongs":"G2010","hebrew":"ἐπιτρέπω","translit":"epitrépō","english":"permitted"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"talking"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"in submission"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"}]},{"v":35,"text":"if they desire to learn anything. “Let them ask their own husbands at home, for it is shameful for a wife to be talking in the assembly.”","understanding":"If they want to μανθάνω — learn — something, they should ἐπερωτάω — ask, inquire — their own husbands at home. It is αἰσχρόν — disgraceful, in that culture — for a wife to be talking in this way in the assembly. Note: the WEB places the second sentence in quotation marks, reflecting the view of some interpreters that it is a Corinthian slogan which Paul answers in verse 36. Most interpreters read it as Paul's own instruction. Either way, the issue is disruptive questioning during the assembly, not women's participation as such.","words":[{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learn"},{"strongs":"G1905","hebrew":"ἐπερωτάω","translit":"eperōtáō","english":"ask"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husbands"},{"strongs":"G149","hebrew":"αἰσχρόν","translit":"aischrón","english":"shameful"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"talking"}]},{"v":36,"text":"What!? Was it from you that the word of God went out? Or did it come to you alone?","understanding":"A sharp double question, aimed at the whole church: did the word of God originate with you, Corinth? Or are you the only ones it has reached? Corinth was acting as if it could make up its own rules for worship, independent of “all the assemblies of the saints” (v. 33). Paul reminds them that they are recipients, not originators, of the word.","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":37,"text":"If any man thinks himself to be a prophet, or spiritual, let him recognize the things which I write to you, that they are the commandment of the Lord.","understanding":"If anyone thinks he is a προφήτης or πνευματικός — a spiritual person, the very title Corinth prized — let him ἐπιγινώσκω — acknowledge, recognise — that what Paul writes is “the ἐντολή, the commandment, of the Lord.” The test of a true prophet is submission to apostolic teaching. A “spiritual” person who rejects Scripture is not spiritual.","words":[{"strongs":"G1380","hebrew":"δοκέω","translit":"dokéō","english":"thinks"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophet"},{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual"},{"strongs":"G1921","hebrew":"ἐπιγινώσκω","translit":"epiginṓskō","english":"recognize"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"}]},{"v":38,"text":"But if anyone is ignorant, let him be ignorant.","understanding":"If anyone ἀγνοέω — ignores, refuses to recognise — this, he is himself not recognised (as the oldest manuscripts read: “he is not recognised,” that is, by God or by the church). The WEB's “let him be ignorant” follows other manuscripts. Either way, the person who rejects apostolic teaching places himself outside it.","words":[{"strongs":"G50","hebrew":"ἀγνοέω","translit":"agnoéō","english":"ignorant"}]},{"v":39,"text":"Therefore, brothers, desire earnestly to prophesy, and don’t forbid speaking with other languages.","understanding":"The summary. So, brothers, ζηλοῦτε — be eager — to prophesy, and do not κωλύω — forbid — speaking in tongues. Paul refuses both extremes: he will not let tongues dominate the church, and he will not let them be banned. Prophecy is to be pursued; tongues are to be permitted and properly ordered.","words":[{"strongs":"G2206","hebrew":"ζηλόω","translit":"zēlóō","english":"desire earnestly"},{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesy"},{"strongs":"G2967","hebrew":"κωλύω","translit":"kōlýō","english":"forbid"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"}]},{"v":40,"text":"Let all things be done decently and in order.","understanding":"The final rule of Christian worship in five words: πάντα εὐσχημόνως καὶ κατὰ τάξιν — everything decently and in order. Εὐσχημόνως — beautifully, fittingly, with dignity. Τάξις — an ordered arrangement, the word used of soldiers in formation or priests serving in their turn (Luke 1:8). Order is not the enemy of the Spirit. It is the shape His freedom takes in a church.","words":[{"strongs":"G2156","hebrew":"εὐσχημόνως","translit":"euschēmónōs","english":"decently"},{"strongs":"G5010","hebrew":"τάξις","translit":"táxis","english":"order"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Trumpet on the Battlefield","verseRef":"v. 7-9","image":"Picture a Roman legion drawn up on a plain at dawn, thousands of men in armour waiting for the signal. Every movement of the army is controlled by the trumpet: one call means advance, another means hold, another means retreat, another means form a square. The commander gives the order, and the trumpeter raises his instrument — and blows a confused, wavering, indistinct sound that matches no known call. Down the line, men look at each other. Do we charge? Do we hold? Some move forward, some stay, some turn back. The line breaks. The enemy sees the confusion and attacks.","meaning":"“For if the trumpet gave an uncertain sound, who would prepare himself for war?” Paul's point is that the gathered church needs clear words to be built up, equipped and mobilised. Uninterpreted tongues in public worship, however genuine and beautiful between the speaker and God, are like a trumpet that gives no recognisable call. They do not build up, instruct or encourage anyone else. And the principle applies far beyond tongues. Preaching that is vague, jargon that only insiders understand, worship that is performance without meaning, prayers no one can follow — all of them are uncertain sounds. The church is at war, and its soldiers need clear signals. Five words with understanding are worth more than ten thousand without."},{"title":"The Visitor Who Fell on His Face","verseRef":"v. 23-25","image":"Picture two scenes. In the first, an unbeliever wanders into a house church in Corinth. Everyone is speaking in tongues at once — loud, ecstatic, unintelligible. It reminds him of the frenzied rites he has seen in the temples of Dionysus. He backs out of the door, shaking his head: these people are mad. In the second scene, the same man walks into a different gathering. People are speaking one at a time, in plain Greek, words from God — about sin, about mercy, about the hidden things people carry. And one of them says something that describes exactly what he did last week, something no one could know. His heart is laid bare. He drops to his knees, then to his face, and says, “God is really among you.”","meaning":"Paul's measure of Spirit-filled worship is not how ecstatic it feels but what it does to the people who encounter it. Chaotic tongues confirm an unbeliever in his unbelief. Intelligible prophecy, spoken in the Spirit's power, convicts him, exposes the secrets of his heart and brings him to worship God. That is what Jesus promised the Spirit would do: “convict the world about sin, about righteousness, and about judgment” (John 16:8). The question for every church is not, ‘How spiritual did that feel?’ but, ‘Did anyone meet God?’ The greatest compliment a visitor can pay a church is not, “What a great experience,” but, “God is among you indeed.”"},{"title":"Waiting Your Turn in the Spirit","verseRef":"v. 29-33","image":"Picture a gathering where someone is prophesying — a genuine word, delivered with passion. While he is speaking, a woman across the room receives a revelation from God. She does not shout over him. She catches his eye or raises her hand. He finishes his sentence and sits down, and she speaks. Then the elders and others in the room quietly weigh what has been said, testing it against Scripture, holding on to what is good. No one is grabbing the floor. No one claims the Spirit forced him to interrupt. There is freedom, and there is order, and they are not at war with each other.","meaning":"“The spirits of the prophets are subject to the prophets, for God is not a God of confusion, but of peace” (vv. 32–33). This is one of the most important principles about the Holy Spirit in the New Testament. Pagan ecstasy overpowered people; the Holy Spirit does not. He works through self-controlled people, and self-control is itself His fruit (Galatians 5:23). That means no one can excuse disorder by blaming the Spirit. It also means prophecy must be weighed (v. 29), because a person speaking under the Spirit's influence can still mix in his own thoughts. Order in worship is not the opposite of the Spirit's freedom. It is the evidence of His presence, because He is the Spirit of the God of peace."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Prophecy Builds Up More Than Uninterpreted Tongues (v. 1-5)","body":"Pursue love and desire spiritual gifts, especially prophecy; tongues speak to God and build up the speaker, prophecy speaks to people and builds up the church — unless tongues are interpreted."},{"label":"The Need for Intelligibility (v. 6-12)","body":"Musical instruments, the battle trumpet and foreign languages all show that sounds without clear meaning communicate nothing; seek gifts that build up the church."},{"label":"Spirit and Mind Together (v. 13-19)","body":"Pray for interpretation; pray and sing with spirit and mind; outsiders cannot say Amen to what they do not understand; Paul speaks in tongues more than all, but in church prefers five intelligible words."},{"label":"Tongues, Prophecy and Unbelievers (v. 20-25)","body":"Be mature in thinking; uninterpreted tongues function as a sign of judgment to unbelievers, while prophecy convicts them and leads them to worship and confess that God is among you."},{"label":"Order in the Gathering (v. 26-36)","body":"Everyone may contribute, but all must build up: two or three tongues in turn with interpretation, two or three prophets with the others weighing; prophets control their own spirits, for God is a God of peace; wives are not to disrupt with questions; the Corinthians are not the source of God's word."},{"label":"The Lord's Command and the Final Rule (v. 37-40)","body":"True prophets will acknowledge Paul's instructions as the Lord's command; desire prophecy, do not forbid tongues, and let everything be done decently and in order."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Love Chooses the Gift That Builds Up","paragraphs":["Chapter 14 is where Paul applies chapters 12 and 13 to the actual problem in Corinth. And it begins by joining them: “Follow after love, and earnestly desire spiritual gifts, but especially that you may prophesy” (v. 1). Love is the way (13:1–13), and love determines which gifts are most to be desired — the ones that build up others most. That is why prophecy is preferred to uninterpreted tongues. Paul's comparison is clear and consistent throughout the chapter: tongues are addressed to God (v. 2), build up the speaker (v. 4) and are unintelligible to others unless interpreted (vv. 2, 5); prophecy is addressed to people and gives them “edification, exhortation, and consolation” (v. 3), building up the church (v. 4).","It is essential to notice that Paul is not against speaking in tongues. “I desire to have you all speak with other languages” (v. 5). “I thank my God, I speak with other languages more than you all” (v. 18). “Don’t forbid speaking with other languages” (v. 39). Some readers of this chapter have concluded that Paul is quietly trying to eliminate tongues; others have concluded that he wants every believer to practise them publicly. Both readings miss his point. Paul values tongues as genuine communication with God — the speaker “speaks mysteries” “in the Spirit” (v. 2) — and he uses the gift himself. But in the assembly, what matters is whether others are built up. The gathered church is not the place for private devotion that excludes others.","Verse 3 also gives the clearest description of New Testament prophecy anywhere in Scripture: it is speech “to men for their edification, exhortation, and consolation.” That is, it builds up, it urges and encourages, and it comforts. It is not primarily prediction, though prediction can be part of it (Acts 11:28; 21:10–11). It is Spirit-prompted speech that applies God's truth to people's lives in a way that strengthens them. And because it is spoken in ordinary language, it engages the mind. Paul's ideal for worship is not mindless ecstasy or spiritless intellectualism, but both together: “I will pray with the spirit, and I will pray with the understanding also. I will sing with the spirit, and I will sing with the understanding also” (v. 15)."]},{"heading":"Five Words Against Ten Thousand: Why Clarity Is Love","paragraphs":["Verses 6 to 19 make one argument from several directions: in public worship, unintelligible speech does not build anyone up. Paul uses analogies the Corinthians would have understood instantly. Musical instruments must make distinct notes or no one can recognise the tune (v. 7). A battle trumpet must sound a clear call or no soldier will know whether to advance (v. 8). A foreign language makes the speaker a βάρβαρος to the hearer, and the hearer a βάρβαρος to the speaker (v. 11) — and in a city proud of its Greek culture, that word meant a foreigner, an outsider, someone whose speech sounded like nonsense. Tongues without interpretation turn brothers and sisters into strangers to one another.","Then verse 16 introduces the ἰδιώτης — the ordinary person, the outsider, the one without this gift. When someone prays or gives thanks only in the spirit, “how will he who fills the place of the unlearned say the ‘Amen’ at your giving of thanks, seeing he doesn’t know what you say?” In early Christian worship, as in the synagogue, the congregation responded to prayers with a spoken Amen — an act of agreement and participation. You cannot say Amen to what you do not understand. Paul grants that the prayer itself may be beautiful: “For you most certainly give thanks well, but the other person is not built up” (v. 17).","And then comes one of the most striking sentences in the chapter: “I thank my God, I speak with other languages more than you all. However in the assembly I would rather speak five words with my understanding, that I might instruct others also, than ten thousand words in another language” (vv. 18–19). Ten thousand — μύριοι — was the largest number in the Greek language. Five intelligible words outweigh the largest number Paul could name. That is the logic of love. In the gathered church, the question is not, “What edifies me?” but, “What edifies everyone?” And the principle reaches far beyond tongues. Every form of Christian communication — preaching, teaching, singing, praying aloud — should be measured by whether ordinary people can understand it and be built up. Jargon, showmanship and spiritual performance may impress, but they do not edify. Clarity is a form of love."]},{"heading":"Ordered Freedom, and the Hard Verses About Wives","paragraphs":["Verses 26 to 33 give a precious picture of early Christian worship: “When you come together, each one of you has a psalm, has a teaching, has a revelation, has another language, has an interpretation” (v. 26). Worship was participatory; many members contributed. And Paul regulates that participation without suppressing it. Tongues: two or three, one at a time, with interpretation; if there is no interpreter, silence in public and prayer in private (vv. 27–28). Prophecy: two or three, with the others weighing what is said, and anyone receiving a revelation free to speak while the first gives way (vv. 29–31). The principle beneath it all: “The spirits of the prophets are subject to the prophets, for God is not a God of confusion, but of peace” (vv. 32–33). The Holy Spirit does not override self-control. Order is not the enemy of the Spirit. It is the character of the God He reveals.","Verses 34 and 35 are among the most debated in Paul's letters, and honesty requires presenting them carefully. “Let the wives be quiet in the assemblies, for it has not been permitted for them to be talking except in submission, as the law also says, if they desire to learn anything. ‘Let them ask their own husbands at home, for it is shameful for a wife to be talking in the assembly.’” Three things are clear from the context. First, this cannot be a command for women to be completely silent in worship, because Paul has already assumed in 11:5 that women pray and prophesy in the assembly, and he regulates rather than forbids it. Second, the same verb σιγάω (be silent) is used of tongue-speakers without an interpreter (v. 28) and of a prophet when another receives a revelation (v. 30); in each case it means to stop speaking in a particular situation, not to be permanently mute. Third, the immediate context is the weighing of prophecies (v. 29), and the specific issue is wives who want “to learn” by asking questions.","The most likely reading, then, is that Paul is addressing wives who were disrupting the evaluation of prophecy — perhaps by publicly questioning or challenging prophecies, possibly including their own husbands' — and he directs that such questions be asked at home, in keeping with the ordered relationship between husbands and wives grounded in creation (“as the law also says” probably refers to Genesis 2, as in 11:8–9). Some interpreters, following a minority of manuscripts that place these verses after verse 40, argue they were not part of Paul's original letter, but every surviving manuscript includes them. Others, as the WEB's quotation marks suggest, think part of verse 35 is a Corinthian slogan that Paul rejects in verse 36. The strongest reading, consistent with 11:5 and the immediate context, is the first: a specific instruction about disruptive questioning during the weighing of prophecy, not a ban on women's ministry in the gathering. Then Paul turns to the whole church in verse 36: the word of God did not originate with Corinth. They must not invent their own practices. And he closes with the rule that sums up the whole chapter: “Let all things be done decently and in order” (v. 40)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["The New Testament's first account of tongues, at Pentecost, shows a gift that was immediately intelligible to the hearers: “everyone heard them speaking in his own language” (Acts 2:6), and they said, “we hear them speaking in our languages the mighty works of God!” (Acts 2:11). Even then, some mocked: “They are filled with new wine” (Acts 2:13). Tongues appear again when the Spirit falls on the Gentiles in Cornelius's house, “speaking in other languages and magnifying God” (Acts 10:46), and on the disciples in Ephesus: “they spoke with other languages and prophesied” (Acts 19:6). In each case tongues are a sign of the Spirit's coming. In Corinth, however, they had become a source of pride and disorder, and needed the regulation of love.","Paul's instruction that prophecies must be weighed (v. 29) is echoed in his earliest letter: “Don’t quench the Spirit. Don’t despise prophesies. Test all things, and hold firmly that which is good” (1 Thessalonians 5:19–21). Notice the balance — do not quench, do not despise, but test. John gives the same instruction: “Beloved, don’t believe every spirit, but test the spirits, whether they are of God, because many false prophets have gone out into the world” (1 John 4:1). The New Testament does not ask believers to choose between openness to the Spirit and discernment. It requires both.","And Paul's vision of worship that engages both spirit and mind, and builds up others, appears again in his later letters. To the Ephesians: “be filled with the Spirit, speaking to one another in psalms, hymns, and spiritual songs; singing, and making melody in your heart to the Lord” (Ephesians 5:18–19). To the Colossians: “Let the word of Christ dwell in you richly; in all wisdom teaching and admonishing one another with psalms, hymns, and spiritual songs, singing with grace in your heart to the Lord” (Colossians 3:16). Notice the phrase “to one another” and “teaching and admonishing one another.” Spirit-filled worship is never merely private. It is the word of Christ dwelling richly in the church and flowing out in ways that build everyone up."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Building up / edification","hebrew":"οἰκοδομή","translit":"oikodomḗ","strongs":"G3619 (verb, build up οἰκοδομέω, oikodoméō, G3618)","verses":"v. 3, 5, 12, 26 (verb in v. 4 ×2, 17)","insight":"Strong's defines οἰκοδομή as “architecture, i.e. (concretely) a structure; figuratively, confirmation,” and οἰκοδομέω as “to be a house-builder, i.e. construct or (figuratively) confirm.” The noun and verb together appear seven times in this chapter, making edification its key word. It is the same image as 3:9 (“You are… God’s building”) and 8:1 (“love builds up”). Paul's test for everything that happens in worship is whether it builds up the church — like a builder adding a stone to a wall. Not whether it impresses, not whether it feels powerful, not even whether it is genuine, but whether it makes the whole house stronger. “Let all things be done to build each other up” (v. 26)."},{"english":"Prophecy","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","strongs":"G4394 (verb, prophesy προφητεύω, prophēteúō, G4395)","verses":"v. 6, 22; the verb in v. 1, 3, 4, 5 (×2), 24, 31, 39","insight":"Strong's defines προφητεία as “prediction (scriptural or other),” and the verb προφητεύω as “to foretell events, divine, speak under inspiration, exercise the prophetic office.” But the New Testament meaning is broader than prediction. Verse 3 is the key: prophecy speaks to people “for their edification, exhortation, and consolation.” It is Spirit-prompted speech in ordinary language that applies God's truth to people's hearts. Verses 24 and 25 show its power: it can expose the secrets of an unbeliever's heart and lead him to worship. And verse 29 shows its limits: New Testament prophecy must be weighed, because it is spoken by fallible people. It is never placed on the same level as Scripture."},{"english":"Unlearned / outsider / ordinary person","hebrew":"ἰδιώτης","translit":"idiṓtēs","strongs":"G2399","verses":"v. 16, 23, 24","insight":"Strong's: “a private person, i.e. (by implication) an ignoramus (compare ‘idiot’).” English gets “idiot” from this word, but in Greek it did not mean stupid; it meant a private individual, a layman, someone without specialised skill. In Acts 4:13 Peter and John are called ἰδιῶται — “unlearned” men without formal rabbinic training. In 14:16 the ἰδιώτης is the person in the assembly without the gift of tongues or interpretation, and in verses 23 and 24 he is an inquirer or newcomer. Paul repeatedly asks the Corinthians to think about this person. Worship must be accessible to the ordinary believer and the curious outsider. If they cannot understand it, they cannot say Amen."},{"english":"Subject to / in submission","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","strongs":"G5293","verses":"v. 32, 34","insight":"Strong's: “to subordinate; reflexively, to obey.” The word combines ὑπό, under, and τάσσω, to arrange or set in order — the root of τάξις, “order,” in verse 40. It describes being placed in an ordered arrangement. It is used of Christ's willing subjection to the Father (15:28), of the church to Christ (Ephesians 5:24), of believers to governing authorities (Romans 13:1) and of believers to one another (Ephesians 5:21). In verse 32 the spirits of the prophets are subject to the prophets: the Holy Spirit works through self-control, not by overpowering. Submission in Scripture is not about inferiority of worth; Christ is subject to the Father and fully God. It is about ordered relationships that reflect the God of peace."},{"english":"Confusion / disorder","hebrew":"ἀκαταστασία","translit":"akatastasía","strongs":"G181","verses":"v. 33","insight":"Strong's: “instability, i.e. disorder.” The word was used of political turmoil, riots, revolutions and social upheaval. Jesus uses it of the “disturbances” that will precede the end (Luke 21:9), and Paul lists it among his sufferings in 2 Corinthians 6:5 (“riots”). James says “where jealousy and selfish ambition are, there is confusion” (James 3:16, using the same word). Paul's point in verse 33 is that worship reflects the God being worshipped. God is not a God of chaos, upheaval and confusion, but of peace. When a church's worship becomes a riot of competing voices, it misrepresents the God it claims to worship. Order is a theological statement."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the way you measure worship, the people you forget when you minister, and the excuse of spontaneity for what is really disorder.","items":[{"title":"Did Anyone Meet God?","body":"You may have measured worship by how it made you feel. The emotion of the music, the intensity of the moment, the sense of personal blessing. The Spirit wants to give you a different measure: what did it do for others? Did it build up the church? Did the ordinary believer understand it? Did the visitor leave saying, “God is among you indeed”? This is not a call to cold, emotionless worship — Paul prays and sings with the spirit. It is a call to love, which always asks about the other person. Next time you worship, ask not only, “Did I meet God?” but, “Did anyone else meet Him, and did I help?”"},{"title":"The Person in the Back Row","body":"Three times in this chapter Paul asks the Corinthians to think about the ἰδιώτης — the ordinary person, the outsider, the newcomer who does not know the language. Who is that person in your world? The new believer who does not understand your church jargon. The visitor who has never been to church. The child, the teenager, the person from a different culture. The Spirit is asking whether your speech, your ministry, your prayers and your worship are clear enough for them to say Amen. Love chooses clarity over display. Five words they can understand are worth more than ten thousand that only impress insiders."},{"title":"Is It the Spirit or Is It You?","body":"“The spirits of the prophets are subject to the prophets.” It is easy to justify impulsiveness, interruption or disorder by calling it spontaneity or the leading of the Spirit. But the Holy Spirit is the Spirit of the God of peace, and self-control is His fruit. He does not override your will; He works through it. The Spirit is asking you to be honest: have you ever used His name to justify your own desire to be heard, to dominate, to be seen as spiritual? True spiritual power waits its turn, submits its words to be weighed, and seeks the good of others. That is not quenching the Spirit. That is walking in step with Him."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Edification Check","body":"Think about how you participate in your church — speaking, singing, praying, serving, sharing. Ask: does my participation build up others, or mainly myself? When I speak, are people encouraged, strengthened and comforted? Write down one way your contribution could become more edifying."},{"title":"The Clarity Check","body":"Recall the last time you spoke about your faith — in church, at home, to an unbeliever. Did you use words the other person could understand? Did you use jargon or insider language? Could a newcomer have said Amen? Write down one phrase you use that might be unclear to an outsider, and how you could say it more simply."},{"title":"The Self-Control Check","body":"Is there any area in your spiritual life where you claim to be led by the Spirit but are actually acting on impulse — interrupting, dominating, refusing correction, insisting your words are from God without allowing them to be weighed? Name it honestly before God."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Praying With Spirit and Mind","body":"This week, practise verse 15 in your personal prayer. Spend ten minutes praying freely from your spirit — with whatever words, groans, silence or (if you have the gift) tongues come. Then spend ten minutes praying with your understanding: choose a passage of Scripture (Psalm 103 or Ephesians 3:14–21 work well) and pray it back to God phrase by phrase, thinking carefully about each line. Notice how each kind of prayer enriches the other. Do this daily for a week."},{"title":"One Word That Builds","body":"Before your next church gathering, pray: “Holy Spirit, give me one word of edification, exhortation or consolation for someone” (v. 3). Then, during or after the gathering, look for the person God puts on your heart, and speak that word to them — simply, clearly, humbly. It may be a verse, an encouragement, a specific thing you have noticed God doing in them, or a gentle comfort. Do not dress it up or claim more authority than it has; let them weigh it. Afterward, write down what happened. This is how prophecy, in its simplest form, builds up the body."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Holy Spirit, I want to pursue love above everything. And in that love, I earnestly desire Your gifts — especially the gifts that build up Your people. Thank You for every gift You give: for tongues that speak mysteries to God, for prophecy that strengthens, encourages and comforts, for interpretation, for teaching, for every song and revelation. Teach me to use them not for myself but for the body. Lord, I want to worship You with my spirit and with my mind. I do not want empty emotion or cold intellect. I want both, joined in love. Forgive me for every time my words in Your name have been an uncertain trumpet — unclear, self-focused, impossible for others to understand. Give me five words with understanding rather than ten thousand that impress. Help me to remember the ordinary person, the newcomer, the outsider, and to speak so that they can say Amen. Father, You are not a God of confusion but of peace. Let the worship of Your church reflect who You are. Give me self-control in the Spirit. Let my words be weighed, and let me receive correction humbly. And let every gathering of Your people be a place where unbelievers fall on their faces and say, ‘God is among you indeed.’ Let all things be done decently and in order, for Your glory. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Ezra Reads, and the Levites Give the Sense","ref":"Nehemiah 8:1-12","relatedVerses":"v. 6-19","summary":"After the wall of Jerusalem was rebuilt, all the people gathered as one in the square before the Water Gate, and asked Ezra to bring the book of the law. He stood on a wooden platform and read from early morning until midday, and all the people stood up when he opened the book. Then the Levites moved among the people, reading from the law distinctly and giving the sense, so that they understood the reading. The people wept as they heard it, and Nehemiah told them that this day was holy, and that the joy of the LORD was their strength. They went away to celebrate with great joy, because they had understood the words. That is 1 Corinthians 14 in Israel's history: the word of God, made clear, building up God's people, producing repentance and joy."},{"title":"Pentecost: Every Man in His Own Language","ref":"Acts 2:1-41","relatedVerses":"v. 2, 21-25","summary":"On the day of Pentecost, the Holy Spirit came on the disciples like a rushing wind and tongues of fire, and they began to speak in other languages. Jews from every nation heard them declaring the mighty works of God, each in his own language. Some were amazed; others mocked and said they were drunk. Then Peter stood up and preached a clear, intelligible sermon about Jesus crucified and risen. The people were cut to the heart and asked what they should do, and about three thousand believed and were baptised that day. Tongues drew a crowd and signalled the Spirit's arrival; plain prophetic preaching brought conviction and conversion. Pentecost shows both gifts in their proper places, and Paul's teaching in 1 Corinthians 14 keeps them there."},{"title":"A Foreign Tongue at the City Wall","ref":"Isaiah 28:9-13; 2 Kings 18:17-36","relatedVerses":"v. 21-22","summary":"Through Isaiah, God warned the leaders of Jerusalem that because they mocked His prophets' plain message, He would speak to them through people of strange lips and a foreign tongue. That warning came true when the Assyrian army came to Jerusalem's walls. The Assyrian commander, the Rabshakeh, shouted his threats to the people on the wall. Hezekiah's officials begged him to speak in Aramaic, the diplomatic language the common people did not understand, rather than in the Judean language. Foreign speech at the wall was the sound of judgment on a nation that had refused to listen to God's clear word. Paul quotes Isaiah 28 in 1 Corinthians 14:21 to explain why uninterpreted tongues do not help unbelievers: unintelligible speech is a sign of judgment, not a means of conversion. God's preferred way to save is a clear word."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":15,"chapterTitle":"If Christ Has Not Been Raised — The Resurrection, the Last Enemy and the Victory","themeTitle":"“Death, Where Is Your Sting?”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Now I declare to you, brothers, the Good News which I preached to you, which also you received, in which you also stand,","understanding":"Paul now addresses the deepest problem in Corinth: some were denying the resurrection of the dead (v. 12). He begins not with an argument but with a reminder: γνωρίζω — I make known again — the εὐαγγέλιον I preached, which you παραλαμβάνω — received — and in which you ἵστημι, stand. The gospel is not something you graduate from. It is the ground under your feet.","words":[{"strongs":"G1107","hebrew":"γνωρίζω","translit":"gnōrízō","english":"declare"},{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"},{"strongs":"G3880","hebrew":"παραλαμβάνω","translit":"paralambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stand"}]},{"v":2,"text":"by which also you are saved, if you hold firmly the word which I preached to you—unless you believed in vain.","understanding":"By this gospel you are being σῴζω — saved; the present tense: it is still saving you — if you κατέχω, hold firmly, to the word as Paul preached it. “Unless you believed εἰκῇ — in vain, without effect.” Paul is not doubting their salvation; he is warning that a faith which lets go of the resurrection was never holding the real gospel.","words":[{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saved"},{"strongs":"G2722","hebrew":"κατέχω","translit":"katéchō","english":"hold firmly"},{"strongs":"G1500","hebrew":"εἰκῆ","translit":"eikē","english":"in vain"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For I delivered to you first of all that which I also received: that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures,","understanding":"Ἐν πρώτοις — as of first importance. Paul παραδίδωμι — handed on — what he himself παραλαμβάνω — received: the technical words for transmitting sacred tradition, as in 11:23. What follows in verses 3 to 5 has the rhythm of an early creed, which many scholars across the theological spectrum date to within a few years of the crucifixion. First article: Christ ἀπέθανεν — died — ὑπὲρ τῶν ἁμαρτιῶν ἡμῶν — for our sins — “according to the γραφαί, the Scriptures” (Isaiah 53).","words":[{"strongs":"G3860","hebrew":"παραδίδωμι","translit":"paradídōmi","english":"delivered"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first of all"},{"strongs":"G3880","hebrew":"παραλαμβάνω","translit":"paralambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"died"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G1124","hebrew":"γραφή","translit":"graphḗ","english":"Scriptures"}]},{"v":4,"text":"that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures,","understanding":"Second article: ἐτάφη — He was buried. The burial confirms the reality of the death — a real body in a real tomb. Third article: ἐγήγερται — He has been raised. The verb is perfect tense: He was raised and remains risen. “On the third day according to the Scriptures” (compare Hosea 6:2; Jonah 1:17, which Jesus Himself applied to His resurrection in Matthew 12:40).","words":[{"strongs":"G2290","hebrew":"θάπτω","translit":"tháptō","english":"buried"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G1124","hebrew":"γραφή","translit":"graphḗ","english":"Scriptures"}]},{"v":5,"text":"and that he appeared to Cephas, then to the twelve.","understanding":"Fourth article: ὤφθη — He appeared, He was seen. First to Κηφᾶς — Peter, using his Aramaic name (compare Luke 24:34, “The Lord is risen indeed, and has appeared to Simon!”). Then to the Twelve — the title for the apostolic group, even though Judas was gone. This list is legal testimony. The women, who were the first witnesses according to all four Gospels, are not named here — very possibly because the list follows the form of formal public testimony, in which a woman's witness was given little weight in that culture.","words":[{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"appeared"},{"strongs":"G2786","hebrew":"Κηφᾶς","translit":"Kēphâs","english":"Cephas"},{"strongs":"G1427","hebrew":"δώδεκα","translit":"dṓdeka","english":"twelve"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Then he appeared to over five hundred brothers at once, most of whom remain until now, but some have also fallen asleep.","understanding":"Then ἐπάνω πεντακοσίοις — to more than five hundred brothers ἐφάπαξ — at one time. Not a private vision or a single excited group; a crowd. And Paul adds a challenge: “most of whom remain until now.” In other words: they are alive; go and ask them. Some have κοιμάω — fallen asleep — the Christian word for death, because death is no longer final.","words":[{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"appeared"},{"strongs":"G1883","hebrew":"ἐπάνω","translit":"epánō","english":"over"},{"strongs":"G4001","hebrew":"πεντακόσιοι","translit":"pentakósioi","english":"five hundred"},{"strongs":"G2178","hebrew":"ἐφάπαξ","translit":"ephápax","english":"at once"},{"strongs":"G2837","hebrew":"κοιμάω","translit":"koimáō","english":"fallen asleep"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Then he appeared to James, then to all the apostles,","understanding":"Then to Ἰάκωβος — James, the Lord's brother, who had not believed in Jesus during His ministry (John 7:5) and became a leader of the Jerusalem church (Galatians 1:19; Acts 15). What changes a sceptical brother into a man who will die for his brother's claims? An appearance of the risen Christ. Then to all the apostles.","words":[{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"appeared"},{"strongs":"G2385","hebrew":"Ἰάκωβος","translit":"Iákōbos","english":"James"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostles"}]},{"v":8,"text":"and last of all, as to the child born at the wrong time, he appeared to me also.","understanding":"Last of all, to Paul — ὡσπερεὶ τῷ ἐκτρώματι, “as to one untimely born.” Ἔκτρωμα means a miscarriage, a stillborn or abnormally born child. It may have been an insult Paul's opponents used of him — an apostle born wrong — which he humbly accepts. He saw the risen Christ on the Damascus road, after the others, out of season.","words":[{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G1626","hebrew":"ἔκτρωμα","translit":"éktrōma","english":"child born at the wrong time"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"appeared"}]},{"v":9,"text":"For I am the least of the apostles, who is not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the assembly of God.","understanding":"Ἐλάχιστος — the least. Paul calls himself the least of the apostles, not ἱκανός — not fit, not sufficient — to be called an apostle, because he διώκω — persecuted — the church of God. He never forgot it. The memory of his violence was not a weight that crushed him; it was the backdrop against which grace shone.","words":[{"strongs":"G1646","hebrew":"ἐλάχιστος","translit":"eláchistos","english":"least"},{"strongs":"G2425","hebrew":"ἱκανός","translit":"hikanós","english":"worthy"},{"strongs":"G1377","hebrew":"διώκω","translit":"diṓkō","english":"persecuted"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"}]},{"v":10,"text":"But by the grace of God I am what I am. His grace which was given to me was not futile, but I worked more than all of them; yet not I, but the grace of God which was with me.","understanding":"One of the great sentences of grace in Scripture. “By the χάρις of God I am what I am.” His grace toward me was not κενή — empty, futile — but I κοπιάω — laboured to exhaustion — more than all of them; “yet not I, but the grace of God which was with me.” Grace does not make you passive. It makes you work harder than anyone, and then makes you unable to take the credit.","words":[{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2756","hebrew":"κενός","translit":"kenós","english":"futile"},{"strongs":"G2872","hebrew":"κοπιάω","translit":"kopiáō","english":"worked"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Whether then it is I or they, so we preach, and so you believed.","understanding":"Whether it is Paul or the other apostles, this is what they all preach, and this is what you believed. There is one gospel, not a Pauline version and a Petrine version. The resurrection is the common confession of every apostle.","words":[{"strongs":"G2784","hebrew":"κηρύσσω","translit":"kērýssō","english":"preach"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believed"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Now if Christ is preached, that he has been raised from the dead, how do some among you say that there is no resurrection of the dead?","understanding":"The problem stated. If Christ is κηρύσσω — proclaimed — as raised from the dead, how can some among you say there is no ἀνάστασις νεκρῶν — resurrection of the dead? They likely did not deny Christ's resurrection outright; Greek thought found bodily resurrection absurd, and some may have believed in only a spiritual survival, or that the resurrection was already past (compare 2 Timothy 2:18). Paul shows that denying the general resurrection destroys Christ's resurrection too.","words":[{"strongs":"G2784","hebrew":"κηρύσσω","translit":"kērýssō","english":"preached"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"}]},{"v":13,"text":"But if there is no resurrection of the dead, neither has Christ been raised.","understanding":"The logic is tight. If there is no such thing as resurrection of the dead, then Christ — who was dead — has not been raised either. You cannot deny the category and keep the example.","words":[{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"}]},{"v":14,"text":"If Christ has not been raised, then our preaching is in vain, and your faith also is in vain.","understanding":"The first consequence. If Christ is not raised, then our κήρυγμα — preaching — is κενός, empty, and your πίστις is empty too. Christianity without the resurrection is not a weaker version of the faith. It is nothing.","words":[{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"},{"strongs":"G2782","hebrew":"κήρυγμα","translit":"kḗrygma","english":"preaching"},{"strongs":"G2756","hebrew":"κενός","translit":"kenós","english":"vain"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Yes, we are also found false witnesses of God, because we testified about God that he raised up Christ, whom he didn’t raise up, if it is so that the dead are not raised.","understanding":"The second consequence. The apostles are found ψευδομάρτυρες — false witnesses — of God, because they μαρτυρέω — testified — that He raised Christ. Paul is not offering an inspiring metaphor. He is making a factual claim that is either true or a lie told about God.","words":[{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G5575","hebrew":"ψευδομάρτυρ","translit":"pseudomártyr","english":"false witnesses"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testified"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For if the dead aren’t raised, neither has Christ been raised.","understanding":"The argument repeated for emphasis: if the dead are not raised, then Christ has not been raised.","words":[{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"}]},{"v":17,"text":"If Christ has not been raised, your faith is vain; you are still in your sins.","understanding":"The third consequence, and the most devastating. If Christ is not raised, your faith is μάταιος — futile, useless, the word used of idols — and “you are still in your sins.” The resurrection is God's public verdict that the sacrifice for sin was accepted (Romans 4:25). Without it, there is no proof that anything was paid.","words":[{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"},{"strongs":"G3152","hebrew":"μάταιος","translit":"mátaios","english":"vain"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Then they also who are fallen asleep in Christ have perished.","understanding":"The fourth consequence. Those who have fallen asleep in Christ ἀπόλλυμι — have perished, are lost. Every believer who died trusting Christ would be simply gone.","words":[{"strongs":"G2837","hebrew":"κοιμάω","translit":"koimáō","english":"fallen asleep"},{"strongs":"G622","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","english":"perished"}]},{"v":19,"text":"If we have only hoped in Christ in this life, we are of all men most pitiable.","understanding":"The fifth consequence. If our hope in Christ is for this life only, we are ἐλεεινότεροι — more to be pitied — than all people. Paul had given up everything for this hope: comfort, status, safety, his body. If there is no resurrection, he has been a fool, and so has every martyr.","words":[{"strongs":"G1679","hebrew":"ἐλπίζω","translit":"elpízō","english":"hoped"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G1652","hebrew":"ἐλεεινός","translit":"eleeinós","english":"pitiable"}]},{"v":20,"text":"But now Christ has been raised from the dead. He became the first fruits of those who are asleep.","understanding":"“Νυνὶ δέ — But now!” One of the great turning points in Scripture. Christ has been raised from the dead, the ἀπαρχή — first fruits — of those who have fallen asleep. At the Feast of Firstfruits, the first sheaf of the harvest was waved before the LORD as a pledge that the whole harvest would follow (Leviticus 23:10–11). Christ's resurrection is the first sheaf. The rest of the harvest is guaranteed.","words":[{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G536","hebrew":"ἀπαρχή","translit":"aparchḗ","english":"first fruits"},{"strongs":"G2837","hebrew":"κοιμάω","translit":"koimáō","english":"asleep"}]},{"v":21,"text":"For since death came by man, the resurrection of the dead also came by man.","understanding":"Death came δι’ ἀνθρώπου — through a man. So the resurrection came through a man. God reversed the catastrophe through the same kind of being who caused it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"}]},{"v":22,"text":"For as in Adam all die, so also in Christ all will be made alive.","understanding":"The two Adams. In Ἀδάμ, all die — the whole human race, united to its first head, shares his death (Romans 5:12). In Christ, all will be ζωοποιέω — made alive. “All” in the second clause means all who are in Christ (v. 23 says “those who are Christ’s”). Your destiny depends on which Adam you belong to.","words":[{"strongs":"G76","hebrew":"Ἀδάμ","translit":"Adám","english":"Adam"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"die"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G2227","hebrew":"ζωοποιέω","translit":"zōopoiéō","english":"made alive"}]},{"v":23,"text":"But each in his own order: Christ the first fruits, then those who are Christ’s, at his coming.","understanding":"But each in his own τάγμα — order, rank; a military word for a division of troops marching in sequence. Christ the first fruits; then, ἐν τῇ παρουσίᾳ αὐτοῦ — at His coming — those who belong to Christ. Παρουσία was used of a king's official visit to a city. The resurrection of believers happens at the King's arrival.","words":[{"strongs":"G5001","hebrew":"τάγμα","translit":"tágma","english":"order"},{"strongs":"G536","hebrew":"ἀπαρχή","translit":"aparchḗ","english":"first fruits"},{"strongs":"G3952","hebrew":"παρουσία","translit":"parousía","english":"coming"}]},{"v":24,"text":"Then the end comes, when he will deliver up the Kingdom to God, even the Father; when he will have abolished all rule and all authority and power.","understanding":"Then the τέλος — the end, the goal. Christ will παραδίδωμι — hand over — the kingdom to God the Father, after He has καταργέω — rendered powerless, abolished — every ἀρχή (rule), every ἐξουσία (authority) and every δύναμις (power) opposed to God. History is not drifting. It is moving toward a handover ceremony.","words":[{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"end"},{"strongs":"G3860","hebrew":"παραδίδωμι","translit":"paradídōmi","english":"deliver up"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G2673","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","english":"abolished"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"rule"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"}]},{"v":25,"text":"For he must reign until he has put all his enemies under his feet.","understanding":"“He must βασιλεύω — reign — until He has put all his ἐχθροί, enemies, under his feet.” An echo of Psalm 110:1, the Old Testament verse most quoted in the New: “Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies your footstool.” Christ is reigning now.","words":[{"strongs":"G936","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","english":"reign"},{"strongs":"G2190","hebrew":"ἐχθρός","translit":"echthrós","english":"enemies"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"}]},{"v":26,"text":"The last enemy that will be abolished is death.","understanding":"Ἔσχατος ἐχθρός — the last enemy — καταργεῖται, will be abolished: θάνατος, death. Death is not a friend, not a natural part of life to be embraced, not a door God designed. It is an enemy — the last one standing — and it has an appointment with its own destruction.","words":[{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G2190","hebrew":"ἐχθρός","translit":"echthrós","english":"enemy"},{"strongs":"G2673","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","english":"abolished"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"}]},{"v":27,"text":"For, “He put all things in subjection under his feet.” But when he says, “All things are put in subjection”, it is evident that he is excepted who subjected all things to him.","understanding":"Paul quotes Psalm 8:6: God “put all things in subjection under his feet.” The psalm speaks of humanity's dominion over creation; Paul applies it to Christ as the true Man who fulfils humanity's destiny. Obviously, “all things” does not include God the Father Himself, who did the subjecting.","words":[{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"subjection"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"}]},{"v":28,"text":"When all things have been subjected to him, then the Son will also himself be subjected to him who subjected all things to him, that God may be all in all.","understanding":"When everything has been ὑποτάσσω — subjected — to the Son, then the Son Himself will be subjected to the Father, “that God may be all in all.” This is not a denial of the Son's deity; the Son is fully God. It describes His role as the Messiah who completes His mission and returns the restored kingdom to the Father, in perfect unity. The goal of history is not merely that we are saved. It is that God is all in all.","words":[{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"subjected"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3956","hebrew":"πᾶς","translit":"pâs","english":"all"}]},{"v":29,"text":"Or else what will they do who are baptized for the dead? If the dead aren’t raised at all, why then are they baptized for the dead?","understanding":"One of the most puzzling verses in the Bible. “What will they do who are baptized ὑπὲρ τῶν νεκρῶν — for (or on behalf of) the dead?” More than forty interpretations have been proposed, and honesty requires saying we do not know exactly what the practice was. Notice that Paul says “they,” not “we” or “you”; he neither commands nor endorses it. His point is simply that the practice, whatever it was, would make no sense if the dead are not raised.","words":[{"strongs":"G907","hebrew":"βαπτίζω","translit":"baptízō","english":"baptized"},{"strongs":"G5228","hebrew":"ὑπέρ","translit":"hypér","english":"for"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"}]},{"v":30,"text":"Why do we also stand in jeopardy every hour?","understanding":"Why do we κινδυνεύω — stand in danger — every hour? Paul's whole life of risk only makes sense if the resurrection is true.","words":[{"strongs":"G2793","hebrew":"κινδυνεύω","translit":"kindyneúō","english":"stand in jeopardy"},{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"hour"}]},{"v":31,"text":"I affirm, by the boasting in you which I have in Christ Jesus our Lord, I die daily.","understanding":"“I die daily.” Paul swears by his καύχησις — his boasting, his pride — in them in Christ. He faced the threat of death every day. Notice: this is not mainly about spiritual self-denial, though it includes that; it is about physical danger for the gospel.","words":[{"strongs":"G2746","hebrew":"καύχησις","translit":"kaúchēsis","english":"boasting"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"die"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"daily"}]},{"v":32,"text":"If I fought with animals at Ephesus for human purposes, what does it profit me? If the dead are not raised, then “let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die.”","understanding":"“If I θηριομαχέω — fought with beasts — at Ephesus.” As a Roman citizen, Paul was unlikely to have been literally thrown to animals; most interpreters take this as a vivid metaphor for savage opposition (compare Acts 19; 2 Corinthians 1:8). If there is no resurrection, what ὄφελος — gain — was it? Then he quotes Isaiah 22:13: “let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die.” Without the resurrection, hedonism is logical.","words":[{"strongs":"G2341","hebrew":"θηριομαχέω","translit":"thēriomachéō","english":"fought with animals"},{"strongs":"G2181","hebrew":"Ἔφεσος","translit":"Éphesos","english":"Ephesus"},{"strongs":"G3786","hebrew":"ὄφελος","translit":"óphelos","english":"profit"},{"strongs":"G4095","hebrew":"πίνω","translit":"pínō","english":"drink"},{"strongs":"G839","hebrew":"αὔριον","translit":"aúrion","english":"tomorrow"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"die"}]},{"v":33,"text":"Don’t be deceived! “Evil companionships corrupt good morals.”","understanding":"“Don’t be deceived!” Then a line from the Greek comic poet Menander, which had become a proverb: “Evil ὁμιλίαι — companionships, conversations — corrupt χρηστὰ ἤθη — good morals.” The people denying the resurrection were a bad influence. What you believe about the future shapes how you live now — and so does who you listen to.","words":[{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceived"},{"strongs":"G3657","hebrew":"ὁμιλία","translit":"homilía","english":"companionships"},{"strongs":"G5351","hebrew":"φθείρω","translit":"phtheírō","english":"corrupt"},{"strongs":"G5543","hebrew":"χρηστός","translit":"chrēstós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2239","hebrew":"ἦθος","translit":"ēthos","english":"morals"}]},{"v":34,"text":"Wake up righteously, and don’t sin, for some have no knowledge of God. I say this to your shame.","understanding":"Ἐκνήψατε — sober up, wake from a drunken stupor — δικαίως, as you ought, and stop sinning. Some have ἀγνωσία — ignorance — of God. “I say this to your shame.” A church that was proud of its knowledge is told that some of its members do not know God at all.","words":[{"strongs":"G1594","hebrew":"ἐκνήφω","translit":"eknḗphō","english":"wake up"},{"strongs":"G1346","hebrew":"δικαίως","translit":"dikaíōs","english":"righteously"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G56","hebrew":"ἀγνωσία","translit":"agnōsía","english":"no knowledge"},{"strongs":"G1791","hebrew":"ἐντροπή","translit":"entropḗ","english":"shame"}]},{"v":35,"text":"But someone will say, “How are the dead raised?” and, “With what kind of body do they come?”","understanding":"Now the objection Paul expected from Greek minds: How are the dead raised? With what kind of σῶμα do they come? A Greek would imagine a rotting corpse reassembled. Paul answers the real question: what kind of body?","words":[{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":36,"text":"You foolish one, that which you yourself sow is not made alive unless it dies.","understanding":"Ἄφρων — foolish one, mindless. The answer is in every garden. What you σπείρω — sow — is not ζωοποιέω, made alive, unless it ἀποθνῄσκω, dies. Death is not the obstacle to new life; it is the doorway to it (compare John 12:24).","words":[{"strongs":"G878","hebrew":"ἄφρων","translit":"áphrōn","english":"foolish"},{"strongs":"G4687","hebrew":"σπείρω","translit":"speírō","english":"sow"},{"strongs":"G2227","hebrew":"ζωοποιέω","translit":"zōopoiéō","english":"made alive"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"dies"}]},{"v":37,"text":"That which you sow, you don’t sow the body that will be, but a bare grain, maybe of wheat, or of some other kind.","understanding":"You do not sow the body that will be, but a γυμνὸς κόκκος — a bare, naked kernel — of σῖτος, wheat, or something else. The seed looks nothing like the plant it will become. Continuity and transformation together.","words":[{"strongs":"G4687","hebrew":"σπείρω","translit":"speírō","english":"sow"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G1131","hebrew":"γυμνός","translit":"gymnós","english":"bare"},{"strongs":"G2848","hebrew":"κόκκος","translit":"kókkos","english":"grain"},{"strongs":"G4621","hebrew":"σῖτος","translit":"sîtos","english":"wheat"}]},{"v":38,"text":"But God gives it a body even as it pleased him, and to each seed a body of its own.","understanding":"God gives it a body as He θέλω — chose — and to each σπέρμα its own body. The same God who designs a wheat stalk from a grain can design a resurrection body from a mortal one.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"pleased"},{"strongs":"G4690","hebrew":"σπέρμα","translit":"spérma","english":"seed"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":39,"text":"All flesh is not the same flesh, but there is one flesh of men, another flesh of animals, another of fish, and another of birds.","understanding":"Creation is full of different kinds of σάρξ — flesh: human, κτήνη (animals), ἰχθύες (fish), πτηνά (birds). God is not limited to one kind of body.","words":[{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G2934","hebrew":"κτῆνος","translit":"ktēnos","english":"animals"},{"strongs":"G2486","hebrew":"ἰχθύς","translit":"ichthýs","english":"fish"},{"strongs":"G4421","hebrew":"πτηνόν","translit":"ptēnón","english":"birds"}]},{"v":40,"text":"There are also celestial bodies, and terrestrial bodies; but the glory of the celestial differs from that of the terrestrial.","understanding":"There are ἐπουράνια — heavenly bodies — and ἐπίγεια — earthly bodies — and each has its own δόξα, glory. If God can make bodies fit for the sky and bodies fit for the earth, He can make bodies fit for the age to come.","words":[{"strongs":"G2032","hebrew":"ἐπουράνιος","translit":"epouránios","english":"celestial"},{"strongs":"G1919","hebrew":"ἐπίγειος","translit":"epígeios","english":"terrestrial"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]},{"v":41,"text":"There is one glory of the sun, another glory of the moon, and another glory of the stars; for one star differs from another star in glory.","understanding":"The ἥλιος (sun), the σελήνη (moon) and the ἀστέρες (stars) each have their own glory, and star διαφέρω — differs — from star in glory. God's creativity is limitless.","words":[{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G4582","hebrew":"σελήνη","translit":"selḗnē","english":"moon"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"stars"},{"strongs":"G1308","hebrew":"διαφέρω","translit":"diaphérō","english":"differs"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]},{"v":42,"text":"So also is the resurrection of the dead. The body is sown perishable; it is raised imperishable.","understanding":"Now four contrasts. Sown ἐν φθορᾷ — in decay, perishability — raised ἐν ἀφθαρσίᾳ — in imperishability, incorruption. The word “sown” is chosen deliberately: Paul speaks of burial as planting.","words":[{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"},{"strongs":"G4687","hebrew":"σπείρω","translit":"speírō","english":"sown"},{"strongs":"G5356","hebrew":"φθορά","translit":"phthorá","english":"perishable"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"},{"strongs":"G861","hebrew":"ἀφθαρσία","translit":"aphtharsía","english":"imperishable"}]},{"v":43,"text":"It is sown in dishonor; it is raised in glory. It is sown in weakness; it is raised in power.","understanding":"Sown ἐν ἀτιμίᾳ — in dishonour; few things are more humiliating than a corpse — raised ἐν δόξῃ, in glory. Sown ἐν ἀσθενείᾳ, in weakness; raised ἐν δυνάμει, in power. Every frailty of the body we bury will be reversed in the body we receive.","words":[{"strongs":"G819","hebrew":"ἀτιμία","translit":"atimía","english":"dishonor"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G769","hebrew":"ἀσθένεια","translit":"asthéneia","english":"weakness"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"}]},{"v":44,"text":"It is sown a natural body; it is raised a spiritual body. There is a natural body and there is also a spiritual body.","understanding":"Sown a σῶμα ψυχικόν — a natural body, animated by the ψυχή, ordinary life — raised a σῶμα πνευματικόν, a spiritual body. “Spiritual” does not mean immaterial, made of spirit, a ghost. It means a body wholly governed and animated by the Holy Spirit. Jesus' risen body could be touched and could eat (Luke 24:39–43). It is more physical, not less — fully alive.","words":[{"strongs":"G4687","hebrew":"σπείρω","translit":"speírō","english":"sown"},{"strongs":"G5591","hebrew":"ψυχικός","translit":"psychikós","english":"natural"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual"}]},{"v":45,"text":"So also it is written, “The first man, Adam, became a living soul.” The last Adam became a life-giving spirit.","understanding":"Paul quotes Genesis 2:7: the first man, Adam, became a ψυχὴ ζῶσα — a living soul, a living being. The last Adam — Christ — became a πνεῦμα ζωοποιοῦν — a life-giving spirit. The first Adam received life; the last Adam gives it.","words":[{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G76","hebrew":"Ἀδάμ","translit":"Adám","english":"Adam"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"soul"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G2227","hebrew":"ζωοποιέω","translit":"zōopoiéō","english":"life-giving"}]},{"v":46,"text":"However that which is spiritual isn’t first, but that which is natural, then that which is spiritual.","understanding":"The order matters: the natural comes first, then the spiritual. We do not begin in the resurrection body; we begin in Adam's body and are transformed. Possibly a correction to some in Corinth who thought they had already attained the spiritual state.","words":[{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual"},{"strongs":"G5591","hebrew":"ψυχικός","translit":"psychikós","english":"natural"}]},{"v":47,"text":"The first man is of the earth, made of dust. The second man is the Lord from heaven.","understanding":"The first man was ἐκ γῆς χοϊκός — from the earth, made of dust (Genesis 2:7). The second man is from οὐρανός — heaven. Christ's origin and nature are heavenly.","words":[{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5517","hebrew":"χοϊκός","translit":"choïkós","english":"made of dust"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"}]},{"v":48,"text":"As is the one made of dust, such are those who are also made of dust; and as is the heavenly, such are they also that are heavenly.","understanding":"As the dusty man, so those who are dusty; as the heavenly man, so those who are heavenly. We share the nature of the Adam we belong to.","words":[{"strongs":"G5517","hebrew":"χοϊκός","translit":"choïkós","english":"made of dust"},{"strongs":"G2032","hebrew":"ἐπουράνιος","translit":"epouránios","english":"heavenly"}]},{"v":49,"text":"As we have borne the image of those made of dust, let’s also bear the image of the heavenly.","understanding":"As we have φορέω — worn, borne, as one wears clothing — the εἰκών — image — of the man of dust, so we shall (or, in some manuscripts, “let us”) bear the image of the heavenly man. The image of God, damaged in Adam, will be fully restored in Christ (Romans 8:29).","words":[{"strongs":"G5409","hebrew":"φορέω","translit":"phoréō","english":"borne"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G5517","hebrew":"χοϊκός","translit":"choïkós","english":"made of dust"},{"strongs":"G2032","hebrew":"ἐπουράνιος","translit":"epouránios","english":"heavenly"}]},{"v":50,"text":"Now I say this, brothers, that flesh and blood can’t inherit God’s Kingdom; neither does the perishable inherit imperishable.","understanding":"Σὰρξ καὶ αἷμα — flesh and blood, humanity in its present mortal condition — cannot κληρονομέω, inherit, the kingdom of God. Nor does φθορά, the perishable, inherit ἀφθαρσία, the imperishable. A transformation is necessary. Our bodies as they are now are not fit for the age to come.","words":[{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G2816","hebrew":"κληρονομέω","translit":"klēronoméō","english":"inherit"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G5356","hebrew":"φθορά","translit":"phthorá","english":"perishable"},{"strongs":"G861","hebrew":"ἀφθαρσία","translit":"aphtharsía","english":"imperishable"}]},{"v":51,"text":"Behold, I tell you a mystery. We will not all sleep, but we will all be changed,","understanding":"Ἰδοὺ μυστήριον — behold, a mystery, something God has now revealed. Not all of us will κοιμάω — sleep, die — but all of us will be ἀλλάσσω, changed. Believers alive at Christ's return will not die; they will be transformed.","words":[{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"},{"strongs":"G2837","hebrew":"κοιμάω","translit":"koimáō","english":"sleep"},{"strongs":"G236","hebrew":"ἀλλάσσω","translit":"allássō","english":"changed"}]},{"v":52,"text":"in a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trumpet. For the trumpet will sound, and the dead will be raised incorruptible, and we will be changed.","understanding":"Ἐν ἀτόμῳ — in an atom of time, an instant so small it cannot be cut further; the word gives us atom. Ἐν ῥιπῇ ὀφθαλμοῦ — in the flick of an eye. At the last σάλπιγξ — trumpet. For it σαλπίσει — will sound — and the dead will be raised ἄφθαρτοι, imperishable, and we will be changed. Not a slow evolution but an instantaneous transformation.","words":[{"strongs":"G823","hebrew":"ἄτομος","translit":"átomos","english":"moment"},{"strongs":"G4493","hebrew":"ῥιπή","translit":"rhipḗ","english":"twinkling"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eye"},{"strongs":"G4536","hebrew":"σάλπιγξ","translit":"sálpinx","english":"trumpet"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G862","hebrew":"ἄφθαρτος","translit":"áphthartos","english":"incorruptible"},{"strongs":"G236","hebrew":"ἀλλάσσω","translit":"allássō","english":"changed"}]},{"v":53,"text":"For this perishable body must become imperishable, and this mortal must put on immortality.","understanding":"This φθαρτόν — perishable — must ἐνδύω, put on like a garment, ἀφθαρσία; this θνητόν — mortal — must put on ἀθανασία, immortality. The image is clothing: the resurrection body is put on over us, not a replacement of us.","words":[{"strongs":"G5349","hebrew":"φθαρτός","translit":"phthartós","english":"perishable"},{"strongs":"G1746","hebrew":"ἐνδύω","translit":"endýō","english":"put on"},{"strongs":"G861","hebrew":"ἀφθαρσία","translit":"aphtharsía","english":"imperishable"},{"strongs":"G2349","hebrew":"θνητός","translit":"thnētós","english":"mortal"},{"strongs":"G110","hebrew":"ἀθανασία","translit":"athanasía","english":"immortality"}]},{"v":54,"text":"But when this perishable body will have become imperishable, and this mortal will have put on immortality, then what is written will happen: “Death is swallowed up in victory.”","understanding":"When that happens, the Scripture will be fulfilled: “Death is καταπίνω — swallowed up, gulped down — εἰς νῖκος, in victory” (Isaiah 25:8). The great swallower is swallowed.","words":[{"strongs":"G5349","hebrew":"φθαρτός","translit":"phthartós","english":"perishable"},{"strongs":"G861","hebrew":"ἀφθαρσία","translit":"aphtharsía","english":"imperishable"},{"strongs":"G2349","hebrew":"θνητός","translit":"thnētós","english":"mortal"},{"strongs":"G110","hebrew":"ἀθανασία","translit":"athanasía","english":"immortality"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G2666","hebrew":"καταπίνω","translit":"katapínō","english":"swallowed up"},{"strongs":"G3534","hebrew":"νῖκος","translit":"nîkos","english":"victory"}]},{"v":55,"text":"“Death, where is your sting? Hades, where is your victory?”","understanding":"Paul bursts into a taunt song, adapted from Hosea 13:14. “Death, where is your νῖκος, victory? Hades (or Death) where is your κέντρον, sting?” The κέντρον is the sting of a scorpion or bee, or the goad used to drive cattle. Death is pictured as a beast whose stinger has been ripped out. You can still see its shape. It can no longer kill you.","words":[{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"Death"},{"strongs":"G2759","hebrew":"κέντρον","translit":"kéntron","english":"sting"},{"strongs":"G86","hebrew":"ᾅδης","translit":"háidēs","english":"Hades"},{"strongs":"G3534","hebrew":"νῖκος","translit":"nîkos","english":"victory"}]},{"v":56,"text":"The sting of death is sin, and the power of sin is the law.","understanding":"Why did death have a sting? Because “the sting of death is ἁμαρτία, sin” — death has power over us because of sin — “and the power of sin is the νόμος, law” — the law exposes and condemns sin (Romans 7). Paul compresses the whole argument of Romans 5 to 7 into one sentence.","words":[{"strongs":"G2759","hebrew":"κέντρον","translit":"kéntron","english":"sting"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"}]},{"v":57,"text":"But thanks be to God, who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ.","understanding":"Χάρις τῷ θεῷ — thanks be to God — who gives us — present tense, keeps giving — the νῖκος, the victory, through our Lord Jesus Christ. Christ dealt with sin on the cross, satisfied the law, and broke death's power in the resurrection. The victory is His, and He shares it.","words":[{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"thanks"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gives"},{"strongs":"G3534","hebrew":"νῖκος","translit":"nîkos","english":"victory"}]},{"v":58,"text":"Therefore, my beloved brothers, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the Lord’s work, because you know that your labor is not in vain in the Lord.","understanding":"“Therefore” — the practical conclusion of the greatest theological chapter in the letter. Be ἑδραῖοι — steadfast, seated firmly — and ἀμετακίνητοι — immovable, not shifted off the hope of the gospel. Be always περισσεύω — overflowing — in the ἔργον of the Lord. Because you know that your κόπος — your labour to the point of exhaustion — is not κενός, empty, in the Lord. The resurrection guarantees that nothing done for Christ is ever wasted.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G1476","hebrew":"ἑδραῖος","translit":"hedraîos","english":"steadfast"},{"strongs":"G277","hebrew":"ἀμετακίνητος","translit":"ametakínētos","english":"immovable"},{"strongs":"G4052","hebrew":"περισσεύω","translit":"perisseúō","english":"abounding"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G2873","hebrew":"κόπος","translit":"kópos","english":"labor"},{"strongs":"G2756","hebrew":"κενός","translit":"kenós","english":"vain"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Five Hundred Witnesses on a Hillside","verseRef":"v. 3-8","image":"Picture a hillside in Galilee in the weeks after Passover. More than five hundred people — fishermen, farmers, women who had followed Jesus, the curious and the grieving — have gathered. And He is there. Not a ghost, not a vision in one person's head, not a rumour. He stands among them, the marks of the nails in His hands, speaking. Five hundred people see Him at the same time. Now fast-forward twenty-five years to a house in Ephesus, where Paul is dictating this letter. He writes: “most of whom remain until now.” In other words: they are still alive. Go to Galilee. Knock on their doors. Ask them what they saw.","meaning":"Paul does not present the resurrection as a private spiritual experience or a beautiful idea. He presents it as public history with named witnesses: Cephas, the Twelve, more than five hundred at once, James, all the apostles, and finally Paul himself. Some were friends who had abandoned Jesus. One was a sceptical brother. One was a violent enemy. All were changed by meeting Him alive. And the creed Paul quotes — “Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures, and that he appeared” — was already being handed on within a few years of the event. Your faith rests on an empty tomb and on eyewitnesses who would not stop telling what they saw, even when it cost them their lives."},{"title":"The Seed in the Ground","verseRef":"v. 35-44","image":"Hold a single grain of wheat in your palm. It is small, dry, hard, brown — it looks dead. Now dig a hole and bury it. Cover it with earth. Walk away. For days nothing happens, and if you dug it up you would find it softening, splitting, decaying. And then one morning a green blade breaks the surface. Weeks later it is a tall stalk, golden, heavy with grain, swaying in the wind — so different from the seed that no one who had only seen the seed could ever have imagined it. And yet it is the same life. The seed did not vanish; it was transformed.","meaning":"This is Paul's answer to the question, “How are the dead raised? With what kind of body?” The same way a seed becomes a plant: through death into transformation. “It is sown perishable; it is raised imperishable. It is sown in dishonor; it is raised in glory. It is sown in weakness; it is raised in power. It is sown a natural body; it is raised a spiritual body” (vv. 42–44). Notice the word “sown.” Every Christian funeral is a planting. And Jesus used the same image of His own death: “unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains by itself alone. But if it dies, it bears much fruit” (John 12:24). The body you lay in the grave is the seed. The body God raises will be as different from it as the wheat stalk from the grain — and it will still be you."},{"title":"The Scorpion Without Its Sting","verseRef":"v. 54-57","image":"Picture a scorpion — black, armoured, its tail curled high over its back, the stinger at the tip glistening with venom. Everyone in the ancient world feared it. Its sting could kill. Now picture a hand reaching down, seizing the tail, and tearing the stinger out. The scorpion still moves. It still raises its tail. It still looks frightening. But there is nothing at the end of the tail anymore. It cannot kill. And Paul stands over it and laughs: “Death, where is your sting? Hades, where is your victory?”","meaning":"Death still comes to believers. We still grieve, still bury those we love, still face our own mortality. The scorpion still moves. But “the sting of death is sin, and the power of sin is the law” (v. 56). Death's power to destroy us came from our sin and the law's condemnation of it. And Christ took both: He bore our sin on the cross and satisfied the law's demands. Then He rose, and the stinger was gone. “But thanks be to God, who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ” (v. 57). Hebrews says Christ died “that through death he might bring to nothing him who had the power of death, that is, the devil, and might deliver all of them who through fear of death were all their lifetime subject to bondage” (Hebrews 2:14–15). You do not have to live in fear of a scorpion without a sting."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Gospel of First Importance (v. 1-11)","body":"Paul reminds them of the gospel they received and stand in: Christ died for our sins, was buried, was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures, and appeared to Cephas, the Twelve, more than five hundred, James, all the apostles and finally to Paul — who is what he is by grace."},{"label":"If Christ Has Not Been Raised (v. 12-19)","body":"If there is no resurrection of the dead, then Christ is not raised; preaching and faith are empty, the apostles are false witnesses, believers are still in their sins, the dead in Christ have perished, and Christians are the most pitiable people on earth."},{"label":"But Now Christ Has Been Raised (v. 20-28)","body":"Christ is the first fruits; as in Adam all die, in Christ all will be made alive, each in order; Christ reigns until every enemy is defeated, the last being death; then He hands the kingdom to the Father, that God may be all in all."},{"label":"Living in Light of the Resurrection (v. 29-34)","body":"Without the resurrection, practices like baptism for the dead and Paul's daily danger make no sense, and hedonism would be logical; bad company corrupts good character — wake up and stop sinning."},{"label":"The Resurrection Body (v. 35-49)","body":"Like a seed that dies and is given a new body, the dead are sown perishable, dishonoured, weak and natural, and raised imperishable, glorious, powerful and spiritual; as we bore the image of the earthly Adam, we will bear the image of the heavenly."},{"label":"The Mystery and the Victory (v. 50-58)","body":"Flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom; at the last trumpet the dead will be raised and the living changed in an instant; death will be swallowed up in victory, its sting removed through Christ — therefore be steadfast, immovable, abounding in the Lord's work."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Creed, the Witnesses and the Grace That Made an Apostle","paragraphs":["Chapter 15 is the longest sustained discussion of the resurrection in the Bible, and Paul saves it for the end of the letter because everything else depends on it. Some in Corinth were saying “there is no resurrection of the dead” (v. 12). They probably did not deny that something spiritual survived death; Greek philosophy widely held that the soul was immortal and the body a prison to escape. What they found absurd was the idea of a bodily resurrection — exactly the reaction of the Athenians when Paul preached the resurrection there (Acts 17:32). Paul's answer begins not with philosophy but with history and tradition: the gospel “which I preached to you, which also you received, in which you also stand, by which also you are saved” (vv. 1–2).","Verses 3 to 5 contain what most scholars, across a wide range of theological positions, recognise as an early Christian creed that Paul received and passed on. Its structure is formal and rhythmic: Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures; He was buried; He was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures; He appeared. Paul likely received it in Damascus or Jerusalem within a few years of his conversion — he visited Peter and James in Jerusalem three years after it (Galatians 1:18–19). That means the core Christian claims about Jesus' death and resurrection were being formulated and taught within a very short time of the events themselves. Notice also the phrase “according to the Scriptures.” The resurrection is not presented as an isolated miracle but as the fulfilment of God's long purpose revealed in Israel's Scriptures, such as Isaiah 53 and Psalm 16.","Then Paul lists the witnesses (vv. 5–8): Cephas, the Twelve, more than five hundred at one time, James, all the apostles, and finally Paul himself. The list functions like courtroom testimony. And Paul adds that most of the five hundred are still alive — an open invitation to check. The women who first discovered the empty tomb and saw the risen Lord, according to all four Gospels, are not mentioned, very possibly because the creed follows the form of public, legal testimony, in which a woman's witness was given little weight in that culture. And then Paul writes about himself with startling humility: “as to the child born at the wrong time… the least of the apostles, who is not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the assembly of God. But by the grace of God I am what I am” (vv. 8–10). The resurrection did not just prove a doctrine to Paul. It turned a persecutor into an apostle. Grace made him, grace drove him to work harder than anyone, and grace took the credit: “yet not I, but the grace of God which was with me.”"]},{"heading":"Everything Hangs on This: The Logic of an Empty Tomb","paragraphs":["Verses 12 to 19 contain one of the most remarkable arguments in Scripture, because Paul deliberately stakes the entire Christian faith on a single historical claim. If there is no resurrection of the dead, then Christ has not been raised (v. 13). And if Christ has not been raised, five devastating consequences follow. Our preaching is empty and your faith is empty (v. 14). The apostles are false witnesses who have lied about God (v. 15). Your faith is futile, and “you are still in your sins” (v. 17) — because the resurrection is God's public declaration that the sacrifice for sin was accepted; as Paul writes in Romans 4:25, Jesus “was raised for our justification.” Those who have died in Christ have simply perished (v. 18). And “If we have only hoped in Christ in this life, we are of all men most pitiable” (v. 19).","Many people today want a Christianity without the resurrection — a moral teacher, an inspiring example, a set of spiritual values. Paul says that such a Christianity is not a weaker version of the faith; it is no faith at all. And he is willing to say that if the resurrection did not happen, Christians are to be pitied above everyone else, because they have given up so much for a hope that is false. This is not the language of someone offering a comforting myth. It is the language of a man making a falsifiable historical claim and staking his life on it. That is why verse 32 says that without the resurrection, the logical response would be, “let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die.” If this life is all there is, hedonism makes sense.","“But now Christ has been raised from the dead” (v. 20). Νυνὶ δέ — the hinge on which history turns. And Paul immediately calls Christ “the first fruits of those who are asleep.” At the Feast of Firstfruits in Israel, the first sheaf of the barley harvest was waved before the LORD as an offering and a pledge that the rest of the harvest would follow (Leviticus 23:10–11). Jesus rose on the first day of the week in the Passover season — the very time when, on the most widely held reckoning, that first sheaf was offered. His resurrection is the first sheaf of a harvest that includes every believer. Then Paul sets out the two Adams: “For as in Adam all die, so also in Christ all will be made alive” (v. 22). The human race is not a collection of isolated individuals. It is gathered under two heads. In Adam, death; in Christ, life. Romans 5:12–21 develops the same comparison at length."]},{"heading":"The Seed, the Trumpet and the Victory","paragraphs":["Verses 23 to 28 lay out the order of events. Christ rose first; then, at His coming, those who belong to Him will be raised; then comes the end, when He hands over the kingdom to the Father after destroying every hostile rule, authority and power (vv. 23–24). “For he must reign until he has put all his enemies under his feet. The last enemy that will be abolished is death” (vv. 25–26). Death is not natural or friendly in Paul's theology. It is an enemy — the last one. And then a sentence that has puzzled readers: the Son Himself will be subjected to the Father, “that God may be all in all” (v. 28). This does not deny the Son's full deity; Paul has just included Him in the Shema (8:6). It describes the Messiah's completed mission: having conquered every enemy, He presents the restored creation to the Father in perfect, willing unity. The goal of history is God's glory filling everything.","Verses 29 to 34 bring the argument down to daily life. Verse 29 mentions people being “baptized for the dead,” and it must be said honestly that no one knows for certain what this practice was; more than forty explanations have been offered. Paul does not endorse it — he says “they,” not “we” — and the practice of proxy baptism developed by later groups finds no support in the rest of Scripture. Paul's only point is that the practice assumes the dead will rise. Then he points to his own life: he faces danger every hour and “dies daily” (vv. 30–31). His reference to fighting “with animals at Ephesus” (v. 32) is probably a vivid metaphor for savage opposition, since a Roman citizen would rarely be condemned to the arena. And he warns that the people denying the resurrection are corrupting the church, quoting a line from the poet Menander that had become a proverb: “Evil companionships corrupt good morals” (v. 33).","From verse 35 Paul answers the question that lay behind the denial: “How are the dead raised? With what kind of body do they come?” His answer is the seed. What you sow does not come to life unless it dies, and what comes up is not the bare seed but a new body that God gives (vv. 36–38). God has made many kinds of bodies — human, animal, fish, bird, sun, moon, stars — each with its own glory (vv. 39–41). The resurrection body will be continuous with the body that is buried, yet gloriously transformed: imperishable, glorious, powerful, spiritual (vv. 42–44). “Spiritual” does not mean non-physical; Jesus' risen body could be touched and could eat (Luke 24:39–43). It means a body fully alive with the Spirit's life. And then the climax: “Behold, I tell you a mystery. We will not all sleep, but we will all be changed, in a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trumpet” (vv. 51–52). Death will be swallowed up in victory (Isaiah 25:8). Its sting, which was sin empowered by the law, has been removed through Christ (vv. 55–57). And therefore — this is where the whole great chapter lands — “be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the Lord’s work, because you know that your labor is not in vain in the Lord” (v. 58)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["The appearances Paul lists are confirmed throughout the New Testament. The disciples in Jerusalem greeted the two from Emmaus with the news, “The Lord is risen indeed, and has appeared to Simon!” (Luke 24:34) — the same appearance to Cephas that heads Paul's list. Luke says Jesus “showed himself alive after he suffered, by many proofs, appearing to them over a period of forty days” (Acts 1:3). And Jesus Himself had declared, before raising Lazarus, “I am the resurrection and the life. He who believes in me will still live, even if he dies. Whoever lives and believes in me will never die. Do you believe this?” (John 11:25–26). The risen Christ appeared to John on Patmos with the same claim in its final form: “I was dead, and behold, I am alive forever and ever. Amen. I have the keys of Death and of Hades” (Revelation 1:18).","Paul's teaching on the resurrection body is developed in his later letters. To the Romans: “if the Spirit of him who raised up Jesus from the dead dwells in you, he who raised up Christ Jesus from the dead will also give life to your mortal bodies through his Spirit who dwells in you” (Romans 8:11), and believers groan “waiting for adoption, the redemption of our body” (Romans 8:23). To the Philippians: we wait for “the Lord Jesus Christ; who will change the body of our humiliation to be conformed to the body of his glory” (Philippians 3:20–21). And to the Thessalonians, in a passage closely parallel to verses 51 and 52: “the Lord himself will descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with God’s trumpet. The dead in Christ will rise first, then we who are alive, who are left, will be caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air. So we will be with the Lord forever” (1 Thessalonians 4:16–17).","And the defeat of death, the “last enemy” of verse 26, is celebrated across the New Testament. Christ “abolished death, and brought life and immortality to light through the Good News” (2 Timothy 1:10). “Christ, being raised from the dead, dies no more. Death no more has dominion over him!” (Romans 6:9). He shared our flesh and blood “that through death he might bring to nothing him who had the power of death, that is, the devil, and might deliver all of them who through fear of death were all their lifetime subject to bondage” (Hebrews 2:14–15). And the final vision of the Bible shows the last enemy gone: “He will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain, any more. The first things have passed away” (Revelation 21:4). That is the day 1 Corinthians 15:54 looks forward to: “Death is swallowed up in victory.”"]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Resurrection / standing up again","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","strongs":"G386","verses":"v. 12, 13, 21, 42","insight":"Strong's: “a standing up again, i.e. (literally) a resurrection from death (individual, genitive case or by implication, (its author)), or (figuratively) a (moral) recovery (of spiritual truth).” The word combines ἀνά, up or again, and ἵστημι, to stand. Resurrection is not merely survival of the soul or a spiritual idea; it is a dead person standing up again in a body. That is precisely what Greek philosophy found unbelievable and what the Corinthians were tempted to deny. Paul uses the word four times in this chapter and builds the whole Christian faith on it. Jesus applied it to Himself: “I am the resurrection and the life” (John 11:25). The One who stood up from the grave is Himself the standing-up of all who belong to Him."},{"english":"First fruits","hebrew":"ἀπαρχή","translit":"aparchḗ","strongs":"G536","verses":"v. 20, 23 (and 16:15)","insight":"Strong's: “a beginning of sacrifice, i.e. the (Jewish) first-fruit (figuratively).” In Israel, the first portion of the harvest was offered to God as an acknowledgement that the whole harvest was His and a pledge that the rest would follow (Leviticus 23:10–11; Exodus 23:19). The first fruits were of the same kind as the full harvest. So when Paul calls Christ “the first fruits of those who are asleep,” he is saying three things: His resurrection belongs to God; it is the beginning of a much larger harvest; and believers will be raised with the same kind of resurrection life He has. Romans 8:23 uses the same word for the Spirit as the first fruits of our future redemption. The harvest is guaranteed."},{"english":"Death","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","strongs":"G2288","verses":"v. 21, 26, 54, 55 (×2), 56","insight":"Strong's: “(properly, an adjective used as a noun) death (literally or figuratively).” Paul treats θάνατος in this chapter not as a natural process but as an enemy — “the last enemy that will be abolished” (v. 26) — that entered through Adam's sin (v. 21; Romans 5:12) and draws its power from sin and the law (v. 56). This is a strong biblical correction to the common modern idea that death is simply a natural part of life to be accepted. It is real, it is terrible, and believers grieve it. But it is defeated. Paul personifies it in verse 55 and taunts it like a conquered enemy: “Death, where is your sting?” The believer faces death not as a friend but as a defeated foe."},{"english":"Changed / transformed","hebrew":"ἀλλάσσω","translit":"allássō","strongs":"G236","verses":"v. 51, 52","insight":"Strong's: “to make different.” The word describes a real transformation, not a replacement. The same verb is used in Hebrews 1:12 of the heavens and earth being changed like a garment. Paul's mystery is that believers alive when Christ returns will not die but will be changed — in an instant, “in the twinkling of an eye” — from mortal to immortal, perishable to imperishable. And the dead will be raised in the same transformed condition. The emphasis is both continuity and difference: you will still be you, but changed. Philippians 3:21 describes the same thing: Christ “will change the body of our humiliation to be conformed to the body of his glory.”"},{"english":"Sting / goad","hebrew":"κέντρον","translit":"kéntron","strongs":"G2759","verses":"v. 55, 56","insight":"Strong's: “a point (‘centre’), i.e. a sting (figuratively, poison) or goad (figuratively, divine impulse).” The word was used of a scorpion's or bee's sting (Revelation 9:10), and of the sharp goad used to drive oxen — as in Jesus' words to Saul, “It is hard for you to kick against the goads” (Acts 26:14). English “centre” comes from it, through the idea of a point. Paul pictures death as a creature with a deadly sting. And he identifies the venom: “The sting of death is sin.” Death's power to destroy comes from sin, which the law exposes and condemns. Remove the sin, and death loses its venom. That is exactly what Christ did at the cross. For the believer, death remains a creature with no sting."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: whether your faith actually rests on the resurrection, the fear of death that still holds you, and the labour you have quietly concluded is wasted.","items":[{"title":"Is Your Faith Standing on an Empty Tomb?","body":"Paul says that if Christ is not raised, your faith is empty, you are still in your sins, and you are to be pitied. That means your Christianity is either built on the most important historical fact in the world or on nothing. The Spirit wants to ask: is your faith resting on the resurrection — or on feelings, tradition, community, or moral values that would survive just as well if Jesus had stayed in the grave? Read the list of witnesses in verses 5 to 8 slowly. Let the reality of the empty tomb settle into your bones. Your sins are forgiven because He is alive. Your future is secure because He is the first fruits. Everything you believe hangs on this. And it holds."},{"title":"The Fear You Have Not Named","body":"Hebrews says people are held in slavery all their lives by the fear of death. You may not think about it often, but it shapes more than you realise: your anxiety about health, your clinging to comfort, your terror of loss, your grief that feels hopeless, your need to control everything. The Spirit wants to name that fear and set you free from it. Death is an enemy — but it is a defeated enemy. Its sting is gone, because Christ took your sin. You will be changed, in the twinkling of an eye. The grave is a planting, not an ending. Today, speak verse 55 aloud over your fear: “Death, where is your sting?” And let the Spirit teach you to live without that chain."},{"title":"Your Labour Is Not in Vain","body":"There is something you have been doing for God that seems to bear no fruit. A prayer you have prayed for years. A child you have raised in faith who has walked away. A ministry that has not grown. Service no one notices. Faithfulness that seems to change nothing. You have quietly begun to wonder whether it is worth it. The Spirit wants you to hear the last word of this great chapter as a personal promise: “your labor is not in vain in the Lord.” Because Christ is risen, nothing done for Him is lost. Every act of faithfulness is a seed in the ground, and the harvest is guaranteed. Do not stop. Be steadfast. Be immovable. Keep abounding."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Foundation Check","body":"Write down, in your own words, why you believe Jesus rose from the dead. If your answer is mainly “because I was taught it” or “because it feels true,” spend time this week with verses 3 to 8 and the Gospel accounts (Matthew 28, Mark 16, Luke 24, John 20–21). Your faith deserves to know its foundation."},{"title":"The Fear-of-Death Check","body":"Ask honestly: how much does fear of death — mine or that of people I love — shape my choices, my anxieties and my priorities? Write down one area where that fear is controlling you. Then write verse 57 beside it: “But thanks be to God, who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ.”"},{"title":"The In-Vain Check","body":"Is there any labour for the Lord that you have stopped, or are tempted to stop, because it seems fruitless? Name it. Then ask: did I stop because God told me to, or because I lost hope? Verse 58 says your labour in the Lord is not in vain. What would it look like to pick it up again?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Rehearsing the Creed","body":"Memorise verses 3 and 4 this week: “Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures.” Say it aloud every morning. Then, at least once this week, share it with someone — a believer who needs encouragement or an unbeliever who is curious. Tell them it is one of the oldest Christian confessions in existence, handed down within a few years of the events. Then tell them what the resurrection means to you personally."},{"title":"Planting Seeds in Hope","body":"Take the labour you named in the In-Vain Check. This week, do one concrete act of faithfulness in that area — pray the long prayer again, write to the child, serve in the ministry, give the gift, show up. Before you do, read verse 58 aloud. Afterward, write down the date and what you did, and title the page “Seeds Sown.” Keep adding to the page whenever you act in faith without seeing results. One day you will look back at that list in the light of the resurrection and see that none of it was wasted."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You died for my sins according to the Scriptures. You were buried. And You were raised on the third day according to the Scriptures. You appeared to Peter, to the Twelve, to more than five hundred at once, to James, to all the apostles, and to Paul — and You have made Yourself known to me. Thank You that my faith is not empty, that my sins are not still on me, that those who have died in You have not perished. You are risen indeed. Thank You that You are the first fruits, and that the harvest is guaranteed. As in Adam I die, so in You I will be made alive. Lord, I confess the fear of death that still grips me. I confess the grief that forgets hope and the anxiety that clings to this life as if it were all there is. Today I declare: death is a defeated enemy. Its sting is sin, and You took my sin. Death, where is your sting? Hades, where is your victory? Thanks be to God, who gives me the victory through my Lord Jesus Christ. And Father, I believe that one day, in a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trumpet, I will be changed. This perishable will put on the imperishable. This mortal will put on immortality. Until then, make me steadfast, immovable, always abounding in Your work — because I know that my labour is not in vain in the Lord. In the mighty name of the risen Jesus, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Valley of Dry Bones","ref":"Ezekiel 37:1-14","relatedVerses":"v. 20-22, 35-44, 52","summary":"The Spirit of the LORD carried Ezekiel to a valley full of bones — very many, and very dry. God asked him whether these bones could live, and Ezekiel answered that only God knew. Then God told him to prophesy to the bones. As he spoke, there was a noise and a rattling, and the bones came together, bone to bone. Sinews and flesh came on them, and skin covered them, but there was no breath in them. Then God told him to prophesy to the breath, and breath came into them, and they lived and stood up, a vast army. God said that He would open their graves and bring His people up from them, and put His Spirit in them. Ezekiel's vision was first about the restoration of Israel from exile, but it uses the language of resurrection, and it shows the God of 1 Corinthians 15: the God who gives bodies to bones and breath to the dead."},{"title":"Lazarus, Come Out","ref":"John 11:1-44","relatedVerses":"v. 26, 54-57","summary":"When Jesus arrived in Bethany, His friend Lazarus had been dead four days. Martha met Him and said that if He had been there, her brother would not have died. Jesus told her that her brother would rise again, and she replied that she knew he would rise in the resurrection on the last day. Then Jesus said, “I am the resurrection and the life.” At the tomb, Jesus wept. Then He told them to roll away the stone, prayed to His Father, and cried with a loud voice, “Lazarus, come out!” And the dead man came out, bound in grave clothes. Lazarus would die again one day. But his raising was a sign of the greater resurrection Jesus Himself would accomplish a few weeks later, and of the resurrection He will bring to all who believe in Him. The last enemy was put on notice in Bethany."},{"title":"Abraham on Mount Moriah","ref":"Genesis 22:1-19; Hebrews 11:17-19","relatedVerses":"v. 3-4, 19-20","summary":"God told Abraham to take Isaac, his only son, whom he loved, and offer him as a burnt offering on a mountain in the land of Moriah. Abraham rose early, travelled three days, and climbed the mountain with Isaac carrying the wood. When Isaac asked where the lamb was, Abraham said that God would provide it. At the last moment, the angel of the LORD stopped his hand, and Abraham saw a ram caught in a thicket and offered it instead. Hebrews says that Abraham reasoned that God was able to raise Isaac even from the dead — and that, figuratively speaking, he received him back from the dead. On the third day of the journey, on that same mountain range, Abraham received his son back. Centuries later, on a hill near Jerusalem, God did not spare His own Son — and on the third day, raised Him from the dead. Abraham's faith in resurrection was answered in Christ."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":16,"chapterTitle":"Let All That You Do Be Done in Love — Giving, Open Doors, Faithful Workers and Maranatha","themeTitle":"“After Fifteen Chapters of Correction, the Last Word Is Love”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Now concerning the collection for the saints, as I commanded the assemblies of Galatia, you do likewise.","understanding":"“Now concerning” — the fifth topic from their letter: the λογεία, the collection, for the saints — the poor believers in Jerusalem (Romans 15:26). Paul had διατάσσω — given directions — to the churches of Galatia, and now gives the same to Corinth. Notice how the letter moves straight from the resurrection to fundraising. Because Christ is risen and your labour is not in vain (15:58), your money matters too.","words":[{"strongs":"G3048","hebrew":"λογία","translit":"logía","english":"collection"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G1299","hebrew":"διατάσσω","translit":"diatássō","english":"commanded"},{"strongs":"G1053","hebrew":"Γαλατία","translit":"Galatía","english":"Galatia"}]},{"v":2,"text":"On the first day of the week, let each one of you save, as he may prosper, that no collections be made when I come.","understanding":"“On the first day of the week” — κατὰ μίαν σαββάτου, every first day after the Sabbath. This is one of the earliest pieces of evidence that Christians gathered on Sunday, the day of the resurrection (compare Acts 20:7). Each one is to τίθημι — set aside — and θησαυρίζω — store up, treasure — as he εὐοδόω, prospers, literally “is helped along the road.” Giving was to be regular, personal, proportional and planned. Not a last-minute emotional appeal when the apostle arrives.","words":[{"strongs":"G3391","hebrew":"μία","translit":"mía","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G4521","hebrew":"σάββατον","translit":"sábbaton","english":"week"},{"strongs":"G2343","hebrew":"θησαυρίζω","translit":"thēsaurízō","english":"save"},{"strongs":"G2137","hebrew":"εὐοδόω","translit":"euodóō","english":"prosper"},{"strongs":"G3048","hebrew":"λογία","translit":"logía","english":"collections"}]},{"v":3,"text":"When I arrive, I will send whoever you approve with letters to carry your gracious gift to Jerusalem.","understanding":"Paul will not handle the money himself. The Corinthians will δοκιμάζω — test, approve — trustworthy people of their own choosing, and Paul will send them with ἐπιστολαί, letters of introduction, to ἀποφέρω — carry — their χάρις to Jerusalem. Notice the word: the gift is called “grace.” Financial integrity and transparency are part of gospel ministry (compare 2 Corinthians 8:20–21).","words":[{"strongs":"G1381","hebrew":"δοκιμάζω","translit":"dokimázō","english":"approve"},{"strongs":"G1992","hebrew":"ἐπιστολή","translit":"epistolḗ","english":"letters"},{"strongs":"G667","hebrew":"ἀποφέρω","translit":"apophérō","english":"carry"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"gracious gift"},{"strongs":"G2419","hebrew":"Ἱερουσαλήμ","translit":"Hierousalḗm","english":"Jerusalem"}]},{"v":4,"text":"If it is appropriate for me to go also, they will go with me.","understanding":"If it seems ἄξιος — fitting — for Paul to go too, the delegates will travel with him. In the end he did go, and Acts 24:17 records him telling the governor Felix that he had come to Jerusalem “to bring gifts for the needy to my nation.”","words":[{"strongs":"G515","hebrew":"ἀξιόω","translit":"axióō","english":"appropriate"},{"strongs":"G4198","hebrew":"πορεύομαι","translit":"poreúomai","english":"go"}]},{"v":5,"text":"But I will come to you when I have passed through Macedonia, for I am passing through Macedonia.","understanding":"Paul's travel plans. He will come to Corinth after διέρχομαι — passing through — Μακεδονία. These are the plans that later changed, prompting the Corinthians' charge of fickleness that Paul answers in 2 Corinthians 1:15–24.","words":[{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G3109","hebrew":"Μακεδονία","translit":"Makedonía","english":"Macedonia"},{"strongs":"G1330","hebrew":"διέρχομαι","translit":"diérchomai","english":"passed through"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But with you it may be that I will stay, or even winter, that you may send me on my journey wherever I go.","understanding":"He may παραμένω — stay on — with them, perhaps even through the winter, when sea travel effectively stopped in the Mediterranean. And then they can προπέμπω — send him on his way, equip him for the next journey. Sending missionaries well was a recognised Christian duty (3 John 6).","words":[{"strongs":"G3887","hebrew":"παραμένω","translit":"paraménō","english":"stay"},{"strongs":"G4311","hebrew":"προπέμπω","translit":"propémpō","english":"send me on my journey"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For I do not wish to see you now in passing, but I hope to stay a while with you, if the Lord permits.","understanding":"He does not want to see them only ἐν παρόδῳ — in passing. He hopes to stay a while “if the Lord permits.” Every plan in this chapter is held under the Lord's sovereignty (compare James 4:13–15).","words":[{"strongs":"G1679","hebrew":"ἐλπίζω","translit":"elpízō","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G1961","hebrew":"ἐπιμένω","translit":"epiménō","english":"stay"},{"strongs":"G2010","hebrew":"ἐπιτρέπω","translit":"epitrépō","english":"permits"}]},{"v":8,"text":"But I will stay at Ephesus until Pentecost,","understanding":"Paul is writing from Ephesus, and will stay there until πεντηκοστή — Pentecost, the fiftieth day after Passover. This, together with the Passover imagery of 5:7–8, suggests he may have been writing in the spring.","words":[{"strongs":"G1961","hebrew":"ἐπιμένω","translit":"epiménō","english":"stay"},{"strongs":"G2181","hebrew":"Ἔφεσος","translit":"Éphesos","english":"Ephesus"},{"strongs":"G4005","hebrew":"πεντηκοστή","translit":"pentēkostḗ","english":"Pentecost"}]},{"v":9,"text":"for a great and effective door has opened to me, and there are many adversaries.","understanding":"Why stay? “A great and effective θύρα has ἀνοίγω — has been opened — to me.” A door is Paul's regular image for an opportunity for the gospel (2 Corinthians 2:12; Colossians 4:3). Μεγάλη καὶ ἐνεργής — great and effective, active, operative. “And there are many ἀντικείμενοι — adversaries, people lying in opposition.” Notice the “and”: the open door and the opposition came together. Opposition is not proof the door is closed. It is often proof that it is open.","words":[{"strongs":"G2374","hebrew":"θύρα","translit":"thýra","english":"door"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G1756","hebrew":"ἐνεργής","translit":"energḗs","english":"effective"},{"strongs":"G480","hebrew":"ἀντίκειμαι","translit":"antíkeimai","english":"adversaries"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Now if Timothy comes, see that he is with you without fear, for he does the work of the Lord, as I also do.","understanding":"Timothy may arrive. “See that he is with you ἀφόβως — without fear.” Timothy was young and apparently timid by temperament (compare 1 Timothy 4:12; 2 Timothy 1:7), and Corinth was an intimidating church. Paul protects him: he does the ἔργον of the Lord, “as I also do.” A young worker doing the Lord's work deserves the same respect as the apostle.","words":[{"strongs":"G870","hebrew":"ἀφόβως","translit":"aphóbōs","english":"without fear"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G2038","hebrew":"ἐργάζομαι","translit":"ergázomai","english":"do"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Therefore let no one despise him. But set him forward on his journey in peace, that he may come to me; for I expect him with the brothers.","understanding":"Μή τις αὐτὸν ἐξουθενήσῃ — let no one despise him, treat him as nothing; the word of 1:28. Instead προπέμπω — send him on — ἐν εἰρήνῃ, in peace, so that he can return to Paul. Paul knew how Corinth could treat a speaker it did not find impressive.","words":[{"strongs":"G1847","hebrew":"ἐξουδενόω","translit":"exoudenóō","english":"despise"},{"strongs":"G4311","hebrew":"προπέμπω","translit":"propémpō","english":"set forward"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Now concerning Apollos, the brother, I strongly urged him to come to you with the brothers; and it was not at all his desire to come now; but he will come when he has an opportunity.","understanding":"“Now concerning Apollos” — the sixth and last “now concerning.” The Corinthians had apparently asked for Apollos to come. Paul πολλὰ παρεκάλεσα — urged him strongly — to go. Apollos was not willing at that time, but will come when he has εὐκαιρία, an opportunity. This tiny verse destroys the idea that Paul and Apollos were rivals. Paul wanted the man some Corinthians preferred to him to go to them. And Apollos, perhaps wisely, did not want to feed the faction that bore his name.","words":[{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"urged"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G2119","hebrew":"εὐκαιρέω","translit":"eukairéō","english":"has an opportunity"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Watch! Stand firm in the faith! Be courageous! Be strong!","understanding":"Four military commands in rapid succession, like a commander's final orders before battle. Γρηγορεῖτε — stay awake, be on watch; the word of Jesus in Gethsemane (Matthew 26:41). Στήκετε ἐν τῇ πίστει — stand firm in the faith; hold your position. Ἀνδρίζεσθε — act like men, be courageous; the word appears in the Greek Old Testament's “be strong and courageous” to Joshua (Joshua 1). Κραταιοῦσθε — be strong, be strengthened. The Corinthians, who were proud and divided, needed courage and stability, not more spectacle.","words":[{"strongs":"G1127","hebrew":"γρηγορεύω","translit":"grēgoreúō","english":"Watch"},{"strongs":"G4739","hebrew":"στήκω","translit":"stḗkō","english":"Stand firm"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G407","hebrew":"ἀνδρίζομαι","translit":"andrízomai","english":"Be courageous"},{"strongs":"G2901","hebrew":"κραταιόω","translit":"krataióō","english":"Be strong"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Let all that you do be done in love.","understanding":"And immediately the balancing command: πάντα ὑμῶν ἐν ἀγάπῃ γινέσθω — let everything you do happen in love. Courage without love becomes harshness; strength without love becomes domination. This one verse is the summary of the whole letter: factions, lawsuits, marriage, food, worship, gifts — all of it must be done in love. It is 1 Corinthians 13 compressed into eight words.","words":[{"strongs":"G3956","hebrew":"πᾶς","translit":"pâs","english":"all"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G1096","hebrew":"γίνομαι","translit":"gínomai","english":"be done"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Now I beg you, brothers (you know the house of Stephanas, that it is the first fruits of Achaia, and that they have set themselves to serve the saints),","understanding":"The household of Stephanas — the first converts in Achaia, ἀπαρχή, “first fruits,” the word of 15:20 — had ἑαυτοὺς ἔταξαν — appointed themselves, devoted themselves — to the διακονία, the service, of the saints. Nobody appointed them to a position; they took up the towel. Paul baptized this household himself (1:16).","words":[{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"beg"},{"strongs":"G3614","hebrew":"οἰκία","translit":"oikía","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G536","hebrew":"ἀπαρχή","translit":"aparchḗ","english":"first fruits"},{"strongs":"G882","hebrew":"Ἀχαΐα","translit":"Achaḯa","english":"Achaia"},{"strongs":"G5021","hebrew":"τάσσω","translit":"tássō","english":"set themselves"},{"strongs":"G1248","hebrew":"διακονία","translit":"diakonía","english":"serve"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"}]},{"v":16,"text":"that you also be in subjection to such, and to everyone who helps in the work and labors.","understanding":"Paul asks the church to ὑποτάσσω — be subject — to such people, and to everyone who συνεργέω — works together — and κοπιάω — labours to exhaustion. Leadership in the church is recognised by service, not claimed by status. Those who serve sacrificially are the ones to follow.","words":[{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"be in subjection"},{"strongs":"G4903","hebrew":"συνεργέω","translit":"synergéō","english":"helps in the work"},{"strongs":"G2872","hebrew":"κοπιάω","translit":"kopiáō","english":"labors"}]},{"v":17,"text":"I rejoice at the coming of Stephanas, Fortunatus, and Achaicus; for that which was lacking on your part, they supplied.","understanding":"Stephanas, Fortunatus and Achaicus had come from Corinth to Paul in Ephesus — very possibly carrying the Corinthians' letter of 7:1. Their παρουσία — presence, coming — made Paul glad, for they ἀναπληρόω — filled up — what was ὑστέρημα, lacking. Fortunatus and Achaicus may have been freedmen or slaves; their names are common slave names. Status did not matter. Their presence did.","words":[{"strongs":"G5463","hebrew":"χαίρω","translit":"chaírō","english":"rejoice"},{"strongs":"G3952","hebrew":"παρουσία","translit":"parousía","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G5303","hebrew":"ὑστέρημα","translit":"hystérēma","english":"lacking"},{"strongs":"G378","hebrew":"ἀναπληρόω","translit":"anaplēróō","english":"supplied"}]},{"v":18,"text":"For they refreshed my spirit and yours. Therefore acknowledge those who are like that.","understanding":"They ἀναπαύω — refreshed, gave rest to — Paul's spirit, and the Corinthians' too. The same word Jesus uses in Matthew 11:28: “I will give you rest.” Some believers are like a cool drink to everyone they visit. “Therefore ἐπιγινώσκω — recognise, acknowledge, honour — such people.”","words":[{"strongs":"G373","hebrew":"ἀναπαύω","translit":"anapaúō","english":"refreshed"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G1921","hebrew":"ἐπιγινώσκω","translit":"epiginṓskō","english":"acknowledge"}]},{"v":19,"text":"The assemblies of Asia greet you. Aquila and Priscilla greet you much in the Lord, together with the assembly that is in their house.","understanding":"The churches of Asia — the Roman province around Ephesus — send ἀσπάζομαι, greetings. And Aquila and Priscilla, Paul's old tentmaking partners from Corinth itself (Acts 18:2–3), greet them warmly, “together with the ἐκκλησία that is in their house.” For the first three centuries, Christians met mainly in homes. Wherever this couple moved — Rome, Corinth, Ephesus, Rome again — a church met in their house.","words":[{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"},{"strongs":"G773","hebrew":"Ἀσία","translit":"Asía","english":"Asia"},{"strongs":"G782","hebrew":"ἀσπάζομαι","translit":"aspázomai","english":"greet"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"}]},{"v":20,"text":"All the brothers greet you. Greet one another with a holy kiss.","understanding":"“Greet one another with a φίλημα ἅγιον — a holy kiss.” A kiss on the cheek was a common greeting between family members. In the church it expressed that believers — slaves and free, Jews and Greeks, rich and poor — were one family. For a church torn by factions, it was a command to embody reconciliation in the most physical, personal way.","words":[{"strongs":"G782","hebrew":"ἀσπάζομαι","translit":"aspázomai","english":"greet"},{"strongs":"G5370","hebrew":"φίλημα","translit":"phílēma","english":"kiss"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"}]},{"v":21,"text":"This greeting is by me, Paul, with my own hand.","understanding":"Paul usually dictated his letters to a scribe (Romans 16:22). Here he takes the pen himself: “by me, Paul, with my own χείρ — hand.” His personal signature authenticated the letter (compare 2 Thessalonians 3:17; Galatians 6:11; Colossians 4:18). Imagine the Corinthians seeing the change in handwriting as the letter was read aloud.","words":[{"strongs":"G783","hebrew":"ἀσπασμός","translit":"aspasmós","english":"greeting"},{"strongs":"G1699","hebrew":"ἐμός","translit":"emós","english":"my own"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"}]},{"v":22,"text":"If any man doesn’t love the Lord Jesus Christ, let him be accursed. Come, Lord!","understanding":"In his own hand, two of the sharpest words in the letter. “If anyone does not φιλέω — love, have affection for — the Lord, let him be ἀνάθεμα” — accursed, devoted to destruction. The test of true faith is not gifts, knowledge or eloquence. It is love for Jesus. Then an Aramaic phrase, left untranslated in a Greek letter: μαρανα θα — Maranatha. Divided one way, it means ‘Our Lord, come!’; divided another, ‘Our Lord has come.’ The prayer of Revelation 22:20, “come, Lord Jesus,” strongly favours the first. Its survival in Aramaic shows that the earliest Aramaic-speaking believers were already praying to Jesus as Lord.","words":[{"strongs":"G5368","hebrew":"φιλέω","translit":"philéō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G331","hebrew":"ἀνάθεμα","translit":"anáthema","english":"accursed"},{"strongs":"G3134","hebrew":"μαρὰν ἀθά","translit":"maràn athá","english":"Come, Lord (Maranatha)"}]},{"v":23,"text":"The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ be with you.","understanding":"The letter that began with grace (1:3–4) ends with grace. After all the rebukes, Paul's final blessing is not a threat but χάρις — the undeserved favour of the Lord Jesus, which is the only thing that could ever heal Corinth.","words":[{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]},{"v":24,"text":"My love to all of you in Christ Jesus. Amen.","understanding":"Paul's last line is unique among his letters: “My ἀγάπη to all of you in Christ Jesus.” All of you — including the factions, the proud, the immoral, the litigants, the ones who despised him. Love is not only what Paul commanded in this letter; it is what he gave in it. A father's correction and a father's love were one thing.","words":[{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3956","hebrew":"πᾶς","translit":"pâs","english":"all"},{"strongs":"G281","hebrew":"ἀμήν","translit":"amḗn","english":"Amen"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Coin Box on the First Day","verseRef":"v. 1-4","image":"Picture a Corinthian believer's home on a Sunday — the first day of the week, the day of the resurrection. Before he goes to the gathering in Gaius' house, a dockworker opens a small wooden box under his bed and drops in a few coins from the week's wages. It is not much. But last week he did the same, and the week before. Across the city, a merchant's wife does the same with a larger sum, a slave with a single small coin. Months later, the boxes are brought together. Men the church trusts are chosen to carry the money across the sea to Jerusalem, to Jewish believers they have never met, who are hungry. And Paul calls the money χάρις — grace.","meaning":"Paul places this instruction right after the triumphant end of chapter 15: “your labor is not in vain in the Lord.” The resurrection makes money matter, because what is given to the Lord is never lost. Notice the pattern of giving: on the first day of the week — connected to worship and the resurrection; each one — every believer, not just the wealthy; set aside and store up — planned, regular, intentional; as he may prosper — in proportion to what God has given. And handled with integrity: people the church approves, with letters, so that no one could question the money. Generous, systematic giving to meet real needs across cultural lines is not a distraction from spiritual life. It is resurrection faith with its wallet open."},{"title":"The Door and the Lions","verseRef":"v. 8-9","image":"Picture Ephesus in the spring: the great temple of Artemis gleaming above the city, the theatre carved into the hillside, crowds of pilgrims, magicians, merchants and silversmiths selling shrines. In a lecture hall Paul has borrowed, he has been teaching every day for two years, and the word has spread through the whole province of Asia (Acts 19:9–10). People are burning their books of magic. And in the shadows, the silversmiths' guild is counting its losses and talking about a riot. Paul writes: a great and effective door has opened to me — and there are many adversaries.","meaning":"Notice the little word “and.” Paul does not say the door is open but there are adversaries. He says both are true together, and he stays. Many believers assume that if God opens a door, it will be easy to walk through; and if opposition comes, the door must have closed. Paul's experience was the opposite. The greatest opportunities often come with the fiercest resistance, because the enemy does not waste his effort on closed doors. Of course, opposition alone does not prove God is in something. But it does not prove He isn't. A year later, the same man would walk away from an open door at Troas because his heart was heavy for this very church (2 Corinthians 2:12–13). Discerning doors takes prayer. But never let adversaries alone convince you that God has closed what He opened."},{"title":"The Hand That Took the Pen","verseRef":"v. 21-24","image":"Picture the scene in Ephesus as the letter is finished. Paul has been dictating for days — perhaps weeks — to a scribe, sentence by sentence, through fifteen chapters of rebuke, instruction, theology and pleading. Now the scribe hands him the pen. Paul's own handwriting, perhaps larger and rougher than the scribe's (compare Galatians 6:11), fills the last lines. And in Corinth months later, as the letter is read aloud in Gaius' house, the reader pauses. He holds it up so they can see the different hand. Then he reads Paul's own words: a solemn warning, an Aramaic prayer, a blessing of grace, and a last line no other letter of his ends with: “My love to all of you in Christ Jesus.”","meaning":"After all the correction — factions, immorality, lawsuits, idolatry, disorder, denial of the resurrection — the last word in Paul's own hand is love. Not love for the faction that liked him; love for all of them. That is how the letter's two halves fit together: the rebukes were love, and the love was not soft. And the warning of verse 22 shows what all of it was for: “If any man doesn’t love the Lord Jesus Christ, let him be accursed.” The final test of a Christian is not giftedness, knowledge or eloquence. It is love for Jesus. And the final prayer is Maranatha — ‘Our Lord, come!’ Paul ends the letter the way the whole Bible ends: “Yes, come, Lord Jesus” (Revelation 22:20). Everything in 1 Corinthians is meant to be lived while waiting for Him."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Collection for Jerusalem (v. 1-4)","body":"On the first day of every week each believer is to set aside a proportion of income; trusted delegates chosen by the church will carry the gift to Jerusalem, perhaps with Paul."},{"label":"Paul's Travel Plans (v. 5-9)","body":"Paul plans to come through Macedonia and perhaps winter in Corinth, but will stay in Ephesus until Pentecost, because a great door has opened and there are many adversaries."},{"label":"Timothy and Apollos (v. 10-12)","body":"Receive Timothy without intimidating him, for he does the Lord's work; Paul urged Apollos to come, but he will come when the time is right."},{"label":"Final Commands (v. 13-14)","body":"Watch, stand firm in the faith, be courageous, be strong — and let everything be done in love."},{"label":"Honour Faithful Servants (v. 15-18)","body":"Submit to servants like the household of Stephanas, who devoted themselves to serving the saints, and recognise those like Stephanas, Fortunatus and Achaicus who refreshed Paul's spirit."},{"label":"Greetings, Warning and Blessing (v. 19-24)","body":"Greetings from Asia, Aquila, Priscilla and the house church; greet one another with a holy kiss; in his own hand Paul pronounces a curse on anyone who does not love the Lord, prays Maranatha, and closes with grace and his love for all."}],"sections":[{"heading":"From the Resurrection to the Offering Box","paragraphs":["It can feel like an anticlimax. Chapter 15 soars to the heights of resurrection hope — “Death is swallowed up in victory” — and chapter 16 opens with fundraising. But that transition is the point. Paul has just written, “be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the Lord’s work, because you know that your labor is not in vain in the Lord” (15:58). The very next sentence is about money. Resurrection faith does not float above practical life. It reaches into the wallet. If what is given to the Lord is never wasted, then generous giving is one of the most reasonable things a resurrection people can do.","The collection was one of Paul's great projects during these years. The church in Jerusalem was poor — possibly because of famine (Acts 11:28–30), persecution, and the economic marginalisation of Jewish believers. Paul gathered gifts from his largely Gentile churches in Galatia, Macedonia and Achaia to relieve them. He explains the theology in Romans 15:27: “if the Gentiles have been made partakers of their spiritual things, they owe it to them also to serve them in fleshly things.” The collection was more than charity; it was a visible demonstration that Jews and Gentiles were one body in Christ. A church that had been divided along social lines at the Lord's Table was now invited to give across ethnic lines to people they had never met.","Verses 2 and 3 give a pattern for Christian giving that is still instructive. It was weekly: “On the first day of the week” — the day of the resurrection, and one of the earliest references to Christians meeting on Sunday (compare Acts 20:7). It was universal: “each one of you.” It was planned: “save,” set aside and store up, not a last-minute response to an emotional appeal. It was proportional: “as he may prosper.” And it was handled with transparent integrity: the church itself chose trusted delegates, who carried letters of introduction (v. 3). Paul would write later, “Having regard for honorable things, not only in the sight of the Lord, but also in the sight of men” (2 Corinthians 8:21). In 2 Corinthians 8 and 9 he will develop the theme at length, including the principle that “God loves a cheerful giver” (9:7). Money is not a separate subject from the gospel. It is one of the clearest places where the gospel shows."]},{"heading":"Open Doors, Opposition and the Workers Who Carry the Work","paragraphs":["Paul's travel plans in verses 5 to 9 are ordinary and human: he hopes to visit, maybe to winter there, but “if the Lord permits.” He holds his plans loosely under God's sovereignty, as James urges: “you ought to say, ‘If the Lord wills, we will both live, and do this or that’” (James 4:15). And these very plans would later change, prompting the Corinthians to accuse him of fickleness — an accusation he answers in 2 Corinthians 1:15–24. But verse 9 contains a principle that every believer needs: “a great and effective door has opened to me, and there are many adversaries.” The opportunity and the opposition came together. Acts 19 describes what that looked like in Ephesus: extraordinary miracles, people burning magic books worth fifty thousand pieces of silver, and then a riot instigated by silversmiths whose shrine business was collapsing.","Verses 10 to 12 give a glimpse of Paul's co-workers. Timothy is coming, and Paul writes to protect him: “see that he is with you without fear... let no one despise him” (vv. 10–11). Timothy was young and apparently timid, and later letters show Paul encouraging him not to be intimidated (1 Timothy 4:12; 2 Timothy 1:7). A proud, gift-obsessed church like Corinth could easily have looked down on a young, unimpressive messenger. Paul insists that Timothy does the Lord's work “as I also do.” And then Apollos: Paul strongly urged him to visit, but Apollos was unwilling at that time (v. 12). This one verse shows that the factions in Corinth did not reflect any rivalry between the leaders. Paul wanted the very man some Corinthians preferred to him to go to them, and Apollos seems to have stayed away precisely to avoid fuelling the faction that used his name.","Verses 15 to 18 honour the faithful servants who often go unnoticed. The household of Stephanas, the first converts in Achaia, “set themselves to serve the saints” (v. 15). No one appointed them; they appointed themselves to serve. Paul asks the church to submit to such people and to everyone who works and labours (v. 16). Christian leadership is not a title that confers authority; it is recognised in those who serve. Then he names three men who travelled from Corinth to Ephesus — Stephanas, Fortunatus and Achaicus — and says they “refreshed my spirit and yours” (v. 18). Fortunatus and Achaicus may well have been slaves or freedmen; their names were typical of that class. In a church obsessed with status, Paul gives public honour to people whose only credential was faithful, refreshing service. “Therefore acknowledge those who are like that.”"]},{"heading":"Watch, Stand, Be Strong — and Do It All in Love","paragraphs":["Verses 13 and 14 are the letter's final summary, and they come in two parts that must be held together. First, four rapid military commands: “Watch! Stand firm in the faith! Be courageous! Be strong!” (v. 13). Γρηγορεῖτε — stay awake, be alert, the word Jesus used in Gethsemane (Matthew 26:41). Στήκετε ἐν τῇ πίστει — stand firm, hold your ground in the faith, like soldiers holding a line. Ἀνδρίζεσθε — act with courage, the word used in the Greek Old Testament's charge to Joshua to “be strong and courageous.” Κραταιοῦσθε — be strengthened. After everything Corinth had done — divisions, compromise, confusion about basic truths — they needed vigilance, stability and courage, not more spiritual excitement.","But then immediately: “Let all that you do be done in love” (v. 14). Strength without love becomes harshness. Courage without love becomes arrogance. Firmness without love becomes rigidity. Paul will not let the church hear the military commands without the command that governs them. And the phrase is all-encompassing: “all that you do.” This one verse summarises the entire letter. Every issue Paul has addressed — factions, discipline, lawsuits, marriage, idol food, head coverings, the Lord's Supper, spiritual gifts — is finally a question of love. It is chapter 13 compressed into a single sentence, and it is the lens through which everything else in 1 Corinthians is to be read and lived.","Then Paul takes the pen from his scribe and writes the final lines himself (v. 21). The first thing he writes in his own hand is shocking: “If any man doesn’t love the Lord Jesus Christ, let him be accursed” (v. 22). He uses φιλέω here, the warmer word for affection — the same word Jesus used when He asked Peter on the beach whether he loved Him (John 21:15–17). The ultimate test of genuine Christianity is not gifts or knowledge but love for Jesus. Then an Aramaic phrase, μαρανα θα, preserved untranslated in a Greek letter to a Greek church — almost certainly ‘Our Lord, come!’ It appears again in one of the earliest Christian worship manuals outside the New Testament, the Didache, in a prayer connected to the Lord's Supper, and it parallels the Bible's final prayer: “Yes, come, Lord Jesus” (Revelation 22:20). Its survival shows that the earliest Aramaic-speaking church prayed to Jesus as Lord. And the letter ends with grace — “The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ be with you” — and with a line unique in Paul's letters: “My love to all of you in Christ Jesus.” All of you. After sixteen chapters of correction, the last word is love."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["The collection Paul organises in verses 1 to 4 runs as a thread through his later letters and Acts. In Romans he explains its purpose: “I am going to Jerusalem, serving the saints. For it has been the good pleasure of Macedonia and Achaia to make a certain contribution for the poor among the saints who are at Jerusalem... For if the Gentiles have been made partakers of their spiritual things, they owe it to them also to serve them in fleshly things” (Romans 15:25–27). In 2 Corinthians 8 and 9 he devotes two whole chapters to encouraging the Corinthians to complete what they had begun, grounding it in the grace of Christ, who “though he was rich, yet for your sakes he became poor” (2 Corinthians 8:9), and in the principle that “God loves a cheerful giver” (2 Corinthians 9:7). And in Acts, before Felix, Paul says, “after some years, I came to bring gifts for the needy to my nation, and offerings” (Acts 24:17). The coins dropped into boxes in Corinth on the first day of the week reached Jerusalem.","The final commands of verse 13 echo throughout the New Testament. Jesus told His disciples in Gethsemane to “Watch and pray, that you don’t enter into temptation” (Matthew 26:41). Paul tells the Ephesians, “be strong in the Lord, and in the strength of his might. Put on the whole armor of God, that you may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil” (Ephesians 6:10–11). Peter writes, “Be sober and self-controlled. Be watchful. Your adversary, the devil, walks around like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may devour. Withstand him steadfast in your faith” (1 Peter 5:8–9). And Paul encourages the young and timid Timothy: “For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control” (2 Timothy 1:7) — power and love together, exactly the pairing of verses 13 and 14.","And the Aramaic cry of verse 22, Maranatha, is answered in the last words of the Bible. The risen Christ says, “Yes, I come quickly,” and the church replies, “Amen! Yes, come, Lord Jesus” (Revelation 22:20). Paul's personal signature, too, links this letter to his others: “The greeting of me, Paul, with my own hand, which is the sign in every letter” (2 Thessalonians 3:17); “See with what large letters I write to you with my own hand” (Galatians 6:11); “The salutation of me, Paul, with my own hand: remember my bonds. Grace be with you” (Colossians 4:18). In every letter the last word in his own hand is grace. In this one he adds love. The pastor who had corrected Corinth so sharply wanted them to know, in his own handwriting, that he loved every one of them."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Collection / contribution","hebrew":"λογία","translit":"logía","strongs":"G3048","verses":"v. 1, 2","insight":"Strong's: “a contribution.” The word appears only in these two verses in the New Testament. Outside the Bible it was used for special collections, sometimes for religious purposes or taxes. Paul never uses the ordinary word for money when describing this project. Elsewhere he calls it χάρις, grace (v. 3; 2 Corinthians 8:4, 6–7), κοινωνία, fellowship (2 Corinthians 8:4; 9:13), διακονία, service (2 Corinthians 8:4; 9:1), and εὐλογία, blessing (2 Corinthians 9:5). The vocabulary itself is a theology of giving. A financial gift for brothers in need is grace in action, fellowship made visible, service rendered to Christ and a blessing that flows both ways."},{"english":"Door","hebrew":"θύρα","translit":"thýra","strongs":"G2374","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's: “a portal or entrance (the opening or the closure, literally or figuratively).” Paul uses an open door as his image for a God-given opportunity to preach the gospel — here, at Troas (2 Corinthians 2:12), and in his prayer request to the Colossians: “that God may open to us a door for the word” (Colossians 4:3). In Acts 14:27 God “had opened a door of faith to the nations.” The risen Christ tells the church in Philadelphia, “I have set before you an open door, which no one can shut” (Revelation 3:8). The important truth in verse 9 is that God's open doors are often surrounded by adversaries. The opposition does not shut what God has opened."},{"english":"Watch / stay awake","hebrew":"γρηγορεύω","translit":"grēgoreúō","strongs":"G1127","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's: “to keep awake, i.e. watch (literally or figuratively).” It is the word Jesus used repeatedly when warning His disciples to be ready for His return: “Watch therefore, for you don’t know in what hour your Lord comes” (Matthew 24:42), and in Gethsemane, “Watch and pray, that you don’t enter into temptation” (Matthew 26:41). Paul uses it in 1 Thessalonians 5:6 — “let’s watch and be sober.” The command is to spiritual alertness: refusing to drift, to fall asleep in comfort, to be caught off guard by temptation or false teaching. Corinth had been dozing — some had even been drunk at the Lord's Table. Paul's first final command is: wake up and stay awake."},{"english":"Be courageous / act like men","hebrew":"ἀνδρίζομαι","translit":"andrízomai","strongs":"G407","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's: “to act manly.” This is the only occurrence of the word in the New Testament. It comes from ἀνήρ, man, and in Greek culture it meant to show courage, to act bravely, to face danger without shrinking. In the Greek Old Testament it appears in the repeated charge to Joshua: “Be strong and courageous” (Joshua 1:6–7, 9), and in Psalm 27:14 and 31:24. Paul is not addressing only men; the whole church is commanded. And the courage he means is not bravado. It is the steady nerve to stand firm in the faith when it is unpopular, to hold the truth when others drift, to endure opposition without retreat. And it must always be followed by verse 14: courage, done in love."},{"english":"Maranatha — Our Lord, come!","hebrew":"μαρὰν ἀθά","translit":"maràn athá","strongs":"G3134","verses":"v. 22","insight":"Strong's: “maranatha, i.e. an exclamation of the approaching divine judgment.” It is an Aramaic phrase written in Greek letters, and can be divided as marana tha, ‘Our Lord, come!’, or maran atha, ‘Our Lord has come.’ The prayer of Revelation 22:20 — “Yes, come, Lord Jesus” — strongly suggests the first. Its presence in a Greek letter to a Gentile church shows that it had become a fixed part of Christian worship, carried over from the very first Aramaic-speaking believers in Jerusalem. That is historically significant: within the first generation, Jewish believers were praying to Jesus as “our Lord” — an address only appropriate to God. Maranatha is the heartbeat of the church between the resurrection and the return: Lord, come."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: whether your resurrection faith has reached your wallet, whether opposition has convinced you that God has closed a door He opened, and whether love is the root of everything you do.","items":[{"title":"Resurrection Faith With an Open Wallet","body":"Paul moves straight from “your labor is not in vain in the Lord” to “On the first day of the week, let each one of you save.” The Spirit is asking whether your belief in the resurrection has reached your money. Do you give regularly, intentionally and proportionally — or only when moved by an appeal? Do you give across lines of culture and distance to brothers and sisters you have never met? Is your giving grace — a joyful response to the grace you have received — or a reluctant tax? If Christ is risen, nothing given to Him is lost. Ask Him today what He wants you to set aside, and start this first day of the week."},{"title":"The Door Is Still Open","body":"You stepped through a door God opened — a ministry, a calling, a relationship, a work — and then the adversaries came. Criticism, resistance, spiritual attack, discouragement. And you have begun to wonder whether you misread God, whether the door has closed. The Spirit wants you to see verse 9 again: “a great and effective door has opened to me, and there are many adversaries.” Both at once. Opposition is not proof that God is absent. Often it is evidence that you are standing in exactly the place the enemy does not want you to be. Pray for discernment, and seek counsel. But do not let the adversaries alone persuade you to walk away from what God has opened."},{"title":"Is Love the Root?","body":"“Let all that you do be done in love.” All. Your service, your correction of others, your theology, your giving, your work, your parenting, your worship, your conversations online. The Spirit is asking whether love is the root of what you do, or whether it has been replaced by duty, pride, fear, competition or habit. And verse 22 gives the deepest form of the question: do you love the Lord Jesus? Not just believe in Him, serve Him or know about Him — love Him, with the warm affection of φιλέω. If that love has grown cold, do not try to manufacture it. Go back to the cross and the empty tomb, and let Him love you again. Then pray the church's oldest prayer: Maranatha."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Giving Check","body":"Look at your giving over the last three months. Is it regular (weekly or monthly)? Is it planned in advance? Is it proportional to what God has given you? Does any of it reach believers in need beyond your own church and culture? Write down one change you will make."},{"title":"The Door Check","body":"Is there an opportunity you have pulled back from because of opposition? Write it down. Then ask honestly: did God close this door, or did I let the adversaries close it? Who could I ask for wise counsel about it?"},{"title":"The Refreshing Check","body":"Stephanas, Fortunatus and Achaicus “refreshed my spirit.” Who in your life refreshes you? Have you acknowledged them? And who would say you refresh them? If no one comes to mind, ask what it would take for you to become that kind of believer."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A First-Day Offering","body":"This coming Sunday, before you leave for church, set aside a specific amount for the Lord — planned, proportional and prayed over. Do it the same way every first day of the week for the next month. In addition, choose one ministry that serves believers in poverty or persecution in another part of the world, and give a gift to it this month. As you give, pray: “Lord, this is grace. Because Christ is risen, my labour — and my giving — is not in vain in You.”"},{"title":"Honouring a Stephanas","body":"Think of one person in your church who has quietly “set themselves to serve the saints” — not because of a title, but because they saw a need and filled it. This week, acknowledge them as verse 18 commands: tell them specifically how they have refreshed you, thank them publicly if appropriate, and ask how you could share their load. Then close the week by praying the last words of this letter over your church, aloud: “Maranatha. The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ be with you. My love to all of you in Christ Jesus.”"}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You are risen, and my labour is not in vain in You. So I give You my money, my plans, my doors and my heart. Teach me to give on the first day of the week with joy, regularly and generously, as grace returned for grace. Let my giving cross every line that once divided me from my brothers and sisters. Father, thank You for every door You have opened. When adversaries come, do not let me mistake their noise for Your voice. Give me the discernment to know when You have closed a door, and the courage to stay when You have opened one. Make me watchful, firm in the faith, courageous and strong — and let all that I do be done in love. Thank You for the faithful servants who have refreshed my spirit. Help me to honour them, to follow their example, and to become a refreshment to others. Lord, I love You. Not only believe in You, not only serve You — I love You. Where my love has grown cold, warm it again with Your grace. And I join the oldest prayer of Your church: Maranatha. Our Lord, come! Until You do, let Your grace be with me, and let Your love flow through me to all. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Be Strong and Courageous","ref":"Joshua 1:1-9","relatedVerses":"v. 13","summary":"After Moses died, the task of leading Israel into the promised land fell on Joshua. Ahead lay the Jordan in flood, fortified cities, and nations stronger than Israel. And God spoke to him three times in nine verses with the same command: be strong and courageous. He promised that no one would stand against Joshua all the days of his life; that as He had been with Moses, He would be with him; that He would not leave or forsake him. And He tied Joshua's courage to the Word: this book of the law was not to depart from his mouth. The Greek Old Testament uses the same verb Paul uses in 1 Corinthians 16:13 for “be courageous.” Joshua's courage was not self-confidence. It was confidence in the God who goes ahead. That is the courage Paul commands: stand firm in the faith, because God is with you."},{"title":"Joseph Stores Up Grain","ref":"Genesis 41:25-57; 47:13-26","relatedVerses":"v. 1-4","summary":"When Joseph interpreted Pharaoh's dreams, he warned of seven years of plenty followed by seven years of famine, and advised that during the good years a portion of the harvest be set aside and stored in the cities. For seven years, the grain was gathered and stored until it could not be measured. When the famine came, there was bread in Egypt, and people came from every land to buy grain — including Joseph's own brothers. Joseph's plan was steady, proportional and planned in advance, and it saved countless lives, including the family through whom the Messiah would come. Paul's instruction in 1 Corinthians 16:2 follows the same wisdom: set aside regularly, as God prospers you, so that when the need comes, the provision is ready. Systematic generosity is not unspiritual. It is how God feeds the hungry."},{"title":"The Room on the Roof for Elisha","ref":"2 Kings 4:8-37","relatedVerses":"v. 15-18","summary":"A wealthy woman in Shunem noticed the prophet Elisha passing through her town and urged him to eat with her. Then she said to her husband that this man was a holy man of God, and suggested they build a small room on the roof with a bed, a table, a chair and a lamp, so that he would have a place to rest whenever he came. No one asked her to do it; she set herself to serve. Elisha was refreshed by her hospitality and asked what he could do for her. Through him, God gave her a son, and later raised that son from the dead. Like the household of Stephanas, the Shunammite woman devoted herself to serving God's servants, and her quiet, practical kindness refreshed the man of God. God honoured her far beyond anything she had given."}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"1 Corinthians","titleEmojis":"✝️❤️","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"The Cross That Reaches Every Room of Your Life","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of 1 Corinthians in one overarching thought, it is this: the crucified and risen Christ is not only how you are saved — He is the measure of everything you do with your gifts, your body, your rights, your church and your future, and the way He measures it is love.","paragraphs":["Start with the man's name, because he chose it himself. Born Saul, after Israel's tall, impressive first king, he became known in the Gentile world by his Roman name, Paulus — a Latin word that means “small.” Whether or not he picked it for its meaning, he lived it. In this letter he calls himself “the least of the apostles, who is not worthy to be called an apostle” (15:9), arrives in Corinth “in weakness, in fear, and in much trembling” (2:3), and describes himself and his companions as “the filth of the world, the dirt wiped off by all” (4:13). And this small man wrote to the most ambitious city in Greece to tell it that God builds His church out of nobodies, “that no flesh should boast before God” (1:29).","First Corinthians is the most practical book Paul ever wrote, because it was written to a church that had everything and was getting almost everything wrong. They had every spiritual gift (1:7) — and they were splitting into fan clubs behind their favourite preachers. They were proud of their knowledge — and tolerating a sexual sin the pagans would not tolerate. They called each other brothers on Sunday — and sued each other on Monday. They celebrated the Lord's Supper — while the rich got drunk and the poor went home hungry. They spoke in tongues — in a chaos that made visitors think they were mad. And some of them had stopped believing that the dead will rise. It is the portrait of a church that had received grace and was using it to compete.","And Paul's answer to every single problem is the same. He does not give them a better technique. He gives them the cross. “For I determined not to know anything among you, except Jesus Christ, and him crucified” (2:2). Factions? “Was Paul crucified for you?” (1:13). Sexual sin? “You were bought with a price” (6:20). Pride in knowledge? “The brother for whose sake Christ died” (8:11). Contempt at the Lord's Table? “You proclaim the Lord’s death until he comes” (11:26). Denial of the resurrection? “If Christ has not been raised, your faith is vain; you are still in your sins” (15:17). Every room in the Corinthians' lives — the meeting hall, the courtroom, the bedroom, the dining room, the temple, the graveyard — gets the same visitor. The cross walks in and rearranges the furniture.","And then, between the chapters on spiritual gifts, Paul writes the chapter the whole world knows — and almost nobody reads in context. First Corinthians 13 is not wedding poetry. It is the scalpel. “If I speak with the languages of men and of angels, but don’t have love, I have become sounding brass, or a clanging cymbal” (13:1). The love Paul describes there — patient, kind, not envious, not proud, not self-seeking, keeping no ledger of wrongs — is the shape of the cross in a human life. It is what Christ is. And it is the one thing that will survive into eternity: “But now faith, hope, and love remain—these three. The greatest of these is love” (13:13). The book ends with the same note, in Paul's own handwriting: “Let all that you do be done in love” (16:14) and “My love to all of you in Christ Jesus” (16:24). Sixteen chapters of correction, and the last word is love."]},"map":{"intro":"First Corinthians is the seventh book of the New Testament and the second of Paul's letters in the canonical order — placed after Romans, though it was written two or three years before it. The order is not chronological; it is pedagogical. Romans teaches you the gospel. First Corinthians shows you that gospel walking into a real church and dealing with real mess. Here is why it sits where it does:","sections":[{"title":"Romans Proclaimed — Corinth Applied","body":"Romans is the gospel argued in order: sin, justification, union with Christ, the Spirit, Israel, and then the life of the body (Romans 12–15). First Corinthians takes nearly every one of those doctrines and drops it into a congregation that is failing to live it. Romans 6 says the old self was crucified; 1 Corinthians 6 says “such were some of you, but you were washed” (6:11). Romans 12 says we are one body with differing gifts; 1 Corinthians 12 shows that body arguing about which gift is best. Romans 14 says not to destroy with your food the one for whom Christ died; 1 Corinthians 8 to 10 shows what that means at an actual dinner in a city of temples. If Romans is the blueprint, 1 Corinthians is the building site — with the mud, the noise and the arguments among the builders."},{"title":"The Only Detailed Window Into a First-Century Church","body":"No other book of the Bible shows you so much about what early Christian congregations were actually like. Here is the earliest written account of the Lord's Supper (11:23–26), written before any of the Gospels. Here is the oldest creed of the resurrection (15:3–5), handed down within a few years of the event. Here is a picture of worship in which “each one of you has a psalm, has a teaching, has a revelation, has another language, has an interpretation” (14:26). Here is one of the earliest pieces of evidence that believers met on the first day of the week (16:2), and the Aramaic prayer “Maranatha” preserved from the very first church (16:22). Without 1 Corinthians, we would know far less about how the first Christians lived, worshipped, argued and loved."},{"title":"The Resurrection Chapter of the Bible","body":"The Old Testament hints at resurrection — Job's “in my flesh shall I see God,” Isaiah 26, Ezekiel's valley of bones, Daniel 12. The Gospels record it. But 1 Corinthians 15 is where Scripture explains it most fully: why it matters (if Christ is not raised, your faith is vain), how it works (Christ the first fruits, then those who are His at His coming), what kind of body it brings (sown perishable, raised imperishable), and what it means for today (your labour is not in vain in the Lord). Paul placed it at the end of the letter on purpose, because every other problem in Corinth — their contempt for the body, their arrogance, their sense that they had already arrived — came from a weak grip on the resurrection. Get the end of the story right, and everything in the middle changes."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: the next two books continue the story of Paul and his churches, and both take what you have learned here into deeper waters. In 1 Corinthians Paul corrected a church from a position of settled authority, as a father with a rod or with gentleness. In the next letter that authority is under attack, and you will see what it costs him. And in the letter after that, the attack is on the gospel itself.","books":[{"book":"2 Corinthians","connection":"Same church, about a year later — and almost everything has gone wrong. First Corinthians did not fix Corinth. Paul made a painful visit, then wrote a severe letter “out of much affliction and anguish of heart… with many tears” (2 Corinthians 2:4), and rivals arrived who mocked him as weak and unimpressive. In 1 Corinthians Paul taught that God chooses the weak things of the world (1:27) and that he came to them in weakness and trembling (2:3). In 2 Corinthians that theology is tested in his own body. He calls himself a cracked jar: “But we have this treasure in clay vessels, that the exceeding greatness of the power may be of God, and not from ourselves” (2 Corinthians 4:7). He begs three times for a thorn to be removed and receives the answer that completes 1 Corinthians 1: “My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness” (2 Corinthians 12:9). And the resurrection hope of 1 Corinthians 15 becomes his strength in suffering: “For he was crucified through weakness, yet he lives through the power of God” (2 Corinthians 13:4). Watch, too, for the man disciplined in 1 Corinthians 5 — or perhaps another offender — as Paul pleads for the church to forgive and restore. The father who taught them in the first letter now shows them his scars."},{"book":"Galatians","connection":"Then the danger moves from behaviour to the gospel itself. In Corinth the problem was a church that took its freedom too far — “All things are lawful for me” — and used it to please itself. In Galatia the problem is the opposite: believers being told that faith in Christ is not enough, that they must also be circumcised and keep the law. Paul's tone is the sharpest in any of his letters: “I marvel that you are so quickly deserting him who called you in the grace of Christ to a different ‘good news’” (Galatians 1:6). And he goes back to the same place he went in Corinth — the cross: “Foolish Galatians, who has bewitched you not to obey the truth, before whose eyes Jesus Christ was openly portrayed among you as crucified?” (Galatians 3:1). Then he holds both errors in a single sentence that 1 Corinthians has prepared you to understand: “For you, brothers, were called for freedom. Only don’t use your freedom for gain to the flesh, but through love be servants to one another” (Galatians 5:13). Corinth abused freedom; Galatia was surrendering it. Both are answered by Christ crucified and by love. You have just spent sixteen chapters learning that freedom exists for love. Galatians will teach you to fight for that freedom."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"The Cross Ends Every Boast","anchorRef":"1 Corinthians 1:18","body":"“For the word of the cross is foolishness to those who are dying, but to us who are saved it is the power of God” (1:18). Corinth measured people by eloquence, wisdom, status and success, and it brought those measurements into the church — “I follow Paul,” “I follow Apollos.” Paul answered with the message that offends everyone: a crucified Messiah, a stumbling block to Jews and foolishness to Greeks, and yet the power and wisdom of God. God deliberately chose the foolish, the weak, the lowly and the despised, “that no flesh should boast before God” (1:29). You now own this truth: in Christ, the ladder of status has been sawn in half. You have nothing you did not receive (4:7). Your preachers belong to you; you belong to Christ (3:21–23). The only boast left is in the Lord."},{"title":"Your Body Belongs to the Lord","anchorRef":"1 Corinthians 6:19–20","body":"“Or don’t you know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit who is in you, whom you have from God? You are not your own, for you were bought with a price. Therefore glorify God in your body” (6:19–20). Corinth believed the body did not matter — so some indulged it without limit and others despised it. Paul answered both from the resurrection: “God raised up the Lord, and will also raise us up by his power” (6:14). The body has a future, so what you do with it now matters forever. Sexual immorality is fled, not managed. Marriage is mutual self-giving, not ownership. Freedom is tested by two questions: does it build up, and will it master me? You were bought in the marketplace with the blood of Christ. Your body is now the Holy of Holies."},{"title":"Freedom Is for Love","anchorRef":"1 Corinthians 8:1; 9:19","body":"“Knowledge puffs up, but love builds up” (8:1). The strong in Corinth were right that idols are nothing — and their correct theology was destroying a weaker brother “for whose sake Christ died” (8:11). Paul showed them a man who had more rights than any of them and refused to use them: “For though I was free from all, I brought myself under bondage to all, that I might gain the more” (9:19). The question is never only, “Am I allowed?” It is, “Will this build up my brother? Will it hinder the gospel? Will it bring me under its power?” And the summary of it all: “Whether therefore you eat, or drink, or whatever you do, do all to the glory of God” (10:31). Freedom is not the right to please yourself. It is the power to serve."},{"title":"One Body, Many Gifts — and the Greatest Is Love","anchorRef":"1 Corinthians 12:27; 13:13","body":"“Now you are the body of Christ, and members individually” (12:27). Every believer has a gift, given by the Spirit as He wills, for the good of all (12:7, 11). No member can say, “I don’t belong,” and no member can say, “I have no need of you.” God gives more honour to the hidden members, so that the body suffers and rejoices together. And the gifts are measured by love: tongues without love are noise, knowledge without love is nothing, sacrifice without love gains nothing (13:1–3). Love is patient and kind, keeps no ledger of wrongs, and never fails. Prophecy and tongues and knowledge will pass away when we see Him face to face. “But now faith, hope, and love remain—these three. The greatest of these is love” (13:13)."},{"title":"The Resurrection Makes Nothing Wasted","anchorRef":"1 Corinthians 15:58","body":"“Therefore, my beloved brothers, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the Lord’s work, because you know that your labor is not in vain in the Lord” (15:58). Paul staked everything on one historical fact: “that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures” (15:3–4). If not, faith is vain and we are still in our sins. But now Christ has been raised, the first fruits of a guaranteed harvest. Death is the last enemy, and it will be swallowed up in victory. Its sting is gone, because Christ took the sin that gave it power. And because the end of the story is resurrection, every act of faithful love in the middle of the story counts forever."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of 1 Corinthians practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":1,"title":"Tear Down the Banner, Take Up the Cross","lesson":"The Lesson: “Is Christ divided? Was Paul crucified for you?” (1:13). The first sin Paul confronts in this letter is not immorality but party spirit — believers attaching their identity to human leaders and looking down on others. And his answer is the cross, which flattens every human hierarchy: “God chose the foolish things of the world that he might put to shame those who are wise” (1:27). The church is built from people the world overlooked, so that no one can boast. Everything you are proud of, you received (4:7).","practice":"The Practice: This week, do three things. First, name your banner — the leader, church, tradition or style you feel superior about — and confess any contempt you have felt for believers who differ from you. Second, reach out to one believer from a different church or tradition, or someone in your own church you have kept at a distance, and do something to mend the net: a meal, a prayer together, a genuine word of thanks for God's work in them. Third, write down your three greatest strengths, and beside each one write how you received it. Read it aloud and finish with 1:31: “He who boasts, let him boast in the Lord.”"},{"step":2,"title":"Give the Temple Back — and Lay Down a Right","lesson":"The Lesson: “You are not your own, for you were bought with a price. Therefore glorify God in your body” (6:19–20). And: “Therefore if food causes my brother to stumble, I will eat no meat forever more” (8:13). Corinth's two great errors were treating the body as unimportant and treating freedom as the right to please oneself. Paul answered both with the cross and the resurrection: your body was purchased and will be raised, and your freedom exists for the sake of your brother and the gospel.","practice":"The Practice: Tonight, dedicate your body to God aloud, part by part — eyes, hands, mouth, appetite, sexuality — and name one specific area where you have been treating your body as your own. Decide on one concrete step to flee that temptation within 24 hours: delete the app, end the conversation, change the route, tell a trusted friend. Then choose one freedom you exercise that might cause a weaker believer to stumble, and lay it down for thirty days as an act of love, praying for that person by name every time you miss it. Map your way of escape (10:13) for your most frequent temptation, and take it next time it comes."},{"step":3,"title":"Do Everything in Love — Because He Is Risen","lesson":"The Lesson: “If I speak with the languages of men and of angels, but don’t have love, I have become sounding brass, or a clanging cymbal” (13:1). And: “your labor is not in vain in the Lord” (15:58). The letter ends with “Let all that you do be done in love” (16:14). Every gift, every act of service, every right and every correction must be soaked in the love that is patient, kind, and keeps no record of wrongs. And the resurrection guarantees that not one act of such love will ever be wasted.","practice":"The Practice: Read 13:4–7 aloud with your own name in place of “love.” Circle the two lines where your voice catches, and ask the Spirit to grow those fruits in you this month. Name one person you have been keeping a ledger against, and close the account in prayer — tear up the list. Then take the labour for the Lord you are most tempted to give up on, and do one faithful act in it this week, writing the date on a page titled “Seeds Sown.” On the first day of next week, set aside a planned, proportional gift for the Lord's work (16:2). And end each day this week by praying the oldest prayer of the church: Maranatha — Our Lord, come."}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Father, I close this letter as a Corinthian who has met the cross. I declare that the word of the cross, which the world calls foolishness, is to me the power of God. I renounce every banner I have carried and every boast I have made. I have nothing that I did not receive. Christ Jesus is my wisdom, my righteousness, my sanctification and my redemption, and I boast in Him alone. I declare that I am Your temple. My body is not my own; I was bought with a price — the precious blood of Jesus. I was washed, I was sanctified, I was justified in the name of the Lord Jesus and by the Spirit of my God. I will flee sexual immorality and I will flee idolatry, and when temptation comes I will look for the way of escape that You, the faithful God, have already made. I declare that my freedom is for love. I will not use my knowledge to inflate myself or my liberty to wound my brother for whom Christ died. Like Paul, I will lay down my rights so that I might win some. Whatever I eat, whatever I drink, whatever I do, I will do it to the glory of God. I declare that I am a member of the body of Christ. I will not say, ‘I don’t belong,’ and I will not say to any member, ‘I have no need of you.’ I will honour the hidden members, suffer with those who suffer and rejoice with those who are honoured. I will use the gift You gave me for the profit of all. And above every gift I will pursue love — love that is patient and kind, that does not envy or boast, that keeps no record of wrongs, that bears, believes, hopes and endures all things, and that never fails. I declare that Christ died for my sins according to the Scriptures, that He was buried, and that He was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures. He is the first fruits, and I am part of His harvest. One day, in a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trumpet, I will be changed. Death, where is your sting? Hades, where is your victory? Thanks be to God, who gives me the victory through my Lord Jesus Christ! Therefore I will be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, because I know that my labour is not in vain in You. I will watch, I will stand firm in the faith, I will be courageous and strong — and I will let all that I do be done in love. Maranatha! Come, Lord Jesus. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"}});
